-export const DEVOTIONALS_2 = [
-  {
-    title: "The Discipline of Being Unimpressive",
-    slug: "devo-being-unimpressive",
-    excerpt:
-      "The kingdom does not run on the thing you were hoping would make you notable.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "4 min",
-    body: `<blockquote>"But God chose what is foolish in the world to shame the wise; God chose what is weak in the world to shame the strong." (1 Corinthians 1:27)</blockquote><p>I have spent more of my life than I want to admit trying to be impressive. Not in the crude way, not the way a teenager flexes. The quiet way. The way a pastor learns to drop a book title into conversation, to have the right answer ready before the question finishes, to be the person in the room others turn toward. I told myself it was excellence. It was hunger to be seen.</p><p>Paul takes that hunger apart in one sentence. The people God builds His work through are not the ones who would have made the shortlist. Not the strong. Not the wise. The overlooked and the ordinary, the ones nobody was watching. God did not pick them despite their smallness. He picked them because their smallness left no room for anyone to mistake the glory as theirs.</p><p>This is not a compliment to mediocrity. Paul is not blessing laziness or calling us to hide our gifts under a bushel. He is naming a deeper thing. The moment my usefulness to God depends on being impressive, I have made the work about me. And the work is never about me. It was never going to be.</p><p>There is a discipline hidden here, and it is harder than fasting. The discipline of doing the good thing where no one will see it. Of serving the person who cannot advance you. Of writing the sentence that will help someone and never carry your name. Of being, on purpose, unimpressive.</p><p>I am still bad at this. I catch myself measuring a day by who noticed it. But I am learning, slowly, that the life God uses is not the life that dazzled. It is the life that was faithful in the room where the lights were off.</p><p>You do not have to be remarkable to be used. You have to be available. The two have almost nothing to do with each other, and most of us spend years confusing them.</p>`,
-  },
-  {
-    title: "What Waiting Is Doing to You",
-    slug: "devo-what-waiting-does",
-    excerpt: "The delay is not empty. Something is being made in it.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "4 min",
-    body: `<blockquote>"I waited patiently for the LORD; he inclined to me and heard my cry." (Psalm 40:1)</blockquote><p>Waiting is the part of faith we would edit out if we could. We would keep the answered prayer and cut the years between the asking and the answer. We treat the wait as dead time, a delay in the delivery, a nothing that has to be survived until the something arrives.</p><p>But the Psalmist does not say he endured a delay. He says he waited, and the waiting itself was the posture that let God incline toward him. The wait was not the absence of God's work. It was the shape of it.</p><p>I have wanted things from God for years. A change I could not force. A person I could not fix. A door that stayed shut long past the point where I thought I had earned its opening. And in the middle of that waiting I assumed nothing was happening, because nothing had changed on the outside. I was wrong. Something was happening. It was happening to me.</p><p>Waiting does a work that arriving never could. It burns off the version of you that wanted the thing for the wrong reasons. It teaches you to want God more than the gift you were asking Him for. It exposes what you were really trusting, because a person who trusts the answer panics in the delay, but a person who trusts God can sit in the dark and still call Him good.</p><p>I am not going to tell you the wait is short. Sometimes it is a lifetime. Some of the most faithful people I have buried died still waiting. But I will tell you that the delay is not empty, and God is not slow because He forgot you. He is patient because He is doing something in you that speed would have ruined.</p><p>The wait is not wasted. It is the room where you are being made ready for what you asked for. And sometimes the readiness is the answer.</p>`,
-  },
-  {
-    title: "The Grief That Prays",
-    slug: "devo-grief-that-prays",
-    excerpt:
-      "The Bible does not ask you to grieve quietly. It hands you words for the loudest kind.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "5 min",
-    body: `<blockquote>"My God, my God, why have you forsaken me? Why are you so far from saving me, from the words of my groaning?" (Psalm 22:1)</blockquote><p>The church I grew up hearing about had no room for this verse. Grief was allowed, but only the composed kind, the kind that kept its voice down and its questions to itself. You could be sad, but you could not accuse. You could cry, but you could not ask God why He had gone.</p><p>Then I actually read the Psalms, and I found out the Bible grieves louder than any of us. A full third of the Psalter is complaint. Not polite disappointment. Complaint. Men shaking their fists at heaven and putting the shaking into a song they sang in worship. The book God gave His people to pray is soaked in a grief that does not behave.</p><p>Psalm 22 is not the prayer of a man who has lost his faith. It is the prayer of a man whose faith is so real that he takes his abandonment straight to the One he feels abandoned by. You do not cry out to a God you have stopped believing in. The complaint is itself an act of trust. It says: I still think You are there, and I still think You should answer.</p><p>And then there is the weight I cannot get past. Jesus prayed this Psalm from the cross. The Son of God, dying, reached for the words of a grieving man and made them His own. He did not grieve quietly either. He grieved in the words the Scriptures had already given Him, and in doing so He sanctified every raw thing you have ever wanted to scream at God and were told to swallow.</p><p>So if you are in the kind of grief that has questions in it, questions that feel too angry to say out loud in a church, I want you to know the Bible got there first. It has words for you. It hands them to you and says: pray this. Do not pray a cleaned-up version. Pray the real one.</p><p>The grief that prays is not weaker faith. It is faith that refused to go silent. It kept talking to God when talking was the hardest thing left to do. That is not the failure of faith. That, on the cross, was the shape of it.</p>`,
-  },
-  {
-    title: "Rest Is Not a Reward",
-    slug: "devo-rest-not-a-reward",
-    excerpt: "You do not earn the Sabbath. You were made for it before you had done anything.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "4 min",
-    body: `<blockquote>"And on the seventh day God finished his work that he had done, and he rested on the seventh day from all his work that he had done." (Genesis 2:2)</blockquote><p>I treat rest like a paycheck. Something I clock in for. I tell myself I will slow down when the list is finished, when the deadline clears, when I have finally earned the right to stop. The rest is always waiting on the other side of the productivity, and the productivity never ends, so the rest never comes.</p><p>Genesis undoes this before it starts. God rested. Not because He was tired. Not because He had earned it. God does not get tired and God does not owe Himself anything. He rested to make a pattern into the fabric of the world, to say that the week does not end in more work but in stopping, in delight, in the good pronounced over what was made.</p><p>And this should have humbled me years ago. Man was made on the sixth day. The first full day Adam lived was the seventh, the day of rest. Before Adam had done a single thing, before he had worked one hour or produced one result, he was invited into rest. His first experience of being alive was not labor. It was Sabbath.</p><p>You did not earn your way into rest. Rest was the gift you were given before you had done anything to deserve it. That order is the whole gospel in miniature. We do not work in order to earn God's favor and then rest. We receive the favor first, and the work flows out of the rest, not the other way around.</p><p>I keep getting this backward. I keep making rest the trophy at the end of a race I run myself ragged to win. But the Sabbath was never a trophy. It was always a table, set before I lifted a finger, and the invitation still stands.</p><p>Stop trying to earn what was already given. Sit down. The work will keep. God rested, and He was not even tired.</p>`,
-  },
-  {
-    title: "The Table Set in the Presence of Enemies",
-    slug: "devo-table-in-presence-of-enemies",
-    excerpt: "God does not remove the enemies before He feeds you. He feeds you in front of them.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "4 min",
-    body: `<blockquote>"You prepare a table before me in the presence of my enemies; you anoint my head with oil; my cup overflows." (Psalm 23:5)</blockquote><p>I always read past this line to get to the still waters and the green pastures, the parts of Psalm 23 that feel like a nap. But this verse is doing something the peaceful images do not. It puts a table in a war zone. God spreads a feast, and the enemies are still there, watching, close enough to see.</p><p>That is not how I would design providence. I would ask God to clear the room first. Remove the enemy, then feed me. Take away the diagnosis, the strained relationship, the fear that sits at the edge of every quiet moment, and then I will feel cared for. I want the peace to come from the absence of the threat.</p><p>David asks for something braver. He does not ask God to remove the enemies. He describes God setting a table in front of them. The care is not the removal of the danger. The care is the meal in the middle of it. God does not always take you out of the hard place. Sometimes He sits you down and feeds you right there, and lets the thing you feared watch Him do it.</p><p>There is a defiance in this that I have felt in the worst rooms of ministry. Standing at a graveside, the enemy of death close enough to touch, and yet there is a strange provision even there, a cup that somehow overflows when by every account it should be empty. God does not always fight the way we want. But He feeds His people in the presence of what they fear, and the feeding is its own kind of victory.</p><p>Your enemies may still be in the room tonight. The table is still set. Sit down and eat anyway. That is not denial. That is trust with a fork in its hand.</p>`,
-  },
-  {
-    title: "When You Are Angry at God",
-    slug: "devo-angry-at-god",
-    excerpt: "The people God commended in the end were not the ones who stayed calm.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "5 min",
-    body: `<blockquote>"Then Job answered the LORD and said: 'I know that you can do all things, and that no purpose of yours can be thwarted.'" (Job 42:1-2)</blockquote><p>Somewhere I picked up the idea that anger at God was the one emotion faith did not permit. Sadness, yes. Fear, if I confessed it fast enough. But anger at God felt like the line you did not cross, the thing that proved your faith had cracked. So when I felt it, and I have, I hid it. I prayed around it. I gave God the polished version and kept the furious one to myself.</p><p>Then I read Job all the way through, not the sanitized summary but the actual book, and it wrecked my theology of anger. Job spends thirty-something chapters not being calm. He accuses God of injustice. He demands an audience. He says he would take God to court if he could find the courthouse. This is not measured lament. This is a man in open protest against the God he still believes in.</p><p>And here is what undid me. At the end, when God finally speaks, He does not condemn Job for the anger. He corrects Job's understanding, He puts the questions back on him, He reminds Job who laid the foundations of the earth. But when it is over, God turns to Job's friends, the ones who kept defending God with tidy explanations, and He says they have not spoken rightly about Him. Job did.</p><p>Read that again. The angry man who accused God spoke more rightly than the calm men who defended Him. Because Job kept talking to God. The friends talked about God, kept Him at the safe distance of a theory, and used their theology to avoid the terror of the actual God. Job walked straight into the whirlwind with his complaint, and that walking was faith.</p><p>I am not telling you anger at God is a virtue. It is not the destination. Job repents in dust and ashes when he finally sees. But the anger was not the sin. The anger was the honest man refusing to pretend, and God can work with honesty in a way He cannot work with the polite lies we tell to keep the peace.</p><p>If you are angry at God tonight, do not take it somewhere else. Take it to Him. He is not fragile. He held Job. He can hold you. The relationship survives the anger. It rarely survives the silence.</p>`,
-  },
-  {
-    title: "The Practice of Beginning Again",
-    slug: "devo-beginning-again",
-    excerpt: "The Christian life is not one repentance. It is ten thousand small returns.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "4 min",
-    body: `<blockquote>"The steadfast love of the LORD never ceases; his mercies never come to an end; they are new every morning; great is your faithfulness." (Lamentations 3:22-23)</blockquote><p>I used to think the goal was to get past the beginning. To reach a point in the faith where I no longer had to start over, where the sin was behind me, the discipline was automatic, the return was no longer needed because I had finally arrived. I treated repentance like a door you walk through once, on the way in.</p><p>It is not a door. It is a rhythm. The Christian life is not a single dramatic turning followed by a straight line. It is the practice of beginning again, over and over, on Tuesday and again on Thursday and again the following week, returning to God from the same tired ground you left Him on yesterday.</p><p>I hate that this is true, because it means I am never finished. It means the same failure I confessed a year ago is back, and I have to bring it again, and the humiliation of the repetition is real. I wanted to graduate. Instead I keep enrolling.</p><p>But Lamentations was written from the ruins of a burned city, by a man watching everything he loved end. And in the middle of the rubble he says the mercies are new every morning. Not renewed. New. A fresh mercy for a day you have not lived yet, waiting for you before you wake, sufficient for a failure you have not committed but almost certainly will.</p><p>The mercy is new every morning because it knows you will need it again. God did not build a faith you finish. He built a faith you return to, daily, and He stocked the return with more mercy than you can exhaust. The repetition is not the failure of the design. It is the design.</p><p>So begin again. Not because you finally got it right, but because the mercy for this morning is already here, and it does not care how many mornings came before it.</p>`,
-  },
-  {
-    title: "What the Body Knows Before the Mind",
-    slug: "devo-body-knows-first",
-    excerpt: "You are not a soul renting a body. You are a body, and God calls it good.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "4 min",
-    body: `<blockquote>"So God created man in his own image, in the image of God he created him; male and female he created them." (Genesis 1:27)</blockquote><p>Much of the Christianity I absorbed treated the body as the problem. The soul was the real you, the pure part, and the body was the drag on it, the appetite that had to be beaten down, the flesh that would one day be shed like a coat. Salvation, in that telling, was mostly about getting the soul out of the body it was trapped in.</p><p>That is not Christianity. That is an old heresy in a churchy accent. The name for it is gnosticism, and the church fought it hard in its early centuries precisely because it is not what the Bible teaches. Genesis says God made a body and called it good. The Word became flesh and dwelt among us. The resurrection is bodily, not the escape of a soul but the raising of a body. The Christian hope is not a ghost in heaven. It is a new body on a new earth.</p><p>I raise five sons, and the body teaches me things my theology books never did. A hug that reaches a boy when words cannot. A shared meal that does more for a strained afternoon than a lecture ever could. The way exhaustion in the body makes a person short and unkind, so that sometimes the most spiritual thing you can do is sleep. The body knows things before the mind admits them.</p><p>This matters because we keep trying to fix our souls while ignoring our bodies, and then we wonder why the peace will not come. But you are not a soul renting a body. You are an embodied soul, a besouled body, one thing, and God made the whole of you and called the whole of you good.</p><p>Tend the body. Feed it, rest it, move it, do not despise it. It is not the enemy of your faith. It is the place your faith is lived, and one day it is the thing God will raise. He is not going to save the part of you that floats. He is going to save you, all of you, the hands and the voice and the tired shoulders. Treat them accordingly.</p>`,
-  },
-  {
-    title: "Solitude Without a Screen",
-    slug: "devo-solitude-without-screen",
-    excerpt: "You cannot hear God over a feed. Neither can I. That is the whole problem.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "4 min",
-    body: `<blockquote>"And rising very early in the morning, while it was still dark, he departed and went out to a desolate place, and there he prayed." (Mark 1:35)</blockquote><p>Jesus went to a desolate place to pray. Alone. In the dark. Away from the crowd that would have followed Him if they could. The Son of God, who had every reason to feel no need of it, built solitude into His life on purpose and defended it against the demands of a needy world.</p><p>I do not have solitude. I have the absence of other people, which is not the same thing. When the room goes quiet, I reach for the phone. The silence lasts about four seconds before I fill it with a feed, a headline, a video, the endless scroll that promises to entertain me and only leaves me more restless than the quiet did. I have not been truly alone with my own thoughts, or with God, in longer than I want to confess.</p><p>The screen is not neutral. It is engineered to make the silence unbearable, to train me to flinch away from stillness, to keep me consuming so I never have to sit with what surfaces when there is nothing to consume. And the thing that surfaces in real solitude is exactly what I am avoiding. My own heart. My unexamined fear. The voice of God, which is quiet, and which I cannot hear over the noise I keep choosing.</p><p>Jesus could have filled His mornings a hundred ways. He chose the desolate place. Not because He liked being alone but because there was a Father to hear there, and the hearing required the quiet, and the quiet required leaving the crowd.</p><p>I am not going to tell you to throw the phone in a lake. I am telling you what I am telling myself. There is a version of solitude you have not had in years, the kind with no screen in it, and God is waiting in it. You will not find Him in the scroll. You have to go to the desolate place. It is still dark out there, and He is still there.</p>`,
-  },
-  {
-    title: "The Bread You Ask for Daily",
-    slug: "devo-daily-bread",
-    excerpt: "God gives you enough for today and not one crumb of tomorrow. That is on purpose.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "4 min",
-    body: `<blockquote>"Give us this day our daily bread." (Matthew 6:11)</blockquote><p>I want a warehouse. I want God to hand me a year of provision, a decade of security, enough of everything stacked in a room where I can see it, so that I never have to feel the vulnerability of needing anything again. I want to be past need. Jesus teaches me to pray for one day.</p><p>Daily bread. Not weekly, not annual. Bread for this day, asked for today, and tomorrow you come back and ask again. The prayer is built to keep you dependent, to keep you returning, to make you a person who needs God every single morning rather than a person who got resupplied and forgot Him until the next shortage.</p><p>There is a story underneath this. In the wilderness, God fed Israel with manna, and He gave a strange instruction. Gather enough for today and no more. What they tried to store overnight bred worms and stank. The only day they could gather extra was the day before the Sabbath. Every other day, the provision came fresh, one day at a time, and the hoarding rotted.</p><p>I have tried to hoard my way out of trusting God, and it rots the same way. The security I build to spare myself the vulnerability of dependence just becomes a new thing to be anxious about protecting. The warehouse does not free me from fear. It gives the fear a bigger object.</p><p>Jesus knew what He was doing when He capped the prayer at one day. He was not being stingy. He was keeping me close. A person who has enough for a year does not pray. A person who has enough for today, and knows it, prays every morning, and the praying is the point.</p><p>You have enough for today. That is not a shortage. That is a Father keeping you near enough to feed you again tomorrow.</p>`,
-  },
-  {
-    title: "When the Feeling Does Not Come",
-    slug: "devo-when-feeling-doesnt-come",
-    excerpt: "Faith is not the presence of the feeling. It is what you do in its absence.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "4 min",
-    body: `<blockquote>"Even though I walk through the valley of the shadow of death, I will fear no evil, for you are with me; your rod and your staff, they comfort me." (Psalm 23:4)</blockquote><p>There are mornings I pray and feel nothing. No warmth, no sense of presence, no lift in the chest that tells me the prayer landed somewhere. The words go up and seem to hit the ceiling and fall back down. And in those seasons I have quietly wondered whether the whole thing was ever real, or whether I had just been feeling my own feelings and calling it God.</p><p>The Psalmist walks through the valley of the shadow of death, and he does not say he felt God's presence. He says God was with him. Those are different claims. The felt presence comes and goes. The actual presence does not depend on whether I can feel it. God is with me in the valley whether the valley feels haunted or holy, and my failure to sense Him is not evidence of His absence. It is evidence that I am in the valley, where the light is dim by definition.</p><p>We live in a culture that has trained us to trust feelings above almost everything, to treat the felt sense as the final proof of what is real. So when the feeling of God goes quiet, we assume God went quiet. But the darkest chapters of every faithful life are precisely the ones where the feeling withdrew and the person kept going anyway. That keeping going, in the absence of the feeling, is not lesser faith. It is the mature kind.</p><p>A love that only shows up when the feeling is strong is not love yet. It is infatuation. The love that stays through the numb seasons, that keeps showing up to the same God with the same words when nothing comes back, that love has been tested and found real.</p><p>If the feeling has not come in a while, do not conclude that God has left. Conclude that you are being taught to trust Him without the crutch of the feeling. That is not a downgrade in your faith. That is the valley doing exactly what the valley does, and He is with you in it, felt or not.</p>`,
-  },
-  {
-    title: "The Mercy That Is New This Morning",
-    slug: "devo-mercy-new-morning",
-    excerpt: "Yesterday's mercy will not cover today. It was never meant to.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "4 min",
-    body: `<blockquote>"But this I call to mind, and therefore I have hope: The steadfast love of the LORD never ceases; his mercies never come to an end." (Lamentations 3:21-22)</blockquote><p>I woke this morning owing more than I woke owning. That is the honest accounting of most of my mornings. The failures of yesterday were still on the ledger, the words I should not have said to my sons, the impatience I let win, the prayer I skipped, the person I meant to call and did not. I woke up in debt, and the debt was mine.</p><p>Lamentations was written by a man in far worse shape than a bad day. His city was ash. His people were carried off. Everything he trusted had been proven fragile and had shattered. And in the wreckage he stops and calls one thing to mind, deliberately, as an act of will against the despair. The mercies of God are new every morning.</p><p>Notice he does not feel this. He calls it to mind. The hope is not a mood that came over him. It is a truth he chose to remember when every feeling he had was pointing the other way. This I call to mind, and therefore I have hope. The hope followed the remembering. It did not come first.</p><p>And what he remembers is that the mercy is new. Not carried over from yesterday, not a rationed remnant of an old supply, but freshly made for this exact day. Yesterday's mercy is spent. It did its work on yesterday's failures and it is gone. But there is a new mercy for today, minted while I slept, sufficient for the debt I woke up carrying.</p><p>I keep trying to live on stale mercy, dragging yesterday's guilt into today as if the mercy expired with the sin. It does not work like that. The mercy is new every morning because the failures are new every morning, and God matched the supply to the need, and the supply never runs out.</p><p>Whatever you woke up owing, there is a mercy for it, made this morning, waiting. Call it to mind. The hope comes after.</p>`,
-  },
-  {
-    title: "What You Do With the Dark Night",
-    slug: "devo-dark-night",
-    excerpt: "The absence of God's felt presence is not always His judgment. Sometimes it is His work.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "5 min",
-    body: `<blockquote>"My soul thirsts for God, for the living God. When shall I come and appear before God? My tears have been my food day and night." (Psalm 42:2-3)</blockquote><p>There is a season some believers enter that no one warns them about. The prayers go dry. The Scriptures that used to burn now sit flat on the page. The God who felt so near becomes an absence, and the person is left thirsting, appearing to have done nothing wrong, doing all the same things they did when God felt close, and getting silence.</p><p>The old spiritual writers had a name for this. They called it the dark night of the soul. John of the Cross, a sixteenth-century Spanish monk, wrote about it as something that could come not because God had withdrawn in anger but because God was doing a deeper work, weaning the soul off the felt consolations so it would learn to love God for Himself and not for the feelings He supplied. The darkness, in his account, was not punishment. It was purification, and it hurt like both.</p><p>I do not want to romanticize this, because when you are in it, it does not feel like purification. It feels like abandonment. The Psalmist calls it tears for food, day and night, a thirst that does not get answered. He is not describing a mild dry spell. He is describing a soul in agony over a God who has gone quiet, and he wrote it down and put it in the songbook of God's people, which means God wanted His people to have words for this exact place.</p><p>Here is what the dark night is doing, when it is the good kind. It is taking away the props. The felt presence, the emotional highs, the sense that you are doing well spiritually, all of it can become a subtle idol, a thing you love more than God because it feels better than God. The dark night strips it. It leaves you with God and nothing else, and it asks whether you will still hold on when there is no reward for holding on but God Himself.</p><p>That is a question you cannot answer in the light. You can only answer it in the dark. And the answer, worked out slowly through gritted teeth over a season you did not choose, becomes the deepest thing your faith will ever know.</p><p>If you are in the dark night, keep going. Keep praying into the silence. Keep opening the flat pages. The living God has not left. He has taken something from you that was standing between you and Him, and what He is making in its place is worth more than the thing you lost. You will not see it now. You are not supposed to. That is what makes it night.</p>`,
-  },
-  {
-    title: "Giving When It Costs You",
-    slug: "devo-giving-when-it-costs",
-    excerpt: "The gift that does not cost is not generosity. It is disposal.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "4 min",
-    body: `<blockquote>"But the king said to Araunah, 'No, but I will buy it from you for a price. I will not offer burnt offerings to the LORD my God that cost me nothing.'" (2 Samuel 24:24)</blockquote><p>Araunah offered David the land, the oxen, everything he needed for the offering, free of charge. It was a generous offer and David could have taken it and no one would have blamed him. Instead David refused. He insisted on paying, and he said the reason plainly. He would not offer God something that cost him nothing.</p><p>I give God a lot of things that cost me nothing. The hour I was not using anyway. The money I did not need. The kindness I could afford without noticing. And I call it generosity, but David names it for what it is. If the gift did not cost me, it was not a sacrifice. It was disposal. I gave God my leftovers and my spare change and my surplus time, and I felt generous doing it, and God knows the difference even when I pretend not to.</p><p>Real giving has a cost you feel. It shows up as an absence somewhere in your own life, a thing you now do without because someone else has it instead. The widow Jesus watched put in two small coins, and He said she gave more than all the rich people combined, because they gave out of abundance and she gave out of her poverty, everything she had to live on. Her gift cost her the next meal. That is why it counted.</p><p>I am not romanticizing poverty and I am not shaming anyone's honest limits. But I am naming the trick I play on myself, where I give in the amounts that do not disturb my comfort and mistake the ease for grace. The gift that costs nothing changes nothing, least of all me. It is the giving that hurts a little, that leaves a mark on my own life, that begins to loosen money's grip on my heart.</p><p>Give God something that costs you this week. Not to earn anything. He cannot be bought and does not need your oxen. Give it because a gift that costs you nothing has not left your hands yet. It is still yours, dressed up as generosity, and your heart knows it.</p>`,
-  },
-  {
-    title: "The Question God Asks First",
-    slug: "devo-question-god-asks-first",
-    excerpt: "The first question in the Bible is not an accusation. It is a search.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "4 min",
-    body: `<blockquote>"But the LORD God called to the man and said to him, 'Where are you?'" (Genesis 3:9)</blockquote><p>The first question God asks a human being comes right after the worst thing a human being had yet done. Adam has eaten, hidden, sewn fig leaves, and crouched in the trees ashamed. And God comes walking in the garden and calls out. Not with a verdict. With a question. Where are you.</p><p>God knew where Adam was. The question was not for God's information. It was for Adam's return. It was the voice of the One who was wronged, coming to look for the one who wronged Him, and framing the search as a question so gentle it barely sounds like the aftermath of catastrophe. The offended party is the one doing the seeking. That order should stop us cold.</p><p>When I have failed, my instinct is Adam's instinct. Hide. Cover. Get out of range of the voice, because I assume the voice, when it comes, will come as accusation. And so I avoid God exactly when I need Him most, because I have God's opening line wrong. I expect where were you, said like a prosecutor. God says where are you, said like a father in the dark calling a child home.</p><p>The whole Bible turns on this. God is the seeker. From the garden to the shepherd who leaves the ninety-nine to the father who runs down the road while the prodigal is still a speck on the horizon, the pattern holds. We hide, and God comes looking, and His first word is not the sentence we brace for. It is a question that leaves the door open for us to step out of the trees.</p><p>Wherever you are hiding tonight, God already knows the coordinates. He is not asking because He lost you. He is asking because He wants you to answer, to come out of the leaves, to be found. The question is still open. Where are you. He is waiting for you to say.</p>`,
-  },
-  {
-    title: "Stillness Is Not Emptiness",
-    slug: "devo-stillness-not-emptiness",
-    excerpt: "Be still and know is not a call to feel nothing. It is a call to stop and remember who holds it all.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "4 min",
-    body: `<blockquote>"Be still, and know that I am God. I will be exalted among the nations, I will be exalted in the earth!" (Psalm 46:10)</blockquote><p>This verse gets printed on so many soft-focus mugs that we have drained it. We hear it as a gentle invitation to relax, to empty the mind, to sit in a quiet room and feel our shoulders drop. And there is nothing wrong with a dropped shoulder. But the verse is not about that, and reading it that way misses the muscle in it.</p><p>Read the Psalm around it. Nations are raging. Kingdoms totter. The earth gives way and the mountains fall into the sea. This is not a spa. This is a world coming apart, and into the chaos God says be still. The stillness is not the absence of the storm. It is a posture held in the middle of it.</p><p>And it is not emptiness. The command is be still and know. Know something. The stillness has content. Stop the frantic self-protective scrambling, the trying to hold the whole collapsing world together with your own two hands, and know that there is a God, and He is exalted, and He does not fall into the sea when the mountains do. The stillness is not a blank mind. It is a mind that has stopped running long enough to remember who actually governs the storm.</p><p>My problem is not that I feel too much in the chaos. It is that I trust myself too much. I stay in motion because motion feels like control, and stopping feels like surrender to forces I cannot manage. Be still is the hardest command in that Psalm, because it asks me to let go of the illusion that my scrambling was holding anything up.</p><p>So the stillness God calls for is not you feeling nothing. It is you stopping long enough to remember that the God who will be exalted in the earth is exalted right now, over the very thing that is frightening you. Be still. Not empty. Anchored. There is a difference, and the difference is the whole point.</p>`,
-  },
-  {
-    title: "The Habit That Outlasts the Feeling",
-    slug: "devo-habit-outlasts-feeling",
-    excerpt: "You will not always feel like praying. Build the kind of life that prays anyway.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "4 min",
-    body: `<blockquote>"When he came to Nazareth, where he had been brought up, he went to the synagogue, as was his custom, on the Sabbath day." (Luke 4:16)</blockquote><p>Three words in that verse do quiet work. As was his custom. Jesus went to the synagogue on the Sabbath because that was His habit, the settled pattern of His life, the thing He did whether or not the particular Sabbath brought a rush of devotion. The Son of God had spiritual habits. He did not run His life on feeling. He ran it on custom.</p><p>I have spent years waiting to feel like it. Feel like praying, and then pray. Feel like opening the Bible, and then open it. Feel drawn to worship, and then go. And because the feeling is fickle, my practice was fickle, and I ended up with a spiritual life that showed up only when my emotions happened to cooperate, which was not often and never on the days I needed it most.</p><p>The feeling is the worst possible foundation for a life of faith, because the feeling is exactly what fails you in the hard seasons. The day of grief, the week of dryness, the stretch of numbness, those are the days no feeling drives you to prayer. If your practice depends on the feeling, your practice collapses precisely when you need it to hold.</p><p>This is what habit is for. A habit is a decision you make once and then do not have to make again every morning at war with your mood. As was His custom. The custom carries you when the feeling will not. You show up to God on the flat gray Tuesday not because you feel His presence but because showing up is simply what you do, and the showing up, repeated ten thousand times, becomes the trellis your whole faith grows on.</p><p>The great danger is thinking the habit is dead because it is not always warm. It is not dead. It is load-bearing. The custom you keep on the cold days is holding up the whole structure, and one morning, inside the habit you kept when you did not feel it, the feeling comes back and finds you already there.</p><p>Build the custom. It will outlast every feeling you have. That is not the failure of your faith. That is its skeleton.</p>`,
-  },
-  {
-    title: "When You Have Nothing to Bring",
-    slug: "devo-nothing-to-bring",
-    excerpt: "The gospel is not for people who cleaned themselves up first. It is for people with empty hands.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "4 min",
-    body: `<blockquote>"Come to me, all who labor and are heavy laden, and I will give you rest." (Matthew 11:28)</blockquote><p>There is a lie I have believed most of my life, and it goes like this. Get yourself together first, then come to God. Clean up the sin, patch the doubt, work up some real devotion, and once you are presentable, approach. The lie treats God like a job interview you have to prepare for, and it keeps the people who need Him most standing outside the door, ashamed of their empty hands.</p><p>Read the invitation again. Come to me, all who labor and are heavy laden. He is not calling the rested and the ready. He is calling the exhausted, the burdened, the ones with nothing left. The qualification for coming to Jesus is not that you have something to bring. The qualification is that you are worn out and you know it. He designed the door for empty hands.</p><p>I came to faith from atheism, and I remember the specific fear of it, the sense that I had nothing to offer, no religious background, no clean record, no impressive spiritual resume, only a life I had run into the ground and a hunch that there might be more. I almost did not come because I thought I had to arrive with something. I had nothing. It turned out nothing was exactly the entry requirement.</p><p>This is the scandal of grace and it never stops scandalizing me. God does not want your cleaned-up version. He wants you, heavy laden, at the end of your own strength, hands empty. If you wait until you have something to bring, you will wait forever, because the whole point is that you cannot furnish what the coming requires. He furnishes it. You just come.</p><p>So come with nothing. That is not a disqualification. That is the ticket. The rest He offers is not the reward for arriving full. It is the gift He presses into hands He finds empty, and yours will do fine.</p>`,
-  },
-  {
-    title: "The Word Made Flesh in Your Kitchen",
-    slug: "devo-word-in-your-kitchen",
-    excerpt: "The God of the universe entered a home with a kitchen and dirty dishes. He is not too grand for yours.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "4 min",
-    body: `<blockquote>"And the Word became flesh and dwelt among us, and we have seen his glory, glory as of the only Son from the Father, full of grace and truth." (John 1:14)</blockquote><p>The Word became flesh. Not a symbol, not an idea, not a voice from a cloud that kept its distance. Flesh. Which means a body that got tired, hands that got dirty, a childhood home with a table and a floor that needed sweeping and, yes, a kitchen. The eternal Word of God grew up in an ordinary house doing ordinary things, and that is not a footnote to the incarnation. It is the incarnation.</p><p>We keep God at the level of the grand. We look for Him in the mountaintop moment, the swelling worship, the answered prayer with fireworks. And then we go home to the sink full of dishes and the kids who need feeding and the same tired repeated tasks, and we assume God is not there, that we left Him back at the sacred place and are now on our own in the profane routine.</p><p>But the Word became flesh and dwelt among us. He dwelt. He did not visit and leave. He moved into the ordinary and stayed, and in doing so He made the ordinary the very place His glory showed up. Thirty years in Nazareth doing nothing the newspapers would cover, and John says we have seen His glory. The glory was in the flesh, in the years of the kitchen and the workbench, not only in the three years of miracles.</p><p>This changes the dishes. It changes the diaper and the commute and the meal you make for the tenth time this month. If God entered the ordinary and filled it with His presence, then your ordinary is not the place God is absent from. It is the place He specializes in. The kitchen is not beneath the God who grew up in one.</p><p>You do not have to escape your ordinary life to find God. He came down into a life exactly this ordinary, on purpose, and He is standing in your kitchen now. The glory is not somewhere else. Look at your own two hands in the dishwater. The Word became flesh, and flesh is where you are.</p>`,
-  },
-  {
-    title: "The Last Prayer of the Day",
-    slug: "devo-last-prayer-of-day",
-    excerpt: "How you end the day tells the truth about who you think is holding it.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "4 min",
-    body: `<blockquote>"In peace I will both lie down and sleep; for you alone, O LORD, make me dwell in safety." (Psalm 4:8)</blockquote><p>The last thing I do most nights is scroll. I lie down with the day's failures still running, the tomorrow list already assembling, the low hum of everything unresolved, and I reach for the phone to numb the noise until sleep finally overtakes me. It is not rest. It is anesthesia. And it tells the truth about who I think is holding the world, which is me, still, even lying down in the dark.</p><p>David ends the day differently. In peace I will both lie down and sleep. He does not lie down and worry. He does not lie down and scroll. He lies down and sleeps, in peace, and he names the reason. The LORD alone makes him dwell in safety. He can let go of the day because Someone else is awake and holding it, and that Someone does not need David's anxious vigilance to keep the world spinning until morning.</p><p>Sleep is an act of trust. It is the daily rehearsal of the fact that you are not God. Every night you lose consciousness, you release your grip on everything you spent the day gripping, and the world goes on without your management, and you wake to find it did not fall apart in your absence. Sleep preaches to you, if you let it, that the universe was never resting on your shoulders.</p><p>The way I end the day is a confession of who I believe is in charge of the night. When I end it in the anxious scroll, I confess that I am, still, even now, and I cannot rest because a god who has to hold everything never gets to sleep. When I end it in prayer, handing the unfinished day back to the God who was actually holding it the whole time, I confess the truth, and the truth lets me close my eyes.</p><p>Make your last prayer of the day a letting go. Put the day, the list, the failures, the fears, into the hands that were already carrying them. Then lie down. He does not sleep, so you can.</p>`,
-  },
-];
