-    body: `<p>We have been taught to treat doubt like an infection, something to be quarantined, cured, and never spoken of again. Get the doubt out of you, the thinking goes, and get back to the healthy state of certainty as fast as possible. I want to argue something the church has half-forgotten, which is that doubt handled rightly is not an infection at all. It is a discipline. It is a practice that can be entered on purpose, worked through with intention, and used to build a faith stronger than the one that never questioned anything.</p>
-
-<p>This is dangerous ground and I know it. There is a kind of doubt that corrodes, and I am not going to pretend it does not exist. But there is another kind, an older and more honorable kind, that the great believers have always practiced deliberately, and treating all doubt as the corroding kind has left the church defenseless against the very questions that could have deepened it. I came to faith from atheism, so I know both kinds intimately. And I have come to believe that the church's fear of doubt has done more damage than doubt itself ever did.</p>
-
-<h2>The Tradition Doubted on Purpose</h2>
-
-<p>Start with the Psalms, because they are the prayer book Jesus himself prayed. Roughly a third of them are laments, and many of those laments are not gentle. Psalm 88 ends in darkness with no resolution. The psalmist accuses God of hiding, of casting him off, of surrounding him with terrors, and the psalm simply stops there, in the dark. Whoever assembled the Psalter did not edit that one out to keep the tone positive. They left it in the middle of Scripture, a permanent permission slip for the believer who prays into a silence.</p>
-
-<blockquote>"O Lord, why do you cast my soul away? Why do you hide your face from me?" (Psalm 88:14)</blockquote>
-
-<p>That is a doubt spoken directly to God, and God preserved it in his own book. Think about what that means. The God who could have given us only the confident psalms chose to give us the doubting ones too, which tells you that he considers the honest question a form of worship rather than a betrayal of it. The medieval theologians had a phrase, fides quaerens intellectum, faith seeking understanding, which Anselm used in the eleventh century as the subtitle of his Proslogion. Faith was not the end of questioning. It was the thing that made the questioning possible, because you can only question honestly when you trust that the answer will not destroy you.</p>
-
-<h2>The Difference Between the Two Doubts</h2>
-
-<p>Everything turns on a distinction the church usually collapses. There is a doubt that is looking for an exit, and there is a doubt that is looking for the truth. They can feel identical from the inside, but they move in opposite directions. The doubt looking for an exit gathers objections and refuses answers, because the answers threaten the exit it wants. The doubt looking for truth gathers objections and actually weighs the answers, willing to be persuaded either way, because it wants what is real more than it wants any particular outcome.</p>
-
-<p>The philosopher and mathematician Blaise Pascal understood this in the seventeenth century. In the Pensees, published after his death, he distinguished between those who seek God and have not found him and those who neither seek nor find. Of the seekers he said they were miserable and reasonable. Of the non-seekers he said they were miserable and unreasonable. The point is that seeking doubt, doubt that is actually looking, is a reasonable and honorable state, even when it has not yet arrived. God is not offended by the seeker. He is grieved by the one who has stopped looking and calls the stopping wisdom.</p>
-
-<p>So the first discipline of doubt as a practice is to interrogate your own doubt and ask which kind it is. Are you looking for the truth, or are you looking for permission to leave. If you are looking for permission to leave, no answer will satisfy you, because you have already decided. But if you are looking for the truth, then your doubt is not the enemy of faith. It is faith's advance scout, sent ahead into the hard country to find out whether the ground will hold.</p>
-
-<h2>How to Doubt on Purpose</h2>
-
-<p>Doubt as a practice means bringing the questions into the light on a schedule rather than letting them ambush you at three in the morning. I tell people to actually write down the doubt in its strongest form, the version their most intelligent unbelieving friend would recognize, not a straw version they can easily knock down. Writing it strong is an act of respect for the truth. If your faith can only survive the weak version of the objection, it cannot survive, and better to know that now.</p>
-
-<p>Then, having stated the doubt at its strongest, you go looking for the strongest answer, not the first answer, not the pat answer from a youth group, but the answer that has satisfied serious minds. This is why the practice requires reading. The theologian C.S. Lewis, who like me came to faith from atheism, wrote in his 1955 memoir Surprised by Joy about how his own unbelief was slowly dismantled not by a single argument but by an honest and sustained looking, a refusal to settle for either easy faith or easy denial. Lewis called himself, in the year of his conversion, the most dejected and reluctant convert in all England. His faith was strong precisely because it had been forced through doubt rather than around it.</p>
-
-<p>The practice also means sitting with the questions that do not resolve, and this is the part most people skip. Some doubts do not get answered in this life. The problem of a child's suffering does not resolve into a tidy theodicy that makes the suffering fine. The discipline is not to force a resolution that is not there but to hold the unresolved question inside the faith rather than letting it drive you out. You can believe firmly in a God whose ways you do not fully understand. In fact you must, because a God you fully understood would be a God small enough to fit inside your head, which is to say an idol.</p>
-
-<h2>The Wound That Taught Me to Doubt Well</h2>
-
-<p>I was raised without a father, and one of the things that absence teaches you is that some questions do not get answered by anyone showing up to answer them. As a boy I asked where he was and why he left, and the questions hung in the air unanswered for years. That could have made me a person who demands an answer before he will trust anything. Instead, strangely, it made me a person who learned to live with an open question without letting the openness eat me. When I came to faith, that skill transferred. I did not need God to answer every question before I would trust him. I had already learned, the hard way, that you can love and trust in the presence of a question that will not close.</p>
-
-<p>This is what I mean by doubt as a practice. It is the muscle of holding faith and question in the same hand, and that muscle is built by exercise. Every time you bring a doubt into the light, examine it honestly, weigh the strongest answer, and then keep trusting God whether or not the doubt fully resolved, you have strengthened the muscle. The believer who has never doubted has a faith like an untested bridge. It may hold. But no one knows, least of all them, and the first real weight may bring it down. The believer who has doubted on purpose and kept believing has a bridge that has been load-tested, and they know what it can carry.</p>
-
-<p>There is a further gift in this that the certain never receive. The believer who has practiced doubt becomes safe for other doubters. He does not flinch when someone confesses that they are not sure, because he has been not sure, on purpose, in daylight, and lived. The certain believer, however sincere, often cannot help the doubter, because he has never been where the doubter is and treats the doubt as an alien thing to be feared or fixed. The one who has practiced doubt can sit in it with another person, unhurried, unafraid, because he knows the ground on the far side of it. In this way the discipline of your own doubt becomes an instrument of care for someone else's, and the very thing you were ashamed of becomes the thing that lets you meet another person in the dark and not leave them there alone. Few gifts in the church are rarer than a believer who is not frightened of a question.</p>
-
-<h2>The Company of the Doubting Faithful</h2>
-
-<p>One reason doubt feels like exile is that we imagine the great believers as men and women of untroubled certainty, and so our own trouble feels like disqualification. The history says otherwise. Mother Teresa, whose private letters were published in 2007 as Come Be My Light, spent nearly fifty years in what she described as a spiritual darkness so profound that she often could not feel God at all, and she served the dying of Calcutta the entire time. She doubted the felt presence of God for decades and gave her whole life to Christ anyway. If that is not faith, the word has no meaning. Her doubt did not disqualify her service. It was the very soil her service grew in.</p>
-
-<p>The point is not that doubt is comfortable. Teresa's letters are agonized. The point is that doubt and deep, world-changing faithfulness have coexisted in the same soul across the whole history of the church, and the person who treats their own doubt as proof of their unfitness has simply never been told the truth about the people they most admire. Thomas doubted and became an apostle. The father in Mark 9 doubted and his son was healed. Teresa doubted and fed the dying for half a century. You are in company you did not know you had, and the company is not the company of the failed. It is the company of the faithful, who happened to be honest about the cost.</p>
-
-<p>This is why the practice of doubt, done rightly, ends not in isolation but in solidarity. When you stop treating your doubt as a private shame and start seeing it as a shared feature of the deepest faith, the whole thing changes. You are no longer the one broken believer in a church of the certain. You are one more name in a long line of people who loved God through the dark, and that line includes some of the ones the church has canonized. Your doubt did not put you outside the tradition. Handled with honesty, it put you inside its most honest chamber.</p>
-
-<h2>Where the Practice Must Be Guarded</h2>
-
-<p>I will not pretend this is safe for everyone in every season. There is a state of mind where doubt is not a discipline but a spiral, where the questions do not sharpen the faith but dissolve it, where every answer becomes a new occasion for a deeper terror. If you are in that state, this article is not asking you to doubt harder. It is asking you to notice that you are in the spiral, and to reach for a hand rather than for another argument.</p>
-
-<p>The practice of doubt requires a floor to stand on, and if you have lost the floor entirely, if the questioning has become a fall rather than an examination, then the discipline is not more questioning. It is stopping, resting, and getting help. Tell your pastor. Tell a counselor. Tell someone who can sit with you while the spiral slows. There is no shame in this. Even the fittest athlete does not train when they are injured, and a soul in free fall does not need more doubt. It needs the ground. And if the fall has become a darkness that frightens you, if you have begun to feel that there is no point in continuing at all, then set the questions down entirely and call someone right now, a crisis line or a friend or a doctor, because the darkness is lying about the permanence of the dark, and it always has.</p>
-
-<h2>The Verdict</h2>
-
-<p>Doubt is not the opposite of faith. Certainty is not the same as faith either. Faith is trust held in the presence of what you cannot prove, and doubt, handled as a discipline, is one of the ways that trust is deepened rather than destroyed. The tradition knew this. The Psalms doubt out loud. Anselm made faith the ground of questioning. Lewis was dragged to belief through doubt rather than around it. And a God who preserved Psalm 88 in his own Scripture is not a God who fears your honest question.</p>
-
-<p>So doubt on purpose, if you are able. State the objection at its strongest. Weigh the strongest answer. Sit with what does not resolve, and keep trusting the God whose ways were always going to exceed your understanding. Do this and you will not lose your faith. You will finally own it, forged in the fire rather than handed to you cold. That is a faith that can carry weight, because it has already carried the weight of your hardest questions and did not break.</p>`,
-  },
