-export const MARRIAGE_ARTICLES_2 = [
-  {
-    title: "Blended Families: The Theology Nobody Prepared You For",
-    slug: "blended-family-theology",
-    excerpt:
-      "The church has a theology of first marriages. It barely has a theology of second ones. And it has almost nothing to say to the family trying to become a family when half the people at the table did not choose each other.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "12 min",
-    body: `<p>There is a moment in nearly every blended family when the illusion breaks. It might come at the dinner table, when a child looks at the new spouse and says, flatly, "You are not my dad." It might come at Christmas, when the logistics of who goes where and when reduce a holy day to a spreadsheet. It might come on an ordinary Tuesday, when a stepparent disciplines a child and the biological parent feels a surge of protectiveness so fierce it frightens them both.</p>
-
-<p>The church has a theology of first marriages. It has wedding liturgies, premarital counseling curricula, and an entire sermonic tradition built around the assumption that two people who have never been married are standing at the altar. What it barely has — what it has mostly refused to develop — is a theology for the family that forms after the first one ended. Not because these families are rare. They are everywhere. In every congregation of any size, blended families sit in the pews carrying questions the sermon will not address. They came because they believed the church had something to say about family. They stay despite growing evidence that the church has almost nothing to say about theirs.</p>
-
-<p>This silence is not neutral. It is a theological failure with pastoral consequences, and it is long past time to name it.</p>
-
-<h2>The Myth of the Clean Slate</h2>
-
-<p>The first thing the church gets wrong about blended families is the assumption that a new marriage creates a new start. The wedding happens, the vows are spoken, and the expectation — usually unspoken but always present — is that this family will now function like a first family. The stepparent will love the children as their own. The children will accept the stepparent with gratitude. The ex-spouse will fade into the background. The past will be, if not forgotten, at least overcome.</p>
-
-<p>None of this happens. It cannot happen, because a blended family is not a first family with complications. It is a fundamentally different kind of family, one that operates by different rules, faces different challenges, and requires a different theology to understand.</p>
-
-<p>In a first family, the couple relationship precedes the parent-child relationship. The adults chose each other, and the children arrived into a household that already had its shape. In a blended family, the parent-child relationship precedes the couple relationship. The children were there first. Their loyalties, their grief, their sense of home — all of it was established before the new spouse arrived. The new spouse enters a system that is already in motion, already has its rhythms, already has its unspoken rules about who sits where and what we eat on Friday nights.</p>
-
-<p>This is not a problem to be solved. It is a reality to be honored. And the church's failure to honor it — its insistence on treating the blended family as a repaired version of the first family — has caused immeasurable harm.</p>
-
-<h2>What the Children Carry</h2>
-
-<p>The hardest truth about blended families is one the adults prefer not to face: the children did not choose this. They did not choose the divorce or the death that ended the first family. They did not choose the new person their parent fell in love with. They did not choose the new house, the new routines, the new siblings who eat differently and laugh at different jokes and take up space in rooms that used to be theirs.</p>
-
-<p>Children in blended families carry a particular kind of grief that the church has almost no language for. It is not the grief of loss — the first parent is usually still alive, still present, still a parent. It is the grief of displacement. The world they knew has been rearranged without their consent, and the people who rearranged it keep telling them how wonderful it is going to be.</p>
-
-<p>A child who resists the new family is not being difficult. A child who refuses to call the stepparent "Mom" or "Dad" is not being disrespectful. A child who acts out after a weekend at the other parent's house is not being manipulative. These are the behaviors of a human being trying to maintain their identity in a world that keeps shifting under their feet. And the church, if it has anything to offer at all, ought to begin by taking that grief seriously rather than rushing past it toward the happy ending.</p>
-
-<p>The Psalms know something about this. "How long, O Lord? Will you forget me forever?" That is not a question that needs an answer. It is a cry that needs a witness. The children in blended families are crying out — sometimes loudly, sometimes in silence — and the first theological act is not to explain but to hear.</p>
-
-<h2>The Stepparent's Impossible Position</h2>
-
-<p>If the children carry grief, the stepparent carries ambiguity. They are asked to love children who may not want their love. They are expected to discipline without the authority that comes from having been there from the beginning. They are told to be patient, to earn trust, to not take it personally — and then they go to bed at night wondering whether they have married into a family that will never fully include them.</p>
-
-<p>The church's counsel to stepparents is almost always some version of "love them like your own." This is well-meaning and catastrophically unhelpful. You cannot love a child like your own when the child already has their own parent, when that parent's photograph sits on the shelf, when the child goes to that parent's house every other weekend and comes back speaking a different emotional language. The love a stepparent offers must be a different kind of love — not lesser, but different. It is the love of someone who chose to be present without being chosen in return. It is closer to the love of God for Israel than the love of a parent for a newborn, and the church would do well to say so.</p>
-
-<p>I have watched stepparents in my congregation pour themselves out for children who gave them nothing back. Year after year, showing up, making lunches, attending games, absorbing hostility without retaliating. Some of them eventually earned a relationship so deep it surprised everyone, including themselves. Some of them never did. Both outcomes are faithful. The church needs to stop promising the first and start honoring the second.</p>
-
-<h2>The Ex-Spouse in the Room</h2>
-
-<p>Here is a truth the church does not want to hear: in a blended family, the ex-spouse is a permanent member of the family system. Not a member of the household. Not a welcome presence. But a permanent influence whose decisions, moods, parenting choices, and remarriage prospects will shape the blended family for decades.</p>
-
-<p>The church's instinct is to treat the ex-spouse as the past — the mistake that was made, the chapter that closed. But when children are involved, no chapter closes. The ex-spouse will be at graduations and weddings. They will have opinions about schools and bedtimes and whether the child can get a tattoo at seventeen. They will, by their very existence, remind everyone in the blended family that this family was built on the rubble of another one.</p>
-
-<p>A theology adequate to this reality would need to account for the ongoing presence of someone who is no longer a spouse but is still a co-parent. It would need to speak about forgiveness not as a one-time event but as a daily practice — forgiving the same person for the same things, week after week, in the school parking lot and the text message thread. It would need to acknowledge that the co-parenting relationship is, in its own way, a covenant: not a marriage covenant, but a covenant of shared responsibility for the lives of children who did not ask to be caught between two households.</p>
-
-<p>Paul wrote to the Philippians about "working out your salvation with fear and trembling." That phrase has always struck me as the most honest description of the co-parenting relationship after divorce. It is a working out. It is fearful. And it trembles.</p>
-
-<h2>What Scripture Actually Offers</h2>
-
-<p>The Bible does not contain a theology of blended families, but it contains the raw materials for one. The story of Scripture is, from beginning to end, a story of God forming a family out of people who did not start together.</p>
-
-<p>Abraham's household was a blended family in every meaningful sense — Isaac and Ishmael, Sarah and Hagar, competing loyalties and unresolved grief. Jacob's household was worse: two wives, two concubines, twelve sons, and a level of dysfunction that makes modern blended families look functional by comparison. The story of Ruth is the story of a woman who chose to enter a family she was not born into, who committed herself to a mother-in-law she could have abandoned, and who became, through that choice, an ancestor of the Messiah.</p>
-
-<p>And then there is Joseph. Not the patriarch — the carpenter. The man who married a woman already pregnant with a child who was not his. The man who raised a son he did not conceive, who taught that son to work with wood, who fled with him to Egypt, who — the Gospels suggest — died before the boy became the man who changed the world. Joseph is the patron saint of stepparents, and the church has almost never said so.</p>
-
-<p>What these stories share is not a formula. They share a pattern: God works through families that are messy, complicated, and formed by circumstances no one would have chosen. The blended family is not a deviation from God's plan. It is, in a fallen world, one of the shapes God's plan takes.</p>
-
-<h2>What the Church Owes</h2>
-
-<p>The church owes blended families three things it has mostly withheld.</p>
-
-<p>First, honesty. Stop pretending that the blended family, if it just prays hard enough and loves well enough, will eventually feel like a first family. It will not. It will feel like what it is: a brave, complicated, sometimes beautiful, sometimes painful attempt to build something real out of materials that include both hope and wreckage. That is not a failure. That is the human condition.</p>
-
-<p>Second, patience. Family researchers consistently say that blended families take four to seven years to develop their own identity and rhythms. Four to seven years. The church, which can barely sit through a three-minute silence in worship, needs to learn to sit with blended families through years of awkwardness, conflict, and incremental progress. Stop asking, "How is everyone adjusting?" Start saying, "We are here. However long it takes."</p>
-
-<p>Third, theology. Real theology, not warmed-over first-marriage theology with a few adjustments. A theology that takes seriously the grief of children, the ambiguity of stepparents, the permanence of co-parenting relationships, and the strange grace that operates in families that were not anyone's first choice but might yet become the place where God does God's deepest work.</p>
-
-<p>The blended family sitting in your pew next Sunday did not come because they wanted a lecture about God's ideal for marriage. They came because they are trying to build a life together, and it is harder than anyone told them it would be, and they are wondering whether God has anything to say to people whose family looks like theirs.</p>
-
-<p>The answer to that question will determine whether they come back.</p>`
