-    body: `<p>He stopped getting out of bed on a Tuesday in October. Not dramatically — not with an announcement or a crisis or a reason his wife could point to. He just did not get up. And the next day he did not get up. And by Friday she was standing in the bedroom doorway, watching him sleep at two in the afternoon, wondering which version of the question to ask: "Are you okay?" — which he would answer with a lie, or "What is wrong with you?" — which would make her the villain in a story she did not write.</p>
-
-<p>Marriage and mental health exist in a relationship the church has barely begun to explore. We have a theology of marriage. We have, belatedly, begun to develop a theology of mental health. But we have almost no theology of what happens when these two realities collide — when the person you married disappears into depression, when anxiety turns your spouse into someone you do not recognize, when the illness that lives in your partner's brain moves into your house and takes up permanent residence.</p>
-
-<p>This is not a marginal issue. The National Institute of Mental Health estimates that nearly one in five American adults lives with a mental illness in any given year. In a congregation of two hundred, that is forty people — and forty spouses trying to love someone through something the church has historically treated as a spiritual problem rather than a medical one.</p>
-
-<h2>The Spouse Who Is Ill</h2>
-
-<p>Depression does not announce itself as depression. It announces itself as fatigue, as irritability, as a loss of interest in things that used to matter. The spouse who is sinking into a depressive episode does not think, "I am depressed." They think, "I am tired." They think, "Nothing sounds good." They think, "I do not know why I cannot make myself care about this." And because they do not name it as illness, they do not seek treatment for illness. They try harder, or they withdraw further, and the marriage absorbs the impact either way.</p>
-
-<p>Anxiety operates differently but with similarly devastating marital effects. The anxious spouse lives in a state of perpetual vigilance — scanning for threats, catastrophizing outcomes, needing reassurance that cannot reassure because the problem is not the specific fear but the fear itself. The anxious spouse may become controlling — not out of malice but out of terror. If I can control enough variables, the thinking goes, I can prevent the disaster I feel is always imminent. The marriage becomes a system organized around the management of one spouse's anxiety, and neither person chose this arrangement or knows how to dismantle it.</p>
-
-<p>Bipolar disorder, obsessive-compulsive disorder, PTSD, personality disorders — each of these conditions shapes a marriage in specific and predictable ways, and the church has naming language for almost none of them. We say "spiritual warfare" when we mean panic attacks. We say "lack of faith" when we mean clinical depression. We say "selfishness" when we mean the executive-function deficits that accompany ADHD. Our language is not just inadequate. It is harmful, because it locates the problem in the sufferer's character rather than in their neurology, and it makes the illness worse by adding guilt to every other symptom.</p>
-
-<h2>The Spouse Who Is Well</h2>
-
-<p>The well spouse — and the term is relative, because no one remains entirely well while living with someone who is ill — occupies a position of extraordinary difficulty that the church rarely acknowledges. They are simultaneously a partner and a caregiver, and the two roles exist in perpetual tension.</p>
-
-<p>As a partner, they want reciprocity. They want the marriage to be a mutual exchange of support, affection, and engagement. As a caregiver, they provide support that is not reciprocated, manage logistics their spouse cannot manage, and absorb emotional weight that was supposed to be shared. Over time, the caregiver role begins to crowd out the partner role, and the marriage shifts from a relationship between equals to a relationship between a patient and a provider.</p>
-
-<p>This shift is not anyone's fault. It is the natural consequence of chronic illness in a closed system. But it is devastating, and the well spouse is not allowed to say so — because who complains about a sick person? Who resents someone for being ill? The well spouse does. And the guilt of that resentment — the sense of being a bad person for feeling what they feel — is one of the heaviest burdens in marriage.</p>
-
-<p>I have sat across from well spouses who whispered their truth as if confessing a crime: "I am angry at him for being depressed." "I resent her anxiety." "Sometimes I wish I had married someone else." These are not sins. They are the honest expressions of a person who is carrying more than they signed up to carry and who has no one to help them set the weight down. The church must be the place where those words can be spoken without judgment. If it is not, it is not serving these marriages at all.</p>
-
-<h2>The Myth of Prayer as Treatment</h2>
-
-<p>The church's most damaging contribution to the intersection of marriage and mental health has been the persistent suggestion that mental illness is, at root, a spiritual problem — that depression is a failure of faith, that anxiety is a failure of trust, that the right combination of prayer, Scripture, and spiritual discipline will heal what medication and therapy cannot.</p>
-
-<p>This is not just wrong. It is dangerous. The depressed spouse who is told to pray harder instead of seeing a psychiatrist may die. This is not hyperbole. Untreated depression is a leading risk factor for suicide, and the church that substitutes spiritual counsel for medical treatment is, in the most literal sense, putting lives at risk.</p>
-
-<p>Prayer is not treatment. Prayer is prayer. It is communion with God, it is the soul's honest cry, it is the practice of placing oneself in the presence of the One who made us. It is vital and irreplaceable and it does not adjust serotonin levels. The God who created the brain also created the science that understands the brain, and the faithful response to mental illness is not to choose between prayer and psychiatry. It is to use both, grateful for both, and to stop treating one as a betrayal of the other.</p>
-
-<p>The marriage in which one spouse is mentally ill and untreated is a marriage under unbearable strain. The church that discourages treatment — actively or passively, explicitly or by omission — is complicit in that strain. This must be said clearly, because it is not said clearly enough: if your spouse is suffering from a mental health condition, getting them to a qualified professional is not a failure of faith. It is an act of love.</p>
-
-<h2>What Mental Illness Does to Intimacy</h2>
-
-<p>The most insidious effect of mental illness on marriage is the erosion of intimacy — not just physical intimacy, though that is often affected, but the deeper intimacy of knowing and being known. The depressed spouse withdraws into a private darkness that the well spouse cannot enter. The anxious spouse is so consumed by their fears that they cannot be present to anyone else's experience. The spouse with PTSD may be triggered by touch, by certain words, by situations that the well spouse cannot predict or understand.</p>
-
-<p>Over time, both spouses become lonely. The ill spouse is lonely because their illness isolates them — because the interior country of depression or anxiety or psychosis is, by its nature, inaccessible to anyone who is not inside it. The well spouse is lonely because the person they married is present in body and absent in every other way that matters. They live together and they are alone.</p>
-
-<p>This loneliness is the real crisis of the mentally ill marriage, and it is the crisis the church is most equipped to address — if it will. The church knows something about presence in darkness. The church has a liturgical tradition built around sitting with suffering, naming what is broken, and refusing to abandon the sufferer. Holy Saturday — the day between crucifixion and resurrection, the day when God was dead and nothing had been resolved — is the patron feast of the mentally ill marriage. It is the day that says: sometimes faithfulness looks like sitting in the dark with no assurance that the light is coming.</p>
-
-<h2>Medication, Therapy, and the Marriage</h2>
-
-<p>When the ill spouse begins treatment — medication, therapy, or both — the marriage enters a new phase that is often more difficult than anyone anticipates. Medication may help, but it also changes things. The depressed spouse who starts an SSRI may find their mood lifting and their libido disappearing. The anxious spouse who begins a benzodiazepine may feel calmer and also flatter — less anxious but also less joyful, less present, less themselves. The well spouse, who prayed for their partner to get help, may discover that the medicated version of their spouse is not the version they fell in love with.</p>
-
-<p>Therapy introduces its own complications. The ill spouse may begin to understand their condition in ways that reframe the marriage — recognizing patterns, naming dynamics, gaining language for things they could not previously articulate. This is good. It is also destabilizing, because it changes the rules of a game both spouses had learned to play. The well spouse may feel left behind, or threatened, or resentful that a therapist seems to understand their partner better than they do.</p>
-
-<p>These are not signs that treatment is failing. These are signs that treatment is working — that the marriage is being disrupted by health, which is just as disorienting as being disrupted by illness. The church must prepare couples for this disruption and normalize it. Getting better does not mean getting easier. It means getting more honest, and honesty is always harder before it is better.</p>
-
-<h2>What the Marriage Needs</h2>
-
-<p>The marriage in which one spouse is mentally ill needs what every marriage needs, but more of it: honesty, patience, professional support, and a community that will not abandon them when the crisis extends past the casserole phase.</p>
-
-<p>It needs the ill spouse to accept their illness as illness — not as identity, not as punishment, not as a character defect, but as a condition that requires treatment and management and the humility to accept help. It needs the well spouse to accept that their role will, for seasons, be disproportionate — that they will give more than they receive, carry more than they should, and need their own support system to sustain them. It needs both spouses to grieve together what the illness has taken from their marriage, without pretending the grief is faithlessness.</p>
-
-<p>And it needs the church to show up. Not with platitudes. Not with Bible verses offered as prescriptions. With presence. With meals. With the willingness to watch someone's children so the well spouse can go to their own therapy appointment. With the honesty to say, "I do not understand what you are going through, but I am here, and I am not leaving."</p>
-
-<p>The marriage that carries mental illness is carrying one of the heaviest crosses two people can share. The church that calls itself the body of Christ — the community that claims to know something about bearing one another's burdens — must learn to help them carry it.</p>
-
-<p>We have been silent too long. The silence has not protected anyone. It has only made the carrying lonelier.</p>`
