-    body: `<p>The second wedding is different from the first. Not necessarily smaller or less festive, though it often is both. Different in texture. The vows land differently in a mouth that has spoken them before. The promise to love until death do us part carries a different weight when you know, from experience, that the part can come long before death. The guests clap and smile, and some of them are thinking what the couple is thinking but will not say: we hope this one works.</p>
-
-<p>Second marriages are the church's most awkward category. We do not quite know what to do with them. We believe in the permanence of marriage, and here are two people whose first marriages were not permanent. We believe in new beginnings, and here is a beginning that is not entirely new — it is built on the foundation of something that ended, and the ending is part of the structure whether we acknowledge it or not. We believe in grace, but we are not sure how far grace extends. Does it extend to a second chance at the thing you failed at the first time? Does it extend to happiness after the covenant was broken? Does grace have a statute of limitations?</p>
-
-<p>It does not. But the church has often acted as though it does, and second marriages have paid the price.</p>
-
-<h2>What the Second Spouse Inherits</h2>
-
-<p>The person who marries someone with a previous marriage inherits more than a partner. They inherit a history — a history that includes wounds they did not inflict, patterns they did not create, and sensitivities they will trip over in the dark for years before they learn where they are.</p>
-
-<p>The previously married spouse carries reflexes from the first marriage. If the first marriage ended because of criticism, they will flinch at anything that sounds like criticism in the second. If the first marriage ended because of emotional withdrawal, they will panic at any sign of distance. If the first marriage ended because of infidelity, they will scan for threats with a vigilance that feels, to the new spouse, like mistrust. And it is mistrust — not of the new spouse, but of marriage itself. The first marriage taught them that marriage can fail, and they carry that knowledge into the second marriage like a scar that aches before rain.</p>
-
-<p>The second spouse did not cause these reflexes. They did not earn this mistrust. But they must live with it, and the living-with-it requires a patience that nobody warns them about. "I am not your ex" is the sentence that every second spouse eventually says, and it is true and it is necessary and it does not solve the problem, because the problem is not rational. It is somatic. It lives in the body, in the nervous system, in the place where the first marriage wrote its lessons in a language the conscious mind cannot always read.</p>
-
-<h2>The Baggage Everyone Mentions and Nobody Defines</h2>
-
-<p>People speak of "baggage" in second marriages the way they speak of "issues" in therapy — vaguely, as if the word itself explains something. It does not. Baggage, in the context of second marriage, means specific things, and naming them is the beginning of dealing with them.</p>
-
-<p>Financial baggage: the debt from the first marriage, the alimony or child support payments, the lifestyle adjustment that comes with dividing one household into two. The second marriage starts with less than the first marriage had, and the disparity between expectation and reality can generate resentment before the honeymoon is over.</p>
-
-<p>Relational baggage: the ex-spouse who is still in the picture because children require co-parenting, the in-laws from the first marriage who are now the grandparents of the new spouse's stepchildren, the friendships that were forged during the first marriage and may not survive the transition to the second. The second marriage does not begin with a clean relational slate. It begins in the middle of a relational web that was already in place.</p>
-
-<p>Emotional baggage: the grief of the first marriage's failure, the guilt (whether warranted or not) about the role one played in that failure, the fear that history will repeat itself, the defensive patterns developed during the worst of the first marriage that now get deployed in the best of the second. The second spouse reaches for their partner's hand and the partner pulls away — not because they do not want to be touched, but because the last person who reached for their hand used touch as a prelude to a fight.</p>
-
-<p>The church cannot remove this baggage. But it can stop pretending it does not exist. The pastoral preparation for a second marriage should differ substantially from the preparation for a first marriage — not because second marriages are less holy, but because they face different challenges. The couple entering a second marriage needs to name their baggage explicitly, to each other and to their community, and they need a community that can hold that naming without judgment.</p>
-
-<h2>The Ghost of the First Marriage</h2>
-
-<p>Every second marriage has a ghost. The ghost is the first marriage — not the ex-spouse as a person, but the first marriage as a template, a reference point, an invisible standard against which the second marriage is constantly measured.</p>
-
-<p>The measurement works in both directions. Sometimes the second marriage is measured favorably: "He never listened to me the way you do." "She was never this affectionate." These comparisons feel like compliments, but they are not. They are reminders that the new spouse exists in relation to the old one, that their virtues are defined not on their own terms but in contrast to someone else's failures. No one wants to be loved as the correction of a previous mistake.</p>
-
-<p>Sometimes the measurement works unfavorably, and this is worse. "My first husband always remembered my birthday." "My first wife kept a cleaner house." These comparisons, even when unspoken, create an impossible standard — a standard defined by selective memory, because the first marriage is now remembered not as it was but as it is wished it had been. The dead marriage, like the dead in general, gets idealized. Its faults fade. Its virtues sharpen. And the living spouse — the one who is present, imperfect, doing their best — cannot compete with a memory.</p>
-
-<p>The second marriage must exorcise this ghost, and the exorcism is simple to describe and difficult to execute: stop comparing. Not "stop saying it out loud." Stop doing it. This requires a discipline of the mind that borders on spiritual practice — the deliberate choice, repeated hundreds of times, to judge the current marriage on its own terms rather than against the template of the previous one. It is the decision to let this marriage be this marriage, with its own shape, its own rhythms, its own particular combination of strengths and weaknesses that has never existed before and will never exist again.</p>
-
-<h2>Grace and the Second Chance</h2>
-
-<p>The theological question at the heart of the second marriage is whether grace extends to second chances — and if so, what that grace looks like. The church has historically been ambivalent about this, and the ambivalence shows in the way we treat second marriages: we perform them, but with less enthusiasm. We celebrate them, but with a footnote. We welcome them, but we wonder, privately, whether God blesses what God did not originally intend.</p>
-
-<p>This ambivalence is understandable. It is also unnecessary. The entire narrative of Scripture is a story of second chances — of God who makes covenant, watches the covenant broken, grieves the breaking, and makes covenant again. Abraham lied about Sarah. Twice. God renewed the covenant. David committed adultery and murder. God did not abandon the Davidic line. Peter denied Jesus three times. Jesus restored him three times. The pattern is relentless: human beings break things, and God, instead of writing them off, offers them another chance to build something faithful.</p>
-
-<p>If this is the pattern of God's dealing with humanity, then the church must stop treating the second marriage as a concession and start treating it as an instance of the pattern. Grace does not erase the past — the first marriage happened, the failure happened, the pain happened, and none of it is undone by the second ceremony. What grace does is make the past usable. It transforms the failure from a disqualification into a teacher. It says: what you learned in the fire, bring it into this new thing. Let the scars inform the love. Let the grief deepen the gratitude. Let the failure make you humble enough to succeed.</p>
-
-<h2>What the Second Marriage Needs from the Church</h2>
-
-<p>The second marriage needs the church to do four things.</p>
-
-<p>First, welcome without asterisk. The couple entering a second marriage does not need to be reminded that their first marriage failed. They know. They carry it every day. What they need is a community that receives them as they are — with their history, their children, their complications — and says, "You are home here."</p>
-
-<p>Second, pastoral care that acknowledges the complexity. Second marriages face challenges that first marriages do not: blended family dynamics, co-parenting with ex-spouses, the integration of different family cultures, the management of financial obligations from previous relationships. The church that applies its standard first-marriage counseling to a second marriage is not being efficient. It is being lazy.</p>
-
-<p>Third, patience with the timeline. Second marriages take longer to reach their stride. The couple is not just learning each other — they are learning each other while unlearning the patterns of a previous relationship. This takes time. More time than the church usually offers before it starts asking, "How are things going?" with that particular pastoral inflection that means, "Are you sure this was a good idea?"</p>
-
-<p>Fourth, the freedom to grieve. The second marriage does not erase the grief of the first marriage's ending. That grief will surface — at unexpected times, triggered by unexpected things — and the second spouse must be allowed to feel it without the new partner interpreting it as a commentary on their relationship. The person who grieves their first marriage while building their second is not being unfaithful to the second. They are being human. And the church must make room for the grief alongside the new beginning.</p>
-
-<p>The second marriage is an act of courage that the church does not always recognize as such. It is the decision to try again — knowing what failure costs, knowing what the odds are, knowing that the scars of the first attempt will ache in weather they cannot predict. It is the most human thing in the world: to be broken and to choose, despite the brokenness, to love again.</p>
-
-<p>The God who makes all things new does not require that the old things never happened. God requires only that we bring them — all of them, the joy and the wreckage — and lay them down, and begin.</p>`
