-<p>The church created the conditions for this abuse by teaching a theology of marriage that concentrates authority in one person without building in any mechanism of accountability. When the husband is the head and the wife is the body, and the head answers to God and not to the body, there is no check on the head's exercise of power. The system depends entirely on the head being benevolent. And when the head is not benevolent — when the head is controlling, manipulative, cruel — the system has no corrective. It simply tells the body to submit harder.</p>
-
-<h2>What Abuse Actually Looks Like</h2>
-
-<p>The church needs a clear definition of abuse, because the absence of a definition has allowed abusers to operate in plain sight. Abuse is a pattern of behavior — not a single incident but a pattern — in which one person systematically uses power, control, fear, or intimidation to diminish, isolate, or subjugate another person.</p>
-
-<p>Physical abuse is the form the church recognizes most readily, and even this recognition is inadequate. I have known pastors who counseled women to return to husbands who hit them, on the grounds that the violence was provoked, or infrequent, or the result of stress that would pass. These pastors were not evil. They were untrained, and their training gap had consequences measured in bruises, broken bones, and occasionally in death.</p>
-
-<p>But physical abuse is only one form. Emotional abuse — the systematic degradation of a person's sense of self — is equally destructive and far harder to see. The husband who never hits his wife but tells her, daily, that she is stupid, incompetent, unattractive, and spiritually immature is abusing her. The wife who never raises her hand but controls her husband through shame, manipulation, and the threat of public humiliation is abusing him. These forms of abuse leave no visible marks, and the absence of visible marks has allowed the church to pretend they do not exist.</p>
-
-<p>Spiritual abuse is the form most unique to the church, and it deserves particular attention. Spiritual abuse occurs when one person uses spiritual authority — whether the authority of a pastor, an elder, or a husband exercising "headship" — to control another person's behavior, restrict their freedom, or punish their independence. It includes using prayer as a weapon ("I am praying that God will convict you of your rebellion"), using Scripture as a bludgeon ("The Bible says you are to submit, and your refusal to submit is sin"), and using church discipline as a threat ("If you leave me, I will tell the elders, and they will discipline you").</p>
