-
-<p>Couples counseling, in particular, is dangerous in abusive situations. The premise of couples counseling is that both parties share responsibility for the relational dynamic and both need to change. In an abusive relationship, this premise is false. The abuser is responsible for the abuse. The victim is not contributing to a "dynamic." They are surviving a pattern of control. Couples counseling in this context gives the abuser information — the victim's vulnerabilities, their complaints, their emotional needs — that the abuser will weaponize in the next argument. Many domestic violence experts identify couples counseling as a contraindication in abusive relationships, and the church must learn this distinction.</p>
-
-<p>The institutional failure is the most painful to name. The church has an institutional interest in keeping marriages together. Divorce is bad for the church's public image. It complicates the community. It raises questions about the church's teaching. And so the church's instinct, when confronted with abuse in a marriage, is to counsel reconciliation — to send the victim back, to give the abuser another chance, to believe the abuser's repentance because believing it is easier than dealing with the alternative.</p>
-
