-
-<h2>What Repentance Looks Like — and What It Does Not</h2>
-
-<p>The abuser who is confronted will almost always express remorse. He will cry. He will quote Scripture. He will say he has seen the light, that he is a changed man, that God has spoken to him. He may be sincere. He may not. The point is that sincerity, in the moment of confrontation, is not the same as repentance, and repentance is not the same as change.</p>
-
-<p>Genuine repentance in an abuser has specific, observable markers, and the church must learn to identify them. Genuine repentance accepts full responsibility — not "I was wrong, but you provoked me," but "I was wrong." Genuine repentance does not minimize — not "I only raised my voice," but "I used my voice to control and frighten you, and that is abuse." Genuine repentance submits to accountability — not the accountability of a small group that meets weekly and asks how he is doing, but the accountability of a structured program designed specifically for abusive men, led by professionals trained in the dynamics of abuse.</p>
-
-<p>Genuine repentance also takes time. The abuser who changes in a week has not changed. He has adapted. The patterns of abuse are deeply ingrained — they involve beliefs about power, entitlement, and gender that were formed over decades — and they do not shift because of a crisis or a confrontation or a powerful Sunday sermon. They shift, if they shift at all, through years of sustained, difficult, professionally guided work. The church that accepts a quick repentance and sends the victim home is not practicing grace. It is practicing negligence.</p>
-
