-
-<p>Third, it must believe victims. The default response when someone discloses abuse should be: "I believe you. You are not crazy. This is not your fault. How can I help?" Not: "Have you tried submitting more?" Not: "What did you do to provoke him?" Not: "Are you sure you are not exaggerating?" Believe the victim. The research is clear: false allegations of domestic abuse are rare. The far more common reality is underreporting — victims who do not disclose because they have been taught, by the church's theology and the church's silence, that what is happening to them is normal.</p>
