-<p>I have watched couples arrive at this yes after years of wandering in the no. I have watched marriages that should have ended — by every measure of human wisdom, by every calculus of cost and benefit — survive because two people decided that the covenant was more real than the pain. Their marriages are not fairy tales. They are scarred, limping, hard-won things. But they carry a weight that the easy marriages do not. They have been tested. They know what they are made of. And what they are made of is not their own strength. It is the faithfulness of a God who does not break His promises, even when the people inside those promises break everything else.</p>
-
-<blockquote>We do not stay married because marriage is easy. We do not stay married because we are strong. We stay married because we made a promise in the presence of a God who keeps His promises, and we have staked our lives on the wager that His pattern — not ours — is the one worth following.</blockquote>
