-    body: `<p>The complaint arrives in my office in different words, but it is always the same complaint. "He is there but he is not there." "She has shut down and I cannot reach her." "We live in the same house and I have never been lonelier." The presenting issue varies — sometimes it is framed as a communication problem, sometimes as a conflict about parenting or money or sex. But underneath the presenting issue, the wound is the same: one spouse has become emotionally absent, and the other is starving.</p>
-
-<p>Emotional absence is not dramatic. It does not announce itself with a crisis or a revelation. It is quiet, gradual, almost invisible. The absent spouse still comes home. They still eat dinner at the table. They still sleep in the same bed. They may even be pleasant, competent, responsible. They pay the bills, mow the lawn, drive the kids to practice. From the outside, nothing is wrong. From the inside, everything is wrong, because the one thing that makes a marriage a marriage — the emotional connection between two people — has been withdrawn.</p>
-
-<p>This is the most common and least discussed form of marital suffering. It does not make the headlines like infidelity. It does not trigger the alarm bells like addiction. It is a slow starvation — a death by inches — and the person experiencing it often cannot explain what is wrong because nothing identifiable has happened. The spouse has not done anything wrong. That is precisely the problem. They have not done anything at all.</p>
-
-<h2>What Emotional Absence Looks Like</h2>
-
-<p>Emotional absence manifests in patterns that are recognizable once you know what to look for. The absent spouse does not initiate conversation about anything beyond logistics. They do not ask how your day was and mean it. They do not notice when you are upset. They do not respond when you try to share something that matters to you — a worry about the children, a frustration at work, a moment of beauty that you wanted to share. They are not hostile. They are not cold, exactly. They are simply elsewhere — their attention directed inward or outward, anywhere but at you.</p>
-
-<p>The absent spouse often does not realize they are absent. This is not an excuse. It is a diagnostic observation. Many emotionally absent people are not withholding intentionally. They are living inside a pattern they did not choose and cannot see — a pattern formed in childhood, reinforced by culture, and sustained by the sheer momentum of adult life. They learned early that the safest place to be was inside themselves, that emotional exposure was dangerous, that the best way to survive was to become a fortress. The fortress kept them safe as children. It is killing their marriage as adults.</p>
-
-<p>The absent spouse may also be absent because they are overwhelmed. The demands of work, parenting, financial pressure, and the constant low-grade anxiety of modern life can consume the emotional resources that the marriage needs. The spouse who comes home after a twelve-hour workday, having managed people and problems and crises for someone else's profit, may simply have nothing left. The tank is empty. And the marriage, which requires fuel to run, stalls. This form of absence is not a character flaw. It is a structural problem — a problem of a culture that extracts more from its workers than human beings were designed to give.</p>
-
-<p>The attachment literature is helpful here. John Bowlby, the British psychiatrist who developed attachment theory in the mid-twentieth century, identified a pattern he called "avoidant attachment" — a relational style formed in early childhood when the primary caregiver was consistently unavailable or unresponsive. The child learns that emotional needs will not be met, and rather than continuing to reach out into empty space, the child adapts. The child learns to suppress the need, to self-soothe rather than seek comfort, to build an internal world that does not depend on anyone else.</p>
-
-<p>This adaptation is brilliant in childhood. It is a survival strategy of remarkable sophistication. And it is catastrophic in adulthood, because the strategy that was designed to protect the child from the pain of unmet attachment needs now prevents the adult from meeting their spouse's attachment needs. The avoidant partner is not choosing to be distant. They are running software written thirty years ago, in a context that no longer exists, for a purpose that no longer applies.</p>
