-<h2>The Gendered Dimension</h2>
-
-<p>I need to say something about gender here, because the research consistently shows a gendered pattern in emotional absence, and ignoring the pattern does not serve the couples who live inside it.</p>
-
-<p>Men are disproportionately the emotionally absent partner. Not exclusively — women can be emotionally absent, and the experience is equally painful when they are. But the pattern skews male, and the reasons are not mysterious. Boys in Western culture are systematically trained out of emotional expression from an early age. "Boys don't cry." "Man up." "Don't be so sensitive." The socialization is relentless, and by adulthood, many men have lost access to their own emotional interior — not because the emotions are not there, but because the capacity to identify, name, and express them has atrophied from disuse.</p>
-
-<p>Niobe Way's longitudinal research, published in "Deep Secrets," documented that adolescent boys in the United States have rich, emotionally intimate friendships in early adolescence that they systematically abandon by late adolescence, under the pressure of masculine norms that equate emotional closeness between males with weakness or homosexuality. By adulthood, the average American man has zero intimate friendships — zero people to whom he can say what he actually feels. His wife is often the only person in his life with whom emotional intimacy is even theoretically possible. And he has been trained, for his entire life, to be incapable of it.</p>
-
-<p>This is not an excuse for emotional absence. It is a context. And the church, which has largely reinforced the masculine norms that produce emotional absence — the strong, silent leader, the provider who does not need, the man who leads his family with quiet authority rather than emotional vulnerability — bears a particular responsibility for addressing it.</p>
-
-<h2>What the Absent Spouse Must Learn</h2>
-
-<p>The emotionally absent spouse must learn something they were never taught: that emotional presence is a skill, not a personality trait, and that skills can be acquired. This learning is painful, because it requires the absent spouse to re-enter the emotional terrain they spent their childhood learning to avoid. It requires them to feel things they have been suppressing for decades. It requires them to be bad at something in front of the person whose opinion matters most.</p>
-
-<p>The learning begins with awareness — the recognition that the absence is real, that it has consequences, and that the spouse's complaints about it are not nagging but a legitimate cry of pain. This recognition is often the hardest step, because the absent spouse's defensive system is designed to minimize the significance of emotional connection. "I am here, aren't I?" "I work hard to provide for this family." "What more do you want?" These responses are not malicious. They are the sound of a defensive system protecting itself from a demand it does not know how to meet.</p>
-
-<p>The learning continues with practice — small, deliberate acts of emotional engagement that, over time, begin to rewire the patterns. Asking a question and waiting for the answer. Noticing when your spouse is upset and saying so. Sharing something you feel — not something you think, but something you feel — without being prompted. Putting the phone down during conversation. Making eye contact. These are elementary things. For the emotionally absent spouse, they feel like crossing a minefield.</p>
-
-<p>The learning is sustained by therapeutic support. I cannot say this strongly enough: the pattern of emotional absence is rarely broken without professional help. The defensive system that produces the absence is deeply embedded, often rooted in childhood experiences that the absent spouse has never processed. A competent therapist — particularly one trained in emotionally focused therapy or attachment-based approaches — can guide the absent spouse into the emotional territory they have been avoiding, with the safety and structure that makes the re-entry survivable.</p>
-
-<h2>What the Pursuing Spouse Must Learn</h2>
-
-<p>The pursuing spouse's learning is equally difficult, though it takes a different form. The pursuer must learn to regulate their own anxiety without depending on the absent spouse's response. This does not mean suppressing the need for connection. It means finding ways to soothe the attachment fear that drives the pursuit — developing the internal resources that allow them to tolerate the gap between what they need and what their spouse can currently provide.</p>
-
-<p>The pursuer must also learn to translate their anger into vulnerability. The absent spouse does not respond to anger. Anger triggers their defensive system and drives them further into withdrawal. But vulnerability — the soft, exposed expression of the need beneath the anger — can sometimes reach the absent spouse in a way that anger cannot. "I miss you" lands differently than "You never talk to me." "I am scared we are losing each other" lands differently than "You do not care about this marriage." The message is the same. The delivery determines whether it is received.</p>
-
-<p>This is not fair. I know it is not fair. The pursuing spouse should not have to translate their legitimate pain into a form the absent spouse can receive. But fairness and effectiveness are not the same thing, and the goal is not to win the argument but to reach the person. And reaching the person sometimes requires speaking their language rather than demanding that they learn yours.</p>
-
-<h2>What the Church Must Do</h2>
-
-<p>The church must stop celebrating emotional absence as spiritual maturity. The quiet, steady, unemotional man is not the model of Christian masculinity. He may be the victim of a socialization process that has cut him off from half of his own humanity. The church that holds up stoicism as a virtue is holding up a wound as a trophy, and the marriages in its pews are paying the price.</p>
-
-<p>The church must create spaces where emotional honesty is possible — where men can say what they actually feel without being judged, where vulnerability is treated as strength rather than weakness, where the pastoral leadership models emotional presence rather than emotional performance. This requires pastors who are themselves willing to be emotionally present — not performed vulnerability for a sermon illustration, but genuine, risky, unscripted honesty about their own inner lives.</p>
-
-<blockquote>The emotionally absent spouse is not a villain. They are a person who learned, long before they learned to love, that the safest place in the world was inside themselves. The work of marriage — and the work of the Spirit — is to show them that there is a safer place: the space between two people who have chosen to stay, to feel, and to be fully present to each other, not because it is natural but because it is necessary, and not because it is easy but because it is love.</blockquote>
-
-<p>That work is long. It is humbling. It does not produce the dramatic breakthroughs that make for good testimonies. It produces small, incremental changes — a question asked, an emotion named, a hand held in silence — that accumulate over years into something that begins to resemble the intimacy both partners have been craving.</p>
-
-<p>I have watched this happen in my own marriage. I have been the emotionally absent spouse — present in body, absent in soul, giving the church everything and giving home the remnants. I have sat across from my wife and realized, with a shame I cannot adequately describe, that she was telling me something important and I had not heard a word. Not because I was not listening. Because I had nothing left to listen with. The empathy had been spent on parishioners. The attentiveness had been deployed in counseling sessions. The capacity for emotional presence had been distributed across forty hours of pastoral work, and the well was dry.</p>
-
-<p>The recovery was not dramatic. It was not a single conversation or a breakthrough moment. It was a slow, humbling relearning of skills I should never have let atrophy — the skill of asking a question and caring about the answer, the skill of putting the phone down without being asked, the skill of noticing the look on my wife's face and saying, "Something is wrong. Tell me." These are elementary skills. I had to learn them like a child learning to walk. And the relearning required a willingness to be bad at something in front of the person whose judgment I feared most. That willingness, more than any technique or insight, was the beginning of the return.</p>
-
-<p>The intimacy will not look like the movies. It will look like two imperfect people, fumbling toward each other in the dark, learning to say the things they were never taught to say. It will be awkward and halting and sometimes painful. It will also be real. And real, in a marriage, is worth more than any performance could ever be.</p>`
