-    body: `<p>In fifteen years of pastoral ministry, I have never seen a couple fight about money. I have seen them fight about what money represents — security, freedom, control, identity, the fear of becoming their parents, the fear of failing their children — but never about money itself. Money is the surface. It is always the surface. And the couples who spend their energy arguing about the surface never get to the substance underneath, which is why the arguments recur with such depressing regularity. You cannot resolve a conflict you have not correctly diagnosed.</p>
-
-<p>The research confirms what pastoral experience suggests. A study published in "Family Relations" in 2012 by Jeffrey Dew, Sonya Britt, and Sandra Huston found that arguments about money were the strongest disagreement-type predictor of divorce — more than arguments about sex, parenting, or in-laws. But the same research found that the predictive power was not about the amount of money. Couples with high incomes fought about money with the same intensity and frequency as couples with low incomes. The variable was not the balance in the account. It was the meaning assigned to the balance. And meaning, as every theologian knows, is where the real battles are fought.</p>
-
-<p>I have five sons. I grew up without a father. The intersection of those two facts means that my relationship with money is shaped by scarcity and responsibility in ways I did not understand for most of my adult life. I provided fiercely because I had not been provided for. I worried obsessively because worry felt like vigilance, and vigilance felt like love. It took years — and a wife who refused to accept my anxiety as virtue — to see that the money story I was living was not a financial plan. It was a wound wearing a suit.</p>
-
-<h2>The Money Stories You Inherited</h2>
-
-<p>Before you ever opened a joint checking account, you had a money story. It was written for you in childhood, by parents and circumstances you did not choose, and it was inscribed so deeply into your operating system that by the time you married, you did not even know it was there. You thought you were just being "responsible" or "generous" or "careful." You were actually enacting a script that was written decades ago, in a household you no longer live in, by people whose financial anxieties became your own.</p>
-
-<p>The child who grew up in poverty carries a particular relationship with money into adulthood. Money is scarce. Money can disappear. Money must be hoarded, protected, worried over. Every purchase is a risk. Every expenditure is a potential catastrophe. The adult who carries this story may have a six-figure income and a full savings account and still feel the anxiety of the child who watched the power get shut off. The rational brain knows the bills are paid. The child brain does not trust it.</p>
-
-<p>The child who grew up in affluence carries a different relationship. Money is available. Money is a tool. Money solves problems. The adult who carries this story may spend freely, give generously, and feel genuinely confused when their spouse panics about the credit card statement. They are not being irresponsible. They are living inside a story in which money is not a source of fear.</p>
-
-<p>When these two stories marry each other — and they frequently do, because opposites attract precisely because they compensate for each other's anxiety — the result is a conflict that neither party can resolve by argument, because the conflict is not about the current expenditure. It is about the childhood wound that the expenditure has activated. He sees a dining room table she wants to buy and feels the panic of a child whose family could not afford furniture. She sees a savings account he obsesses over and feels the suffocation of a child whose family's frugality was actually control. Both are responding to the present through the lens of the past, and neither knows it.</p>
-
-<p>The first step in resolving money conflicts in marriage is not a better budget. It is a better autobiography. Each spouse must tell the story of money as they experienced it growing up — not the financial details, but the emotional reality. What did money feel like in your home? Was it discussed openly or hidden? Was it a source of anxiety or a source of freedom? What was the worst money moment of your childhood? What did you swear you would never repeat? These questions do not appear in any financial planning software. They are more important than any spreadsheet.</p>
-
-<h2>The Four Money Personalities</h2>
-
-<p>Financial counselors and researchers have identified broad patterns in how individuals relate to money. The specific taxonomies vary, but the general categories are consistent across the literature. Savers hoard. Spenders deploy. Avoiders refuse to engage. Risk-takers gamble on future returns. Every person carries a primary orientation, and every marriage involves the collision of two orientations that are almost never the same.</p>
-
-<p>The saver-spender marriage is the most common combination and the most volatile. The saver sees the spender as reckless, irresponsible, a threat to the family's stability. The spender sees the saver as controlling, fearful, unwilling to enjoy the resources they have worked to earn. Both are partially right. Both are projecting their own money story onto their spouse's behavior. And the argument about whether to take the vacation or add to the emergency fund is never, ever about the vacation or the emergency fund. It is about two competing visions of what money is for, rooted in two different childhoods, expressing two different fears.</p>
-
-<p>The avoider-engaged marriage is less volatile but equally damaging. One spouse handles the finances. The other checks out — does not look at the statements, does not participate in the budgeting, does not want to know. The engaged spouse carries the burden and the anxiety alone. The avoiding spouse enjoys the comfort of ignorance. And the power imbalance — because financial control is power, whether or not the controlling spouse intends it to be — grows wider with every year of avoidance.</p>
-
-<h2>The Theology of Money in Marriage</h2>
-
-<p>The Bible treats money as a diagnostic tool. It does not tell you how much to save or how much to spend. It tells you that your relationship with money reveals your relationship with God. "Where your treasure is, there your heart will be also" (Matthew 6:21). The verse is not a prohibition against having treasure. It is a warning that your treasure — whatever you value most, whatever you protect most fiercely, whatever you worry about losing — will reveal the location of your heart. And in a marriage, the way each spouse relates to money reveals the location of their heart with uncomfortable precision.</p>
-
-<p>The spouse who cannot stop saving is placing their security in the account balance rather than in God. I say this not as judgment but as diagnosis, because I recognize the pattern in myself. The anxiety that drives compulsive saving is the anxiety of a heart that does not trust God to provide. It is the functional atheism of the Israelites who gathered more manna than they needed, despite God's explicit instruction that each day's portion would be sufficient. The extra manna rotted. It always does. Because the security we manufacture for ourselves is not the security God offers. It is the illusion of security, sustained by vigilance and anxiety, and it consumes the very life it was meant to protect.</p>
-
-<p>The spouse who cannot stop spending is placing their identity in acquisition rather than in God. The purchase provides a momentary relief from the emptiness, a brief injection of significance, a temporary sense that life is abundant. But the relief fades, the significance evaporates, and the emptiness returns, demanding another purchase. Ecclesiastes 5:10: "Whoever loves money never has enough; whoever loves wealth is never satisfied with their income." The wisdom is three thousand years old. The pattern has not changed.</p>
-
-<p>Neither saving nor spending is inherently sinful. Both are necessary. The question is what drives them — whether the saving is prudence or fear, whether the spending is generosity or compulsion, whether the money is a tool in service of the marriage or an idol in competition with it.</p>
-
-<h2>The Power Dynamics</h2>
-
-<p>Money is power in a marriage, and the refusal to acknowledge this truth does not make it less true. It makes it more dangerous, because unacknowledged power operates without accountability.</p>
-
-<p>In marriages where one spouse earns significantly more than the other — or where one spouse earns and the other does not — the earning spouse holds a structural power advantage that shapes every financial decision. The non-earning spouse may technically have equal access to the accounts. They do not have equal standing in the negotiation. The unspoken reality — "I earned this money" — hangs over every conversation about how the money is spent, even when it is never said aloud. Especially when it is never said aloud, because the unspoken has a power that the spoken cannot match.</p>
-
-<p>The stay-at-home parent is particularly vulnerable to this dynamic. Their contribution to the household — the cooking, the cleaning, the child-rearing, the emotional labor that keeps the family functioning — is invisible in the economy's accounting. It does not generate a paycheck. It does not appear on a balance sheet. And in a culture that equates value with earning power, the stay-at-home parent absorbs a subtle but persistent message: your work does not count. This message, absorbed over years, erodes the parent's sense of agency in financial decisions and can produce a dependency that feels less like partnership and more like permission.</p>
-
-<p>The covenant of marriage demands the dismantling of this power dynamic. Not because power differences are inherently sinful but because the covenant transforms two individuals into one economic unit, and the resources of that unit belong to the unit, not to the individual who generated them. Paul's language in 1 Corinthians 7 about the body applies to the finances: "The husband's income does not belong to the husband alone, and the wife's income does not belong to the wife alone." I am paraphrasing, but the principle holds. The one-flesh union does not permit the financial sovereignty of either partner.</p>
-
-<h2>The Practice of Financial Unity</h2>
-
-<p>What does financial unity look like in practice? It looks like two people sitting at the same table, looking at the same numbers, with equal voice and equal information, making decisions together. This is simple to describe and agonizing to practice, because it requires both spouses to expose their money stories, their fears, their desires, and their shame — and money shame is among the deepest and least discussed shames a person can carry.</p>
-
-<p>The practice begins with full transparency. Both spouses must know the complete financial picture — all income, all debt, all assets, all obligations. The spouse who hides a credit card balance or a savings account or a financial obligation is not protecting the marriage. They are lying to it. And financial deception, while it lacks the dramatic force of sexual infidelity, is a betrayal of the covenant that erodes trust with the same corrosive effect.</p>
-
-<p>The practice continues with regular financial conversations — not the crisis conversation that happens when the credit card bill arrives, but the proactive, scheduled, calm conversation about where the money is going, where it should go, and what each spouse values. These conversations will be uncomfortable. They will expose differences that feel irreconcilable. They will surface the childhood wounds that neither spouse has fully processed. And they are absolutely necessary, because the alternative — financial silence, financial avoidance, financial unilateralism — produces the very outcomes that both spouses fear.</p>
-
-<p>The practice is sustained by a shared vision. What are we building together? What do we want our money to make possible? What would our finances look like if they reflected our actual values rather than our unexamined habits? These questions are not merely financial. They are theological. They ask what the marriage is for, what the family serves, and whether the life being built reflects the kingdom or merely the culture.</p>
-
-<h2>Generosity as Marital Practice</h2>
-
-<p>I will say one more thing, because it is true even though it sounds counterintuitive: one of the most powerful things a couple can do for their marriage is give money away. Generosity — regular, sacrificial, deliberate giving — does something to a marriage that no budget software can replicate. It breaks the grip of scarcity. It dismantles the idol of security. It forces both spouses to confront the question that every money fight is ultimately about: do we trust God to provide, or do we trust ourselves?</p>
-
-<p>The couples in my congregation who tithe — who give ten percent or more of their income to the church and to the poor — are not wealthier than the couples who do not. But they fight about money less. Not because they have solved the financial puzzle but because generosity has loosened money's hold on their hearts. The money has been put in its place. It is a tool, not a god. And a tool does not generate the anxiety that a god demands.</p>
-
-<blockquote>Every money fight is an invitation — an invitation to go deeper, to name the fear, to excavate the story, to ask what the money actually represents and whether the thing it represents can be found in a balance sheet or only in the God who owns everything and gives freely to those who ask.</blockquote>
-
-<p>The invitation is uncomfortable. It always is. But the couples who accept it — who sit down together and do the hard, humbling work of understanding not just what they earn and spend but why they earn and spend the way they do — those couples discover something that the money itself could never buy: the freedom of two people who are no longer controlled by the thing they thought they were controlling.</p>
-
-<p>I learned this in my own marriage. I grew up without a father, and the absence left me with a money story written in fear — the fear that provision could disappear without warning, the fear that I would not be enough, the fear that the people I loved would go without. I saved compulsively. I budgeted rigidly. I said no to things that would have brought my family joy because the joy cost money and money, in my internal economy, was the only barrier between my family and catastrophe. My wife did not share this fear. She grew up in a household where money was present and shared freely, where generosity was practiced without anxiety, where the future was trusted. She saw my saving as control. I saw her spending as recklessness. We were both wrong. We were both right. And the resolution did not come through compromise — split the difference, save half, spend half. It came through the slow, painful, deeply personal work of telling each other our stories and listening to what the stories revealed.</p>
-
-<p>That freedom is available. It is costly. And it begins not with a better budget but with a braver conversation.</p>`
