-<p>John Gottman's research on the transition to parenthood found that 67 percent of couples experienced a significant decline in marital satisfaction within three years of the birth of their first child. The decline was steeper for couples who had not developed strong friendship and conflict-resolution skills before the baby arrived. The 33 percent who did not experience the decline were not better parents. They were not wealthier or more rested or more temperamentally suited to the chaos of infant care. They were couples who had built a relational foundation strong enough to absorb the shock of parenthood without collapsing.</p>
