-<h2>Rediscovery</h2>
-
-<p>The word "rediscovery" implies that something was lost. It was. In the chaos of parenting — the diapers, the feedings, the school runs, the homework battles, the adolescent crises — the couple who fell in love got buried under the weight of what they built together. The love did not die. It went underground, displaced by demands that were more urgent even if they were not more important.</p>
-
-<p>Rediscovery begins with curiosity. When was the last time you asked your spouse what they are reading, thinking, struggling with, hoping for? When was the last time you looked at them and saw not the co-parent, not the co-manager, not the person who forgot to switch the laundry, but the person you chose — the specific, irreplaceable, fascinating person you chose?</p>
-
-<p>The person is still there. They have changed, certainly — as you have changed, as everyone changes across the decades of adult life. But the core of them — the thing that drew you, the quality that made you think, "This one. This is the one I want" — that core has not disappeared. It has been covered over. And the work of the parenting years, alongside the work of raising the children, is the work of uncovering what was buried, of remembering who you married, and of choosing them again.</p>
-
-<p>The choice must be daily and it must be specific. It is not enough to say, "I love you." You must say, "I noticed that you handled that situation with the kids beautifully." You must say, "I was thinking about you today, and I remembered the trip we took before the children came." You must say, "I want to know what you are dreaming about, because I realize I have not asked in months." Specificity is the language of attention, and attention is the currency of love. The spouse who receives specific attention knows they are seen. The spouse who receives only generic affirmation suspects they are being managed.</p>
