-    body: `<p>The house gets quiet. It does not get quiet gradually, the way the mornings lengthen in spring. It gets quiet all at once — a door closing, a car pulling out of the driveway, a bedroom that is suddenly just a room. The child who filled that room — who filled it with noise and mess and need and presence — is gone. Not gone permanently, not gone irretrievably, but gone in the way that matters for the daily life of the household. The dinner table has an empty chair. The bathroom counter has space. The schedule, which for two decades was organized around someone else's needs, is suddenly and disoriently organized around nothing.</p>
-
-<p>And the two people who remain — the couple who built this life, who raised these children, who spent twenty or twenty-five or thirty years in the consuming project of parenthood — look at each other across the quiet house and realize that they are, in some essential sense, strangers. Not hostile strangers. Not indifferent strangers. But strangers in the way that two people become strangers when they have spent decades standing side by side, facing the same direction, attending to the same tasks, without ever turning to face each other.</p>
