-<p>The empty nest is not a vacation. It is a reckoning. And the marriage that has not prepared for it — that has used the children as the connective tissue between two people who stopped connecting with each other years ago — will find the reckoning devastating.</p>
-
-<h2>The Statistics and the Silence</h2>
-
-<p>The divorce rate among adults over fifty has doubled since 1990, according to research by Susan Brown and I-Fen Lin at Bowling Green State University. The phenomenon is significant enough to have earned its own label: "gray divorce." And while gray divorce has multiple causes — longer lifespans, reduced stigma, financial independence of women, the influence of the baby boom generation's cultural values — one of the most common precipitating factors is the departure of the last child.</p>
-
-<p>The AARP survey of midlife divorce, covering ages forty to sixty-nine, found that 66 percent were initiated by women. The most commonly cited reason was not infidelity or abuse. It was emotional disconnection — the sense of having grown apart, of living with someone who no longer knew them, of enduring a marriage that was functional but dead. The women who left did not leave in anger. Many left in grief — grief for the marriage they had wanted, grief for the intimacy they had never received, grief for the years they had spent waiting for the relationship to become what it had never been.</p>
-
-<p>The men, by and large, were blindsided. They thought the marriage was fine. They had been providing, protecting, working, doing the things they understood marriage to require. The fact that their wives were starving — emotionally, conversationally, romantically — did not register, because the language of emotional starvation was not in their vocabulary. They were not bad men. They were men who had mistaken presence for connection and provision for partnership, and the mistake, uncorrected across decades, proved fatal.</p>
