-<p>I pastor men in this exact situation. I have watched the bewilderment on their faces when they discover, in their fifties, that the wife they thought was content was actually enduring. The bewilderment is genuine. They did not know. They did not know because they did not ask, and they did not ask because the culture that shaped them did not teach them that asking was part of the job description. The failure is real. The responsibility is theirs. But the context matters, because understanding how we got here is the first step toward getting somewhere else.</p>
-
-<h2>What the Children Were Covering</h2>
-
-<p>The departure of the children does not create the distance between spouses. It reveals it. The distance was always there, hidden beneath the noise and activity of family life. The children provided a shared project, a common purpose, a daily structure that made the marriage feel active even when the marriage itself was dormant. You do not need to connect with your spouse when every evening is consumed by homework supervision, sports practice transportation, bedtime routines, and the logistics of managing multiple small lives. The children filled the space. The children generated the conversation. The children provided the excuse not to face each other.</p>
-
-<p>I do not say this to blame the children. The children did not ask to be the marriage's connective tissue. They did not volunteer to be the buffer between two people who had stopped doing the work of intimacy. They simply filled the available space, because that is what children do. And when they left, the space they had filled revealed what had been there all along: two people who had forgotten how to be married to each other.</p>
-
-<p>This is a particular grief — the grief of discovering, in your fifties or sixties, that the marriage you thought you had was actually a parenting partnership that has now concluded its primary project. The love is not gone. The history is not meaningless. But the relationship, stripped of its organizing principle, feels hollow. And the hollowness is terrifying, because it suggests that the best years of the marriage were not really about the marriage at all.</p>
