-
-<p>The empty nest triggers an identity crisis in both spouses, and the crisis is sharper for the spouse whose identity was most fully invested in the parenting role. For many women — particularly those who stayed home during the parenting years or who organized their professional lives around the children's needs — the departure of the last child is an existential event. The role that gave shape to their days, purpose to their decisions, and meaning to their sacrifices has ended. They are still alive, still married, still capable. But they do not know who they are outside the identity of "Mom."</p>
-
-<p>Men face a parallel crisis, though it typically manifests differently. The father who defined himself by his ability to provide and protect discovers that there is no one left who needs his provision or protection in the immediate, daily sense. The house is secure. The college fund is depleted. The work that once felt meaningful — providing for a family — now feels purposeless. What are you working for when the people you were working for have gone?</p>
