-    body: `<p>You drive to church alone. Or you sit in the pew with an empty seat beside you — the seat your spouse would occupy if they believed, or cared, or were willing to pretend. You sing the songs and hear the sermon and smile at the people who ask, "Where is your husband today?" or "Is your wife not feeling well?" You have a dozen answers rehearsed, all of them true enough to end the conversation without revealing the truth: your spouse does not share your faith. Your spouse may not believe in God at all. And the most important relationship in your life is divided at the deepest possible level — the level of ultimate commitment, ultimate meaning, the level where you answer the question of what the world is for and what happens when it ends.</p>
-
-<p>I came to faith from atheism. I know what it looks like from the other side of the divide. I know the intellectual objections that are not merely intellectual but existential — the problem of suffering, the exclusivity claims, the institutional failures that make the whole enterprise look like a well-meaning fraud. I know that the believing spouse, however sincere, often does not understand what they are asking when they ask their spouse to believe. They are not asking them to add a Sunday activity. They are asking them to reorganize their entire understanding of reality around a set of claims that, to the unbelieving spouse, appear indistinguishable from wish fulfillment.</p>
-
-<p>And I know, from the believing spouse's side, the particular grief of loving someone who cannot share the thing that matters most to you. The grief of praying beside a person who does not pray. The grief of raising children between two worldviews. The grief of knowing that the deepest part of your life — your relationship with God — is the one part your spouse cannot enter.</p>
-
-<p>This is one of the loneliest experiences available to a married person. And the church, for all its talk about marriage, has almost nothing substantive to say about it.</p>
-
-<h2>What Paul Actually Said</h2>
-
-<p>The primary text is 1 Corinthians 7:12-16, and it deserves more careful attention than the church typically gives it. Paul writes: "To the rest I say (I, not the Lord) that if any brother has a wife who is an unbeliever, and she consents to live with him, he should not divorce her. If any woman has a husband who is an unbeliever, and he consents to live with her, she should not divorce him. For the unbelieving husband is made holy because of his wife, and the unbelieving wife is made holy because of her husband."</p>
-
-<p>Several things stand out. First, Paul explicitly identifies this as his own pastoral counsel, not a direct dominical command — "I, not the Lord." This is not a demotion of the counsel's authority. It is a specification of its type. Paul is applying the principles of the gospel to a situation Jesus did not directly address, and he does so with the full weight of his apostolic authority while maintaining the intellectual honesty to distinguish between a direct quotation from Jesus and his own apostolic reasoning. The church would do well to practice the same honesty in its own counseling.</p>
-
-<p>Second, Paul's counsel is to stay. Not to proselytize. Not to evangelize. Not to conduct a sustained campaign of spiritual persuasion within the marriage. To stay. The staying itself, Paul argues, has a sanctifying effect — the unbelieving spouse is "made holy" by the believing spouse's presence. The Greek word is hagiazo, and its precise meaning in this context has been debated for centuries. It does not mean that the unbelieving spouse is automatically saved. It means that the believing spouse's faithfulness creates a context in which holiness is possible — a proximity to grace that would not exist if the marriage were dissolved.</p>
-
-<p>Third, Paul offers no guarantee: "For how do you know, wife, whether you will save your husband? Or how do you know, husband, whether you will save your wife?" (v. 16). The uncertainty is the point. Paul does not promise conversion as the reward for faithfulness. He acknowledges that the outcome is unknown, and he counsels faithfulness anyway. This is the shape of Christian faith in nearly every domain: act faithfully without certainty of results, and trust the God whose timeline is not yours.</p>
-
-<p>Fourth — and this is the element most often overlooked — Paul includes a provision for departure. "But if the unbelieving partner separates, let it be so. In such cases the brother or sister is not enslaved" (v. 15). Paul does not demand that the believing spouse endure every possible situation without recourse. If the unbelieving spouse chooses to leave, the believing spouse is free. The principle is voluntariness. The believing spouse should not initiate departure. But neither should they be imprisoned by a partner who will not stay.</p>
-
-<h2>The Temptation to Evangelize Your Spouse</h2>
-
-<p>The most natural instinct of the believing spouse is to try to convert the unbelieving spouse. This instinct is understandable and almost always counterproductive. The marriage is not a mission field. The dining table is not a pulpit. The spouse who left a book on the nightstand, who forwards podcast episodes, who quotes Scripture during arguments, who prays conspicuously in the hope of provoking spiritual curiosity — that spouse is not doing evangelism. They are doing coercion, and the unbelieving spouse can feel the difference with absolute precision.</p>
-
-<p>The coercion is damaging not only because it does not work but because it violates the integrity of the relationship. The unbelieving spouse begins to feel that they are not loved for who they are but targeted for who they might become. Every conversation becomes suspect — is this genuine interest in my day, or is this the setup for another spiritual redirect? Every kindness carries a potential agenda. The marriage, which should be the safest place in the unbelieving spouse's life, becomes the place where they feel most surveilled, most evaluated, most insufficient.</p>
-
-<p>Peter's counsel in 1 Peter 3:1-2 is directed specifically to wives with unbelieving husbands, but its principle applies broadly: "Likewise, wives, be subject to your own husbands, so that even if some do not obey the word, they may be won without a word by the conduct of their wives, when they see your respectful and pure conduct." The critical phrase is "without a word." Not without theology. Not without faith. Without a word. The witness is the life, not the lecture. The sermon is the conduct, not the conversation. And the believing spouse who cannot trust God to work through their life rather than their arguments has a faith problem of their own.</p>
-
-<h2>The Grief of Spiritual Loneliness</h2>
-
-<p>Even when the believing spouse accepts Paul's counsel — stays, does not pressure, trusts the process — there remains a grief that must be named, because naming it is the first step toward surviving it.</p>
-
-<p>The grief is the grief of spiritual solitude within an intimate relationship. You cannot share your prayer life with someone who does not pray. You cannot process a sermon with someone who was not there. You cannot turn to your spouse in a moment of spiritual crisis — doubt, fear, the dark night of the soul — and find a partner who understands the terrain. The very thing that gives your life its deepest meaning is the thing you must carry alone.</p>
-
-<p>This grief is compounded in the parenting years. How do you raise children in the faith when your spouse does not share it? How do you answer your child's theological questions when your spouse's answers will be different? How do you take the children to church on Sunday without it becoming a source of marital tension? How do you explain to a twelve-year-old why Daddy does not come to church without either condemning Daddy or minimizing the faith?</p>
-
-<p>There are no clean answers to these questions. There are only honest negotiations — conversations between two people who love each other and disagree about the most fundamental questions of human existence, trying to find a way to raise children that honors both perspectives without pretending that the disagreement does not exist. The conversations are hard. They are necessary. And they require a level of mutual respect that is itself a form of sanctification, though it may not feel like it.</p>
-
-<p>The grief also has a particular sharpness during the moments when faith is most needed. When a parent dies. When a child is in the hospital. When the world breaks in a way that drives the believing spouse to their knees, and they look to their left and their partner is standing, arms folded, unable or unwilling to kneel beside them. The isolation of those moments is a particular species of suffering that the church has not adequately addressed, perhaps because the church assumes that everyone sitting in its pews has a believing spouse at home. Many do not.</p>
-
-<h2>What the Unbelieving Spouse Needs</h2>
-
-<p>The unbelieving spouse needs to be loved without agenda. Fully. Unconditionally. Without the subtext of hoped-for conversion coloring every interaction. They need to know that their believing spouse married them — not the person they might become if God answers a certain prayer, but them. The actual person who does not believe, who has legitimate reasons for not believing, whose skepticism is not a spiritual pathology but an honest intellectual position that deserves respect even from those who disagree with it.</p>
-
-<p>The unbelieving spouse needs to not be made to feel inferior. The implicit hierarchy that faith creates — the saved and the unsaved, the found and the lost, the redeemed and the unredeemed — is theologically real but relationally toxic when it enters the marriage. The believing spouse who treats their partner as a spiritual project rather than an equal partner has replaced the covenant with a rescue mission, and the unbelieving spouse can feel the condescension even when it is wrapped in love.</p>
-
-<p>The unbelieving spouse needs to be free — free to attend or not attend, free to participate or not participate, free to ask questions without being cornered, free to be honest about their doubts without those doubts being treated as a problem to be solved. Freedom is the condition of genuine faith. The God who will not coerce belief has given us the model. The believing spouse who cannot extend the same freedom to their partner has not understood the God they claim to serve.</p>
-
-<h2>What the Believing Spouse Needs</h2>
-
-<p>The believing spouse needs community. Not as a substitute for the spouse but as a supplement — a circle of people who share the faith, who can pray together, who can discuss the sermon, who can hold the believing spouse's spiritual life when the spouse at home cannot. The church must be this community. And the church must be this community without treating the believing spouse as an object of pity or the unbelieving spouse as a project.</p>
-
-<p>The believing spouse needs permission to grieve. The grief of spiritual loneliness in marriage is real, and it does not go away. It may lessen. It may become familiar enough that it no longer feels acute. But it remains — a low-grade ache, a persistent absence, the knowledge that the person you love most in the world cannot share the thing that matters most in your life. The church that acknowledges this grief — that says, "We see you, and we know this is hard, and you are not failing" — is doing more than most churches do.</p>
-
-<p>The believing spouse needs theology that is honest about uncertainty. The verse that promises "train up a child in the way he should go, and when he is old he will not depart from it" (Proverbs 22:6) is a proverb, not a guarantee. The prayer for the unbelieving spouse's conversion may be answered in a year, in a decade, or never in this lifetime. The God who is sovereign over all things has not promised to operate on your timeline, and the faith that demands a specific outcome on a specific schedule is not faith. It is a negotiation disguised as prayer.</p>
-
-<h2>The Marriage Itself as Witness</h2>
-
-<p>Here is what I have seen, over and over, in marriages where one spouse believes and the other does not: the most powerful witness is not the believing spouse's words. It is the marriage itself. The unbelieving spouse watches. They watch how their partner handles stress, how they respond to suffering, how they treat the people who cannot repay them. They watch whether the faith produces kindness or judgment, humility or arrogance, patience or frustration. They watch whether the believing spouse is becoming a better person — not a more religious person, but a genuinely better human being.</p>
-
-<p>And sometimes — not always, not on schedule, not in the way anyone expected — the watching produces something. A question asked quietly, in the dark, in bed, when the defenses are down: "What do you actually believe?" A willingness to attend a service, not out of obligation but out of curiosity. A crack in the certainty that there is nothing beyond the material world. The crack may widen into a door. The door may open. Or it may not. Both outcomes are within the scope of God's sovereignty.</p>
-
-<p>The believing spouse's job is not to open the door. It is to live in such a way that the door is visible — not through argument, not through pressure, not through the subtle coercion of spiritual superiority, but through the daily, embodied demonstration of what a life transformed by grace actually looks like. If the transformation is real, it will be evident. If it is not evident, the problem is not the unbelieving spouse's resistance. It is the believing spouse's inconsistency.</p>
-
-<blockquote>The marriage between a believer and an unbeliever is not a mistake to be corrected. It is a calling to be lived — a calling to love without condition, to witness without coercion, to grieve without bitterness, and to trust a God whose work in the human heart operates on a timeline that no human being controls.</blockquote>
-
-<p>The calling is hard. The loneliness is real. The temptation to force the issue is constant. But the God who called you to this marriage — the God who holds both of you, whether both of you know it or not — is at work. The work is slow. The work is often invisible. And the work may not produce the outcome you are praying for in the way you are praying for it.</p>
-
-<p>But the God who raised the dead is not constrained by your expectations. And the love that sustains a marriage across the deepest divide two people can inhabit is itself a testimony — a testimony that some things are stronger than agreement, that some commitments outlast understanding, and that the covenant, held in the hands of a faithful God, can bear the weight of questions that have no easy answers.</p>
-
-<p>Stay. Love. Pray. And leave the results to the One who started the work and has promised to finish it — in His time, in His way, and by means you cannot yet imagine.</p>`
-  }
-];
