-    body: `<p>Somewhere in the life of nearly every churchgoing Christian there is a low-grade, chronic guilt about the tithe. You know the number — ten percent — and you know you are probably not hitting it, and you know the pastor is going to preach on stewardship sometime around the fall budget season, and you brace for it the way you brace for a dentist appointment. The tithe has become one of the great sources of ambient shame in the Christian financial imagination. And the strange thing is that almost no one can actually tell you where the ten percent comes from, whether it is binding, or what it is supposed to mean.</p>
-
-<p>So let us do the thing the guilt prevents us from doing. Let us actually look at what the Bible says about the tithe, and let us be honest about where the conventional teaching is standing on solid ground and where it is standing on nothing at all.</p>
-
-<h2>The Old Testament Tithe Was Not What You Think</h2>
-
-<p>Start with the surprise. The tithe in the Old Testament was not a single ten percent. It was a system of multiple tithes that, taken together, amounted to something closer to twenty or twenty-three percent of annual produce. There was the Levitical tithe that supported the priestly tribe who had no land inheritance. There was the festival tithe that funded the annual pilgrimages and feasts. And there was a third-year tithe stored in the towns to feed the widow, the orphan, the foreigner, and the Levite. The tithe was fundamentally agricultural, tied to the produce of the land and the increase of the flocks, and it functioned as something like a combined religious tax and social welfare system for the covenant nation.</p>
-
-<p>This complicates the modern sermon considerably. When a pastor stands up and says "the Bible commands you to tithe ten percent of your income," he is compressing a complex, multi-layered agricultural system into a single number and applying it to a wage economy the Torah never envisioned. That does not make the compression illegitimate, but it should make the preacher humble about how directly he can wield the word command. I have preached the ten percent as though it fell from heaven with a decimal point already attached. It did not.</p>
-
-<h2>Abraham and Jacob: Before the Law</h2>
-
-<p>The tithe predates the law of Moses, which is the strongest argument that it reflects something more permanent than the Sinai covenant. Abraham gave a tenth of the spoils to Melchizedek in Genesis 14, a spontaneous act of worship after a military victory, long before there was any commandment requiring it. Jacob, fleeing his brother, vowed at Bethel that if God brought him home safely he would give God a tenth of everything.</p>
-
-<p>These two episodes are the favorite proof texts of the pro-tithing preacher, and they carry real weight, but notice what they actually show. Both are voluntary. Both are responses to grace already received — Abraham to victory, Jacob to a promise. Neither is a religious tax paid under obligation. They establish that giving God a proportion of what you have is an ancient instinct of worship. They do not establish that the proportion is legally fixed at ten percent for all people in all times.</p>
-
-<h2>Jesus and the Tithe</h2>
-
-<p>Here is the text that gets left out of the stewardship sermon. In Matthew 23, Jesus rebukes the Pharisees: "You give a tenth of your spices — mint, dill and cumin. But you have neglected the more important matters of the law — justice, mercy and faithfulness. You should have practiced the latter, without neglecting the former." (NIV) This is the only place Jesus directly addresses the tithe, and it is a stunning statement. He affirms the tithe — you should have practiced the former — while making clear that it is the least of the commandments, a matter so minor that a person can scrupulously obey it while missing the entire point of the law.</p>
-
-<blockquote>The Pharisees tithed their herb garden and swallowed injustice whole. Jesus did not tell them to stop tithing. He told them the tithe had become a way of not loving anyone.</blockquote>
-
-<p>This is the danger the tithe carries, and it is a danger I have watched play out in my own congregation and my own heart. The number becomes a ceiling instead of a floor. You write the check, you cross the line, you feel the small warm glow of obligation discharged, and you close your hand around the remaining ninety percent as though it were now entirely and unquestionably yours. But the New Testament nowhere teaches that ninety percent is yours. It teaches that one hundred percent is God's, and that you are a steward of all of it.</p>
-
-<h2>The New Testament's Silence and Its Meaning</h2>
-
-<p>The most important fact about the tithe in the New Testament is how little it appears. Paul, who wrote more about money and giving than any other apostle, never once commands the tithe. When he takes up the great collection for Jerusalem in 2 Corinthians 8 and 9, he does not say "remember your ten percent." He says something both freer and more terrifying: "Each of you should give what you have decided in your heart to give, not reluctantly or under compulsion, for God loves a cheerful giver." (NIV) And in 1 Corinthians 16 he asks each person to set aside a sum in keeping with their income — proportional, but not fixed.</p>
-
-<p>Some read this silence as liberation: the tithe is abolished, give whatever you feel like. But that reading gets the direction exactly backward. The New Testament does not lower the bar below the tithe. It removes the bar and replaces it with the whole of your life. Under the old covenant you owed ten percent and the rest was, in a sense, negotiable. Under the new covenant you owe everything, because you have been bought with a price and you are not your own. The tithe was a training-wheel proportion for a people learning to give. The gospel invites you off the training wheels into a generosity with no ceiling at all.</p>
-
-<h2>So Should You Tithe?</h2>
-
-<p>Here is where I have landed after fifteen years of preaching this, and I offer it as a pastor rather than a lawyer. The ten percent is not a New Testament command, and any preacher who tells you it is has overstated his case. But it is an extraordinarily wise starting place. It is a proportion old enough to predate the law and specific enough to be actionable, and it forces a question that vaguer instructions let us dodge. If you tell people to give as their heart decides, most hearts will decide on very little, because the heart is an accomplished self-deceiver, and mine is the most accomplished of all.</p>
-
-<p>So I teach the tithe not as law but as trellis — a structure to grow on. For the person who has never given systematically, ten percent is a bracing and clarifying goal that will reorder your finances and expose your idols faster than any sermon. For the person who has been giving faithfully for years, ten percent may be far too low, a comfortable plateau you have long since outgrown, and the honest question is not whether you are tithing but whether you are still being stretched.</p>
-
-<h2>The Guilt Question</h2>
-
-<p>What about the guilt? The chronic, low-grade shame that the word tithe produces in so many faithful people? I want to name it directly, because I do not think it comes from God. Guilt that drives you toward generosity as an escape from condemnation is not gospel motivation; it is precisely the compulsion Paul rejects. God does not want your ten percent extracted from you by fear. He wants your heart, and the giving is meant to follow the heart, not manufacture it.</p>
-
-<p>If you are giving out of guilt, the problem is not that you are giving too little. The problem is that you have not yet believed the gospel deeply enough to give freely. The cure is not a larger check written in the same spirit. The cure is to sit long enough under the fact that Christ became poor for you, that everything you have is already grace, that you cannot buy God's approval because it has already been given, until the check stops being a payment and becomes a thank-you. A person who truly believes she has received everything does not tithe reluctantly. She gives, and gives past the tithe, and wonders why she waited so long, and cannot imagine going back.</p>
-
-<h2>The Malachi Problem</h2>
-
-<p>No conversation about tithing can avoid the one text that dominates every stewardship sermon: Malachi 3, where God says through the prophet, "Bring the whole tithe into the storehouse. Test me in this, and see if I will not throw open the floodgates of heaven and pour out so much blessing that there will not be room enough to store it." (NIV) This is the verse printed on the offering envelope, quoted from the platform, wielded as the clincher. And it is almost always misused.</p>
-
-<p>Read in context, Malachi is addressing a specific covenant people under a specific covenant, accusing them of robbing God by withholding the tithes and offerings that supported the temple and the Levites. It is a national indictment of covenant unfaithfulness, not a universal formula for personal financial return. When it is ripped out of that context and turned into a promise that individual Christians who tithe will receive material blessing, it becomes a soft form of the prosperity gospel — give to get, tithe as investment strategy, God as the guarantor of your financial upside. That is not what Malachi teaches, and the preacher who uses it that way has traded the text's actual meaning for a fundraising slogan. God is not a vending machine, and the tithe is not a coin you insert to trigger a payout.</p>
-
-<p>This does not empty Malachi of meaning. It genuinely does indict the withholding heart, the heart that clutches what belongs to God and calls it prudence. But the blessing Malachi promises is covenant faithfulness restored, not a guaranteed return on a religious investment. I have preached this verse badly, leaning on its promise of overflow because it moved people to give, and in doing so I taught them to give for the wrong reason and to expect the wrong reward. The honest use of Malachi confronts the robbery without promising the payout.</p>
-
-<h2>Giving as Warfare Against the Self</h2>
-
-<p>Underneath the whole tithing debate lies a truth that both sides tend to miss: the primary beneficiary of your giving is not the church. It is you. Giving is spiritual warfare against the most stubborn enemy you will ever face, which is your own grasping heart. Every time you release money that your instincts scream at you to keep, you strike a blow against the idol of mammon that wants to rule you. The tithe, whatever its precise status as command or principle, is training in that warfare — a disciplined, repeated act of loosening your grip on the thing you most want to hold.</p>
-
-<p>This is why the question is finally not whether the ten percent is legally binding but whether you are being changed. A person can give the exact tithe for forty years and remain as tightfisted and anxious as the day they started, because the giving never touched the heart. And a person can give with such freedom and joy that the money means nothing next to the God who gave it all. The number is a starting place. The transformation is the point. Give, then — begin with the tithe if you have no better starting place — but give in order to be freed, and keep giving until the day comes when you cannot remember why you ever found it hard.</p>
-
-<h2>Where the Money Goes</h2>
-
-<p>A question that the tithing debate usually neglects is where the tithe should actually go, and it matters more than we admit. The default assumption in most churches is that the tithe belongs entirely to the local congregation — that giving to the church budget is what tithing means. But the Old Testament tithe supported not only the religious institution but the poor, the foreigner, the vulnerable. The third-year tithe was specifically for the destitute. To reduce the tithe to funding the local church's budget, much of which goes to buildings and staff, is to narrow something that Scripture kept broad.</p>
-
-<p>This does not mean you should stop supporting your church, which needs resources to do its work. It means the biblical vision of proportional giving was always wider than the offering plate — encompassing the poor, the work of mission, the care of the vulnerable — and that a healthy giving life reflects that breadth. The person who gives faithfully to their church and never gives directly to the poor has captured only part of the picture. The tithe, in its fullest biblical sense, was always partly for the least of these, and our giving is impoverished when it forgets them.</p>
-
-<h2>Teaching Children to Give</h2>
-
-<p>One of the most lasting things a parent can do about money is to teach their children to give before the culture teaches them to accumulate, and this is where the practical wisdom of the tithe becomes a gift rather than a burden. A child who learns early to set aside a portion of every gift and every earning for God and for others is being formed in a habit that will guard them for life against the grasping that captures almost everyone else. The specific proportion matters less than the pattern: giving first, giving regularly, giving as a normal and joyful part of how one relates to money.</p>
-
-<p>I have watched this formation take root, and I have watched its absence. The child raised to give grows into an adult for whom generosity is second nature, who does not have to overcome a lifetime of accumulation to open their hand. The child raised only to save and spend grows into an adult who finds giving painful and unnatural, who must fight against decades of formation every time they release a dollar. If the tithe is finally a trellis for growing generous people, there is no better time to set up the trellis than in childhood, when the vine is young and can be trained toward open hands from the start.</p>`,
