-    body: `<p>The average American household carries thousands of dollars in credit card debt, tens of thousands in auto loans, and often hundreds of thousands in mortgage and student debt. We have built an entire economy on the assumption that you will buy things you cannot afford with money you do not have and pay for them slowly, with interest, over years. Debt is not a moral failing in this system. It is the system. And Christians have absorbed it as thoroughly as everyone else, which raises a question we rarely ask honestly: what does the Bible actually say about owing money, and does it have anything to say to a person drowning in it?</p>
-
-<p>The answer is more nuanced than the two loud extremes suggest. There is a fringe teaching that all debt is sin, that a faithful Christian would never borrow, that a mortgage is a mark of unbelief. And there is the far more common practical assumption, held by most of us who would never say it aloud, that debt is simply a neutral financial tool with no spiritual dimension whatsoever. Both are wrong, and the space between them is where the biblical wisdom lives.</p>
-
-<h2>What the Bible Says and Does Not Say</h2>
-
-<p>The Bible never flatly prohibits borrowing. The law of Moses assumes that lending and borrowing will happen and regulates them rather than banning them. Deuteronomy anticipates that Israelites will lend to one another and legislates the sabbatical cancellation of those debts. Jesus assumes borrowing in his parables. So the position that all debt is sin cannot be sustained from the text. It reads a personal preference back into Scripture.</p>
-
-<p>But the Bible is deeply wary of debt, and it says so plainly. Proverbs 22 states it as a proverb, which is to say as observed wisdom rather than absolute law: "The rich rule over the poor, and the borrower is slave to the lender." That single sentence contains the whole biblical concern. Debt is not primarily a financial arrangement. It is a power relationship. It transfers a portion of your freedom to another party. The borrower is not the master of his own future; part of his future is already spoken for, already owed, already mortgaged to someone else's claim.</p>
-
-<blockquote>Debt does not merely cost you money. It costs you options. And the loss of options is the loss of a certain kind of freedom that the gospel means to give you.</blockquote>
-
-<p>This is why Paul, in Romans 13, writes "let no debt remain outstanding, except the continuing debt to love one another." He immediately spiritualizes the financial language — the one debt you should always carry is love — but the choice of metaphor is telling. He treats freedom from obligation as the normal Christian condition and love as the single exception, the one debt you can never fully pay off and should never want to.</p>
-
-<h2>The Interest Question</h2>
-
-<p>There is a long and largely forgotten Christian tradition on the subject of interest that would astonish most modern believers. For over a thousand years the church regarded charging interest on loans — usury — as a grave sin. The Old Testament forbade Israelites from charging interest to their poor countrymen. The church fathers thundered against it. Basil, Ambrose, and later Aquinas all condemned lending at interest as a way of profiting from another person's need, a way of selling time itself, which belongs to God.</p>
-
-<p>We have quietly discarded this entire tradition, and mostly for good reasons — a modern productive economy genuinely depends on credit in ways an ancient agrarian one did not, and there is a real difference between lending capital to a business that will multiply it and lending bread to a starving neighbor at twenty percent. But we should not discard the tradition without noticing what it was protecting. The concern was always the exploitation of desperation. The predatory lender, the payday loan operation charging four hundred percent to people with no other options, the credit card company profiting most from the customers least able to pay — these are the direct descendants of the usury the church once condemned, and the church's silence about them is not neutrality. It is complicity.</p>
-
-<h2>The Two Kinds of Debt</h2>
-
-<p>It helps to distinguish, as the Bible implicitly does, between debt that funds consumption and debt that funds production. Borrowing to buy something that loses value and produces nothing — the vacation you cannot afford, the newer car to replace a working one, the lifestyle you finance because you cannot bear to wait — is the debt that most resembles the slavery Proverbs warns about. It mortgages your future to your present appetite. It is the financial expression of a heart that cannot delay gratification, and delayed gratification is very close to the center of what discipleship is.</p>
-
-<p>Borrowing to acquire something that produces value or meets a genuine need — a home you will live in for decades, an education that will let you serve, tools for a trade — sits in a different category. It is not automatically righteous, but it is not obviously foolish either. Even here the biblical wariness applies: how much, at what risk, with what margin for the unexpected. But the distinction matters. Not all debt is equal, and a theology that treats the mortgage and the credit card binge identically is not being biblical. It is being lazy.</p>
-
-<h2>The Spiritual Weight of Debt</h2>
-
-<p>I want to speak now to the person actually carrying debt that is crushing them, because the theological discussion means nothing if it does not reach that person. Debt is not just a number on a statement. It is a weight you carry into every room. It changes your marriage, because money conflict is one of the great destroyers of marriages and debt is money conflict compounded. It changes your prayers, because it is very hard to trust God for your daily bread when you are already forty thousand dollars behind. It changes your capacity to be generous, to say yes to a calling, to take a risk for the kingdom, because your options are already spent.</p>
-
-<p>And it produces shame — a private, isolating shame that keeps people from telling anyone, including their pastor, that they are in trouble. I have sat across from people who could confess an affair more easily than they could confess their debt, because the affair felt like a moral failure that grace could cover and the debt felt like proof of stupidity, of being a bad and foolish person, and they could not bear to say it out loud.</p>
-
-<p>Hear this clearly: your debt is not your identity, and getting into it does not make you unlovable. Most people in debt got there through some combination of hard circumstance, cultural pressure, poor teaching, and ordinary human weakness, and God is not standing over your statement with a red pen. He knows the number. He knew it before you did. And he is not surprised, and he is not finished with you.</p>
-
-<h2>The Way Out</h2>
-
-<p>The path out of debt is not spiritual in the sense of mystical; it is spiritual in the sense of costly. It requires the two hardest disciplines in the Christian life applied to money: honesty and self-denial. Honesty means looking at the actual numbers, telling someone you trust the truth, ending the pretense. Self-denial means spending less than you make for long enough to reverse the direction, which will feel, in a culture built on consumption, like a kind of death. Because it is. It is a small daily death to the appetite that got you here, and the Christian life has always been about that particular death leading somewhere.</p>
-
-<p>Do not do it alone. The isolation is part of the bondage, and confession is part of the freedom. Tell someone. Get help — from a counselor, a program, a wiser friend, a pastor who will not shame you. And do it not to earn God's love but because you already have it, and because the God who owns the cattle on a thousand hills does not want his children enslaved to the lender when he has already paid the only debt that could not be paid.</p>
-
-<h2>The Debt That Was Paid</h2>
-
-<p>There is a reason the New Testament reaches so instinctively for the language of debt when it describes what Christ accomplished, and it is worth dwelling on, because it changes how a Christian in financial debt understands their situation before God. Sin, in the Scriptures, is repeatedly figured as a debt — something owed that cannot be paid, an obligation that accumulates and enslaves. The Lord's Prayer itself, in its most literal rendering, asks God to forgive us our debts as we forgive our debtors. Paul writes that the record of debt that stood against us, with its legal demands, was nailed to the cross and cancelled. The cross is, among other things, the payment of an unpayable debt.</p>
-
-<p>This means the Christian struggling under financial debt lives inside a gospel whose central image is precisely the cancellation of a debt they could never have cleared themselves. The One who forgave your infinite debt is not standing over your finite one with contempt. He knows what it is to see an obligation that cannot be met, and he knows the freedom of having it cancelled by another's payment. Your financial debt is real and must be dealt with, but it does not touch your standing before God, because the only debt that could have condemned you has already been paid in full. You can face the credit card statement as a forgiven person, and that changes everything about the shame.</p>
-
-<h2>The Discipline of Enough</h2>
-
-<p>The most effective long-term defense against debt is not a budgeting technique; it is a converted appetite. Most consumer debt is the financial residue of an inability to be content, a chronic reaching for more than we have because we have absorbed the culture's teaching that we are what we own and that more will finally be enough. The person who has learned the discipline of enough — who can look at what they have and say, this is sufficient, I do not need the newer thing to be whole — is largely immune to the trap that catches everyone else.</p>
-
-<p>This is where the theology of contentment meets the practical problem of debt. Paul learned to be content in plenty and in want, and that contentment was a wall against the endless wanting that drives people into bondage. A Christian who is genuinely content is a Christian who is very hard to sell things to, and being hard to sell things to is, in a consumer economy built on debt, a form of freedom. The way out of debt is partly arithmetic and partly appetite. Fix the arithmetic, yes. But do not neglect the appetite, because if the appetite that created the debt is not converted, no amount of arithmetic will keep you free. The borrower is slave to the lender, and the deepest slavery is not to the bank but to the wanting that sent you to the bank in the first place.</p>
-
-<h2>When the Debt Is Not Your Fault</h2>
-
-<p>I have written so far as though debt were primarily a matter of appetite and choice, and for many people it is. But honesty requires acknowledging that a great deal of crushing debt is not the result of consumer weakness at all. Medical debt from an illness no one chose. Debt taken on to survive a job loss or a family crisis. The debt of the person who did everything right and was still caught by circumstances beyond their control — a collapsing economy, a betrayal, a catastrophe. To speak of all debt as though it were the fruit of undisciplined wanting is to add cruelty to injury for the many whose debt came upon them like a storm they did not summon.</p>
-
-<p>For that person, the word is different, and it needs to be spoken clearly. Your debt is not evidence of your failure or your foolishness. It is evidence that you live in a fallen world where hard things happen to faithful people, and God does not read your balance sheet as a verdict on your character. The same wisdom about honesty, help, and steady steps toward freedom applies to you, but without the layer of shame that the appetite-driven debtor may need to face. You did not sin your way into this. You were caught by it. And the God who provides is no less committed to walking with you toward freedom because your debt arrived through circumstance rather than choice. Do not let anyone, including yourself, add false guilt to a burden that is already heavy enough.</p>
-
-<h2>Lending as the Church</h2>
-
-<p>There is one more dimension to the Christian and debt that we almost never consider, and it turns the whole question around: the church's role not as borrower but as a community that could keep its own people out of the hands of predatory lenders. The Old Testament envisioned a community that lent to its poor without interest, that cancelled debts, that would not let a brother or sister fall permanently into the debt-slavery the surrounding cultures accepted as normal. The early church shared so completely that there was no destitute person among them driven to borrow at ruinous rates.</p>
-
-<p>What if the church recovered even a fraction of this? What if a congregation refused to let its own members fall into the clutches of the payday lender charging four hundred percent, and instead met the emergency need directly, generously, without interest, as an act of family? This is not fantasy; some churches do it. It requires seeing the debt of the person beside you not as their private problem but as a claim on the community, the way the biblical vision always did. The church cannot fix the whole debt economy. But it could ensure that no one within its own fellowship is handed over to the lender when the family of God has the resources to help. That would be a small jubilee, and it is entirely within reach.</p>`,
