-  {
-    title: "When Your Vocation Is Your Calling",
-    slug: "vocation-as-calling",
-    excerpt:
-      "The language of calling has been cheapened into a self-help slogan about finding your passion. Recovering its theological weight changes both the fortunate who love their work and the many who do not.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "12 min",
-    body: `<p>The word calling has fallen on hard times. It has been absorbed into the vocabulary of the personal-brand economy, where it means something like the intersection of your passion and your talent, the work you were born to do, the thing that makes you come alive. Find your calling, we tell young people, as though the goal of life were self-actualization through employment. And so calling has become a source of anxiety for the many who do not feel any such thing about their jobs, and a source of quiet self-congratulation for the few who do.</p>
-
-<p>The Christian doctrine of vocation is both richer and stranger than this. The word vocation comes from the Latin vocare, to call, and it presupposes a caller. You cannot be called by an impersonal universe or a career aptitude test. Calling requires a voice, and the doctrine of vocation locates that voice in God. This single fact reorients the entire conversation. The question is not what do I want to do with my life but what is God calling me to do — and the difference between those two questions is the difference between a slogan and a theology.</p>
-
-<h2>The Primary Calling</h2>
-
-<p>Os Guinness, in his book <em>The Call</em>, makes a distinction that clears away most of the confusion. There is a primary calling and there are secondary callings, and we get into trouble when we collapse the two. The primary calling — the one that is true of every Christian without exception — is the call to God himself. You are called first not to a task but to a person. "Come to me," Jesus says. The primary calling is to belong to Christ, to follow him, to be his. Everything else is downstream of that.</p>
-
-<p>The secondary callings are the particular tasks, roles, relationships, and works to which God directs each person: this marriage, this job, this neighborhood, this responsibility. These are real callings, but they are secondary, and the moment we invert the order — the moment the job becomes the primary calling and God becomes the resource that helps us succeed at it — we have committed a fundamental idolatry, and we will suffer for it. The person whose identity is fused with their work will be destroyed when the work is taken away, because they have made a secondary thing carry a primary weight.</p>
-
-<blockquote>You are not called to a job. You are called to a Person, and then sent into a job. Reverse those and the job will eventually consume you, because it was never strong enough to be first.</blockquote>
-
-<h2>The Gift and the Danger of Loving Your Work</h2>
-
-<p>Let me speak to the fortunate first, because they need a warning that the culture will never give them. If you love your work — if it fits your gifts, engages your mind, serves your neighbor, and pays your bills — that is a genuine grace, and you should receive it with gratitude. Not everyone gets it. To have work that feels like calling is a gift, and gifts are meant to be enjoyed.</p>
-
-<p>But the gift carries a specific danger, and it is precisely the danger of the fortunate: the work becomes the identity. When your job is your calling in the deepest sense, when it is where you find meaning and worth and purpose, it is terrifyingly easy for it to displace God as the center of your life. You will pray about your work, tithe from your work, thank God for your work, and never notice that the work has become your functional god and the actual God has become its assistant. The people most at risk of this are not the workers who hate their jobs. They are the pastors, the artists, the surgeons, the missionaries — the people whose work is so obviously good that they never suspect it might be an idol.</p>
-
-<p>I have felt this in ministry. The work is holy; the calling is real; and it is exactly the holiness of the work that lets it quietly become the thing I serve. When my worth rises and falls with the size of the crowd or the response to the sermon, I have made the calling into my god, and it is a cruel god, and it will never give me enough.</p>
-
-<h2>The Calling of the Unfulfilled</h2>
-
-<p>Now to the majority, because most people do not experience their work as calling in the passionate sense, and the self-help gospel has nothing to say to them except that they should quit and find something better, which most of them cannot do. Here the doctrine of vocation offers something the culture cannot: dignity without passion.</p>
-
-<p>The Reformation understanding of vocation never required that you feel called to your work. Luther's milkmaid was not called to milking because milking made her come alive. She was called to it because it was the station God had placed her in, the work through which she served her neighbor and provided for her family, and it was holy for that reason alone. Feelings did not enter into it. A calling, in this understanding, is not a passion you discover but a duty you receive — the actual work in front of you, done faithfully as unto God, whether or not it thrills you.</p>
-
-<p>This is enormously freeing for the person stuck in a job they would not have chosen. Your work is not less of a calling because you do not love it. The single father working two jobs he despises to keep his children fed is living out his calling as truly as any celebrated professional living their dream. More truly, perhaps, because he is doing it without the reward of enjoyment, purely for the love of the ones he serves. That is closer to the cross than any dream job.</p>
-
-<h2>Discerning the Secondary Callings</h2>
-
-<p>But God does direct people to particular works, and the question of how to discern that direction is a real one, especially for the young and for those at a crossroads. The doctrine of vocation offers wisdom here without offering the false certainty the culture promises. Frederick Buechner's famous line — that vocation is the place where your deep gladness and the world's deep hunger meet — captures part of it, though it is more romantic than the Reformers would have been. Real discernment involves attending to several things at once: your gifts and abilities, the needs around you, the doors God opens and closes, the counsel of wise people who know you, and your own honest desires held loosely before God.</p>
-
-<p>None of these is decisive alone. Your desires can deceive you. Your gifts can be used in many fields. The needs of the world are endless and you cannot meet all of them. The doors that open are not always the right ones. Discernment is not solving an equation; it is the patient, prayerful work of a person who wants God's direction more than their own preference, and who is willing to be surprised. And it is rarely a lightning bolt. Most people discover their calling by walking, not by waiting — by taking faithful next steps and looking back to see the path.</p>
-
-<h2>Holding It All Loosely</h2>
-
-<p>The final word on calling has to be about holding it loosely, because callings change and end. The person called to raise young children is not called to that forever; the children grow up. The person called to a career gives it up to retirement or has it taken by illness or economic collapse. If your calling is fused with your identity, every ending is a death. But if your primary calling is to God, then the secondary callings can come and go, rise and fall, be given and taken, and you remain — because your worth was never in the work but in the one who called you before you did any work at all.</p>
-
-<p>Find your calling, then. But find the primary one first. Be called to God, belong to Christ, become his — and then receive whatever secondary work he gives you, whether you love it or endure it, as the place he has sent you to serve. That is a calling no economy can take away and no failure can revoke, and it will hold you when every other calling has ended.</p>
-
-<h2>The Tyranny of the Dream Job</h2>
-
-<p>Our culture has done something cruel to the young in the name of encouragement. It has told them that somewhere out there is a perfect job, a dream career, a calling that will fit them like a glove and make every Monday a joy, and that their task is to find it. This sounds inspiring. It is actually a form of tyranny, because it sets up an impossible standard against which every real job will disappoint, and it produces a generation perpetually restless, perpetually convinced that the meaningful work is elsewhere, perpetually unable to commit to the good work in front of them because it is not the mythical perfect work they were promised.</p>
-
-<p>The doctrine of vocation dismantles this tyranny. It says there is no perfect job, because you are a fallen person working in a fallen world, and every job will involve thorns. It says that calling is not primarily about finding the work that fulfills you but about serving faithfully wherever God has placed you. It frees the young person from the anxious search for the dream and gives them permission to commit — to plant themselves in real work, imperfect and demanding as it is, and to find their meaning not in the perfection of the task but in the faithfulness of the service and the God they serve through it. The dream job is a myth. The faithful life is available to anyone, in almost any work, right now.</p>
-
-<h2>When the Calling Changes</h2>
-
-<p>One of the most disorienting experiences in the Christian life is the sense that a calling that was once clearly yours is now ending or shifting. The work you were sure God had given you dries up. The passion fades. The doors that were open close, and new ones open toward something you never planned. People often interpret this as failure or as evidence that they misheard God the first time. Usually it is neither. It is simply the nature of secondary callings, which are given for seasons and not forever.</p>
-
-<p>God led Israel through the wilderness by a cloud that moved, and when the cloud moved, they moved, and when it stopped, they stopped. The calling of God is often like that cloud — it settles over a work for a time, and then it lifts and moves on, and the faithful response is not to cling to the place the cloud has left but to follow it to the new place. The person whose identity is fused with a particular calling cannot follow the cloud; they stay camped over an empty spot, grieving a work God has already released them from. But the person whose primary calling is to God himself can follow wherever the cloud leads, holding each secondary calling with open hands, trusting that the God who gave the last work is the one giving the next. Callings change. The Caller does not.</p>
-
-<h2>The Hidden Callings</h2>
-
-<p>We tend to reserve the language of calling for jobs and careers, but the doctrine of vocation is far broader, and reclaiming its breadth relieves much of the pressure the career-obsessed version creates. You are called not only to your work but to your marriage, your children, your friendships, your neighborhood, your church, the particular people God has placed in your life. These are callings as real as any occupation, and for many people they are the more important ones. The person who pours themselves into a demanding career while neglecting the calling to their spouse and children has not fulfilled their vocation by succeeding at work; they have failed the deeper callings while excelling at the shallower one.</p>
-
-<p>This matters because our culture ranks the callings entirely by their public visibility and economic reward, and the doctrine of vocation refuses that ranking. The father's calling to his children is not less than his calling to his job; it may be greater. The friend's calling to be present in another's suffering is a genuine vocation, though no one pays for it and no resume records it. When you understand that God calls you to a whole life and not merely to a career, the crushing weight of finding the perfect job lifts, because the job is only one of many callings, and often not the most important. A person can have an unremarkable job and a magnificent vocation, faithful in the hidden callings that the world never sees but heaven counts as the substance of a life well lived.</p>
-
-<h2>Faithfulness Over Fruitfulness</h2>
-
-<p>There is one final correction the doctrine of vocation offers to a culture obsessed with impact, and it is perhaps the most freeing of all. We are taught to measure a calling by its results — the difference it makes, the lives it changes, the mark it leaves. But Scripture measures a calling by faithfulness, not fruitfulness. The servant in the parable is commended not for the size of the return but for being faithful with what was entrusted. The prophet Jeremiah was faithful for decades and saw almost no fruit; by the world's metric of impact, his ministry was a failure. God's verdict was otherwise.</p>
-
-<p>This releases the person whose faithful labor seems to produce little. You may pour yourself into a calling and see no visible result — the parent whose child rebels, the teacher whose students forget, the worker whose good labor goes unrecognized, the servant whose sacrifice changes nothing anyone can measure. In the economy of impact, you have wasted yourself. In the economy of the kingdom, you have been faithful, and faithfulness is the only thing God finally asks. The results belong to him; the faithfulness belongs to you. Find your calling, then, and be faithful in it, and leave the fruit to the God who alone can produce it and who measures your life not by what it accomplished but by whether you were faithful with what he gave you.</p>`,
-  },
-  {
-    title: "Retirement and Stewardship",
-    slug: "retirement-and-stewardship",
-    excerpt:
-      "The modern dream of retirement — decades of leisure earned by decades of work — is a recent invention the Bible never imagined. That does not make it wrong, but it means we should examine it before we worship it.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "11 min",
-    body: `<p>There is a picture that hovers over the American working life like a promised land: the day you stop. The retirement. The reward at the end of the long labor — the golf course, the cruise, the grandchildren, the freedom to finally do nothing you do not want to do. We save for it, plan for it, structure our entire financial lives around reaching it. It is the carrot at the end of forty years of work, and it is held out to us as the obvious goal of a life well managed.</p>
-
-<p>I want to say something careful here, because it is easy to be misunderstood. I am not against retirement. I am not going to tell you that saving for old age is unspiritual or that a Christian should work until they drop. But I am going to suggest that we have absorbed a vision of retirement so uncritically, so completely, that we have never asked whether it is a Christian vision at all — and that when you hold it up against Scripture, some of its assumptions look distinctly foreign.</p>
-
-<h2>A Recent Invention</h2>
-
-<p>Start with the historical fact that grounds everything else: retirement as we know it is barely a century old. For nearly all of human history, the idea that a person would spend the last two or three decades of life in funded leisure would have been incomprehensible. People worked until they could not, and then were cared for by family and community. The modern retirement — a distinct life stage of extended leisure financed by a lifetime of saving — is a product of industrial wealth, pension systems, and rising life expectancy. It is a genuine achievement of a prosperous society, and it is also, from the standpoint of Scripture, an utter novelty.</p>
-
-<p>This matters because we tend to treat retirement as though it were a fixed feature of reality, a natural right, the way things simply are. It is not. It is a specific cultural arrangement, and cultural arrangements are worth examining rather than assuming. The Bible has a great deal to say about old age, about work, about rest, about provision — but it never once imagines the retirement we take for granted, and that silence should at least make us curious.</p>
-
-<h2>What the Bible Does Say About Aging</h2>
-
-<p>What Scripture offers instead of retirement is a vision of aging as continued usefulness and honored wisdom. The old are to be respected, their counsel sought, their experience valued. The picture of the elderly in the Bible is not of people withdrawn from contribution but of people whose contribution shifts from physical labor to wisdom, mentorship, prayer, and testimony. Psalm 92 speaks of the righteous still bearing fruit in old age, staying fresh and green. The vision is fruitfulness to the end, not a productive first act followed by a leisured epilogue.</p>
-
-<blockquote>The Bible does not ask whether you will stop working. It asks what your later years are for. And leisure is not a large enough answer.</blockquote>
-
-<p>The one Levitical text that touches something like retirement is instructive. The Levites were to serve in the tent of meeting from age twenty-five to fifty, and after fifty they were to withdraw from the strenuous work but continue to assist their brothers. Even the biblical version of stepping back is not a step into leisure. It is a step into a different kind of service — the older assisting, guiding, supporting the younger. The work changes. It does not stop.</p>
-
-<h2>The Rich Fool Revisited</h2>
-
-<p>There is a parable that speaks directly to the retirement dream, and it is uncomfortable precisely because the man in it did nothing most financial advisors would call wrong. The rich fool of Luke 12 had a bumper crop, more than his barns could hold. His plan was entirely sensible by modern standards: build bigger barns, store the surplus, and then — in his own words — "take life easy; eat, drink and be merry." He had done what we all aspire to do. He had accumulated enough to stop.</p>
-
-<p>And God called him a fool, and required his soul that very night. The problem was not that he saved. The problem was the horizon of his imagination. His entire vision of the good life ended at his own comfort. He had no thought of God, no thought of others, no thought of purpose beyond his own ease. "This is how it will be," Jesus concludes, "with whoever stores up things for themselves but is not rich toward God." The parable is not an attack on saving. It is an attack on a life whose telos is leisure, whose great goal is to reach the day when you owe nothing to anyone and can finally please yourself.</p>
-
-<p>I have to sit with this one, because the retirement I quietly imagine for myself looks uncomfortably like the rich fool's plan with a Christian veneer. Work hard, save well, and then rest — as though rest for myself were the point of the whole enterprise. The parable does not forbid me to save. It forbids me to make my own ease the summit of my hope.</p>
-
-<h2>Stewardship, Not Ownership</h2>
-
-<p>The deeper issue underneath the retirement question is the doctrine of stewardship, and it cuts against a particular assumption baked into how we save. We tend to think of our retirement account as ours — money we earned, set aside, and are entitled to spend on ourselves in old age. And in one sense it is. But the whole biblical teaching on wealth insists that it is God's before it is ours, entrusted to us for purposes larger than our own comfort. The retirement account is not exempt from this. It is not a private zone where the demands of the kingdom no longer apply.</p>
-
-<p>This does not mean it is wrong to save for old age. Provision for the future is wisdom; Proverbs commends the ant who stores in summer. It means that even our saving must be held before God as stewardship, with the question always open: is this provision or is this hoarding, is this wise preparation or is this the barn-building of a heart that wants to secure itself against ever needing to trust God again. Only you can answer that, and the answer is not in the size of the account but in the posture of the heart that holds it.</p>
-
-<h2>Retirement as Redeployment</h2>
-
-<p>Here is what I have come to believe, and I offer it as a pastor rather than a financial planner. The retirement years, for the Christian who has the means to step back from career, are not a reward to be consumed but a resource to be deployed. Think of what a retired person has that the working world desperately lacks: time, experience, financial stability, freedom from the pressure to earn. The retired Christian is potentially the most available and unencumbered servant in the entire church. Freed from the treadmill of career, they can give themselves to the kingdom in ways they never could while raising children and building a career.</p>
-
-<p>The tragedy is how rarely this happens — how often the golden years are spent on a golf course while the church starves for exactly the wisdom, presence, and availability that retired believers possess in abundance. Some of the most fruitful ministry I have ever witnessed came from people in their seventies who decided that retirement meant redeployment rather than withdrawal. They mentored the young, they served the poor, they prayed with a depth that only decades produce, and they discovered that the last third of life could be the most fruitful of all.</p>
-
-<h2>The Fear Underneath the Planning</h2>
-
-<p>If we are honest about why we save so obsessively for retirement, we will often find fear at the bottom of it — not simple prudence, but a deep anxiety about being vulnerable, dependent, unable to provide for ourselves, at the mercy of others in our old age. The retirement account becomes a hedge against that terror, a way of guaranteeing that we will never have to trust anyone or ask for help or face the future without a cushion. And this is where the spiritual danger enters, because that particular fear is precisely the fear the gospel is meant to heal.</p>
-
-<p>Jesus spent a remarkable amount of his teaching addressing anxiety about provision. Do not worry about your life, what you will eat or drink or wear. Look at the birds; your heavenly Father feeds them. Consider the lilies. Your Father knows what you need. Seek first the kingdom, and all these things will be added to you. This is not a promise that you will never lack, but it is a direct assault on the anxiety that drives so much of our financial behavior. The question the retirement account quietly poses is whether we are saving from wisdom or from unbelief — whether our planning flows from stewardship or from a refusal to trust the Father who has promised to know our needs and meet them. Only you can discern which it is, and the discernment matters more than the balance.</p>
-
-<h2>Finishing Well</h2>
-
-<p>Scripture is full of people who did their most significant work at the end of their lives. Moses began his real vocation at eighty. Caleb asked for a mountain to conquer at eighty-five, declaring himself as strong for war as the day he was sent out. Anna, the prophetess, was well into her eighties, worshiping night and day in the temple, and it was to her that God gave the privilege of recognizing the infant Messiah. Paul wrote some of his most enduring letters from prison as an old man. The biblical pattern is not that the last third of life is a wind-down but that it can be a culmination.</p>
-
-<p>The goal of the Christian life is not to retire but to finish well — to run the race to the end, to be found faithful when the last day comes, to hear well done not because you reached a comfortable stopping point but because you kept serving until you could serve no more. This reframes the entire question of retirement. The issue is not whether you stop working for pay, which is often reasonable and good. The issue is whether you keep offering yourself to God and others until the very end, or whether you cash out of the kingdom the moment you can afford to. Finishing well is available to every Christian, whether wealthy or poor, retired or laboring, and it is the only ending Scripture holds up as worthy of a life.</p>
-
-<h2>Inheritance and the Next Generation</h2>
-
-<p>Closely tied to the retirement question is the matter of what we leave behind, and here too the culture and the Scriptures diverge in instructive ways. Proverbs commends leaving an inheritance to one's children's children, and there is genuine wisdom in providing for the generations that follow. But the modern impulse to accumulate a vast estate to pass on can become its own kind of idolatry, and it can do real harm to the very children it means to bless. Wealth handed to those who did not earn it and are not prepared to steward it has destroyed more heirs than it has served.</p>
-
-<p>The best inheritance is rarely primarily financial. It is character, faith, wisdom, the memory of a life lived generously and faithfully, the example of parents and grandparents who held money loosely and served God freely. A child who inherits a great fortune and no formation in how to hold it has been given a burden disguised as a gift. A child who inherits modest means and a rich legacy of faith and generosity has been given the better portion. The retirement years are, among other things, the time to think about what you are actually passing on, and to consider whether the accumulation you are tempted to hoard for your heirs might do them more good deployed now, in generosity they can witness, than stored up for a windfall that may undo them.</p>
-
-<h2>The Grace of Diminishment</h2>
-
-<p>There is a harder word about aging that a theology of retirement must include, because not every later life is a story of vigorous continued usefulness. Some grow frail. Some lose their faculties. Some become entirely dependent, able to give nothing, contribute nothing, do nothing but receive care. And the culture that measures worth by productivity has nothing to say to this except pity, because in its terms the dependent elderly are worthless, a burden, a problem to be managed.</p>
-
-<p>The gospel says something radically different. The worth of a human being was never in their usefulness, and it does not diminish when the usefulness ends. The person who can do nothing but receive is no less an image-bearer, no less loved, no less precious to God than the vigorous servant in their prime. There is even a strange grace in diminishment, a final stripping away of every prop of self-sufficiency until nothing remains but the naked fact of being loved by God apart from anything you can do. The dependent elderly Christian, unable to serve, entirely reliant on the care of others, is being conformed to a truth the rest of us spend our lives avoiding: that we were always utterly dependent, always kept alive by grace, always loved not for our output but for ourselves. Finishing well may finally mean finishing helpless, and receiving that helplessness not as defeat but as the last and deepest lesson in grace.</p>
-
-<p>So save wisely. Provide for your family. But do not let the culture convince you that the goal of your working life is to reach the day you become useless to the kingdom in exchange for being comfortable. God is not finished with you when you clock out for the last time. He may, in fact, be just getting started.</p>`,
