-    body: `<p>We live in a time of staggering economic inequality, and most churches have decided to say nothing about it. The wealthiest fraction of the population controls a share of the resources that would have been unimaginable to previous generations, while millions work full-time and cannot afford a place to live. This is not a marginal feature of the economy. It is one of the defining facts of our age. And in the face of it, the vast majority of pulpits are quiet — not because the Bible is quiet, but because we have decided that the subject is political, and political means dangerous, and dangerous means we would rather preach about something else.</p>
-
-<p>I want to argue that this silence is itself a theological position, and a bad one. The prophets were not silent about the gap between rich and poor. Jesus was not silent. The early church was not silent. When the modern church chooses silence, it is not staying neutral. It is siding, by default, with the arrangement as it stands. And that is a choice the Scriptures will not let us make comfortably.</p>
-
-<h2>The Prophets Would Recognize This Instantly</h2>
-
-<p>Read Amos, Micah, and Isaiah with the newspaper open beside you and the resonance is uncanny. The prophets addressed a specific historical situation: eighth-century Israel and Judah were experiencing tremendous economic growth, and the fruits of that growth were flowing overwhelmingly to a wealthy elite while the small farmers were being pushed off their land, loaded with debt, and reduced to servitude. The rich were not necessarily breaking the letter of the law. They were manipulating the system — seizing fields, rigging the courts, tilting the scales — in ways that were technically defensible and morally monstrous.</p>
-
-<p>Micah names it exactly: "They covet fields and seize them, and houses, and take them. They defraud people of their homes, they rob them of their inheritance." Isaiah names it: "Woe to you who add house to house and join field to field till no space is left and you live alone in the land." The word woe is not a policy critique. It is a funeral cry. The prophets were pronouncing death over an economic arrangement that concentrated wealth by dispossessing the vulnerable, and they were doing it in the name of God, to a people who considered themselves faithful.</p>
