-
-<p>This is the piece we most want to avoid. We are comfortable condemning theft, fraud, individual greed — sins that can be pinned on bad individuals. The prophets went further. They condemned systems. They indicted an entire economic order that produced injustice through mechanisms no single person controlled, and they held the whole society, especially its beneficiaries, responsible for it. That kind of critique makes us deeply uncomfortable, and it should, because most of us reading this are the beneficiaries.</p>
-
-<h2>The Structural and the Personal</h2>
-
-<p>Here is where the conversation usually splits into two camps that talk past each other. One camp says the problem is personal: individuals need to be generous, greed is a sin of the heart, and if enough people were converted the economic problems would take care of themselves. The other camp says the problem is structural: the systems themselves are unjust, and personal generosity is a bandage on a wound that requires surgery. Each camp accuses the other of missing the point, and each is half right.</p>
-
