-
-<p>Now the hard part, the part that implicates me and the institution I serve. The church is not merely a bystander to economic inequality. In many cases it is a participant and a beneficiary. Consider how church communities often sort themselves by class — the wealthy suburban congregation and the struggling urban one worshiping the same God a few miles apart and almost never in the same room. Consider how the largest and most resourced churches are frequently found where the money already is, while the neighborhoods that most need ministry are the ones least able to sustain it. Consider how easily the American church has baptized the pursuit of wealth, blessed the accumulators, and left the poor to feel that their poverty is somehow a spiritual failure.</p>
-
-<p>James, the brother of Jesus, wrote directly about this failure, and it is one of the most pointed passages in the New Testament. He describes a church that gives the wealthy visitor the good seat and tells the poor man in shabby clothes to stand or sit on the floor, and he calls it what it is: discrimination, judging with evil thoughts, dishonoring the poor whom God has chosen. The early church already had this problem, and James did not soften it. He did not say be nice to everyone. He said you have made distinctions that God does not make, you have honored what God honors least and shamed what God honors most, and you have done it inside the assembly of Christ.</p>
-
-<h2>The God Who Sides with the Poor</h2>
-
-<p>There is a phrase that makes many comfortable Christians nervous — the preferential option for the poor — and it is worth confronting rather than dismissing. It does not mean God loves the poor more than the rich, or that poverty is virtue and wealth is vice. It means something the Scriptures state repeatedly and unmistakably: that God pays particular attention to the vulnerable, that he hears the cry of the oppressed, that he identifies himself especially with those whom the powerful ignore. "Whoever is kind to the poor lends to the Lord." "He who oppresses the poor shows contempt for their Maker." Jesus began his ministry by reading Isaiah's announcement of good news to the poor, and he identified himself so completely with the least that he said what you did to them you did to him.</p>
-
-<p>This is not a marginal theme you have to hunt for. It runs through the entire canon like a spine. And it means that a church that ignores the wealth gap is not merely neglecting a social issue. It is ignoring one of God's most consistent and passionate concerns. It is loving the God of the Bible while turning away from the very people that God has said he especially sees.</p>
-
-<h2>What Faithfulness Requires</h2>
-
-<p>So what does faithfulness look like, short of the political prescriptions that would divide any congregation in half? It begins with refusing the silence. The pulpit must be willing to name what the prophets named — not with partisan solutions, but with prophetic honesty about the reality that the gap between rich and poor is a matter God cares about intensely, and that the beneficiaries of the gap, which is most of us, are implicated in it.</p>
-
-<p>It continues with the church becoming a place where the class divide is actually crossed rather than reinforced — where rich and poor sit at the same table, share the same bread, know one another by name, and discover that the categories the world uses to sort people mean nothing at the foot of the cross. This is harder than writing a check to a poverty program, and it is the thing James actually demanded. A congregation that is economically homogeneous has, whether it means to or not, conformed to the world's sorting rather than the kingdom's.</p>
-
-<p>And it requires personal reckoning from those of us who benefit. I am, by any global or historical standard, wealthy. The temptation is to hear the prophetic word about the wealth gap as a message for someone richer than I am — the billionaire, the executive, the person one rung up the ladder. That is the oldest evasion in the book, and I have used it. The word is for me. It asks what I am doing with what I have, whom I have failed to see, and whether my comfort has been built, in ways I have never bothered to examine, on an arrangement that leaves others out in the cold. The prophets did not let their hearers off with that question, and I cannot let myself off either.</p>
-
-<h2>The Danger of the Political Escape Hatch</h2>
-
-<p>There is a move both sides of the political spectrum make to avoid the prophetic word, and it deserves to be named, because it is how comfortable Christians dodge the whole subject. The one side says the answer is entirely governmental — pass the right laws, redistribute through the state, and my personal responsibility is discharged by voting correctly. The other side says the answer is entirely personal and voluntary — charity, not policy, and any structural remedy is an intrusion on freedom. And here is the trick: each position becomes a way of doing nothing. The first outsources the obligation to the government so the individual heart is never touched. The second outsources it to a voluntary charity that somehow never quite happens at the scale required.</p>
-
-<p>The prophetic word cuts through both escape hatches. It will not let the individual hide behind policy, and it will not let the defender of freedom hide behind a charity he never actually practices. It comes for the heart and the hands directly. Amos did not tell the wealthy of Israel to vote differently or to feel differently. He told them their behavior was crushing the poor and that God was going to hold them personally accountable for it. Whatever one concludes about the proper role of government in addressing inequality — and Christians of good faith will disagree — no political conclusion relieves the individual believer of the direct, personal, unavoidable obligation to see the poor, to open the hand, to refuse to build comfort on the backs of the vulnerable. The politics is a real question. It is not a hiding place.</p>
-
-<h2>The Test of a Church</h2>
-
-<p>In the end, the wealth gap poses a test to the church that is finally simpler than all the policy debates, and it is the test James laid down: when the rich and the poor are both in the room, which one gets the good seat? A church reveals its true theology not in its statement of faith but in whom it honors, whom it centers, whom it is organized to serve. A church that has, without ever deciding to, become a comfortable club for the prosperous while the poor feel their poverty as shame the moment they walk in has failed the test, however orthodox its doctrine.</p>
-
-<h2>The Comfort That Blinds</h2>
-
-<p>There is a specific spiritual danger that attends the beneficiaries of inequality, and it is not primarily cruelty. It is blindness. The rich man in the parable of Lazarus is not described as actively tormenting the beggar at his gate; his sin is that he did not see him, day after day, as he stepped over him on the way to his feasts. Comfort has a way of narrowing vision, of making the suffering of others recede into background scenery, of rendering the poor invisible precisely to those with the most power to help them. The wealthy do not have to hate the poor to fail them. They only have to stop seeing them, and comfort makes not-seeing effortless.</p>
-
-<p>This is why the wealth gap is a spiritual problem for the comfortable and not merely a policy problem for the society. It is doing something to us, dulling our sight, insulating us from the reality of need, allowing us to live untroubled beside suffering we have trained ourselves not to notice. The gated community, the segregated neighborhood, the curated feed, the careful avoidance of the parts of town where poverty lives — these are the mechanisms by which the modern rich, myself included, keep from seeing the Lazarus at the gate. And the not-seeing is not innocent. It is the very sin the parable condemns. To recover sight, to deliberately place ourselves where we cannot avoid seeing the poor, to refuse the comfort that blinds, is a spiritual discipline the affluent Christian desperately needs and almost never practices.</p>
-
-<p>The early church passed this test in a way that astonished the watching Roman world. Pagan observers noted with bewilderment that these Christians cared for each other across every social line, that the wealthy shared with the poor, that widows and orphans and the destitute were absorbed into a community that treated them as family. It was one of the most compelling apologetics the early church possessed — not an argument but a demonstration, a community in which the wealth gap that structured the whole ancient world simply did not determine who mattered. The gap is real. God sees it. And the church's silence has gone on long enough. It is time we became again the community that the gap cannot divide.</p>`,
