-    body: `<p>Here is a strange fact about the modern church. We have gotten reasonably good, over the last generation, at talking honestly about hard things. We talk about sexual brokenness, about depression and anxiety, about doubt, about marriage in crisis, about addiction, about grief. The culture of confession and vulnerability has entered the church, and mostly for the better. A person can stand up in many congregations today and admit to almost anything.</p>
-
-<p>Except money. Money remains the last taboo. You will hear a man confess an affair before you hear him tell you his salary. You will hear a woman describe her battle with anxiety before she tells you how much debt she is carrying. We will discuss the most intimate failures of the flesh and the spirit, and then fall completely silent about the number in the bank account. Something is being protected by that silence, and it is worth asking what.</p>
-
-<h2>The Manipulation Hangover</h2>
-
-<p>Part of the reason is that the church has genuinely poisoned this well. A generation of Christians grew up watching money-hungry ministries treat their faith as a revenue stream — the televangelist demanding seed faith offerings from people who could not afford them, the prosperity preacher flying private jets purchased with the widow's mite, the constant, grinding appeal for funds that made the whole enterprise feel like a business dressed up as worship. Anyone who lived through that has a reflexive suspicion of any preacher who brings up money, and honestly, they are right to.</p>
-
-<p>So thoughtful pastors overcorrect. Determined not to be the money-grubbing charlatan, we avoid the subject almost entirely. We preach on money once a year, apologetically, hedged with disclaimers, and then retreat to safer ground. And in fleeing the manipulator, we have abandoned the field to him. The only people talking about money in the church are the ones exploiting it, precisely because the honest teachers have decided the topic is too dirty to touch. The abuse has silenced the very people who should be correcting it.</p>
-
-<blockquote>We were so afraid of being the preacher who talks about money for the wrong reasons that we became the preacher who never talks about it at all. And Jesus talked about it constantly.</blockquote>
-
-<h2>Money as Confession</h2>
-
-<p>There is a deeper reason for the silence, and it goes to the heart of why money is so hard to discuss. Your finances are the most honest confession you will ever make. You can lie about almost anything else. You can present a curated version of your marriage, your faith, your character. But your bank statement does not lie. It records exactly what you value, what you fear, what you serve. It is a spiritual X-ray, and it shows the bones.</p>
-
-<p>This is why money talk feels so threatening. To open your finances to another person is to let them see what you actually worship, not what you claim to worship. A man might genuinely believe he loves God and trusts him for the future, right up until his spending reveals a life organized entirely around self-protection and status. The gap between our stated values and our spending is often enormous, and money conversations expose that gap in a way nothing else does. We keep money private not because it is unimportant but because it is too revealing. It tells the truth about us, and we would rather not know it.</p>
-
-<h2>The Idol That Hides Best</h2>
-
-<p>Jesus said no one can serve two masters, and he named the second master specifically: money. Not power, not sex, not pride — money, mammon, the accumulation of wealth. He treated money as a rival deity, a competing god with its own priesthood and its own promises, capable of commanding the whole allegiance of a human heart. And the thing about the idol of money is that it is the idol that hides best. The greedy person almost never knows they are greedy. Every rich person can point to someone richer and feel comparatively modest. The love of money disguises itself as prudence, as responsibility, as good stewardship, as simply being sensible.</p>
-
-<p>This is why the silence is so dangerous. The idol thrives in the dark. As long as we never talk honestly about money, the god of mammon can rule half the hearts in the congregation without anyone noticing, because everyone is too polite to bring it up. We would confront a man about his drinking or his temper. We will not confront him about his greed, because greed is respectable, because greed is what our whole culture is built to produce, and because confronting it would require examining our own accounts first, which none of us wants to do.</p>
-
-<h2>The Discipleship We Skip</h2>
-
-<p>Consider what this silence costs in terms of actual discipleship. If Jesus talked about money more than heaven and hell combined, and we talk about it once a year with apologies, then we have systematically failed to disciple people in one of the areas Jesus considered most important. We form Christians who have thought carefully about theology, prayer, evangelism, and sexual ethics, and who have never once been discipled in how to hold money, how to give, how to work, how to resist the god their entire economy is training them to serve.</p>
-
-<p>The result is a church full of people who are theologically orthodox and financially indistinguishable from their unbelieving neighbors — same debt loads, same consumption patterns, same anxiety about money, same identity wrapped up in what they earn and own. We have discipled their doctrine and left their wallets in the hands of the culture. And then we wonder why the faith seems to make so little practical difference in how people actually live, when we have deliberately avoided the one topic Jesus used to expose the real condition of the heart.</p>
-
-<h2>Learning to Speak</h2>
-
-<p>Recovering the ability to talk about money honestly does not mean returning to manipulation. It means the opposite. It means speaking about money the way Jesus did — frequently, directly, without embarrassment, and always in a way that exposes the idol rather than exploiting the anxiety. It means a pastor being willing to say hard things about greed without any personal financial benefit riding on the sermon. It means creating a church culture where a person can actually confess their financial struggles the way they confess their other struggles, and find help rather than judgment.</p>
-
-<p>And it means self-implication, always. I cannot preach honestly about money if I am hiding my own relationship to it. The pastor who thunders about greed from a position of concealed comfort is a hypocrite, and the congregation can smell it. The only credible way to talk about money in the church is to talk about it as a fellow sinner still wrestling with the same idol, still finding the love of mammon rooted deeper in my own heart than I would like to admit, still learning to hold what I have with open hands. That kind of honesty is disarming precisely because it refuses the two positions people expect from a preacher on money — the manipulator who wants theirs and the coward who says nothing.</p>
-
-<h2>The Class Discomfort</h2>
-
-<p>There is another layer to the silence, and it is one we are especially reluctant to admit: money talk exposes class differences that the church would rather pretend do not exist. When a congregation contains both the struggling family and the comfortable professional, honest money conversation becomes socially treacherous. The wealthy member does not want to seem to boast or to condescend; the struggling member does not want to seem to complain or to beg. So everyone agrees, without ever agreeing, to keep the actual numbers hidden, and the class divide is preserved by a mutual politeness that looks like kindness and functions like avoidance.</p>
-
-<p>But this politeness comes at a cost. It means the struggling family suffers in silence, too ashamed to say they cannot afford the retreat, the youth trip, the giving campaign. It means the wealthy member never has their comfort challenged by real proximity to need. It means the church remains sorted by class beneath a surface of fellowship, everyone smiling and no one honest. The refusal to talk about money is partly a refusal to let the classes actually see each other, and in that refusal the church quietly accepts the world's arrangement rather than the kingdom's. Real community across the money divide requires the very honesty we have agreed to avoid.</p>
-
-<h2>What Honest Money Talk Sounds Like</h2>
-
-<p>It is worth picturing what a healthier culture would actually sound like, because the alternative to manipulation is not silence but honesty, and honesty has a particular texture. It sounds like a pastor preaching on money as often and as directly as Jesus did, without apology and without personal benefit. It sounds like a small group where a person can say, I am in debt and I am afraid, and be met with help rather than judgment. It sounds like a mature believer being willing to say out loud that they have wrestled with greed, that they have felt the pull of mammon, that they do not have this fully figured out either.</p>
-
-<p>It sounds, above all, like confession — the same confession we have learned to make about our other struggles, extended at last to the one we have kept hidden. When a Christian can say I have loved money more than God and I need help with this, the same way they might say I have struggled with lust or anger or doubt, the idol loses its power to hide. Confession drags it into the light, and in the light it withers. The church that recovers honest money talk is not a church that has become obsessed with money. It is a church that has finally brought the last dark corner of the heart into the healing light of the gospel.</p>
-
-<h2>The Sermon That Costs the Preacher</h2>
-
-<p>There is a particular reason money is hard to preach that goes beyond manipulation and class, and it strikes closest to those of us in the pulpit. To preach honestly about money is to preach against ourselves, because we are as caught by mammon as anyone in the pews, and often more comfortably so. The pastor who denounces greed must first face the greed in his own heart, the anxiety about his own salary, the quiet ways he has organized his own life around security and status. It is far easier to preach on sins we do not struggle with than on the one we share with our congregation, and money is the sin we all share.</p>
-
-<p>This is why the honest money sermon has a distinctive quality: it must be self-implicating, or it is a lie. The preacher who thunders about the love of money from a position of concealed comfort, who aims the warning at wealthier people down the road while quietly nursing his own attachment, has forfeited his credibility, and the congregation can sense it even when they cannot name it. The only sermon on money that lands is the one preached by a fellow struggler, standing inside the indictment rather than above it, confessing the pull of the idol in his own life even as he names it. I have preached money badly when I preached it safely, from a distance, as though it were someone else's problem. It is my problem. It is the problem of every one of us, and the sermon only works when the one preaching it admits he is the first sinner in the room.</p>
-
-<h2>Recovering the Joy</h2>
-
-<p>It would be a mistake to leave the impression that recovering honest money talk is only about confronting sin, because the deepest reason to break the silence is joy. The Christians who have learned to talk openly about money, to give freely, to hold their possessions loosely, are not grim ascetics burdened by obligation. They are among the freest and most joyful people I know. They have escaped the anxiety that grips those who serve mammon, the endless comparison, the fear of loss, the exhausting vigilance of the accumulator. They give with gladness and receive with gratitude and hold everything with the open hands of people who have discovered they lack nothing that matters.</p>
-
-<p>This joy is the great missing testimony in the church's silence about money. As long as we cannot speak of it, we cannot celebrate the freedom that comes from holding it rightly, and we leave people trapped in an anxiety the gospel means to heal. The point of breaking the taboo is not merely to expose the idol but to invite people into the liberty on the other side of it — the liberty of the person who has finally understood that they cannot serve God and money, and has chosen God, and has found, to their surprise, that in losing their grip on money they gained a joy no amount of it could ever buy. The last taboo needs to fall. Not so we can extract more from people, but so we can finally disciple them in the thing Jesus cared about most and we have avoided most, and lead them into the joy that waits on the far side of the silence. It is time to name it, starting with the one in our own accounts.</p>`,
