-    body: `<p>A great deal of Christian writing about work assumes a reader with options. It talks about finding your calling, pursuing your passion, aligning your gifts with your labor, and it presumes that if you are unhappy in your work you can simply find something better. This is advice for the fortunate. It is written for people with education, mobility, savings, and choices. And it has almost nothing to say to the vast number of people who hate their jobs and cannot leave them — the ones supporting families on work they despise, trapped by circumstance in labor that drains them, going back on Monday to something they dread because the alternative is not having a job at all.</p>
-
-<p>I want to write to that person, because they are more common than the Christian bookstore suggests, and because the honest truth is that most human work throughout history has not been fulfilling. The peasant did not love the field. The factory worker did not find their passion on the line. The idea that work should be personally meaningful is a modern, affluent expectation, and holding it up as the standard leaves the majority feeling like spiritual failures for not enjoying labor that was never designed to be enjoyed. There is a better word than that, and it comes from the Scriptures themselves.</p>
-
-<h2>The Curse Is Real</h2>
-
-<p>Begin with honesty about the source of the misery, because the Bible is more honest about it than most Christian encouragement. When Adam and Eve fell, part of the curse fell specifically on work. "Cursed is the ground because of you; through painful toil you will eat food from it all the days of your life. It will produce thorns and thistles for you. By the sweat of your brow you will eat your food." Work, which was good, became frustrating, futile, and hard. The thorns are not a metaphor. They are the built-in resistance of a broken world to human effort — the projects that fail, the labor that goes unrewarded, the toil that produces exhaustion more than satisfaction.</p>
-
-<p>This means that if you hate your job, you are not necessarily doing anything wrong, and you are certainly not alone in the human story. The frustration of work is a feature of the fallen world, experienced by nearly everyone who has ever labored. The Bible does not promise that faithful people will love their work. It tells the truth: that work east of Eden involves thorns, and that the sweat and the futility are part of the deal for all of us. Naming this is itself a mercy, because it releases you from the false guilt of thinking your misery is a personal spiritual failure. It is the human condition, and Scripture says so plainly.</p>
-
-<blockquote>The Bible never promised you would love your work. It promised thorns. If you hate your job, you are not a bad Christian. You are a laborer in a cursed field, like nearly everyone who has ever worked.</blockquote>
-
-<h2>Working as Unto the Lord</h2>
-
-<p>The most powerful word for the person trapped in hated work comes from Paul, and it was written to actual slaves — people in a condition of forced labor far worse than any modern job. "Whatever you do, work at it with all your heart, as working for the Lord, not for human masters, since you know that you will receive an inheritance from the Lord as a reward." Slaves. Working for masters who owned them, in conditions they did not choose and could not leave. And Paul tells them their labor can be offered to Christ, seen by Christ, and rewarded by Christ — regardless of the injustice of their situation or the character of their earthly master.</p>
-
-<p>This changes everything for the person who hates their job, and I want to be careful not to make it into a cheap slogan, because Paul was not offering a slogan to slaves. He was offering a genuine reframe. Your real employer is not the company you resent. Your real employer is God, and God sees the work you do even when your earthly boss does not, even when the work is thankless, even when no one notices whether you do it well or badly. The excellence you bring to hated work is not for the company. It is an offering to God, seen and treasured by him. This does not make the job enjoyable. It gives it a dignity the job itself could never provide.</p>
-
-<h2>Providing as Love</h2>
-
-<p>There is a second reframe that reaches the person supporting a family on work they despise, and it is this: the work you hate is an act of love for the people you are providing for. The father who drags himself to a job he dreads so his children can eat is not merely enduring; he is loving. The mother working a shift she hates so the rent gets paid is pouring herself out for the ones who depend on her. Paul says that anyone who does not provide for their household has denied the faith. Providing is not a lesser calling than passion. It is love made concrete in labor, and there is something in it closer to the cross than any dream job could offer.</p>
-
-<p>This matters because it locates the meaning of the hated work not in the work itself but in the people it serves. You may find no meaning in the task. You may find enormous meaning in the fact that this task keeps your children fed and housed and clothed. The meaning was never supposed to come from the labor alone; it comes from the love the labor serves. When you understand that your despised job is the vehicle of your love for your family, the job does not become enjoyable, but it becomes bearable in a different way — not as pointless suffering but as costly love.</p>
-
-<h2>Honest About the Limits</h2>
-
-<p>I do not want to spiritualize away real problems, so let me be honest about the limits of these reframes. Some jobs are genuinely destructive — abusive, exploitative, degrading in ways that damage the soul. The call to work as unto the Lord is not a call to remain forever in situations that are crushing you, if there is any way out. Paul told slaves to work faithfully, but he also told them that if they could gain their freedom, they should. Contentment in hard circumstances is not the same as passivity in the face of injustice, and the person in a genuinely toxic situation should seek to change it if they possibly can. Faithfulness in a hated job is not the same as resignation to abuse.</p>
-
-<p>And even in a job that is merely unpleasant rather than destructive, it is right to seek better if the door opens. Nothing in Scripture requires you to stay miserable when you could serve God and your family more fully elsewhere. The reframes are not meant to keep you trapped. They are meant for the person who, right now, cannot leave — to give them a way to live faithfully in the meantime, whatever the meantime turns out to be.</p>
-
-<h2>The Weight and the Hope</h2>
-
-<h2>The Small Redemptions</h2>
-
-<p>Even in work you hate, there are usually small openings for meaning that the theology of neighbor-love can help you find, and they are worth naming because they can make the unbearable bearable. You may despise the job itself, but there are people around you — coworkers, customers, the person at the next station — and the way you treat them is not dictated by the job. You can bring dignity, kindness, and honesty into a workplace that has none of these, and in doing so you become a small mercy in someone else's hard day. The job may be meaningless; the way you do it never is.</p>
-
-<p>I have known people who transformed their experience of hated work not by changing the work but by changing what they paid attention to. The security guard who decided to genuinely see and greet every person who passed his desk. The clerk who made it her mission to be the one kind interaction in her customers' frustrating errands. The factory worker who quietly looked out for the new hires no one else helped. They did not love their jobs any more than before, but they found within the jobs a field for love, and the love gave the drudgery a meaning the task itself could never supply. This is the neighbor-love theology applied at the most practical level: even where the work is empty, the neighbor is not, and the neighbor is always an opportunity to serve the God you cannot serve through the task.</p>
-
-<h2>Holding On to the Ending</h2>
-
-<p>Finally, the person trapped in hated work needs the long view that Scripture provides, because the misery feels endless when you are inside it, and the endlessness is part of what crushes. But it is not endless. The curse on work is not the final word; it is a temporary condition of a world in the process of being redeemed. The same Bible that describes the thorns and thistles promises a day when the creation itself will be liberated from its bondage to decay, when the futility that groans through all our labor will be lifted, when work will become again what it was in the garden before the fall — pure joy, pure fruitfulness, labor without frustration in the presence of God.</p>
-
-<p>This is not escapism; it does not tell you to endure now because it will all be over later and nothing you do in the meantime matters. It tells you the opposite: that your faithful labor now, offered to God in the cursed field, is connected to that coming restoration, that you are not merely surviving until the end but participating, however dimly, in a redemption that is already underway and will one day be complete. The thorns are real, and one day they will be gone.</p>
-
-<h2>The Difference Between Hating and Being Destroyed</h2>
-
-<p>I want to draw a careful line, because there is a difference between a job you hate and a job that is destroying you, and the counsel differs for each. A job you merely hate — that is boring, thankless, beneath your abilities, or simply unpleasant — is a job you can endure faithfully, offering the drudgery to God and finding meaning in the love it serves. Most hated jobs are of this kind, and the reframes of faithfulness and provision apply directly to them. You can be miserable in such a job and still be spiritually whole, still be faithful, still be growing.</p>
-
-<p>But some jobs cross a line into genuine destruction. The workplace that is abusive, that demands you compromise your integrity, that crushes your spirit so relentlessly that it is deforming your character and your relationships and your faith, is not a job to be nobly endured indefinitely. There is a point at which faithfulness means seeking a way out rather than staying put, at which remaining is not endurance but self-destruction. Discerning the difference requires wisdom and often the counsel of others who can see what you cannot, because the person inside a destructive job often loses the ability to judge how bad it has become. If your work is not merely making you unhappy but genuinely damaging your soul, the call to work as unto the Lord does not require you to stay. It may, in fact, require you to leave.</p>
-
-<h2>The God Who Was a Worker</h2>
-
-<p>The final comfort for the person who hates their work is that the God they serve is not a stranger to hard labor. Jesus worked as a carpenter for most of his adult life, in a trade that involved real physical toil, in an obscure town, doing ordinary work that no one recorded and no one celebrated. The Son of God spent the majority of his earthly years not preaching to crowds but working with his hands in a village workshop, and there is no indication that the work was easy or the days were exciting. He knows what it is to labor in obscurity, to do work that goes unnoticed, to feel the sweat and the weariness of ordinary toil in a fallen world.</p>
-
-<p>This means the God you cry out to from your hated job is not distant from your experience but has shared it. He is not a deity who only knows the mountaintops of ministry; he knew the long grind of daily work, and he sanctified it by doing it faithfully himself. When you drag yourself back to the job you dread, you are not alone, and you are not doing something beneath the notice of God. You are doing something the Son of God himself did for years, and he sees you, and he understands, in a way no one who has not labored could understand. The carpenter from Nazareth is with you in the cursed field, because he worked it too.</p>
-
-<p>So to the person who will go back on Monday to work they dread: your misery is real, and it is not a sign of spiritual failure. It is the shape of labor in a fallen world, shared by nearly everyone. But your work is seen by a God who misses nothing, offered to a Lord who will reward it, and poured out in love for people who depend on you. Until the day the thorns are gone, you work in the cursed field like the rest of us, faithfully, as unto the Lord, in love for those you carry. That is not nothing. In the eyes of heaven, it may be everything.</p>`,
