-    body: `<p>Buried in Leviticus 25, in a book most Christians never read, sits one of the most radical economic ideas in human history. Every fifty years, the law commanded, Israel was to sound the trumpet and proclaim liberty throughout the land. Debts would be cancelled. Slaves would go free. And most astonishingly, all ancestral land that had been sold or lost over the preceding half-century would return, without payment, to the families who had originally held it. The whole economic order would be reset. The accumulation of wealth and the accumulation of poverty that had built up over two generations would be dissolved in a single year, and the nation would start again from something closer to equality.</p>
-
-<p>This is the jubilee, and modern readers do not know what to do with it. It is routinely dismissed as impractical, utopian, perhaps never even fully implemented, a curious relic of an ancient agrarian society with no bearing on modern economic life. And it is true that jubilee, taken as a literal legal prescription, does not transfer to a modern industrial economy. But to dismiss it on those grounds is to miss the point entirely. The jubilee was never merely a piece of legislation. It was a theological declaration about the nature of ownership, the danger of permanent inequality, and the God who refuses to let the poor stay poor forever. And that declaration may be exactly what the church most needs to recover.</p>
-
-<h2>The Land Is Mine</h2>
-
-<p>The jubilee is grounded in a single sentence that undermines the entire modern conception of property. "The land is mine," God says, "and you reside in my land as foreigners and strangers." This is the theological foundation, and it is worth sitting with, because it contradicts an assumption so basic we never examine it: that what we own, we own absolutely. In the biblical vision, no human being ever owns land absolutely. God owns it. Human beings are tenants, stewards, temporary holders of what ultimately belongs to another. The land could not be sold permanently, because it was never truly the seller's to sell. At most, one could sell the use of the land until the next jubilee, when it reverted to its true owner's arrangement.</p>
-
-<p>This reframes ownership entirely. If God owns everything and we are tenants, then our property rights are real but limited, held under an authority that has its own claims and purposes. The jubilee was the periodic enforcement of God's ultimate ownership against the human tendency to treat possession as absolute. It said, in effect: you may hold this for a time, but you do not own it forever, and the God who does own it has decreed that it will not remain permanently concentrated in the hands of the few while the many are dispossessed. Ownership is stewardship under a Lord who cares how the resources of his land are distributed.</p>
-
-<blockquote>The jubilee rests on four words that dismantle our whole economics: the land is mine. You do not own it forever. You hold it, as a tenant, under an owner who will not let the poor stay poor.</blockquote>
-
-<h2>The Problem Jubilee Addresses</h2>
-
-<p>To understand why jubilee matters, you have to understand the problem it was designed to solve, and it is a problem no economy has ever escaped. Over time, wealth concentrates. The able and the fortunate and the ruthless accumulate more; the unlucky and the vulnerable fall behind; debt loads the poor and dispossesses them; and generation by generation the gap widens until a small elite holds most of the resources and a large underclass holds almost nothing. This is not a bug in fallen economies. It is their default trajectory. Left alone, every economy drifts toward oligarchy, with wealth and land and power pooling at the top and hardening into permanence.</p>
-
-<p>The jubilee was God's structural interruption of this drift. It did not prevent inequality from developing over the decades — people would still rise and fall, gain and lose, within the fifty-year cycle. But it prevented inequality from becoming permanent and hereditary. Every fifty years, the drift was reversed. The family that had lost everything got its inheritance back. The debt that had accumulated was cancelled. No family could be permanently locked into poverty, and no family could permanently monopolize the land. The jubilee built a limit into the system — a horizon beyond which dispossession could not extend, a guarantee that poverty would not become a life sentence passed down to the children and the children's children.</p>
-
-<h2>Jesus and the Jubilee</h2>
-
-<p>The jubilee is not left behind in the Old Testament. When Jesus stood up in the synagogue at Nazareth to announce his ministry, he read from Isaiah 61: "The Spirit of the Lord is on me, because he has anointed me to proclaim good news to the poor. He has sent me to proclaim freedom for the prisoners and recovery of sight for the blind, to set the oppressed free, to proclaim the year of the Lord's favor." (NIV) That last phrase — the year of the Lord's favor — is jubilee language. Isaiah was drawing on the imagery of the jubilee year, and Jesus was applying it to himself. He was announcing that his ministry was the true jubilee, the great release, the year of the Lord's favor come in person.</p>
-
-<p>What Jesus did with the jubilee is instructive. He did not enact it as national legislation; he was not a political reformer in that sense. But he took its meaning — release, freedom, good news to the poor, the reversal of the accumulated weight of debt and bondage — and made it the center of his mission. The kingdom he proclaimed was a jubilee kingdom, one in which debts are forgiven, the poor are lifted, the captives are freed, and the accumulated injustices of the world are being undone. The Lord's Prayer itself carries the jubilee impulse: forgive us our debts as we forgive our debtors. The jubilee did not disappear. It was transfigured and placed at the heart of the gospel.</p>
-
-<h2>What It Asks of Us Now</h2>
-
-<p>So what does jubilee economics ask of the church today? Not, I think, a legislative program that any congregation could adopt, and not a naive attempt to reimpose Mosaic land law on a modern nation. But it asks for something, and the something is real. It asks us to hold our own property as tenants rather than absolute owners, knowing that God owns it and has purposes for it beyond our comfort. It asks us to be a people of forgiveness in the economic sense — releasing what is owed to us, refusing to demand the last ounce, practicing in our own dealings the release the jubilee proclaimed. It asks us to care intensely about whether the poor in our reach are locked into permanent poverty, and to work against that entrenchment wherever we can.</p>
-
-<p>And it asks the church, perhaps most pointedly, to be an outpost of jubilee in a world that runs on its opposite. Where the world accumulates, the church releases. Where the world lets inequality harden, the church works to reverse it. Where the world treats ownership as absolute, the church treats everything as held in trust from the God who owns it all. The church that forgives debts — literal and relational — that lifts the fallen, that refuses to let poverty become permanent among its people, that shares its resources so that no one among it is in serious need, is enacting the jubilee in miniature. This is not utopian. It is precisely what the early church in Acts did when they shared their possessions so that there was no needy person among them. That was jubilee, embodied in a community.</p>
-
-<h2>The Trumpet We Have Silenced</h2>
-
-<p>I suspect the reason we have dismissed the jubilee as impractical is not primarily that it is impractical. It is that it is inconvenient. Jubilee threatens the accumulation that most of us, myself included, are quietly organizing our lives around. It is easier to declare it a relic than to let it interrogate our own grip on what we hold. The trumpet that was meant to sound liberty across the land has been silenced, not because it could not be sounded, but because we would rather not hear what it says about our debts and our dispossessions and our absolute ownership of things that were never truly ours.</p>
-
-<h2>Why the First Sermon Was a Jubilee</h2>
-
-<p>It is worth pausing on the fact that Jesus chose the jubilee for his inaugural announcement, because the choice was not accidental and its implications are enormous. Of all the texts he could have read to launch his public ministry, of all the ways he could have summarized what he came to do, he selected the passage that proclaims the year of the Lord's favor — release for captives, sight for the blind, freedom for the oppressed, good news for the poor. He defined his entire mission in the vocabulary of the jubilee, and then he sat down and said, today this scripture is fulfilled in your hearing.</p>
-
-<p>This means the gospel is, at its heart, a jubilee gospel. The salvation Jesus brings is release — release from the debt of sin, release from the bondage of the law of sin and death, release from every accumulated weight that the fall has piled on the human race. And it means the community that receives this gospel cannot be indifferent to the economic release the jubilee also proclaimed, because the spiritual release and the material release flow from the same heart of God and cannot finally be separated. A church that preaches release from spiritual debt while ignoring the economic bondage of the poor at its gate has torn apart what Jesus held together in a single sermon. The jubilee gospel liberates the whole person, and it forms a people who long to see liberation extend into every corner of a bound and burdened world.</p>
-
-<h2>The Practice of Release</h2>
-
-<p>How does a community actually live jubilee in an economy structured entirely against it? Not by waiting for the legislation that will never come, but by becoming, in its own life, a colony of the coming kingdom where the jubilee logic already operates. It begins small and concrete: forgiving actual debts owed to us when we are able, refusing to demand the last dollar from someone drowning, structuring our generosity so that the accumulated weight on the poor among us is genuinely lifted rather than merely acknowledged.</p>
-
-<p>It extends to how we hold our own resources — as tenants rather than absolute owners, ready to release what God directs us to release, unwilling to let our wealth become the permanent fortress the jubilee was designed to prevent. And it extends to the character of our common life, a community where, as in Acts, the sharing is real enough that there is no destitute person left unhelped in the fellowship. None of this reforms the national economy. All of it makes the church a living sign of the jubilee it proclaims, a foretaste of the great release, a people who have heard the trumpet and begun, however imperfectly, to live as though the year of the Lord's favor had actually arrived.</p>
-
-<h2>The Objection of the Prudent</h2>
-
-<p>The immediate objection to jubilee economics comes from the prudent, and it deserves a hearing rather than a dismissal. If debts are cancelled every seven years and land returns every fifty, the objector asks, who would ever lend, and who would work hard to acquire what they cannot permanently keep? Does jubilee not punish diligence and reward improvidence, dissolving the incentives that make an economy function at all? It is a serious question, and the law itself anticipated it. Deuteronomy explicitly warns against the hard-hearted person who refuses to lend as the year of release approaches, calculating that the debt will soon be cancelled, and commands generosity anyway.</p>
-
-<p>This is precisely the point at which jubilee economics confronts the logic of pure self-interest and refuses it. The law knew that jubilee cut against the grain of ordinary economic calculation, and it commanded the harder thing anyway, because the purpose of the economy in God's vision is not the maximization of individual acquisition but the flourishing of the whole community, especially its weakest members. Jubilee subordinates efficiency to justice, incentive to mercy, the accumulation of the strong to the protection of the vulnerable. It is not that jubilee ignores the concerns of the prudent; it is that it ranks them below the concern for the poor, and it asks the prudent to accept a less optimized economy in exchange for one that will not permanently dispossess anyone. That trade-off is the whole argument, and where you land on it reveals what you believe an economy is finally for.</p>
-
-<p>The jubilee will not be legislated back into a modern economy, and I am not calling for that. But its trumpet can still sound in the life of the church and the conscience of the believer. It sounds every time a debt is forgiven, every time the accumulated weight on a poor family is lifted, every time a Christian holds their property as a tenant of God rather than a lord of their own domain, every time a community shares so that none among it is destitute. The year of the Lord's favor has been proclaimed. Jesus said it was fulfilled in the hearing of those who listened. The only question is whether the church that bears his name will let the jubilee it announced reach as far as our own bank accounts, or whether we will keep insisting, against our own Lord, that the land is finally ours.</p>`,
