-  {
-    title: "Treasures in Heaven",
-    slug: "treasures-in-heaven",
-    excerpt:
-      "Jesus told us to store up treasures in heaven, and we have quietly filed this away as poetic and impractical. But he meant it as an actual investment strategy — the only one that survives death.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "11 min",
-    body: `<p>Jesus said something about money that we have almost entirely failed to take literally. "Do not store up for yourselves treasures on earth, where moths and vermin destroy, and where thieves break in and steal. But store up for yourselves treasures in heaven, where moths and vermin do not destroy, and where thieves do not break in and steal. For where your treasure is, there your heart will be also." We treat this as beautiful poetry, a nice sentiment about not being too materialistic, a general encouragement toward spiritual-mindedness. And in treating it that way, we have missed that Jesus was giving concrete financial instruction. He was describing an actual investment strategy, and claiming it was the only one that ultimately made sense.</p>
-
-<p>The passage is not vague spirituality. It is a comparison of two portfolios, and a recommendation to move your assets from one to the other. Jesus, the master teacher, was making an argument that any investor should recognize: one of these investments is guaranteed to be destroyed, and the other is guaranteed to last forever, so you would be a fool to keep your treasure in the perishable one. The strangeness is not that he used financial language. The strangeness is that we have refused to believe he meant it.</p>
-
-<h2>The Argument from Permanence</h2>
-
-<p>Follow the logic Jesus actually lays out, because it is more rigorous than we give it credit for. Earthly treasure, he says, is subject to three threats: moth, rust, and theft. In his agrarern context, wealth was stored as fine cloth that moths ate, as metal goods that corroded, as valuables that thieves stole. His point was that all earthly treasure is, by its nature, perishable and vulnerable. It decays, it corrodes, it can be taken, and even if none of these befall it in your lifetime, death will separate you from it completely. No earthly treasure survives your own death. Every dollar you accumulate, you will leave behind. The hearse does not pull a trailer.</p>
-
-<p>Heavenly treasure, by contrast, is immune to all three threats. It does not decay, cannot be stolen, and is not lost at death — indeed, it is precisely what death delivers you into. Jesus is making an argument from permanence, and it is unanswerable on its own terms. If you have a choice between investing in something guaranteed to be destroyed and something guaranteed to last forever, and if you are going to exist forever, then the rational choice is obvious. To pour your life into accumulating what you cannot keep, while neglecting to accumulate what you cannot lose, is not merely unspiritual. It is, by Jesus' logic, foolish. It is bad investing.</p>
-
-<blockquote>Jesus was not being poetic. He was being practical. He looked at your portfolio and said: you are heavily invested in an asset guaranteed to be destroyed. Move your money.</blockquote>
-
-<h2>What Heavenly Treasure Is</h2>
-
-<p>The natural question is what, exactly, treasure in heaven consists of, and how one actually stores it up. Jesus and the apostles give surprisingly concrete answers. In Luke, immediately after telling the parable of the rich fool, Jesus says: "Sell your possessions and give to the poor. Provide purses for yourselves that will not wear out, a treasure in heaven that will never fail." Storing treasure in heaven is directly linked to generosity toward the poor. The act of giving away earthly wealth is the mechanism by which heavenly wealth is accumulated. You transfer your treasure from the perishable account to the imperishable one by giving it away — a transaction that looks like loss to the world and is actually the shrewdest possible investment.</p>
-
-<p>Paul makes the same connection explicit in his instructions to the rich, and it is one of the most remarkable passages in Scripture on this theme. Command those who are rich, he says, to be generous and willing to share, and in this way they will "lay up treasure for themselves as a firm foundation for the coming age, so that they may take hold of the life that is truly life." The generous use of wealth is described as an investment that stores up treasure for the age to come. This is not metaphor stacked on metaphor. It is a claim about the actual mechanics of eternal reward: what you give away for the sake of Christ and his kingdom is not lost but transferred, laid up, stored where it cannot be destroyed, waiting for you.</p>
-
-<h2>The Heart Follows the Treasure</h2>
-
-<p>There is a further dimension to this teaching that is easy to miss, and it concerns the direction of causation between treasure and heart. "Where your treasure is, there your heart will be also." Notice that Jesus does not say where your heart is, there your treasure will be. He says the reverse. Your heart follows your treasure. This is a profound observation about human nature: we come to love what we invest in. The things we pour our resources into become the things we care about. If you invest heavily in earthly things, your heart will be bound to earth. If you invest in heavenly things, your heart will be drawn toward heaven.</p>
-
-<p>This means that generosity is not only a way of storing up eternal reward; it is a way of reorienting your own heart. When you give to the kingdom, your heart follows the gift, and you find yourself caring more about the kingdom, more about the poor you have invested in, more about the eternal things your treasure is now accumulating. The person who wants to love God and the things of God more deeply has a concrete tool available: move your treasure there, and your heart will follow. Generosity is heart-surgery you perform on yourself, redirecting your affections by redirecting your investments. This is why the miser is trapped and the giver is free — the miser's heart is chained to what he clutches, while the giver's heart is drawn upward toward what he has sent ahead.</p>
-
-<h2>The Only Investment That Survives Death</h2>
-
-<p>What makes this teaching so urgent is the one fact we spend our whole lives avoiding: we are going to die, and death is the great liquidator of every earthly portfolio. The wealthiest person who ever lived left it all behind. The most successful career ends in a coffin. Every earthly treasure, no matter how carefully accumulated and guarded, is confiscated in full at the moment of death, and we take nothing with us. This is not morbid; it is simply true, and Jesus builds his entire argument on it. In light of certain death, an investment strategy that only pays off before death is a strategy with a guaranteed total loss at the end.</p>
-
-<p>Treasure in heaven is the only investment that survives the one event that liquidates everything else. What you give away for Christ does not die when you die; it is waiting for you on the other side. This reframes the whole logic of accumulation. The person storing up earthly wealth is investing everything in an account that will be zeroed out at death. The person storing up heavenly treasure through generosity is investing in the only account that death cannot touch, that in fact matures at death. Seen clearly, generosity is not the sacrifice of wealth; it is the transfer of wealth from the account that dies to the account that lives forever. The giver is not the one losing money. The hoarder is.</p>
-
-<h2>Believing It Enough to Do It</h2>
-
-<p>The reason we do not live this way is not that we disagree with Jesus' argument; it is that we do not really believe it, not down in the places where our checkbooks are decided. We nod at the teaching on Sunday and invest in the perishable account on Monday, hedging our bets, treating the heavenly treasure as a nice hope while treating the earthly treasure as the real thing. Our spending reveals what we actually believe, and most of our spending confesses a functional unbelief in the very thing Jesus said was the only sound investment. I include myself in this indictment fully; my own account balances testify against my stated theology more days than not.</p>
-
-<h2>The Shrewd Manager</h2>
-
-<p>There is a parable of Jesus so strange that most preachers avoid it entirely, and it turns out to be one of the clearest illustrations of the treasures-in-heaven principle in all his teaching. In Luke 16, a dishonest manager, about to be fired, uses his last days of authority to slash the debts owed to his master, currying favor with the debtors so they will welcome him after he loses his job. And astonishingly, the master commends the dishonest manager for acting shrewdly. Jesus then draws the lesson: use worldly wealth to gain friends for yourselves, so that when it is gone, you will be welcomed into eternal dwellings.</p>
-
-<p>The point is not that we should imitate the man's dishonesty but that we should imitate his shrewdness — his clear-eyed recognition that his current position was temporary and his use of present resources to secure a future beyond it. He understood that the wealth in his hands was about to be lost, and he leveraged it, while he still had it, to secure a welcome on the other side. This is exactly the treasures-in-heaven logic. Our earthly wealth is temporary; we are about to lose it all at death; and the shrewd thing to do is to use it now, while we have it, to secure friends and welcome and reward in the eternal home that awaits us. Jesus is calling us to be as shrewd about eternity as the dishonest manager was about his unemployment — to see clearly that the money will not last and to invest it accordingly.</p>
-
-<h2>What We Send Ahead</h2>
-
-<p>There is a moving way the older Christian tradition sometimes spoke about this, drawn from the missionary Jim Elliot and echoing ancient wisdom: the only things we truly keep are the things we give away, because what we hold we lose at death, and what we give for Christ we send ahead where it waits for us. This inverts the entire common-sense understanding of possession. We think we possess what we cling to and lose what we release. Jesus says the reverse: we lose what we cling to, because death confiscates it, and we keep what we release for his sake, because it is stored where death cannot reach.</p>
-
-<p>This means the generous person is not depleting their estate but transferring it — moving their wealth, piece by piece, from the account that will be zeroed out to the account that will be waiting. Every gift to the poor, every resource poured into the kingdom, every dollar released for Christ is not gone but relocated, sent ahead to a home we have not yet reached but will not leave. The hoarder, who feels so secure behind their accumulated pile, is the one actually losing everything, because none of it will cross the threshold of death with them. The giver, who looks to the world like they are throwing wealth away, is the shrewdest investor of all, quietly building a treasure in the one place the universe cannot take it from them.</p>
-
-<h2>The Eye and the Heart</h2>
-
-<p>In the middle of the treasures passage, Jesus says something that seems, at first, like a strange non sequitur: "The eye is the lamp of the body. If your eyes are healthy, your whole body will be full of light. But if your eyes are unhealthy, your whole body will be full of darkness." Placed between the teaching about treasure and the declaration that you cannot serve two masters, this saying is not a digression; it is the hinge. In the idiom of Jesus' day, a good eye meant a generous person and a bad eye meant a stingy, grasping one. He is saying that the way you see, your fundamental orientation toward money and possession, determines whether your whole life is filled with light or darkness.</p>
-
-<p>This is a profound observation about how money shapes perception. The grasping person, the one with the bad eye, does not merely have a financial problem; their entire vision of the world is darkened by their greed. They see other people as threats or resources, they see their possessions as extensions of themselves, they see the future as something to be secured against rather than trusted to God. The generous person, the one with the good eye, sees differently — sees others as neighbors to love, possessions as tools to deploy, the future as held by a faithful Father. Your relationship to money is not one compartment of your life among others; it is the lens through which you see everything, and it fills your whole existence with either light or darkness. This is why Jesus takes it so seriously. It is never just about money. It is about whether you can see at all.</p>
-
-<h2>The Rich Man and the Regret</h2>
-
-<p>Jesus told a story about a rich man who died and found himself in torment, looking across an unbridgeable gulf at a poor man named Lazarus who had begged at his gate and was now comforted. The rich man's great desire, too late, was to warn his five brothers so they would not come to the same place. And the answer he received was that they had Moses and the prophets; if they would not listen to them, they would not be persuaded even if someone rose from the dead.</p>
-
-<p>The story is a warning delivered from the far side of death, and its terror is precisely that the rich man cannot go back and reinvest. His portfolio is closed. The wealth he enjoyed in life, the fine clothes and sumptuous feasts, bought him nothing that lasted, and the poor man he ignored at his own gate turned out to be the one who mattered. The chance to store up treasure in heaven, to use his wealth to gain an eternal welcome, was available to him every single day, and he spent it all on himself, and now it is gone, and the regret is eternal. Jesus tells this story to the living, who still have time, whose portfolio is still open, who can still move their treasure while the market is open. The rich man cannot warn his brothers. But Jesus can, and does, and the warning is for us: the window in which you can send your treasure ahead is called your life, and it will not stay open forever.</p>
-
-<p>To actually believe Jesus is to reorder the portfolio. It is to give generously not out of guilt or obligation but out of the shrewd recognition that this is the wisest possible use of resources that will otherwise be destroyed — to send treasure ahead where it cannot be lost, to store up what death cannot take, to invest in the age that does not end. Jesus was not asking us to be impractical for the sake of piety. He was inviting us to be more practical than the world, to see through the illusion that earthly treasure is the safe investment, and to put our money where it will still exist a thousand years from now. Where your treasure is, there your heart will be also. He told us where to send it. The only question is whether we believed him enough to do it.</p>`,
-  },
-];
