-export const PARENTING_ARTICLES_2 = [
-  {
-    title: "Teaching Kids to Pray (Not Perform)",
-    slug: "teaching-kids-to-pray",
-    excerpt:
-      "The first prayer my son ever prayed out loud was a lie, and I taught it to him. Not on purpose. But I taught it just the same.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "11 min",
-    body: `<p>The first prayer my son ever prayed out loud was a lie, and I taught it to him. Not on purpose. But I taught it just the same. He was four, sitting at the dinner table, and I asked him to thank God for the food. He folded his hands the way he had seen me fold mine, closed his eyes the way he had seen me close mine, and said the words he had heard a hundred times: "Thank you God for this food and this day and my family, amen." Perfect. Fluent. And empty. He was not talking to God. He was performing a shape he had learned from watching me, and I applauded, because the shape was correct.</p>
-
-<p>I want to be careful not to make this sound worse than it was. He was four. Four-year-olds imitate everything, and imitation is how children learn most of what they know, including how to speak to God, so there was nothing sinister in the moment itself. The problem was not that he imitated. The problem was what he was imitating, and the fact that I could not tell the difference between a child learning to pray and a child learning to sound like he was praying, because I had long since lost the ability to tell that difference in myself.</p>
-
-<p>I did not notice for years what I had done. I thought I was teaching him to pray. I was teaching him to sound like someone who prays. Those are not the same skill, and the second one is easier to teach, easier to measure, and quietly poisonous.</p>
-
-<h2>The Prayer That Impresses the Room</h2>
-
-<p>Every church has a child who prays beautifully. Composed, articulate, theologically tidy, the kind of prayer that makes grandparents cry and other parents feel behind. And every one of us who has raised such a child knows the private truth that we do not say out loud: we coached it. We praised the eloquence. We beamed when the prayer landed well in front of company. And in doing so we taught the child that prayer has an audience, and the audience is not God.</p>
-
-<p>Jesus said something about this that we have managed to file under "the Pharisees" so we never have to file it under "us." He said that when the hypocrites pray, they love to stand and be seen, and that they have their reward already. The reward is the being-seen. That is the whole transaction, closed and settled. He was not describing a first-century sect. He was describing what happens to any human being, at any age, who discovers that religious language earns approval. My four-year-old discovered it before he could read. I handed him the discovery.</p>
-
-<p>I have had to sit with this. The performance prayer is not a failure of my child's heart. It is a reflection of mine. He learned to perform because he was watching a father who performs. The public prayer at the church potluck, warm and rounded and slightly better than my private prayers deserve to sound. The grace before the meal that gets more graceful when guests are present. He absorbed the whole grammar of it, and he was faithful to what he absorbed. Children are not fooled by our prayers, but they are formed by them, and what forms them is not the words we choose but the direction our attention is actually facing while we say them.</p>
-
-<h2>What Prayer Actually Is</h2>
-
-<p>Before we can teach a child to pray, we have to remember what the thing is. Prayer is not a performance and it is not a technique. It is a relationship conducted in words, and sometimes without them. Eugene Peterson spent a career insisting that the Psalms are the school of prayer precisely because they are so unpolished. They complain. They rage. They accuse God of sleeping. They ask, in Psalm 88, and receive no comfort by the end, and the psalm just stops in the dark. That is in the prayer book. That is what God included when he taught his people to talk to him.</p>
-
-<blockquote>We have handed our children a prayer life with the complaints edited out, and then wondered why it feels fake to them. It feels fake because we made it fake. We removed everything true and kept everything nice.</blockquote>
-
-<p>A child who is only allowed to pray gratitude and requests has been given half a language. The Psalms give the whole language. They give lament, which is the honest naming of pain in the presence of God. They give confession, which is the honest naming of failure. They give the raw questions that a well-behaved prayer is not permitted to ask. If I want my sons to pray for real, I have to let them pray angry. I have to let them tell God they do not understand and are not sure he is listening. That is not irreverence. That is Psalm 13, four times over, "How long, O Lord."</p>
-
-<p>Consider what it means that God gave his people a book of prayers to pray, rather than leaving them to invent prayer from scratch. It means that the human heart, left to itself, will pray too small. It will pray only the acceptable things. God knew this, and so he gave a hundred and fifty prayers that include cursing and despair and confusion and the wild swings of a real interior life, and he said, here, pray these, this is how my people talk to me. A child raised only on the sunny prayers has been quietly told that half of their actual heart is not welcome in the conversation. And a child who learns that half their heart is unwelcome will eventually take the whole heart elsewhere.</p>
-
-<h2>The Silence I Was Afraid Of</h2>
-
-<p>One of my sons went through a stretch where he did not want to pray at all. He was maybe eleven. And my first instinct, the instinct of the performer, was to fix it, to find the right encouragement, to get him praying again so that the household would look right and I would feel like I had done my job. What I did instead, mostly by accident and partly by exhaustion, was let it be. I said something like, God is patient, and he is not keeping score, and when you have something to say you can say it. Then I stopped managing it.</p>
-
-<p>That was the most honest thing I ever taught him about prayer, and I taught it by not performing a solution. Because a God who requires you to pray on schedule, in the right tone, with the right words, or else, is not the God of the gospel. He is a manager. And a child raised to pray to a manager will either become an anxious performer or walk away from the whole thing the moment he is free to. I have watched both happen, in other families and nearly in mine.</p>
-
-<p>The stretch of not praying lasted longer than I would have liked, and I will admit that I checked on it more than I let on, watching for signs that the faith was still there underneath the silence. It was. What returned, eventually, was not the fluent little prayers of his early childhood but something quieter and more his own, a few honest words at night, sometimes nothing, occasionally a real question thrown at the ceiling. I would not trade that hard-won, halting, genuine prayer for a hundred of the polished ones I used to be so proud of. The silence, it turned out, was not the death of his prayer life. It was the death of his performance, and what grew up in the cleared ground was the real thing.</p>
-
-<h2>Praying in Front of Them, Badly</h2>
-
-<p>The most useful thing I have done as a father is to let my children hear me pray when I am not doing well. Not the composed dinner grace. The stumbling one. The one where I stop in the middle because I do not know what I am asking for. The one where I say, God, I am angry about this, and I am not going to pretend I am not.</p>
-
-<p>My son once heard me pray after a hard day at the church, a day involving a family in the congregation coming apart, and I prayed badly and briefly and I did not tie it up. I said something like, I do not know what you are doing here and I hate it. Amen. And I worried afterward that I had modeled irreverence in front of my boy. What I had actually modeled was faith. Because only someone who believes God is real talks to him like that. You do not complain to a fiction. The child who hears his father complain to God is watching a father who is convinced God is in the room.</p>
-
-<blockquote>A polished prayer teaches a child that God wants your best performance. A broken prayer teaches a child that God wants you. Those two lessons produce two completely different adults.</blockquote>
-
-<p>There is a risk here, and I do not want to pretend there is not. A parent can tip from honest lament into a kind of performative brokenness, a display of raw prayer that is just as much a show as the polished version, only aimed at looking authentic instead of looking pious. Children can smell that too. The point is not to perform brokenness any more than to perform composure. The point is to actually pray, in front of them, as the person you actually are on that particular day, which some days is grateful and some days is furious and some days has nothing to say at all. What forms them is not the tone. It is the reality.</p>
-
-<h2>The Words We Give Them</h2>
-
-<p>None of this means we abandon form. Children need words to borrow before they have words of their own, the same way they need to hear a language spoken before they can speak it. The Lord's Prayer is not a performance script. It is training wheels for the heart, given by Jesus himself to people who asked, teach us to pray, because they did not know how either. Written prayers, the ones the church has prayed for centuries, are not the enemy of sincerity. They are a scaffold for it. A child who prays the words of a saint who died a thousand years ago is not performing. He is joining a chorus.</p>
-
-<p>The difference is not written versus spontaneous. The difference is whether the child understands that the words are aimed at a real Person who is actually listening, or whether the words are aimed at the people in the room. You can pray the Lord's Prayer as a performance. You can pray a stumbling, made-up prayer as a performance. The form is not the problem. The audience is the problem. And children learn who the audience is by watching where our eyes go, literally and otherwise, when we pray.</p>
-
-<p>So I have kept the forms in our house. We still pray the Lord's Prayer. We still say grace, most nights, though I have loosened my grip on how it sounds. What I have tried to change is not the presence of form but the presence of God underneath it, the sense in the room that we are actually addressing Someone and not reciting for one another. That is harder to teach than a formula, because it cannot be taught directly at all. It can only be caught, from a parent who is actually doing it, which means the whole thing comes back, as it always does, to whether the father in the room is praying to God or to the table.</p>
-
-<h2>What I Am Trying to Undo</h2>
-
-<p>I have five sons. I have taught all of them to pray, and I have taught some of them to perform, and the honest work of the last several years has been distinguishing the two after the fact and trying to loosen what I tightened. I have told them, more than once, that I got this wrong early. That when they were small I liked it too much when their prayers sounded good. That a prayer that sounds good in front of Grandma and a prayer that God hears are not automatically the same prayer, and I confused them, and I am sorry.</p>
-
-<p>Telling your children you were wrong is itself a form of prayer, or close to it. It is the confession of a father who performed, spoken to the people he performed in front of. And it does something the performance never could. It tells them that the man teaching them to pray is himself still learning, still stumbling, still not fluent. Which means the whole enterprise is not about arriving at a polished product. It is about staying in the conversation.</p>
-
-<p>The goal was never a child who prays impressively. The goal is a child who, at fifty, in the dark, when everything has gone wrong and no one is watching and there is nothing to perform, still believes there is Someone on the other end of the silence, and talks. That child will not need the right words. That child will need to know he was never performing in the first place. I am not sure I have given my sons that yet. I am sure it is the only thing worth giving them.</p>
-
-<p>So I am still at the table. Still asking them to pray. But I have stopped grading it. And when one of them prays something clumsy and true, something that would embarrass me at a potluck, I have learned to be more grateful for that than for all the fluent, empty, correct prayers I used to applaud. The clumsy true one is the one God is in. I taught them the other kind first. I am trying, late, to teach them this one.</p>`,
-  },
-  {
-    title: "Death, Grief, and Children: How to Talk About Hard Things",
-    slug: "death-grief-children",
-    excerpt:
-      "My son asked me if he was going to die, and I lied. I said not for a very long time. I do not know that. No parent knows that.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "12 min",
-    body: `<p>My son asked me if he was going to die, and I lied. He was five, in the bath, and he asked it the way children ask everything, with no warning and full attention. And I said, not for a very long time, buddy. Then I changed the subject to something about a boat. I do not know that he will not die soon. No parent knows that. I said it because I could not bear the alternative, which was to sit in the bathwater with my son and tell him the truth, that everyone dies, that I do not control when, and that this is the one certainty I cannot protect him from.</p>
-
-<p>We think we are protecting our children from death. We are usually protecting ourselves from their questions.</p>
-
-<h2>The Death We Hide</h2>
-
-<p>Ours is the first culture in human history to keep children away from death almost entirely. For most of human history a child would see death by the time they were old enough to remember anything, a grandparent laid out in the front room, a sibling lost to fever, animals slaughtered for the table. Death was in the house. It was terrible, but it was known. We have moved death to hospitals and hospices and funeral homes, sanitized it, professionalized it, and in the process we have raised children who reach adulthood without ever having stood next to a body. We think this is mercy. It may be the opposite.</p>
-
-<p>Because the child still knows. Children figure out death astonishingly early, usually around four or five, when they realize that dead is not a temporary condition, that the goldfish is not coming back, that this applies to grandparents and eventually to parents and eventually to them. They know. What they do not have, if we have hidden it, is any framework for what they know. They have the terror without the words and without the presence of an adult who will stay in the room while they feel it.</p>
-
-<p>What we have actually done, by removing death from the ordinary landscape of childhood, is not spare our children from it but abandon them to it. The knowledge arrives anyway. It arrives through a dead pet, a news story, a classmate's grandmother, and it arrives to a child who has never once seen an adult handle death with steadiness and faith and tears all at the same time. So the child handles it alone, in the dark, at three in the morning, constructing whatever terrifying theory a five-year-old constructs when the biggest fact in the universe lands on them and the adults have quietly agreed not to discuss it. We did not protect them. We left them to face the largest thing there is without a guide.</p>
-
-<h2>What Not to Say</h2>
-
-<p>The church has developed a set of phrases we deploy at children in grief, and most of them are quietly damaging. We tell a child that Grandpa went to sleep, and then we wonder why the child develops a terror of bedtime. We tell a child that God needed another angel, and we hand them a picture of a God who kills people because he is lonely, a God to be feared and resented rather than trusted. We tell a child that Grandma is in a better place, before the child has had a single second to be angry that she is not in this place, at this table, where she belongs.</p>
-
-<blockquote>Every comforting lie we tell a grieving child is really a comfort we are giving ourselves. The child does not need the metaphor. We need it, because the truth is unbearable and the metaphor lets us leave the room.</blockquote>
-
-<p>The worst one, the one I have heard from well-meaning saints a hundred times, is do not cry, she is in heaven. This tells a grieving child that their grief is a theological error. That if they had more faith they would not be sad. It weaponizes the resurrection against the natural, God-given, entirely appropriate response of a heart that has lost someone it loves. Jesus wept at the tomb of Lazarus, a man he was about to raise from the dead. He knew Lazarus was coming back in five minutes and he still wept. If the certainty of resurrection did not cancel the tears of Christ, it does not get to cancel the tears of my child.</p>
-
-<p>These phrases are not malicious. They come from adults who are themselves frightened, reaching for something to make the moment stop hurting, for the child and for themselves. But a frightened adult reaching for a comforting formula is exactly what a grieving child does not need. What the child needs is an adult who is not so frightened of death that they have to paper over it instantly, an adult who can say the true and terrible thing gently and then stay in the room while it lands. That adult is rare, because it requires the adult to have made some peace with death themselves, and most of us have not, and the phrases are how we hide that we have not.</p>
-
-<h2>Grief Is Not the Enemy of Faith</h2>
-
-<p>Somewhere we absorbed the idea that a faithful Christian does not grieve, or grieves only briefly and then rallies to the resurrection. Paul says the opposite. He says we do not grieve as those who have no hope, which is not the same as we do not grieve. We grieve. We grieve hard. We just grieve toward something. The hope does not delete the grief. It gives the grief a direction.</p>
-
-<p>When I teach my children about death, the most important thing I can give them is permission to be devastated. That the sadness is not a lack of faith, it is love with nowhere to go. That God is not offended by their tears, he collects them, the psalm says he keeps them in a bottle. A child who is allowed to grieve fully, in the presence of a parent who is also grieving and not pretending otherwise, learns something they cannot learn any other way, that the worst thing is real, that it can be survived, and that they are not alone in it. A child who is rushed past grief to the tidy comfort learns to distrust their own heart and to distrust the comfort.</p>
-
-<p>There is a long-term cost to teaching a child that grief is faithlessness, and I have seen it in grown adults sitting in my office decades later. They lost a parent as a child, were told to be strong, were praised for how well they were handling it, and so they buried a grief that had nowhere to go, and it went underground, and it shaped them into adults who cannot feel loss, cannot cry, cannot let anything reach the buried place. They were faithful children. They were also, without knowing it, wounded ones, wounded not by the death but by the instruction to skip the grief. The resurrection does not require us to skip the grief. It promises to meet us on the far side of it, which is a different thing entirely.</p>
-
-<h2>Telling the Truth About What We Do Not Know</h2>
-
-<p>Children ask the questions we have spent our whole adult lives avoiding. Where is Grandpa now. What is it like to be dead. Will it hurt. Will you die. Will I die. Why did God let it happen. And the temptation, always, is to answer with false certainty because false certainty feels like strength and the truth feels like abandoning them.</p>
-
-<p>But false certainty is a debt that comes due. The child who is told exactly what heaven is like, in confident detail, will one day discover that we were guessing, and will wonder what else we were guessing about. The stronger answer, the one that actually holds up over a lifetime, is honest uncertainty inside a larger trust. I do not know exactly what it is like to be with God. The Bible gives us pictures, not a map. But I know God is good, I know Jesus went there first, and I know that wherever Grandpa is, he is not lost, because God does not lose people. I can say that and mean it. I cannot say Grandpa is watching over you from a cloud and mean it, so I do not say it.</p>
-
-<blockquote>Children can carry the words I do not know if you carry them for them. It is not uncertainty that terrifies a child. It is uncertainty in the arms of a parent who is also terrified and pretending not to be.</blockquote>
-
-<p>This is the discipline that costs the most, because it requires the parent to tolerate their own not-knowing in front of a child who is begging for certainty. Every instinct in a parent's body wants to end the child's distress with a confident answer. But the confident answer we do not actually possess is a loan against a future the child will have to repay, and the honest answer, held steadily and warmly, is a gift they can keep. I do not know, but I trust him, and here is why. That sentence, from a parent who is clearly not panicking, does more for a child's faith than a dozen confident descriptions of the geography of heaven that the parent secretly made up.</p>
-
-<h2>Let Them See You Grieve</h2>
-
-<p>My father died, and I made a decision, half-conscious, that my sons would see me cry. Not perform grief for their benefit, but not hide it either. Because I had been raised to believe that a man does not let his children see him break, and I have spent decades unlearning the damage of that belief, and I was not going to hand it down.</p>
-
-<p>So they saw me cry at the funeral. They saw me quiet for weeks. They saw their father, the pastor, the one who is supposed to have answers, sit at the kitchen table and not have them. And I have come to believe that this was one of the most important things I ever gave them. Because now they know that grief does not end a man. That you can be shattered and still make breakfast. That faith is not the absence of tears, it is what you hold onto while you cry. If I had hidden my grief to protect them, I would have taught them that grief must be hidden, and I would have handed them, wrapped in love, the exact loneliness that nearly broke me at their age.</p>
-
-<p>I grew up without a father, which means I never saw a man grieve, never saw a father cry, never had a model for how a man survives loss. I had to build that model from nothing, badly, in real time, in front of my own sons. And the thing I most wanted them to see was the thing I had never seen: a man who feels the full weight of a loss and does not pretend otherwise and does not let it destroy him and does not hide it from the people who love him. I do not know if I gave them that. I know I tried, at my father's funeral, with my own sons watching, to be the man I had needed and never had.</p>
-
-<h2>The Long Conversation</h2>
-
-<p>Death is not a single conversation. It is a conversation that reopens at every age with new questions. The five-year-old asks if you will die. The nine-year-old asks what it means that a classmate's parent died. The thirteen-year-old asks whether heaven is even real. The seventeen-year-old, sitting up late after a friend's suicide, asks the question underneath all the others, whether anything means anything if it all ends. Each of these is the same child, coming back to the same edge, needing to know that the parent is still standing there, still honest, still unafraid to say I do not know and still sure that God is good.</p>
-
-<p>What ruins this is a single early lie, delivered with confidence, that later gets exposed. The bathtub lie I told my son, not for a very long time, buddy, was a small one, and I have since gone back and told him a truer version, that I do not control when anyone dies, that I hope for a long life for him and cannot promise it, and that whatever happens, God does not lose the people he loves. He took it better than the lie deserved. Children usually do. They are stronger than our fear of their fear.</p>
-
-<h2>What the Resurrection Actually Offers a Child</h2>
-
-<p>At the end, after all the honesty about grief and uncertainty, there is a thing we get to say to our children that no other worldview gets to say. Not that death is not sad. It is. Not that we know all the details of what comes after. We do not. But that death is not the last word. That the tomb was empty. That the God who wept at the grave of his friend is the same God who called him out of it. That the story does not end in the dark, even when the psalm does, even when the funeral does, even when the bathwater conversation does.</p>
-
-<p>We do not give children this by skipping the grief. We give it to them by walking through the grief with them, all the way down, and then, from the bottom, pointing east. The hope is not a shortcut around the sorrow. It is a light that only makes sense once you have been in the dark long enough to need it. Our job is not to keep our children out of the dark. It is to be in there with them, holding their hand, telling the truth, and refusing to let go until the light comes up. It will come up. That is the one thing I am willing to promise them. It is the only thing I know for certain, and it is enough.</p>`,
