-<p>It felt like a lie because it was one. Not the content. The content was true. The lie was in the theater, the performance of family worship that everyone was enduring and no one was receiving, the daily ritual whose real function had become allowing me to feel like a spiritual leader while my children learned to associate the Bible with boredom and dad's mild coercion. We were not worshiping. We were completing an assignment I had given us, and grading myself on whether we completed it.</p>
