-<blockquote>The most spiritually formative thing in my house was never the devotional. It was the car. It was the dinner table when something hard happened. It was the moment a son asked a real question and I did not reach for the book, I just answered him, honestly, as his father.</blockquote>
-
-<p>This does not mean formal family worship is worthless. Rhythm and ritual have real power, and there is genuine value in the family that gathers to read Scripture and pray together, and I am not telling anyone to abandon it. I am telling you that if the formal thing has become a joyless performance that no one receives, the fix is probably not to double down on the performance. The fix is to make it shorter, simpler, more honest, and less frequent if necessary, while pouring your real energy into the all-day, informal saturation that the command actually describes. A five-minute reading and a genuine prayer beats a thirty-minute grind that everyone hates. And a spontaneous conversation about God in the car beats both.</p>
