-
-<p>That is what children need, and it is the one thing the workbook cannot provide. They do not need a father who has it all together dispensing tidy spiritual lessons. They need a father who is honestly following God, out loud, in front of them, stumbling included. The moment I let my devotions become honest instead of impressive, my sons started actually engaging, because now there was something real happening rather than a ritual being executed. Honesty is more formative than curriculum. It always was.</p>
-
-<p>I remember the specific night this shifted. We had reached a hard passage, one of the ones about God's judgment that does not fit the tidy picture, and instead of smoothing it over with the answer from the study guide, I said, honestly, this one troubles me, I have never fully made peace with it, and here is what I hold onto anyway. And for the first time in years, a son leaned in. He had a question, a real one, not a performance of interest but genuine engagement, because for the first time the thing in the room was not a father performing certainty but a father actually thinking, in front of him, about a hard thing. That was worth more than every polished devotion I had ground out before it.</p>
