-<h2>Let It Be Small</h2>
-
-<p>Here is permission that I wish someone had given me twenty years ago. Let it be small. A short Scripture and a real prayer at dinner is a family devotion. Talking about God in the car on the way to school is a family devotion. Praying together, briefly, when someone is scared or hurting is a family devotion. Pointing to God in a moment of beauty or grief as it happens is exactly what Deuteronomy commanded, and it counts, and it may count for more than the elaborate program you feel guilty for not sustaining.</p>
-
-<p>The families whose children keep the faith are almost never the ones with the most impressive devotional systems. They are the ones where God was normal, discussed, present, real, woven through the ordinary hours in a way that made faith feel like the water the family swam in rather than a subject the family studied. You cannot manufacture that with a workbook. You grow it by living, out loud, as a person who actually knows God, in front of children who are always watching how you talk about him when you are not trying to teach them anything.</p>
-
-<p>I want to guard this against a misreading, because the loosening I am describing can easily become an excuse for nothing. Letting go of the joyless formal program is not the same as letting go of God in the home. The temptation, once you drop the guilt, is to drift into a house where God is not discussed formally and also not discussed informally, where the loosening became simply an absence, and the children grew up with a father who was relieved of his devotional duty and never replaced it with anything at all. That is not the fix. The fix is not less God in the home. It is more God in the home, distributed differently, poured out of the artificial container and into the whole vessel of the ordinary day. If dropping the formal devotional means God comes up more, in the car and at the table and in the hard moments, then you have done the right thing. If it means God comes up less, or not at all, you have simply used my permission as cover for neglect, and your children will inherit not a living faith woven through the hours but a household where God was quietly retired. Loosen the form. Do not lose the God.</p>
