-export const PARENTING_ARTICLES: ParentingArticle[] = [
-  {
-    title: "Faith Formation by Age: What Your Child Can Handle",
-    slug: "faith-formation-by-age",
-    excerpt:
-      "We hand children answers before they have questions, and we call it discipleship. What if formation is less about what we deposit and more about what they can carry at each stage?",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "13 min",
-    body: `<p>I have five sons. That fact changes how I read every parenting book ever written, including the ones I have preached from. Because a book can describe a child. It cannot describe five of them at once, at different ages, with different fears, at the same kitchen table on the same Tuesday night. What worked on the oldest failed on the third. What settled the third undid the fourth. Anyone who tells you they have a system has not raised enough children to be humbled yet.</p>
-
-<p>The question I want to sit with is not whether to form faith in a child. Of course we do. The question is what a child can actually carry at each age, and whether we have been loading weight onto shoulders that were built for something lighter. Because I did that. I loaded my oldest with certainty when he needed presence. I handed him answers to questions he had not yet asked, and I mistook his silence for agreement. I have spent a good deal of the years since trying to understand what I was actually doing in those early seasons, and most of what I now believe about formation is what I learned by getting it wrong.</p>
-
-<h2>The lie of the miniature adult</h2>
-
-<p>Much of what passes for children's ministry treats a child as a small adult with a smaller vocabulary. Give them the same doctrine in simpler words, the reasoning goes, and you have discipled them. But a four-year-old does not think like an adult who lacks information. A four-year-old thinks like a four-year-old. Jean Piaget spent his career documenting this, and whatever you make of his stages, he was right about the central thing: a child's mind is not an empty version of yours. It is a different kind of mind, doing different kinds of work.</p>
-
-<p>When we forget this, we do damage. We teach a six-year-old that God sees everything, and because a six-year-old cannot yet hold the paradox of a God who both sees and forgives, the child hears surveillance. We teach that God is a father, and the child whose father left, or drank, or raged, receives a wound dressed as good news. I know this one from the inside. I was raised without a father. When someone first told me God was a father, it did not comfort me. It named an absence. It would be years before anyone helped me understand that the metaphor was aimed precisely at the hole in my life, and not at some intact family I was supposed to already have.</p>
-
-<blockquote>We are not depositing content into a container. We are handing weight to a person who can only carry so much, and the wrong weight at the wrong age does not build strength. It bends the frame.</blockquote>
-
-<p>The instinct to treat children as miniature adults comes from a good place. We want them to have the truth, all of it, as soon as possible. But truth handed to a mind that cannot yet hold it does not become faith. It becomes confusion, or fear, or the particular kind of resentment that grows in a child who has been asked to carry what they were not built to carry. The developmental reality is not an obstacle to formation. It is the very ground on which formation has to happen, and to ignore it is to build on sand.</p>
-
-<h2>The early years: presence before proposition</h2>
-
-<p>Before a child can reason about God, the child feels the world. The theologian James Loder wrote about the way the earliest experiences of being held, fed, and returned to form the deep grammar of trust that all later faith either rests upon or fights against. This is not soft sentiment. It is the architecture of a soul. A toddler who learns that the world is safe enough, that crying summons comfort, that the adult who leaves the room comes back, is being formed for faith long before a single Bible verse is memorized.</p>
-
-<p>This means that in the earliest years, the most theological thing you do is show up. Not teach. Show up. The consistency of your presence teaches a child what it means to be kept. And I confess that in the season when three of my sons were under six, I was so busy building the apparatus of a Christian home, the devotions and the routines and the memory verses, that I sometimes missed the simpler and harder work of being reliably, calmly present. My children did not need my curriculum. They needed my face.</p>
-
-<p>The great secular scholar of moral development, Robert Bellah, argued that all of the deepest human commitments are formed less by argument than by belonging, by being drawn into a community whose practices become the child's before the child can evaluate them. A young child does not decide to belong. They simply do, or they do not, and that primal sense of belonging or its absence becomes the emotional grammar of everything they will later believe. What we do in the years before a child can reason is not preliminary to formation. It is the foundation of it, and everything we build afterward stands or falls on how well we laid it.</p>
-
-<h2>The elementary mind: the age of the concrete</h2>
-
-<p>Somewhere around six or seven, a child becomes a marvelous literalist. This is the age of rules, of fairness, of the sharp line between what is allowed and what is not. It is also the age when stories land with tremendous force. Tell an eight-year-old the account of the crossing of the sea, or the boy with five loaves, and the story enters not as abstraction but as event. It happened. Someone was hungry and then was fed.</p>
-
-<p>The mistake we make at this stage is rushing to the moral. We tell the story and then, unable to trust the story to do its work, we tack on the lesson, the application, the tidy point. Eugene Peterson warned about this instinct all his life. The story is not a delivery mechanism for a principle. The story is the thing itself. A child who lives inside the biblical narratives, who knows them the way they know the layout of their own house, has been given something sturdier than a list of principles. They have been given a world to live in.</p>
-
-<p>At this age, questions begin. Simple ones, then sharper ones. Where do people go when they die. Why did God let the dog get hit by the car. Who made God. The temptation is to answer completely, to close the question with authority. But a question closed too fast is a question the child learns not to bring you. What an elementary child needs is not the full answer. They need to learn that you are a person who does not flinch when the hard thing is named. The content of your reply matters less than what your face does when the question arrives. If your face says the question is dangerous, the child files that away, and files away the next question too, until the flow of their real inner life goes somewhere you cannot follow.</p>
-
-<h2>Adolescence: from inherited to owned</h2>
-
-<p>The move from childhood faith to adolescent faith is the move from a faith that was given to a faith that must be chosen, and there is no way to make this transition painless. The developmental psychologist James Fowler described a stage where a young person begins to see their own tradition from the outside, to notice that other people believe other things with the same conviction their parents believe theirs. This is not the beginning of unbelief. It is the beginning of belief that could actually be theirs.</p>
-
-<p>But it feels like loss to a parent. The child who prayed sweetly at seven now shrugs at fourteen. The one who sang in the front row now sits in the back with earbuds in. And here is where fear takes over, and fear is a poor teacher. Fear tightens the grip. Fear demands the performance of a faith that has gone underground to do its real work. I have watched parents, and I have been the parent, who could not tell the difference between a child losing their faith and a child taking possession of it. Those two things look almost identical from the outside. Only time tells them apart.</p>
-
-<blockquote>The doubt of a fifteen-year-old is not the enemy of faith. It is often faith trying to become load-bearing. Do not stand between your child and the necessary difficulty of owning what they believe.</blockquote>
-
-<p>Charles Taylor described the modern condition as one in which belief is no longer the default, in which every faith is held against the awareness that it could be otherwise. Our children are born into that condition. They cannot inherit an unexamined faith even if we wanted them to, because the very air they breathe is full of alternatives. This is not a catastrophe. It means the faith they end up with, if they end up with one, will have been chosen against real options, and a faith chosen against real options is a faith that can actually hold weight. The task of the adolescent years is not to prevent the questioning. It is to keep the relationship intact while the questioning does its work, so that the young person doubts within reach of your love rather than beyond it.</p>
-
-<h2>What each age cannot carry</h2>
-
-<p>If formation is about matching weight to shoulders, then it helps to name what each age cannot yet hold. A young child cannot hold the problem of evil. Do not hand it to them prematurely by dwelling on hell, on judgment, on the darkness of the world. They will meet the darkness soon enough, and when they do, you want their earliest formation to be a memory of being held, not of being frightened.</p>
-
-<p>An elementary child cannot hold sustained abstraction. Do not bury them in doctrine they cannot picture. Give them story, ritual, and the felt sense of belonging to a people who worship. The abstraction can come later, and it will land better on a foundation of lived practice than on a foundation of premature theory. James K. A. Smith has argued at length that we are formed far more by what we love than by what we think, and that our loves are shaped by practices and liturgies long before they are shaped by ideas. A child who has knelt, sung, shared bread, and belonged to a worshiping people has been formed in a way no curriculum could match.</p>
-
-<p>An adolescent cannot hold your certainty in place of their own searching. Do not answer every question so thoroughly that there is nothing left for them to discover. Leave room. Leave the question slightly open, so they have to walk toward it themselves. The faith they argue their way into will hold when the faith they were handed falls apart. And every age, from the youngest to the oldest, cannot carry the weight of your anxiety about their soul. Anxiety is contagious, and a child raised in the anxious grip of a parent terrified for their salvation absorbs the fear far more reliably than the faith.</p>
-
-<h2>The thing I got wrong</h2>
-
-<p>For years I ran our family devotions like a small seminary. I wanted my sons to have what I did not have, a father who taught them about God, and there is nothing wrong with that desire. But the desire curdled when it became about my performance as a spiritual father rather than about their actual formation. I was measuring whether I was doing it right. I was not asking whether they could carry what I was giving.</p>
-
-<p>One of my sons told me, years later, that he spent his childhood feeling like faith was a test he was failing. That undid me. Because I never once said the word test. I did not have to. My anxiety said it. My correction of his wrong answers said it. The subtle disappointment when he did not respond the way I hoped said it. I had loaded a boy with the weight of my own need to be a good Christian father, and he had carried it, silently, until it nearly crushed the very thing I was trying to build.</p>
-
-<p>This is the danger no one warns you about. It is possible to do everything the manuals recommend and still form a child badly, because the thing being transmitted is not the content of the lessons but the spirit in which they are given. A father anxious about outcomes transmits anxiety. A father performing faithfulness transmits performance. My sons did not need me to succeed at fathering them spiritually. They needed me to stop treating their souls as the measure of my adequacy, and to love them freely enough that faith could feel like a gift rather than an examination.</p>
-
-<h2>Formation as patience</h2>
-
-<p>What I believe now, further down the road than I was, is that faith formation is mostly patience. It is the long refusal to force what can only grow. It is trusting that the God who formed the child in the womb is more invested in that child's faith than I am, and is not in the hurry I am in. It is planting things I will not see bloom, and being at peace with that.</p>
-
-<p>This does not mean passivity. You still teach. You still pray with them, still bring them to the gathered church, still name the name of Jesus in the ordinary hours. But you do it the way a gardener works, not the way an engineer works. You do not manufacture the growth. You make conditions in which growth becomes possible, and then you wait, and the waiting is the hardest and most faithful thing you will do.</p>
-
-<p>My sons are further along now. Some of them walk closely with God. Some of them are in the middle of the wilderness. I do not get to know how it ends. That is the part no book prepares you for, that you invest everything and then you have to release the outcome to a God whose timeline is not yours. I planted. Someone else will water. The growth was never mine to give.</p>
-
-<p>So when you ask what your child can handle, hold the question gently. The answer changes every year, sometimes every month. What they can handle is less than you fear and more than you think, and the only way to find the line is to walk close enough to feel it. Stay close. Load lightly. Trust the long work of a patient God, who has never once been in the hurry we are in, and who loves your child past the reach of your best and worst efforts alike.</p>`,
-  },
-  {
-    title: "Parenting Toddlers: Foundation Before Performance",
-    slug: "parenting-toddlers",
-    excerpt:
-      "The toddler years feel like survival, not discipleship. But what a two-year-old learns about whether the world is safe becomes the ground every later faith stands on.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "11 min",
-    body: `<p>No one warns you that the most theologically significant years of your child's life may be the ones you barely remember because you were too exhausted to think. The toddler years are a blur of spilled milk and interrupted sleep and the particular despair of a tantrum in a grocery store. It does not feel like formation. It feels like survival. And yet what happens in these years lays down the deepest layer of a person, the layer that all later belief will either rest on or wrestle against.</p>
-
-<p>I have watched five toddlers become five different men, and I can tell you that the version of me who parented the first toddler and the version who parented the fifth were not the same man. The first time, I was terrified and controlling. By the fifth, I was tired and, mercifully, a little wiser. What I learned across those years is that toddlers do not need us to perform. They need us to be a foundation. And a foundation does not perform. It holds.</p>
-
-<h2>The theology hidden in a peekaboo game</h2>
-
-<p>When a toddler learns that the parent who leaves the room comes back, something enormous is happening. The child is learning what the psychologists call object permanence, but they are also learning something deeper. They are learning that absence is not abandonment. That the one who goes away returns. That the world can be trusted to give back what it takes.</p>
-
-<p>This is not a small thing dressed up as a big thing. This is the seedbed of faith. Before a child can believe in a God they cannot see, they must first learn that things unseen are still real, still there, still coming back. The peekaboo game, silly as it is, rehearses the very structure of trust that faith will one day require. A child who learns reliability at two has a place to put faith at twelve. A child who learns that the world is unpredictable and adults disappear without warning has to overcome that formation before faith can take root.</p>
-
-<blockquote>You are not just keeping a toddler alive. You are teaching a soul whether the universe can be trusted. Every time you come back, you preach a sermon they will not remember and never forget.</blockquote>
-
-<p>The research on early attachment, going back to the work of John Bowlby and the generations of scholars who followed him, keeps arriving at the same conclusion from a hundred different angles. The child who experiences a caregiver as a secure base, reliably present, reliably responsive, develops a fundamental posture toward existence that is trusting rather than defended. This posture is laid down below language, below memory, in the body itself. And whatever theology that child later encounters will be received by a soul already shaped either to trust or to guard. We are writing on our children before they can read what we write.</p>
-
-<h2>The tyranny of the well-behaved child</h2>
-
-<p>Somewhere along the way, Christian parenting culture absorbed a value that has nothing to do with the gospel: the well-behaved child as evidence of good parenting. The quiet toddler in church. The one who sits still, who does not interrupt, who performs calm. We reward this, and we judge ourselves against it, and in doing so we teach our children the most anti-gospel lesson possible, that love is earned by performance.</p>
-
-<p>I confess I wanted well-behaved sons. In part because I loved them and wanted them to flourish, but in part, if I am honest, because their behavior was a report card on me. When my toddler melted down in the church lobby, I felt exposed, judged, found out as a fraud who could not control his own house. And in that shame, I would correct him not for his sake but for mine. I was managing my reputation through my child's behavior. That is not discipline. That is using a two-year-old to protect my ego.</p>
-
-<p>A toddler is supposed to melt down. A toddler has a nervous system that has not yet learned to regulate itself, and the whole point of these years is that the child borrows your regulation until they can grow their own. When you stay calm in the face of the storm, you are not just surviving the tantrum. You are lending your steadiness to a small person who does not have any yet. That is the work. It is invisible, it is exhausting, and it is holy. The parent who can remain a non-anxious presence in the face of a screaming child is doing something far more formative than the parent who successfully produces silence through fear.</p>
-
-<h2>Grace before they can understand grace</h2>
-
-<p>Here is what moves me about parenting a toddler. You extend grace to a person who cannot yet comprehend it. You forgive before they can ask. You keep loving through the phase where they hit you, bite you, scream that they hate you and want the other parent. And in doing so, you enact the gospel toward a person who has no theology to make sense of it.</p>
-
-<p>They will not remember these specific moments. But the pattern sinks below memory into the body. The child who is met with warmth after wrongdoing learns, in their bones, that wrongdoing does not end love. Long before they can define grace, they will have lived inside it. And when someone later tells them that God is like this, that God's love is not withdrawn by their failure, some deep part of them will recognize it, because it will feel like home.</p>
-
-<p>This is why the toddler years, exhausting as they are, matter so much for what a child will one day be able to believe. Grace is difficult to accept for anyone raised to believe love is conditional. If a child's earliest and deepest experience of being loved was an experience of love that had to be earned through good behavior, then the gospel of unearned grace will run against the grain of everything they know, and they will spend years fighting to believe what should be good news. But a child who lived inside unconditional love from the beginning has a place to receive the gospel, because the gospel will sound like the truth they already lived.</p>
-
-<h2>Foundation is not the same as leniency</h2>
-
-<p>Let me be careful here, because grace is easily counterfeited as permissiveness. A foundation holds, and holding requires boundaries. The toddler who is allowed to do anything is not being loved. They are being abandoned to their own chaos, and chaos frightens a small child even when they are the ones creating it. Boundaries are not the opposite of grace. Boundaries are a form of it. The fence around the yard is what makes the yard safe to play in.</p>
-
-<p>So you say no. You remove them from the danger. You hold the line on the things that matter. But you hold it without contempt, without shame, without the withdrawal of your affection. The no and the love arrive together. This is the hardest thing to do when you are exhausted, because exhaustion strips away our patience and leaves the raw material of our anger. I lost my temper with my toddlers more times than I want to count. The recovery, the coming back, the getting down on their level and saying I was wrong to yell, that was the real formation. Not my failure. My return after the failure.</p>
-
-<blockquote>A parent who never fails their toddler teaches nothing about grace. A parent who fails and then repairs teaches everything. The repair is the sermon.</blockquote>
-
-<p>What a small child needs is not a perfect parent, which does not exist, but a parent who ruptures and repairs, who fails and returns, who loses patience and comes back with tenderness. The relational scientists call this rupture and repair, and they have found that it is not the absence of rupture that builds security but the reliable presence of repair. Every time you come back after failing your toddler, you teach them that relationships survive failure, that love is durable, that a bond can be broken and mended. That is a lesson they will draw on for the rest of their lives, in every relationship they ever have, including their relationship with God.</p>
-
-<h2>The exhaustion is not a distraction from the work</h2>
-
-<p>For a long time I treated the fatigue of the toddler years as an obstacle to spiritual life. I could not pray the way I used to. I could not read the way I used to. My devotional life, once orderly, was in ruins. I felt like I had lost God in the noise. What I did not understand then, and understand now, is that the noise was the field of formation, mine as much as theirs.</p>
-
-<p>The self-emptying required to parent a toddler is a school of the soul. You are woken at three in the morning by someone who cannot say thank you and will not for years. You give and give and receive tantrums in return. This is not a distraction from the spiritual life. This is the spiritual life, taught to you by a person in diapers. God was not absent in those years. God was in the getting up at three in the morning, teaching me a love that does not keep score, that does not require gratitude to keep giving. I was being discipled by my own children, though I would not have described it that way at the time, because at the time I mostly just wanted to sleep.</p>
-
-<h2>What I would tell a younger father</h2>
-
-<p>If I could reach the version of myself who was drowning in the toddler years, I would tell him a few things. I would tell him to stop measuring. Stop checking whether he was doing it right. The measuring was making him anxious, and his anxiety was contagious, and his toddlers were catching it. A calm parent is worth more than a correct one.</p>
-
-<p>I would tell him that the goal of these years is not obedience. It is attachment. A securely attached child will grow into obedience because they trust the one who leads them. A child driven to obedience without attachment learns compliance and stores up resentment. Build the bond first. The behavior follows the bond, not the other way around.</p>
-
-<p>I would tell him that the sleepless nights are not lost time. They are deposits into an account he cannot see, and one day, when his son is grown and calls him just to talk, he will understand that the man on the other end of the line was formed, in part, in the dark hours when nobody was watching and no one said thank you.</p>
-
-<p>And I would tell him one more thing, the thing I most needed to hear and did not have anyone to say it. I would tell him that he is allowed to find it hard. That the exhaustion and the frustration and the occasional flash of resentment do not make him a bad father or a bad Christian. The sanitized picture of parenting that circulates in Christian circles, all gratitude and wonder and precious moments, is a lie that isolates the parent who is actually drowning. The toddler years are genuinely hard, and the honest father who admits it is closer to grace than the pretending father who performs a joy he does not feel. God is not disappointed in the tired parent. God is near to them, in the getting up again, in the choosing to love when the feeling of love has been ground down by fatigue. That choosing, when the feeling is gone, is the deepest love of all, and it is the love that most resembles the love of God, who keeps loving us through every night we give him nothing back.</p>
-
-<h2>The performance we owe no one</h2>
-
-<p>There is a particular exhaustion that comes not from the toddler but from the audience, real or imagined, that we believe is watching us parent. The mother in the grocery store who feels the eyes of strangers on her screaming child. The father at church who reads judgment in every glance. We parent, too often, for an audience, and the audience is merciless, and the audience does not love our children or us. The performance we put on for the audience drains the very energy we need for the actual work, which has no audience at all, which happens in the dark and the ordinary and the unwitnessed hours.</p>
-
-<p>I have come to believe that the freedom to stop performing is one of the great graces of the gospel applied to parenting. We are not on trial. Our children's behavior is not a verdict on our worth. God does not grade us on the compliance of our toddlers, and neither should we grade ourselves. The parent who is freed from the performance can be present to the child in a way the performing parent never can, because the performing parent is always half-turned toward the audience, and the child feels that turning, and knows they do not have the parent's whole face. Put the audience out of your mind. There is only you, and this small person, and the God who sees what no audience sees.</p>
-
-<p>The toddler years feel like they will never end, and then, abruptly, they are over, and you would give a great deal to have one more ordinary morning of it back. Build the foundation while you can. Not the performance. The foundation. The quiet, unmemorable, world-shaping work of being the reliable love a small person learns to trust. Everything later is built on it, and nothing later can be built on anything else.</p>`,
