-export const ARTICLES_BATCH_1 = [
-  {
-    title: "How Christianity Became an Empire",
-    slug: "how-christianity-became-an-empire",
-    excerpt:
-      "The story of how a persecuted Jewish sect became the official religion of the Roman Empire — and what was gained and lost in the transformation.",
-    pillar: "Theological Depth",
-    readTime: "14 min",
-    body: `<p>Before Constantine, there were no cathedrals. There were no bishops in imperial courts, no Christian holidays on the civic calendar, no crosses atop government buildings. The movement that would become the world's largest religion met in private homes, worshipped in catacombs, and buried its dead in unmarked graves. Its members were disproportionately poor, disproportionately female, and disproportionately enslaved. And they were, by any political measure, utterly powerless.</p>
-
-<p>Understanding how that movement became the official religion of the most powerful empire in the ancient world is not merely a matter of historical curiosity. It is the hinge on which the entire subsequent history of Western civilization turns. Every debate about church and state, every argument about Christian nationalism, every question about whether the faith is fundamentally a religion of power or a religion of the margins — all of it traces back to the fourth century, to a Roman emperor who saw a vision in the sky and changed the world.</p>
-
-<h2>The Pre-Constantinian Church</h2>
-
-<p>The Christianity of the first three centuries looked almost nothing like what most modern Christians would recognize. There were no church buildings until roughly the mid-third century, and even then they were converted houses, not purpose-built structures. The earliest archaeological evidence of a Christian meeting space — the house church at Dura-Europos in modern Syria, dating to around 240 AD — reveals a modest domestic dwelling with one wall knocked out to create a larger room, a small baptistery with frescoes, and nothing that would distinguish it from the outside as a place of worship.</p>
-
-<p>The social composition of these communities has been debated by historians for generations. Adolf von Harnack argued in the early twentieth century that Christianity spread primarily among the urban lower-middle classes. More recently, Rodney Stark has contended that the movement attracted a surprising number of people of means, including women of status who found in the church a degree of agency unavailable to them in Roman civic life. Wayne Meeks, in his landmark <em>The First Urban Christians</em>, painted a picture of Pauline communities that cut across class lines — a radical social experiment in a rigidly stratified society.</p>
-
-<p>What is beyond dispute is that these communities existed under a persistent, if intermittent, threat of persecution. The popular image of three centuries of unrelenting Roman oppression is overstated — persecution was sporadic, localized, and often driven by local magistrates rather than imperial policy. But it was real. The Decian persecution of 250-251 AD demanded that all citizens sacrifice to the Roman gods and obtain a certificate (<em>libellus</em>) proving they had done so. The Great Persecution under Diocletian, beginning in 303 AD, was the most systematic: churches were destroyed, scriptures confiscated, clergy arrested, and ordinary Christians given the choice between sacrifice and death.</p>
-
-<p>This context matters because it shaped the church's self-understanding. To be a Christian in the year 300 was to belong to an illegal religion. Baptism was a genuine risk. The church's theology, its worship, its ethics — all were forged in the knowledge that membership could cost you everything. The church fathers spoke of the blood of the martyrs as the seed of the church. Tertullian's famous formulation was not metaphor; it was observation.</p>
-
-<h2>The Edict of Milan: What It Actually Said</h2>
-
