-<p><em>Sola Scriptura</em> — Scripture alone. The Bible, not the pope or church tradition, is the ultimate authority for Christian faith and practice. This did not mean, as later Protestants sometimes assumed, that tradition was worthless or that individuals could interpret scripture however they pleased. Luther himself relied heavily on the church fathers and was horrified by the radical individualism of some who claimed to follow his principles. But the assertion that scripture stood above all human authorities — including popes and councils — was a fundamental challenge to the Catholic understanding of authority as a three-legged stool of scripture, tradition, and magisterium.</p>
-
-<p><em>Sola Fide</em> — Faith alone. Justification — being made right with God — comes through faith, not through human works or merit. This was, for Luther, not merely one doctrine among many but "the article on which the church stands or falls." His reading of Paul's letter to the Romans, particularly Romans 1:17 ("The righteous shall live by faith"), was the theological earthquake that shook his entire understanding of the Christian life. The medieval system of earning merit through good works, pilgrimages, masses, and indulgences was, in Luther's view, not merely abused but fundamentally misconceived. Humans could not earn God's favor; they could only receive it.</p>
-
-<p><em>Sola Gratia</em> — Grace alone. Salvation is entirely a gift of God, not a human achievement. This was the corollary of <em>sola fide</em>: if justification comes through faith alone, it is because faith itself is a gift of divine grace. The human will, corrupted by sin, cannot turn toward God without God's prior, unmerited initiative.</p>
-
-<p><em>Solus Christus</em> — Christ alone. Jesus Christ is the sole mediator between God and humanity. The medieval practice of invoking the saints as intercessors was, in the reformers' view, a diminishment of Christ's unique role. There was no need for a human mediator between the believer and the divine mediator.</p>
-
-<p><em>Soli Deo Gloria</em> — To God alone be the glory. All glory in salvation belongs to God, not to human effort, institutional authority, or ecclesiastical hierarchy. This was the doxological summary of the other four solas: if salvation is by grace alone, through faith alone, in Christ alone, according to scripture alone, then all praise belongs to God alone.</p>
-
-<h2>Calvin and Geneva: The Reformed Experiment</h2>
-
-<p>If Luther was the Reformation's prophet, John Calvin was its architect. A French lawyer turned theologian, Calvin settled in Geneva in 1536 and, over the next twenty-eight years (apart from a three-year exile in Strasbourg), built a model Reformed community that would influence Protestantism far more profoundly than Wittenberg ever did.</p>
-
-<p>Calvin's theology was, in many respects, more systematic and more radical than Luther's. His <em>Institutes of the Christian Religion</em>, first published in 1536 and expanded in subsequent editions until 1559, was the first comprehensive Protestant systematic theology. Its central theme was the sovereignty of God — a sovereignty so absolute that it encompassed not only creation and redemption but the eternal destiny of every human soul. Calvin's doctrine of double predestination — that God had, before the foundation of the world, elected some to salvation and others to damnation — was not original to him (Augustine had taught something similar), but Calvin stated it with an unflinching clarity that shocked many of his contemporaries and has provoked debate ever since.</p>
