-
-<p>The English Reformation was, in its origins, the least theological of all the Reformation movements. Henry VIII did not break with Rome because he had read Paul's letter to the Romans and found the Catholic understanding of justification wanting. He broke with Rome because the pope would not annul his marriage to Catherine of Aragon so that he could marry Anne Boleyn. The Act of Supremacy in 1534, which declared the king to be the "Supreme Head of the Church of England," was a jurisdictional assertion, not a theological one. Henry remained, in his own mind, a Catholic in everything except his allegiance to the pope. He continued to affirm transubstantiation, clerical celibacy, and the other tenets of Catholic faith. He executed Protestants for heresy and Catholics for treason, sometimes on the same day.</p>
-
-<p>The theological content of the English Reformation would come later, under Edward VI and Elizabeth I, and it would be characteristically English in its moderation: a "middle way" (<em>via media</em>) between Rome and Geneva that retained Catholic liturgical and institutional structures while incorporating Protestant theology. The result — Anglicanism — was neither fully Catholic nor fully Protestant, a compromise that has been variously praised as a model of theological generosity and criticized as a failure of theological nerve.</p>
