-
-<p>The Catholic Church's response to the Reformation was not merely reactive. The Council of Trent (1545-1563), convened by Pope Paul III, was simultaneously a response to Protestant challenges and an internal reform effort that addressed many of the abuses the reformers had identified. The council condemned the sale of indulgences for profit, reformed clerical education, standardized the liturgy, and reaffirmed — with greater precision — the doctrines the Protestants had challenged.</p>
-
