-<p>The Second Great Awakening, which unfolded in the early decades of the nineteenth century, completed the transformation that the First had begun. If the First Great Awakening democratized religious experience, the Second Great Awakening democratized theology itself — stripping away the Calvinist framework that had governed American Protestantism and replacing it with an Arminian emphasis on human choice and moral effort.</p>
-
-<p>The key figure was Charles Grandison Finney, a lawyer-turned-evangelist who developed the "new measures" that would become the template for American revivalism. Finney rejected the Calvinist doctrine of election. Conversion, he insisted, was not a mysterious work of divine grace but a predictable result of the right methods. "A revival is not a miracle," Finney declared. "It is a purely philosophical result of the right use of the constituted means." His innovations — the "anxious bench" (a front-row seat where seekers were subjected to intense spiritual pressure), the protracted meeting (multi-day revivals designed to wear down resistance), and the direct appeal for an immediate decision — transformed revival from an unpredictable movement of the Spirit into a technology of persuasion.</p>
-
-<p>Finney's theology was, by historic Christian standards, a revolution. He rejected original sin, taught that humans had the natural ability to turn to God, and defined sin as a voluntary act of selfishness rather than an inherited condition. His emphasis on human agency, individual decision, and the efficacy of technique reflected the broader American culture of the early republic: optimistic, pragmatic, suspicious of inherited authority, and convinced that the right methods could produce the right results.</p>
-
-<p>The Second Great Awakening also produced the great reform movements of the antebellum period: abolitionism, temperance, women's suffrage, and prison reform. The theological conviction that individuals could choose to be converted led naturally to the social conviction that individuals could choose to reform society. The link between evangelicalism and social reform that characterized this period would later dissolve — a dissolution with catastrophic consequences — but in the early nineteenth century, personal piety and social justice were inseparable.</p>
-
-<h2>The Civil War and the Fracturing of the Denominations</h2>
-
-<p>The Civil War was the great crisis of American Christianity, and it revealed the moral failure of a religion that had accommodated itself to the culture rather than challenging it. Every major Protestant denomination split along sectional lines: the Methodists in 1844, the Baptists in 1845, the Presbyterians in 1861. In each case, the issue was slavery, and in each case, both sides marshaled biblical arguments for their position.</p>
-
-<p>Southern Christians argued that the Bible explicitly sanctioned slavery: Abraham owned slaves, Paul returned Onesimus to Philemon, and the Mosaic law regulated the institution without condemning it. Northern abolitionists argued that the Bible's overarching trajectory — from creation in God's image, through the exodus from bondage, to Paul's declaration that in Christ there is "neither slave nor free" — pointed toward liberation, even if specific texts did not explicitly command it.</p>
-
-<p>The debate exposed a fundamental problem with the Protestant principle of <em>sola scriptura</em>. If the Bible alone was the authority, and both sides could cite the Bible for their position, then the Bible alone could not settle the dispute. The historian Mark Noll has called this "the theological crisis of the Civil War" — the moment when American Protestantism's confidence in the perspicuity of scripture collided with the reality that equally sincere, equally learned Christians could read the same text and reach opposite conclusions.</p>
-
-<p>The war's aftermath left a lasting scar on American Christianity. The southern churches, defeated politically and militarily, turned inward — developing a theology of personal piety and individual salvation that was deliberately apolitical (on every issue except race). The connection between evangelicalism and social reform that had characterized the antebellum period was severed, and it would not be restored for over a century.</p>
-
-<h2>The Social Gospel vs. Fundamentalism</h2>
-
-<p>The late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries produced the fault line that still defines American Christianity: the division between liberal and conservative Protestantism, between the Social Gospel and fundamentalism.</p>
-
-<p>The Social Gospel movement, most closely associated with Walter Rauschenbusch, argued that the kingdom of God was not merely a future hope but a present demand — that Christians were called not only to save individual souls but to transform the social structures that perpetuated poverty, exploitation, and injustice. Rauschenbusch, a Baptist pastor who had served in the Hell's Kitchen neighborhood of New York City, wrote in <em>Christianity and the Social Crisis</em> (1907) that "the essential purpose of Christianity was to transform human society into the kingdom of God by regenerating all human relations and reconstituting them in accordance with the will of God."</p>
-
-<p>Fundamentalism arose as a direct reaction to both the Social Gospel and the higher criticism of the Bible that was arriving from German universities. The movement took its name from <em>The Fundamentals</em>, a series of ninety essays published between 1910 and 1915 that defended the "fundamental" doctrines of Protestant orthodoxy: the inerrancy of scripture, the virgin birth, the substitutionary atonement, the bodily resurrection, and the literal second coming of Christ. The fundamentalists were not, in their early years, the anti-intellectual caricatures they would later become. Contributors to <em>The Fundamentals</em> included respected scholars like B.B. Warfield of Princeton Theological Seminary.</p>
-
-<p>But the movement hardened as the century progressed, and the Scopes Trial of 1925 — in which a Tennessee teacher was prosecuted for teaching evolution — became the symbolic turning point. William Jennings Bryan, the fundamentalist champion, won the legal case but lost the cultural war. H.L. Mencken's merciless coverage portrayed fundamentalists as ignorant rubes, and the movement retreated from public life for a generation — building its own network of Bible colleges, radio stations, and parachurch organizations that would later form the infrastructure of the Religious Right.</p>
-
-<h2>Billy Graham and the Neo-Evangelical Project</h2>
-
-<p>The figure who brought conservative Protestantism back into the mainstream was Billy Graham, whose career spans the second half of the twentieth century like a colossus. Graham was not a fundamentalist, though he emerged from the fundamentalist world. He was a "neo-evangelical" — a term coined by Harold Ockenga in 1947 to describe a movement that shared the fundamentalists' theological convictions but rejected their separatism, anti-intellectualism, and cultural disengagement.</p>
-
-<p>Graham's 1949 Los Angeles crusade, boosted by the patronage of William Randolph Hearst (who reportedly instructed his editors to "puff Graham"), launched a career that would make him the most famous Protestant in the world. His preaching was simple, direct, and focused on a single message: you must make a decision for Christ. His crusades were meticulously organized, his organization was financially transparent, and his personal integrity was, by all accounts, genuine. He was the anti-Elmer Gantry — a traveling evangelist who could not be dismissed as a fraud.</p>
-
-<p>Graham's significance, however, extended beyond his evangelistic work. He was a confidant of presidents from Eisenhower to Obama, and his access to power represented a new model for evangelical engagement with American politics. Graham was not a political organizer; he was a spiritual advisor. But his presence in the corridors of power — his prayers at inaugurations, his phone calls with presidents — established the expectation that evangelical Christianity and American political power belonged in the same room.</p>
-
-<p>This expectation would have enormous consequences when the next generation of evangelical leaders proved far more willing to exercise political power directly.</p>
-
-<h2>The Moral Majority and the Political Turn</h2>
-
-<p>The politicization of American evangelicalism in the late 1970s and 1980s was one of the most consequential developments in modern American history. The conventional narrative attributes it to Roe v. Wade (1973), but the historical record, as the historian Randall Balmer has argued, is more complicated. The initial catalyst for the Religious Right's political mobilization was not abortion but segregation — specifically, the IRS's 1970 policy denying tax exemption to racially discriminatory schools, under which Bob Jones University's exemption was revoked in 1976. It was this issue, not abortion, that initially activated figures like Jerry Falwell and Paul Weyrich. Abortion was adopted as the movement's signature issue later, partly because it was a more morally persuasive rallying point than the defense of segregated schools.</p>
-
-<p>Jerry Falwell's Moral Majority, founded in 1979, was the organizational vehicle for the political mobilization of conservative Protestants. Its agenda was explicitly political: opposition to abortion, opposition to the Equal Rights Amendment, support for prayer in public schools, and a robust Cold War anti-communism. Falwell was not subtle about his goals: "The idea that religion and politics don't mix was invented by the Devil to keep Christians from running their own country," he declared.</p>
-
-<p>The alliance between evangelicalism and the Republican Party, consolidated during the Reagan era, transformed both. The Republican Party gained a reliable, highly motivated voting bloc. Evangelicalism gained political influence but at the cost of identifying the gospel with a particular political platform. The theological claim that Jesus is Lord — a claim with radical implications for every political order — was domesticated into the political claim that Christian values aligned with conservative Republican policies. The prophetic distance that the church is supposed to maintain from all earthly powers was collapsed into partisan loyalty.</p>
-
-<h2>The Prosperity Gospel</h2>
-
-<p>No development in American Christianity is more revealing of the tradition's accommodation to American culture than the prosperity gospel — the teaching that God wants believers to be financially wealthy, physically healthy, and personally successful, and that faith, positive speech, and financial giving are the means to obtain these blessings.</p>
-
-<p>The movement's roots lie in the post-World War II healing revival, particularly in the ministry of Oral Roberts, who combined Pentecostal healing with a message of divine prosperity. Kenneth Hagin, a Pentecostal pastor in Texas, provided the theological framework through what he called the "Word of Faith" teaching: the spoken word has creative power, and believers who "name it and claim it" can materialize divine blessings through the force of their declarations.</p>
-
-<p>The theology, such as it is, draws on a selective reading of scripture. Deuteronomy 28 promises material blessings for obedience. Malachi 3:10 promises that God will "open the windows of heaven" for those who tithe. 3 John 2, in the King James Version, expresses the wish that the reader may "prosper and be in health." The prosperity teachers treat these texts as unconditional promises, ignoring the vast weight of biblical testimony — from Job to the Psalms to the teachings of Jesus himself — that contradicts the equation of faithfulness with material success.</p>
-
-<p>Joel Osteen, the pastor of Lakewood Church in Houston (the largest congregation in the United States, meeting in a former basketball arena), represents the prosperity gospel in its most polished contemporary form. Osteen's sermons are relentlessly positive, theologically thin, and laser-focused on personal success. God, in Osteen's telling, wants you to be a winner, not a whiner. The cross, the resurrection, sin, repentance, justice — these themes are largely absent from Osteen's preaching, replaced by a therapeutic optimism that owes more to Norman Vincent Peale and the American self-help tradition than to the apostle Paul.</p>
-
-<p>The prosperity gospel is, in the most precise sense, a heresy — a distortion of the Christian message so fundamental that it constitutes a different religion. It inverts the gospel's logic: where Jesus said "Blessed are the poor," the prosperity teachers say "Blessed are the rich." Where Paul boasted of his weaknesses, the prosperity teachers boast of their private jets. Where the early church shared its possessions, the prosperity churches extract wealth from their congregations — often from the poorest and most vulnerable members — and concentrate it in the hands of celebrity pastors. It is the American gospel par excellence: faith as investment strategy, God as cosmic ATM, the church as spiritual marketplace.</p>
-
-<h2>Megachurch Culture and the Consumer Model</h2>
-
-<p>The megachurch — defined as a Protestant congregation with two thousand or more weekly attendees — is a distinctly American phenomenon, and its growth over the past four decades tells us as much about American consumer culture as about American religion. There are roughly 1,750 megachurches in the United States today, and their combined weekly attendance exceeds the population of many major cities.</p>
-
-<p>The megachurch model, pioneered by figures like Robert Schuller (Crystal Cathedral), Bill Hybels (Willow Creek), and Rick Warren (Saddleback), applied the principles of marketing, consumer research, and organizational management to the life of the church. Hybels famously surveyed unchurched people in the suburbs of Chicago, asking them why they did not attend church, and then designed a worship experience that addressed their objections: no guilt, no religious jargon, no pressure to give money, professional-quality music, practical sermons on felt needs, and ample parking. The "seeker-sensitive" model was, in many respects, a work of organizational genius. It drew enormous crowds.</p>
-
-<p>The theological question is whether the crowds were drawn to the gospel or to a consumer experience that happened to be located in a building called a church. The megachurch model assumes that the church must compete with other leisure and entertainment options for people's time and attention. The music must be as good as a concert. The children's programs must rival theme parks. The coffee must be excellent. The parking must be convenient. The implicit theology is that people are consumers and the church is a provider of religious goods and services.</p>
-
-<p>This is a long way from the house churches of the first century, from the desert fathers, from the Anabaptist communities that met in barns and caves under threat of execution. Whether it is a legitimate adaptation of an ancient faith to modern conditions or a fatal capitulation to the forces of consumerism is a question that American Christianity has not yet honestly confronted.</p>
-
-<h2>Where American Christianity Stands Today</h2>
-
-<p>American Christianity in the third decade of the twenty-first century is fractured, politicized, and declining. The share of Americans who identify as Christian has fallen from 90 percent in the 1970s to roughly 64 percent in recent surveys. The "nones" — people who claim no religious affiliation — have grown from 5 percent to nearly 30 percent. Mainline Protestantism has been in freefall for half a century. Evangelicalism, long assumed to be immune to decline, has begun to show the same symptoms.</p>
-
-<p>The causes are multiple and debated. The association of Christianity with right-wing politics has alienated younger Americans, particularly on issues of LGBTQ rights and racial justice. The clergy sex abuse scandals — not only in the Catholic Church but increasingly in Protestant denominations — have shattered the moral authority of institutional religion. The rise of digital culture has created alternative communities and meaning-making systems that compete with traditional religious participation. And the internal contradictions of American Christianity — its accommodation to consumerism, its alliance with political power, its racial segregation (Martin Luther King Jr.'s observation that eleven o'clock on Sunday morning is the most segregated hour in America remains substantially true) — have produced a credibility crisis from which the tradition may not recover.</p>
-
-<blockquote>The deepest problem, however, is theological. American Christianity has, over four centuries, so thoroughly absorbed American values — individualism, pragmatism, optimism, consumerism, nationalism — that it has become difficult to distinguish the faith from the culture. When the faith and the culture are indistinguishable, the faith has nothing distinctive to offer. It becomes, in the words of the theologian H. Richard Niebuhr, "a God without wrath who brought men without sin into a Kingdom without judgment through the ministrations of a Christ without a Cross."</blockquote>
-
-<p>The question facing American Christianity is not whether it will survive — some form of it certainly will. The question is whether it can recover a faith robust enough to challenge the culture that has domesticated it. This would require, at minimum, a willingness to disentangle the gospel from American nationalism, to recover a theology of suffering and sacrifice in a culture of comfort and success, to practice genuine racial reconciliation in the most racially divided institution in American life, and to rediscover the prophetic tradition that speaks truth to power rather than seeking proximity to it.</p>
-
-<p>Whether American Christianity has the theological resources and the institutional will to undertake this recovery is the most important religious question of the twenty-first century. The history surveyed in this article offers both hope and warning. The American tradition has produced genuine theological brilliance (Edwards, the Niebuhrs, King), extraordinary institutional creativity (the voluntary denomination, the parachurch organization, the historically Black church), and a commitment to religious liberty that, at its best, has been a gift to the world. But it has also produced theological superficiality, cultural captivity, racial injustice, and a confusion of faith with national identity that has distorted the gospel beyond recognition.</p>
-
-<p>The path forward is not back — not to the Puritans, not to the revivals, not to the Christian America that never quite existed. The path forward requires a reckoning with the full weight of the tradition: its achievements and its failures, its insights and its blind spots, its prophets and its profiteers. American Christianity became American. The question now is whether it can become Christian again.</p>`,
-  },
