-export const ARTICLES_BATCH_2 = [
-  {
-    title: "The Rise and Fall of Mainline Protestantism",
-    slug: "the-rise-and-fall-of-mainline-protestantism",
-    excerpt:
-      "For most of the twentieth century, mainline Protestantism was American Christianity. Its decline is not merely a story about shrinking pews. It is a story about what happens when a faith tradition succeeds so completely at shaping a culture that it can no longer distinguish itself from it.",
-    pillar: "Theological Depth",
-    readTime: "13 min",
-    body: `<p>If you had attended a suburban church in the United States in 1955, there is a strong chance it would have been a mainline Protestant congregation. The minister would have been educated at a reputable seminary. The hymnal would have been vetted by denominational committees. The congregation would have included bankers, schoolteachers, lawyers, and local politicians. The church building itself would have been architecturally substantial, set prominently on a main street. And the theology preached from the pulpit, while perhaps not systematic, would have carried the quiet confidence of a tradition that believed it spoke for the nation.</p>
-
-<p>That world is gone. Not destroyed by persecution or suppressed by the state, but eroded from within and overtaken from without. The story of mainline Protestantism's rise and fall is one of the most consequential narratives in American religious history, and most Americans have never heard it told clearly.</p>
-
-<h2>What "Mainline" Actually Means</h2>
-
-<p>The term "mainline" refers to a cluster of Protestant denominations that dominated American religious and civic life from the colonial era through the mid-twentieth century. The label itself derives from the "Main Line," a stretch of affluent suburbs along the Pennsylvania Railroad west of Philadelphia, where these churches were as much a part of the social furniture as the country clubs and prep schools. The term was not originally theological. It was sociological. These were the churches of the American establishment.</p>
-
-<p>The so-called "Seven Sisters" of mainline Protestantism are the United Methodist Church, the Presbyterian Church (U.S.A.), the Episcopal Church, the Evangelical Lutheran Church in America, the United Church of Christ, the Christian Church (Disciples of Christ), and the American Baptist Churches USA. Together, these denominations and their predecessors constituted the institutional spine of American Protestantism for more than two centuries.</p>
-
-<p>Their influence extended far beyond Sunday worship. Mainline Protestants founded the majority of America's elite universities: Harvard, Yale, Princeton, Columbia, Dartmouth, and dozens more. They built hospitals, orphanages, and settlement houses. They shaped public school curricula. Their clergy were invited to pray at presidential inaugurations. Their moral assumptions saturated American law, literature, and manners. To be "mainline" was to be the religious expression of the American middle and upper-middle class, with all the privilege and blind spots that entailed.</p>
-
-<h2>The Ecumenical Vision</h2>
-
-<p>One of the mainline's most distinctive contributions was its commitment to ecumenism, the effort to overcome denominational division and pursue Christian unity. The Federal Council of Churches, founded in 1908, brought mainline denominations together for cooperative social witness decades before evangelicals developed comparable institutions. The World Council of Churches, established in Amsterdam in 1948, represented the global extension of this vision, an institutional expression of the conviction that Christian division was a scandal to be overcome rather than a tradition to be defended.</p>
-
-<p>This ecumenical impulse was rooted in a specific theological conviction: that denominational boundaries were historical accidents rather than divine mandates. The prayer of Jesus in John 17, "that they may all be one," was taken not as a pious aspiration but as a programmatic directive. Mainline theologians argued that a divided church could not credibly proclaim a gospel of reconciliation. The fragmentation of Protestantism into thousands of competing denominations was, on this view, a failure of nerve and an obstacle to mission.</p>
-
-<p>The ecumenical movement produced landmark achievements. The Consultation on Church Union (1962) brought ten denominations into formal merger discussions. The Leuenberg Agreement (1973) established full communion among Protestant churches across Europe. The Baptism, Eucharist and Ministry document of 1982 represented decades of patient theological dialogue between traditions that had not spoken to each other for centuries. These were genuine accomplishments, and their significance should not be diminished by the institutional decline that followed.</p>
-
-<h2>The Golden Age: 1940s to 1960s</h2>
-
-<p>Mainline Protestantism reached its zenith in the decades following World War II. Returning veterans married, moved to suburbs, and joined churches in staggering numbers. Between 1945 and 1960, church membership in the United States grew faster than the population itself. The Methodist Church alone counted over ten million members by the mid-1960s, and the United Methodist merger of 1968 began with roughly eleven million. The Presbyterian Church in the U.S.A. peaked at nearly four million. The Episcopal Church, though smaller, wielded influence wildly disproportionate to its size, producing presidents, Supreme Court justices, and Wall Street titans.</p>
-
-<p>This was the era of what sociologist Will Herberg described in his 1955 book <em>Protestant-Catholic-Jew</em>: a tripartite American civil religion in which to be a good American meant belonging to one of these three traditions. And among the three, Protestantism, specifically mainline Protestantism, was first among equals. It set the cultural tone. Its ministers appeared on the cover of <em>Time</em> magazine. Its theologians, Reinhold Niebuhr chief among them, were consulted by presidents and cited in foreign policy debates. Niebuhr's <em>The Irony of American History</em> (1952) remains one of the most penetrating analyses of American power ever written, and it was composed by a man whose primary vocation was preaching.</p>
-
-<p>The National Council of Churches, the institutional expression of mainline Protestant cooperation, functioned as a quasi-official religious voice of the nation. Its pronouncements on race, poverty, and international affairs carried weight in Washington. Its member denominations ran some of the largest charitable and educational operations in the country. The mainline was not merely a religious phenomenon. It was a civilizational one.</p>
-
-<h2>Theological Liberalism: What It Actually Is</h2>
-
-<p>To understand the mainline, one must understand liberal theology, a term almost universally misused in popular discourse. Theological liberalism is not simply "being progressive on social issues." It is a specific intellectual tradition with identifiable founders, methods, and commitments.</p>
-
-<p>The tradition begins with Friedrich Schleiermacher (1768-1834), often called the father of liberal theology. Writing in the wake of the Enlightenment, Schleiermacher argued that religion should not be reduced to doctrine or morality but understood as rooted in a "feeling of absolute dependence," a pre-cognitive experience of the infinite. This move was revolutionary. It grounded religion not in propositions about God but in human experience of the divine. Critics, then and now, argue that this effectively made human subjectivity the measure of theology.</p>
-
-<p>Paul Tillich (1886-1965) pushed this further, defining God not as a being among beings but as "the ground of being," the depth dimension of all reality. Tillich's method of correlation sought to connect the questions arising from human existence with the answers provided by Christian symbols. The cross, for example, was not merely a historical event but a symbol of the courage to accept meaninglessness. This was extraordinarily sophisticated philosophy. It was also, for many laypeople, almost entirely unintelligible.</p>
-
-<p>Rudolf Bultmann (1884-1976) applied existentialist categories to the New Testament, arguing that the mythological framework of the Gospels, demons, miracles, a three-tiered universe, needed to be "demythologized" to reveal the existential truth beneath. The resurrection, in Bultmann's reading, was not a historical event but an expression of the disciples' experience of new life through the proclaimed word. These ideas filtered through seminary education into mainline pulpits, sometimes carefully, sometimes crudely. A generation of ministers was trained to think critically about Scripture but often lacked the pastoral skill to communicate what remained after criticism had done its work.</p>
-
-<h2>The Social Justice Commitment</h2>
-
-<p>Whatever its theological complexities, the mainline produced an extraordinary legacy of social engagement. The Social Gospel movement of the early twentieth century, led by figures such as Walter Rauschenbusch, argued that the Kingdom of God was not merely an otherworldly hope but a mandate for transforming unjust social structures. Rauschenbusch's <em>Christianity and the Social Crisis</em> (1907) remains a landmark in the integration of theology and social ethics.</p>
-
-<p>This impulse bore fruit across the century. Mainline churches were among the earliest institutional supporters of the civil rights movement. The National Council of Churches organized clergy to join the 1965 Selma to Montgomery marches. An Episcopal seminarian, Jonathan Daniels, was among the civil rights workers murdered in the South. Mainline denominations issued early statements against the Vietnam War, often at significant cost to their internal unity. They ordained women decades before evangelicalism would even consider the question: the Methodist Church in 1956, the Presbyterian Church in 1956, the Lutheran Church in America in 1970.</p>
-
-<p>The mainline's commitment to racial justice, economic equality, and peace was not an aberration or a capitulation to secular liberalism. It flowed from a genuine, if debatable, reading of the prophetic tradition. Amos, Isaiah, and Micah demanded justice. Jesus proclaimed good news to the poor. The question was never whether the church should care about justice. The question was whether justice commitments had become a substitute for everything else the church was supposed to be.</p>
-
-<h2>The Demographic Collapse</h2>
-
-<p>The numbers tell a story that is difficult to dispute. At midcentury, mainline Protestant denominations claimed, by some estimates, between a third and a half of the American population. By 2020, the figure had fallen to roughly one in ten. The United Methodist Church lost nearly four million members between 1970 and 2020, even before the schism over sexuality that would accelerate the decline. The Presbyterian Church (U.S.A.) fell from 4.2 million members in 1965 to under 1.2 million by 2023. The Episcopal Church dropped from 3.6 million to approximately 1.6 million. The United Church of Christ fell from 2 million to under 800,000. The Disciples of Christ went from nearly 1.9 million to around 300,000.</p>
-
-<p>These are not marginal declines. They represent a demographic catastrophe. Entire conferences and synods have closed. Historic churches with century-old endowments have shuttered their doors. Seminary enrollments have plummeted. The average age of a mainline Protestant in the United States is now well above sixty. In some denominations, the median age of clergy exceeds the median age of the congregation, a statistical impossibility in a healthy institution.</p>
-
-<p>Multiple factors converged. Birth rates among mainline Protestants dropped sharply after the 1960s, tracking closely with education and income levels. The baby boom generation, raised in these churches, left in enormous numbers during the cultural upheavals of the late 1960s and 1970s and never returned. Their children were often not baptized, not catechized, and not formed in any tradition at all. Meanwhile, evangelical and Pentecostal churches, with their higher birth rates, more aggressive evangelism, and clearer theological boundaries, grew rapidly, often absorbing former mainline members who wanted something more definite than what the mainline offered.</p>
-
-<h2>Why They Declined</h2>
-
-<p>The causes of mainline decline are debated, but several factors recur in nearly every analysis. The sociologist Dean Kelley, himself a mainline Protestant, argued in his influential 1972 book <em>Why Conservative Churches Are Growing</em> that strict churches, those demanding clear commitment and maintaining sharp boundaries, thrive, while lenient churches, those accommodating to the surrounding culture and lowering barriers to entry, decline. Kelley's thesis was uncomfortable precisely because it came from within the mainline world.</p>
-
-<p>The theologian Thomas Oden, once a committed theological liberal, argued that the mainline had made a Faustian bargain: it had gained cultural respectability by abandoning theological distinctiveness. When the culture moved on, there was nothing left to hold people. If the church's message was indistinguishable from the editorial page of <em>The New York Times</em>, why get up early on Sunday?</p>
-
-<p>The sociologists Roger Finke and Rodney Stark, in <em>The Churching of America</em> (1992), applied economic models to American religion and concluded that mainline decline was predictable: religious organizations that reduce the "cost" of membership, that ask little and offer vague spiritual uplift, lose market share to groups that demand more and deliver a clearer product. This language offends ecclesiological sensibilities, but the pattern it describes has been remarkably consistent across three centuries of American religious history.</p>
-
-<p>There is also the simple matter of institutional sclerosis. Mainline denominations developed enormous bureaucracies during their years of growth. Those bureaucracies proved resistant to reform and slow to adapt. National offices issued statements on geopolitics while local churches struggled to pay heating bills. The gap between denominational elites and congregational life widened into a chasm.</p>
-
-<h2>The Conservative Critique and the Progressive Defense</h2>
-
-<p>The conservative critique is blunt and not entirely wrong: the mainline accommodated to the culture until there was nothing distinctive left to join. It replaced the scandal of the gospel with the respectability of progressive politics. It trained ministers to deconstruct the faith but not to proclaim it. It confused relevance with faithfulness and discovered too late that a church indistinguishable from a progressive nonprofit has no reason to exist as a church.</p>
-
-<p>The progressive defense is more nuanced and also not entirely wrong: the mainline followed the Spirit where it led, even when that meant challenging comfortable orthodoxies. It ordained women when others refused. It welcomed divorced people when others shunned them. It spoke against racism when other churches sanctified it. It opposed unjust wars when other churches wrapped the cross in the flag. If faithfulness means following Jesus into uncomfortable places, the mainline has a record worth defending.</p>
-
-<p>The truth, as is often the case, is more complex than either side admits. The mainline's social witness was frequently prophetic. Its theological seriousness produced some of the finest scholarship in Christian history. But it also suffered from a fatal inability to articulate why any of this required a church. Justice work can be done by secular organizations. Intellectual inquiry can be pursued at universities. Community can be found in clubs, neighborhoods, and online networks. What the church uniquely offers, encounter with the living God, proclamation of a story that reframes all other stories, formation in practices that shape the soul, the mainline increasingly struggled to deliver with conviction.</p>
-
-<h2>What Mainline Decline Means</h2>
-
-<p>The decline of mainline Protestantism is not merely a denominational problem. It is a cultural one. These institutions mediated between faith and reason, between tradition and modernity, between piety and public responsibility. Their disappearance leaves a vacuum that has been filled by two inadequate alternatives: a fundamentalism that refuses engagement with modern thought, and a secularism that refuses engagement with transcendence.</p>
-
-<p>The mainline, at its best, insisted that one could be intellectually honest and spiritually serious, theologically informed and socially engaged, rooted in tradition and open to reform. That combination is rare and precious, and its loss impoverishes not just Christianity but American public life.</p>
-
-<h2>Can They Survive?</h2>
-
-<p>The case against survival is statistical and institutional. Aging congregations, declining giving, deferred building maintenance, seminary debt, clergy shortages in rural areas, and the departure of entire congregations over theological disputes, these trends have compounding effects that are extremely difficult to reverse. Some scholars predict that several mainline denominations will cease to exist as national institutions within two decades.</p>
-
-<p>The case for survival is theological and adaptive. Mainline traditions carry liturgical depth, intellectual seriousness, and institutional memory that cannot be easily replicated. Some congregations are experiencing renewal through liturgical recovery, community engagement, and a willingness to be smaller but more intentional. The Benedictine option, small communities of serious practice, may be the mainline's unexpected future.</p>
-
