-<p>In 1994, the historian Mark Noll, himself an evangelical scholar of impeccable credentials, published <em>The Scandal of the Evangelical Mind</em>. The book's famous opening line was devastating: "The scandal of the evangelical mind is that there is not much of an evangelical mind." Noll argued that evangelicalism had systematically failed to develop a serious intellectual tradition. It had produced gifted preachers and effective organizers but few scholars, artists, or public intellectuals of the first rank.</p>
