-<p>This is not a story about buildings. It is a story about the end of an arrangement that shaped Western civilization for a millennium and a half, an arrangement so pervasive that most people who lived within it could not see its edges. For fifteen centuries, to be European was to be Christian, to be Christian was to be civilized, and to be civilized was to belong to a world ordered by Christian assumptions about God, time, morality, law, art, sex, death, and meaning. That world, called Christendom, is dying. And the death, depending on where you stand, is either the greatest catastrophe in the history of the faith or its greatest opportunity.</p>
