-<p>For the next fifteen centuries, across the medieval and early modern periods, Christendom defined the shape of European civilization. The church baptized kings, legitimated wars, administered education, regulated marriage, kept time (the calendar itself was organized around Christian feasts), and provided the moral vocabulary with which Europeans understood their world. Even the Reformation, which shattered the institutional unity of Western Christianity, did not break the Christendom arrangement. Protestant nations simply created Protestant versions of it: the Church of England, the Lutheran state churches of Scandinavia, the Reformed churches of Switzerland and the Netherlands. The state still enforced religious conformity. The church still provided ideological support for the state. The fusion remained.</p>
