-<p>Taylor traces a long, complex process that he calls the move from a "porous" self to a "buffered" self. In the medieval world, the self was porous, open to spiritual forces, vulnerable to demons and saints, embedded in a cosmos charged with divine meaning. Disenchantment was not an option because the world itself was experienced as enchanted. The modern self, by contrast, is buffered, self-contained, insulated from spiritual forces, capable of treating the world as a neutral object of scientific investigation. In this buffered condition, belief in God becomes one option among many rather than the default setting of consciousness.</p>
