-<p>Bonhoeffer's response was not to mourn this development but to embrace it. He called for a "religionless Christianity," a faith that did not depend on the "religious premise," the assumption that people need religion and will therefore come to the church when they are desperate enough. Instead, Bonhoeffer envisioned a church that would exist not at the center of power but at the margins, not as the chaplain to the status quo but as a community of discipleship, solidarity, and sacrificial service.</p>
-
-<p>Bonhoeffer was executed by the Nazis on April 9, 1945, two weeks before the liberation of the concentration camp where he was held. His fragmentary prison writings have been interpreted in many directions. But his core intuition, that the end of Christendom might be the beginning of authentic Christianity, has proven prophetic. A church that no longer has cultural power might rediscover what it means to follow a crucified Lord.</p>
-
-<h2>Hauerwas: The Church Should Be Glad</h2>
-
-<p>The American theologian Stanley Hauerwas has argued, with characteristic bluntness, that the church should celebrate the death of Christendom. Hauerwas contends that Christendom was always a corruption of authentic Christianity. By accepting Constantine's offer of power, the church traded its prophetic identity for institutional security. It became the chaplain of empire rather than the community of the cross. It produced nominal Christians by the millions, people who were Christian because everyone was Christian, but whose faith had no distinctive shape, no costly commitment, no capacity to resist the dominant culture when that culture was unjust.</p>
-
-<p>Hauerwas's alternative is a church that knows itself to be "resident aliens," a community with its own distinctive practices, narratives, and virtues, existing within but not fully assimilated to the surrounding culture. This church does not seek to run the world. It seeks to be the church, a visible community of worship, mutual care, and prophetic witness that demonstrates what human life looks like when it is ordered by the story of Jesus rather than the story of the nation-state or the market.</p>
-
-<p>The critique of Hauerwas, and it has force, is that his vision can become sectarian, a church so focused on its own internal life that it abandons responsibility for the common good. But his central claim stands: a church that exists to serve the interests of the state, the market, or the culture has ceased to be the church. And Christendom, whatever its other accomplishments, consistently produced exactly that kind of church.</p>
-
-<h2>What Post-Christendom Christianity Looks Like</h2>
-
-<p>If Christendom is dying, what emerges in its place? Several characteristics are already visible.</p>
-
-<p><strong>Voluntary rather than inherited.</strong> In a post-Christendom world, no one is born Christian. Faith is a choice, not a default. This means that Christian communities will be smaller but more intentional. The nominal Christianity of the Christendom era, in which membership was a social convention rather than a personal commitment, will disappear. What remains will be people who have chosen to be there, who have counted the cost and decided that following Jesus is worth it. This is, arguably, a return to the conditions of the early church, which grew not through state support but through the attractiveness of its communal life.</p>
-
-<p><strong>Prophetic rather than chaplaincy.</strong> A church that no longer serves the establishment is free to critique the establishment. Post-Christendom Christianity is liberated from the obligation to bless whatever the nation does. It can speak truth to power without fear of losing its privileged position, because it no longer has a privileged position to lose. The prophetic tradition, suppressed for centuries by the church's entanglement with power, can reemerge as the church's primary public posture.</p>
-
-<p><strong>Communal rather than individual.</strong> In a culture of atomized individualism, the church's distinctive gift is community: embodied, committed, intergenerational relationships formed around shared practices of worship, service, and mutual care. The early church was known not primarily for its doctrines but for its communal life. "See how they love one another," Tertullian reported the pagans saying. In a lonely, fragmented culture, that witness is more needed than ever.</p>
-
-<p><strong>Costly rather than convenient.</strong> Christendom made Christianity easy. You were born into it, confirmed into it, married in it, and buried by it. Post-Christendom faith will be costly. It will require intentional formation, countercultural commitment, and the willingness to be different in a culture that punishes difference. The theologian Dietrich Bonhoeffer distinguished between "cheap grace," grace as a doctrine without discipleship, and "costly grace," grace that calls to follow Jesus on the way of the cross. Christendom was the institutional embodiment of cheap grace. Its death may be the precondition for costly grace to reemerge.</p>
-
-<h2>The Opportunity</h2>
-
-<p>There is a passage in the Gospel of John that is rarely quoted in this context but is strikingly relevant. Jesus says: "Unless a grain of wheat falls into the earth and dies, it remains alone; but if it dies, it bears much fruit" (John 12:24). The principle of death and resurrection is not merely a doctrine about the afterlife. It is the pattern of God's work in history. Institutions die so that new life can emerge. Structures collapse so that what was buried beneath them can grow.</p>
-
-<p>The death of Christendom is not the death of Christianity. It is the death of a particular arrangement, the fusion of faith with cultural power, that served certain purposes for certain centuries but also distorted the faith in ways that are only now becoming fully visible. A church that no longer rules the culture might rediscover the subversive, world-altering power of the gospel. A faith that can no longer be inherited might become, once again, a faith worth choosing. A tradition that has lost its institutional privilege might recover its prophetic voice.</p>
-
-<p>This is not optimism. It is theology. The same God who raised Jesus from the dead is capable of raising the church from its institutional grave. But resurrection, in the Christian tradition, always requires death first. The grain of wheat must fall into the ground. The old wineskins must burst before new wine can be poured. The temple must be torn down before it can be rebuilt in three days.</p>
-
-<p>The death of Christendom is painful, disorienting, and in many ways tragic. But it may also be precisely what the church needs. A church stripped of power, prestige, and cultural dominance might finally become what it was always meant to be: a community of people who follow a crucified Lord, who live by a different story than the one the culture tells, and who bear witness to a kingdom that is not of this world but is, nevertheless, the truest thing in it.</p>`
