-    title: "When Politics Replaced Theology",
-    slug: "when-politics-replaced-theology",
-    excerpt:
-      "The Religious Right did not emerge from a moral awakening. It was constructed — deliberately, strategically, and for reasons that had far more to do with racial segregation than with abortion. Understanding that origin story changes everything about how we evaluate the church's entanglement with partisan power.",
-    pillar: "Prophetic Disruption",
-    readTime: "14 min",
-    body: `<p>In 1965, Jerry Falwell preached a sermon titled "Ministers and Marches" in which he denounced clergy involvement in the civil rights movement. "Preachers are not called to be politicians but to be soul winners," he declared. The church's business was saving souls, not reforming society. Government was Caesar's domain. The pulpit had no business meddling in it.</p>
-
-<p>Fourteen years later, the same Jerry Falwell founded the Moral Majority, one of the most consequential political organizations in modern American history. The man who had insisted that ministers should stay out of politics became the most politically visible minister in the country. This reversal was not hypocrisy in the simple sense. It was something more instructive: it was a theological capitulation so complete that the man who made it did not appear to notice he had made it. The question of when and why American evangelicalism abandoned its apolitical posture for partisan activism is not merely a question about politics. It is a question about what happens when a church trades its prophetic authority for political access — and discovers too late that the exchange is irreversible.</p>
-
-<h2>The Fundamentalist Withdrawal</h2>
-
-<p>To understand the Religious Right, one must first understand what preceded it: a half-century of deliberate disengagement. The fundamentalist-modernist controversy of the 1920s — dramatized most vividly in the 1925 Scopes trial — left conservative Protestantism humiliated and culturally marginalized. William Jennings Bryan won the legal battle in Dayton, Tennessee, but the cultural verdict was devastating. H.L. Mencken's savage reporting painted fundamentalists as ignorant rubes, and the image stuck. For the next four decades, the movement that would eventually become the Religious Right retreated from public life almost entirely.</p>
-
-<p>This withdrawal was not merely strategic. It was theological. Dispensational premillennialism — the dominant eschatological framework among fundamentalists — taught that the world was in irreversible decline, hurtling toward the tribulation and the return of Christ. If the world was a sinking ship, there was no point rearranging the deck chairs. The task of the church was rescue, not renovation. Pull as many souls as possible from the wreckage before the end came. Political engagement was, at best, a distraction from this mission and, at worst, a sign that one had placed too much hope in human systems.</p>
-
-<p>This theology produced a paradox. While mainline Protestants were marching at Selma and lobbying for the Civil Rights Act, fundamentalists and most evangelicals were conspicuously absent. Not because they supported segregation in every case — though many did — but because they had constructed a theological framework that rendered political engagement illegitimate. The irony is brutal: the same theological tradition that would later become the most politically mobilized religious movement in American history spent the mid-twentieth century insisting that politics was none of the church's business.</p>
-
-<h2>The Real Catalyst: Not Roe, but Race</h2>
-
-<p>The conventional story of the Religious Right's origin is simple and widely believed: the Supreme Court legalized abortion in Roe v. Wade (1973), conservative Christians were outraged, and they mobilized politically to fight back. This narrative is almost entirely false. It is a founding myth — retrospectively constructed, carefully maintained, and historically indefensible.</p>
-
-<p>The historian Randall Balmer has documented this with meticulous care. In his research, including interviews with key figures like Paul Weyrich, the conservative activist who coined the term "Moral Majority," Balmer demonstrates that the actual catalyst for evangelical political mobilization was not abortion but racial segregation — specifically, the IRS's decision in 1971 to revoke the tax-exempt status of racially discriminatory private schools, and its application of that ruling to Bob Jones University in 1976.</p>
-
-<p>Bob Jones University, in Greenville, South Carolina, had prohibited interracial dating and marriage among its students. When the IRS revoked its tax exemption on the grounds that racial discrimination disqualified an institution from charitable status, the evangelical world erupted — not in defense of segregation per se, but in defense of what they framed as religious liberty and institutional autonomy. The government, they argued, had no right to tell a Christian institution how to operate. The issue was not race. The issue was government overreach.</p>
-
-<p>Paul Weyrich himself confirmed this sequence in multiple interviews. "I was trying to get those people interested in those issues and I utterly failed," Weyrich told Balmer, referring to his attempts to mobilize evangelicals around abortion, pornography, school prayer, and the Equal Rights Amendment in the mid-1970s. "What changed their mind was Jimmy Carter's intervention against the Christian schools, trying to deny them tax-exempt status on the basis of so-called de facto segregation." It was the tax-exemption issue, not Roe v. Wade, that provided the political energy for the movement.</p>
-
-<p>Abortion was retrofitted as the movement's central cause only after the fact. It served as a more morally compelling rallying cry than the defense of racially segregated schools — for obvious reasons. But the timeline does not lie. The Southern Baptist Convention, the largest Protestant denomination in the country, passed a resolution in 1971 calling for legislation that would allow abortion in cases of rape, incest, fetal deformity, and "carefully ascertained evidence of the likelihood of damage to the emotional, mental, and physical health of the mother." W.A. Criswell, the pastor of First Baptist Dallas and one of the most prominent conservative Southern Baptist leaders, told reporters after Roe v. Wade, "I have always felt that it was only after a child was born and had a life separate from its mother that it became an individual person." These were not fringe voices. They were the establishment.</p>
-
-<p>The rewriting of this history matters enormously. If the Religious Right was born from a righteous response to the murder of the unborn, then its political engagement has a moral foundation that transcends partisanship. If it was born from the defense of racial segregation and then retroactively re-narrated around abortion, then its moral authority rests on a fabrication. The historical evidence overwhelmingly supports the latter reading.</p>
-
-<h2>The Architects of the Movement</h2>
-
-<p>The Religious Right did not emerge spontaneously from the grassroots. It was constructed by a small group of political operatives and religious leaders who recognized the electoral potential of mobilizing white evangelicals as a voting bloc. The key figures are worth naming, because their strategic decisions shaped the movement's DNA.</p>
-
-<p>Paul Weyrich was a Catholic conservative activist who had co-founded the Heritage Foundation in 1973. He understood, before most Republican strategists did, that white evangelicals represented an enormous untapped political resource — tens of millions of voters who had largely stayed home on Election Day. Weyrich provided the political infrastructure: the organizational models, the fundraising networks, the direct-mail strategies that would transform diffuse religious sentiment into coordinated political action.</p>
-
-<p>Jerry Falwell provided the pastoral face. A Baptist minister from Lynchburg, Virginia, Falwell had built Thomas Road Baptist Church into a megachurch and launched a television ministry that reached millions. His founding of the Moral Majority in 1979 gave the movement institutional form. Falwell's genius was translating political positions into the language of moral absolutes. Opposition to abortion was not a policy preference; it was a stand against murder. Opposition to gay rights was not bigotry; it was faithfulness to Scripture. Support for a strong military was not geopolitics; it was obedience to Romans 13. Every political position was baptized in theological language, making disagreement feel like apostasy.</p>
-
-<p>Pat Robertson extended the movement's reach through the 700 Club and his 1988 presidential campaign. Robertson's candidacy was ultimately unsuccessful, but it demonstrated that evangelical voters could be mobilized for electoral politics at the highest level. More significantly, Robertson's Christian Coalition, founded in 1989 under the direction of Ralph Reed, shifted strategy from national spectacle to local organizing. The Coalition trained activists to run for school boards, city councils, and state legislatures — building power from the bottom up with a sophistication that most observers did not recognize until it was too late.</p>
-
-<p>James Dobson, through Focus on the Family, shaped the movement's domestic policy agenda. Dobson's influence was more cultural than directly political, but his radio program reached an estimated 220 million listeners worldwide, and his endorsement could make or break a Republican primary candidate. Dobson's framework was straightforward: the traditional nuclear family was under siege from feminism, homosexuality, and secular humanism, and the church's political task was to defend the family through legislation, judicial appointments, and cultural combat.</p>
-
-<h2>The Single-Issue Reduction</h2>
-
-<p>The most theologically damaging consequence of the Religious Right was not its political engagement per se. Christians have every right — and, arguably, every obligation — to participate in democratic governance. The damage was in the reduction of a complex, multi-dimensional ethical tradition to a handful of partisan litmus tests.</p>
-
-<p>The Hebrew prophets spoke about economic justice, care for the stranger, the abuse of power, the corruption of courts, the exploitation of workers, the neglect of the poor. Jesus's teaching encompassed peacemaking, enemy love, radical generosity, the dangers of wealth, the inclusion of the marginalized, and the subversion of every human hierarchy. The Apostle Paul wrote about reconciliation across ethnic and social boundaries, the mutual submission of all believers, and the fruit of the Spirit: love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness, self-control. The Christian ethical tradition is extraordinarily broad.</p>
-
-<p>The Religious Right compressed this tradition into a short list: abortion, homosexuality, school prayer, and support for Israel. A politician could endorse torture, slash programs for the poor, demonize immigrants, and promote economic policies that concentrated wealth among the already wealthy — and still be considered the "Christian" candidate, provided they opposed abortion and same-sex marriage. A politician who fed the hungry, welcomed the stranger, advocated for peace, and worked to reduce inequality was the "secular" candidate if they were pro-choice.</p>
-
-<p>This was not merely a strategic narrowing. It was a theological distortion of the first order. It taught an entire generation of Christians that faithfulness could be measured by a voter guide. It reduced the prophetic tradition to a checklist. And it made the church's moral witness contingent on partisan outcomes — which meant that when the party failed morally, the church that had yoked itself to the party failed with it.</p>
-
-<h2>The Cost: Prophetic Voice Traded for Political Access</h2>
-
-<p>The transaction at the heart of the Religious Right was simple: the church gave the Republican Party its moral credibility, its voter mobilization infrastructure, and its cultural legitimacy. In return, the church received access — meetings with presidents, invitations to the White House, judicial nominations, and the rhetorical deference of politicians who needed evangelical votes. The question is whether the church got the better end of the deal.</p>
-
-<p>The evidence suggests it did not. After four decades of political engagement, the Religious Right had achieved remarkably little of its stated agenda. Abortion remained legal until the Dobbs decision in 2022, and even that victory has proven more politically complicated than the movement anticipated. School prayer was not restored. The culture moved decisively in favor of LGBTQ rights. The divorce rate among evangelicals remained virtually identical to the national average. The pornography industry grew exponentially. By the movement's own metrics, it lost the culture war.</p>
-
-<p>What it gained instead was power — but power of a specific and corrosive kind. Evangelical leaders gained proximity to political authority. They sat in the Oval Office. They advised presidents. They were consulted on judicial nominees. But this access came at a price that many did not recognize until it was too late: the church's ability to speak prophetically to power was compromised by its dependence on that power. A prophet who needs the king's favor is no prophet at all.</p>
-
-<p>The Trump era exposed this dynamic with brutal clarity. When evangelical leaders embraced a thrice-married casino owner who bragged about sexual assault, paid hush money to a porn star, mocked prisoners of war, and separated children from their parents at the border, the theological emptiness of the movement's political theology was laid bare. The question was no longer whether the church could influence politics. The question was whether politics had consumed the church. For millions of Americans — particularly younger Americans, Black Americans, and those who had experienced the church's judgment firsthand — the answer was obvious.</p>
-
-<h2>The Theological Problem: Kingdom Ethics vs. Partisan Loyalty</h2>
-
-<p>The deepest problem with the Religious Right is not political but theological. The New Testament presents the kingdom of God as a reality that transcends and critiques every human political arrangement. Jesus refused the role of political messiah. He told Pilate that his kingdom was "not of this world" — not meaning it was irrelevant to this world, but that it operated by a fundamentally different logic. The kingdom advances through self-sacrifice, not coercion. Through persuasion, not legislation. Through the slow, patient work of communities living out an alternative vision of human flourishing.</p>
-
-<p>When the church aligns itself with a political party, it inevitably subordinates kingdom ethics to partisan strategy. It must excuse the sins of its allies and exaggerate the sins of its opponents. It must treat electoral victory as evidence of divine favor and electoral defeat as evidence of spiritual warfare. It must, in short, adopt the logic of the political world — a logic of power, competition, and zero-sum calculation — and baptize it in the language of faith.</p>
-
-<p>This is precisely what the prophets condemned. When Amos thundered against Israel, he was not addressing a secular state. He was addressing a religious society that had confused its own prosperity with God's approval. When Jeremiah wept over Jerusalem, he wept over a people who had placed their trust in political alliances and military power rather than in the justice and mercy of God. The prophetic tradition does not reject political engagement. It rejects the idolatry of political power — the belief that the right legislation, the right president, the right Supreme Court can accomplish what only the Spirit of God can accomplish.</p>
-
-<blockquote>"The church that marries the spirit of the age will find itself widowed in the next." This aphorism, often attributed to Dean Inge, describes precisely what has happened to American evangelicalism's political project. The movement married itself to the Republican Party, and as the party's character has changed, the church has found itself bound to positions and personalities that contradict its own stated values.</blockquote>
-
-<h2>The Global Perspective</h2>
-
-<p>The American fusion of evangelical Christianity with right-wing politics is not universal. It is, in fact, an anomaly. In most of the world, politically engaged Christianity looks very different.</p>
-
-<p>In Latin America, the most significant political theology of the twentieth century was liberation theology — a movement that read the Gospel through the lens of the poor and the oppressed and argued that God's preferential option was for those at the bottom of the social hierarchy. Whatever one thinks of liberation theology's Marxist analytical framework, its central claim — that the God of the Bible is not neutral in the face of injustice — is difficult to dismiss on scriptural grounds. Gustavo Gutierrez, Leonardo Boff, and Oscar Romero articulated a vision of political Christianity that was radical, justice-oriented, and rooted in the lived experience of the poor.</p>
-
-<p>In South Africa, the church played a central role in the anti-apartheid movement. Desmond Tutu, Allan Boesak, and Beyers Naude drew on the prophetic tradition to challenge a system of racial oppression that was itself justified by a theological framework — the Dutch Reformed Church's theology of separate development. The Kairos Document of 1985, produced by South African theologians, distinguished between "state theology" (which legitimized the status quo), "church theology" (which offered vague appeals to reconciliation without addressing structural injustice), and "prophetic theology" (which named the sin and demanded concrete change). This taxonomy remains remarkably useful.</p>
-
-<p>In South Korea, the minjung theology movement connected Christian faith with the struggles of workers, students, and democracy activists under authoritarian rule. In the Philippines, the church was instrumental in the People Power Revolution of 1986. In Poland, Catholic social teaching provided the intellectual framework for the Solidarity movement. In each case, political Christianity was oriented toward justice, liberation, and the empowerment of the oppressed — not toward the consolidation of power by those who already possessed it.</p>
-
-<p>The American model, in which Christianity functions as the spiritual legitimation of an existing power structure, is the exception, not the rule. This should give American Christians pause.</p>
-
-<h2>How to Be Engaged Without Being Captured</h2>
-
-<p>None of this means Christians should withdraw from political life. The apolitical fundamentalism of the early twentieth century was its own form of failure — a refusal to love one's neighbor in the public square. The question is not whether Christians should be politically engaged but how they can be engaged without being captured by partisan loyalties that distort their witness.</p>
-
-<p>Several principles emerge from the wreckage of the Religious Right. First, the church must maintain its prophetic independence from every party and every politician. This means the church must be willing to critique its allies as readily as its opponents. A church that speaks only against the sins of the other party is not prophetic. It is partisan with a stained-glass veneer.</p>
-
-<p>Second, Christians must resist the reduction of their ethical tradition to a handful of issues selected for partisan convenience. The full range of biblical ethics — including economic justice, peacemaking, care for creation, hospitality to the stranger, and the dignity of every human being — must inform political engagement. A "whole-life" ethic, as articulated by figures like Ron Sider and the late Cardinal Joseph Bernardin, refuses to let any party claim a monopoly on Christian values.</p>
-
-<p>Third, the church must recover the distinction between penultimate and ultimate loyalties. Political engagement is penultimate — important but not final. The kingdom of God is ultimate. When penultimate loyalties displace ultimate ones — when the party becomes the church, when the president becomes the pastor, when the platform becomes the creed — idolatry has occurred, regardless of how sincerely the participants believe otherwise.</p>
-
-<p>Fourth, the church must learn from the global church. The American evangelical fusion of faith and right-wing politics is not the only model available. Christians in other contexts have demonstrated that it is possible to be politically engaged, passionately committed to justice, and theologically serious without becoming the chaplains of any political movement.</p>
-
-<p>Finally, the church must recover its eschatological patience. The kingdom of God will not arrive through an election. It will not be established by a Supreme Court ruling. It will not be advanced by controlling school boards or winning culture wars. The kingdom comes as it has always come: through the slow, faithful, often invisible work of communities that embody an alternative way of being human. This does not mean politics is unimportant. It means politics is not ultimate. And when the church forgets that distinction, it loses not only its prophetic voice but its very identity.</p>
-
-<p>The story of the Religious Right is a cautionary tale, not a model to be emulated. The church entered politics seeking to change the world and discovered that the world had changed the church. The path forward requires not less engagement but a fundamentally different kind — one rooted in the full breadth of the biblical tradition, accountable to the global church, and freed from the illusion that any political arrangement can substitute for the kingdom of God.</p>`
