-      "The psychological damage inflicted by authoritarian religion is not a failure of individual faith. It is a predictable consequence of theological systems designed to control through fear, shame, and the systematic dismantling of personal agency.",
-    pillar: "Prophetic Disruption",
-    readTime: "12 min",
-    body: `<p>She sat in my office and told me she could not pray without having a panic attack. Not because she had lost her faith — she had not, or at least she was not sure she had — but because every time she closed her eyes and tried to address God, her body flooded with the same terror she had felt at fourteen, kneeling at an altar while a pastor told her that her doubts were evidence of demonic influence. She was thirty-seven. She had a graduate degree, a stable career, a therapist she trusted. And she could not bow her head without her hands shaking.</p>
-
-<p>This is not an unusual story. It is, in fact, so common that researchers have given it a name. What she was experiencing has characteristics consistent with what psychologist Marlene Winell has termed Religious Trauma Syndrome — a condition that, while not yet included in the DSM-5, has been documented with increasing rigor by clinicians working at the intersection of psychology and religion. The church has been slow to acknowledge this reality, and the reasons for that slowness are themselves part of the problem. To admit that religion can traumatize is to admit that the institution charged with healing souls has, in some cases, been the instrument of their wounding.</p>
-
-<h2>What Religious Trauma Is</h2>
-
-<p>Marlene Winell, a human development consultant who has worked with survivors of authoritarian religion for over three decades, first described Religious Trauma Syndrome (RTS) in a 2011 paper in the British journal <em>Cognitive Behaviour Therapy Today</em>. Winell defined RTS as the condition experienced by those who are struggling with leaving an authoritarian, dogmatic religion and coping with the resulting damage to their normal development. She identified it as a complex of symptoms — cognitive, affective, functional, and social — resulting from immersion in a controlling religious environment and the struggle to leave it.</p>
-
-<p>Winell's framework drew on existing research into the psychological effects of high-demand groups. Robert Jay Lifton's work on thought reform and Steven Hassan's BITE model (Behavior, Information, Thought, and Emotional control) identified mechanisms of psychological manipulation that find particularly fertile ground in religious contexts — precisely because religion claims ultimate authority. A political party can demand your vote. An employer can demand your labor. But a religion that claims to speak for God can demand your soul, and the psychological hold this creates is qualitatively different from any secular form of coercion.</p>
-
-<p>It is important to be precise about what religious trauma is and what it is not. It is not the ordinary difficulty of living within a moral framework that makes demands on one's behavior. Every serious ethical commitment involves tension, sacrifice, and the occasional experience of guilt. A teaching that challenges you is not the same as a teaching that damages you. Religious trauma occurs when the religious environment systematically undermines a person's sense of self, their capacity for autonomous thought, their ability to trust their own perceptions, and their fundamental sense of safety in the world. It is the difference between a coach who pushes you hard and a coach who breaks your bones.</p>
-
-<h2>The Sources: How Trauma Gets Produced</h2>
-
-<p>Religious trauma does not emerge from nowhere. It is produced by identifiable structures, practices, and theological frameworks. Understanding these sources is essential because it moves the conversation from individual pathology — "something is wrong with you for being hurt by church" — to systemic analysis: "something is wrong with systems that predictably produce this kind of damage."</p>
-
-<p>Authoritarian leadership is the most commonly cited source. In congregations where a single leader exercises unchecked authority — where questioning is equated with rebellion, where dissent is treated as sin, where the leader claims a direct line to God that ordinary members lack — the conditions for psychological harm are structurally present. This is not about personality types. It is about systems that lack accountability. When a church board serves at the pastor's pleasure rather than as an independent governing body, when the congregation is told that to criticize the leader is to "touch God's anointed," the system is designed to produce abuse regardless of the leader's intentions.</p>
-
-<p>Fear-based theology is a second major source. There is a difference between a theology that takes seriously divine judgment and a theology that weaponizes fear as a tool of behavioral control. When children are told in graphic detail about the torments of hell, when teenagers are warned that a single moment of doubt could consign them to eternal damnation, when adults are taught that God is perpetually angry and surveilling them for infractions, the result is not reverence but terror. Jonathan Edwards's "Sinners in the Hands of an Angry God" (1741) is a masterpiece of homiletical rhetoric, but the theology it represents — a God whose primary posture toward humanity is wrath, held in check only by a fragile thread of grace — has left a long trail of psychological casualties.</p>
-
-<p>Purity culture, particularly as it developed in American evangelicalism in the 1990s and 2000s, has been a significant source of sexual and relational trauma. The movement, popularized by Joshua Harris's <em>I Kissed Dating Goodbye</em> (1997) and reinforced by programs like True Love Waits, taught that sexual purity was the defining characteristic of a person's spiritual worth. Young women were told their virginity was a gift to be presented to their future husband — a framework that reduced female personhood to sexual status and produced devastating shame in those who "failed." Harris himself eventually recanted the book and apologized publicly. But the damage had been done to a generation.</p>
-
-<p>The practice of shunning — cutting off contact with those who leave the faith or violate community norms — represents one of the most psychologically destructive tools in the religious arsenal. While most commonly associated with Jehovah's Witnesses and the Amish practice of Meidung, informal shunning occurs across traditions. The college student who comes out as gay and is told not to come home for Christmas. The seminary graduate who expresses doubts and finds old friends no longer return calls. The message is consistent: belonging is conditional, and the condition is conformity. For social animals like humans, the threat of exile from the community is, at a neurological level, experienced as a threat to survival.</p>
-
-<p>Conversion therapy — the attempt to change a person's sexual orientation or gender identity through religious or psychological means — deserves particular mention. Condemned by every major medical and psychological organization, the practice has produced well-documented harm: depression, anxiety, self-hatred, and suicide. The Trevor Project's 2022 National Survey found that LGBTQ young people who underwent conversion therapy were more than twice as likely to attempt suicide. The theological conviction that homosexuality is sinful is one thing. The attempt to psychologically coerce a person into changing their orientation — under the guise of spiritual care — is another. The former is a theological position. The latter is abuse.</p>
-
-<h2>The Symptoms: What Trauma Looks Like</h2>
-
-<p>Religious trauma manifests across cognitive, emotional, physical, and social domains. Clinicians working with survivors report a consistent cluster of symptoms that, while overlapping with other forms of complex trauma, carry distinctive features rooted in the religious context.</p>
-
-<p>Cognitively, survivors often experience difficulty with independent decision-making. Years of being told that their own judgment was unreliable — that the heart is "deceitful above all things" (Jeremiah 17:9), that human wisdom is foolishness before God — can produce a profound distrust of one's own perceptions. Black-and-white thinking, instilled by binary moral frameworks, persists long after the person has left. The world was divided into saved and lost, righteous and wicked, God's people and the world. Learning to inhabit the gray space between these categories is slow, difficult work.</p>
-
-<p>Emotionally, anxiety and depression are the most commonly reported symptoms. The hypervigilance that served as a survival strategy within the religious environment — constantly monitoring one's thoughts for doubt, one's body for lust, one's speech for insufficient faith — does not switch off when the person leaves. It persists as a free-floating watchfulness, a sense that one is perpetually being observed and evaluated. Guilt metastasizes into a chronic condition unattached to any specific behavior.</p>
-
-<p>The body, as Bessel van der Kolk demonstrated in <em>The Body Keeps the Score</em> (2014), does not forget trauma simply because the conscious mind has moved on. Van der Kolk established that traumatic experiences are stored not only in explicit memory but in the body's nervous system — in patterns of muscular tension, in the hyperactivation of the amygdala, in the dysregulation of the autonomic nervous system. Religious trauma follows the same neurological pathways. The person who was struck during a prayer meeting may flinch at the sound of prayer decades later. The child locked in a room for "spiritual discipline" may experience claustrophobia in churches. The body remembers what the mind tries to forget.</p>
-
-<p>Socially, survivors face a double bind. Leaving the religious community means losing one's entire social network. But remaining means continuing to exist within the system causing harm. Many describe a period of profound isolation after leaving — too wounded to return to church, too shaped by religion to feel at home in secular spaces.</p>
-
-<h2>The Difference Between a Hard Teaching and an Abusive One</h2>
-
-<p>The church's instinctive response to claims of religious trauma is often defensive: "The gospel is supposed to be challenging. Jesus said to take up your cross. If the truth hurts, that does not make it abuse." This response contains a legitimate concern — the fear that any difficult teaching will be labeled traumatic, that the gospel will be softened into therapeutic pablum — but it fundamentally misunderstands the nature of trauma.</p>
-
-<p>A hard teaching is one that challenges your behavior or your assumptions while respecting your dignity and your agency. "Love your enemies" is a hard teaching. "Sell everything you have and give to the poor" is a hard teaching. "Forgive seventy times seven" is a hard teaching. These teachings confront the self without dismantling it. They call for transformation without requiring annihilation.</p>
-
-<p>An abusive teaching is one that systematically undermines your capacity to function as a whole person. "You are worthless apart from God" — stated not as a theological claim about dependence on grace but as a psychological assault on self-worth — is abusive. "Your feelings are not trustworthy" — stated not as a caution against unchecked emotivism but as a blanket dismissal of the person's inner life — is abusive. "If you leave this church, you will lose your salvation" — used not as a pastoral expression of concern but as a threat to prevent departure — is abusive. The distinction is not between comfortable and uncomfortable teachings. It is between teachings that empower growth and teachings that enforce control.</p>
-
-<p>Dietrich Bonhoeffer drew a related distinction between what he called "cheap grace" and "costly grace." Cheap grace was grace without discipleship, forgiveness without repentance, baptism without the cross. Costly grace was the real thing — demanding, remaking, and life-giving precisely because it cost something. But there is a third category that Bonhoeffer did not name because he did not need to: weaponized grace, in which the language of grace is used to maintain power. "God has forgiven the pastor, so you must too" — said to a victim of the pastor's abuse. "Grace means not holding grudges" — said to someone who has been deeply wronged by the institution. This is neither cheap grace nor costly grace. It is the use of theological language to silence legitimate pain.</p>
-
-<h2>The Church's Responsibility</h2>
-
-<p>The church bears a twofold responsibility regarding religious trauma: it must stop causing it, and it must help heal it. Neither task is simple, and both require structural change, not merely attitudinal adjustment.</p>
-
-<p>Stopping the harm requires accountability structures that actually function. This means independent governance boards with the authority to investigate and discipline leaders. It means transparent financial reporting. It means mandatory training in recognizing and preventing abuse. It means ending the culture of pastoral celebrity that insulates charismatic leaders from scrutiny. It means listening to survivors rather than managing their stories.</p>
-
-<p>It also requires theological reform. Fear-based theology must be confronted not merely as pastorally unwise but as theologically deficient. A God whose primary tool is terror is not the God revealed in Jesus Christ. The Johannine assertion that "perfect love casts out fear" (1 John 4:18) is not a sentimental platitude. It is a theological claim about the nature of God — a claim that stands in direct contradiction to preaching traditions built on the manipulation of fear. This does not mean eliminating all talk of judgment from Christian theology. It means ensuring that judgment is always situated within the larger narrative of a God whose fundamental disposition toward creation is love.</p>
-
-<p>Healing the harm requires the church to develop what might be called a trauma-informed theology — a way of thinking about God, Scripture, community, and spiritual formation that takes seriously the reality of psychological damage and the conditions necessary for recovery. This is not a capitulation to secular psychology. It is a recognition that the same God who created the soul also created the psyche, and that attending to the latter is not a betrayal of the former.</p>
-
-<h2>Trauma-Informed Theology in Practice</h2>
-
-<p>What does trauma-informed theology look like in practice? Several principles are emerging from the work of theologians, clinicians, and communities that are taking this question seriously.</p>
-
-<p>First, it prioritizes safety. Before any spiritual formation can occur, people must feel physically, emotionally, and psychologically safe. This means creating worship environments where people are free to participate or not, where emotional expression is neither coerced nor suppressed, where consent is honored in every interaction — from the handshake line to the altar call. For survivors of authoritarian religion, the simple act of being told "you are free to leave at any time" can be profoundly healing.</p>
-
-<p>Second, it restores agency. Authoritarian religion systematically strips people of their capacity for autonomous decision-making. Trauma-informed theology reverses this by treating people as competent moral agents capable of discerning truth, making choices, and bearing responsibility. This does not mean abandoning spiritual authority. It means exercising authority in ways that empower rather than diminish.</p>
-
-<p>Third, it holds complexity. Traumatized people have been taught that the world divides neatly into categories: right and wrong, saved and lost, us and them. Recovery involves learning to hold ambiguity, to sit with unanswered questions. A theology that insists on certainty about everything is not strong. It is brittle. A theology that can hold doubt, lament, and unanswered prayer within its framework — as the Psalms do, as Job does, as Ecclesiastes does — is far more resilient and far more honest.</p>
-
-<p>Fourth, it takes the body seriously. If van der Kolk is right that the body keeps the score, then a theology that treats embodiment as incidental to spiritual life is inadequate. Practices that attend to the body — contemplative prayer, liturgical movement, the physical elements of communion, the laying on of hands (with consent), the simple act of breathing together in silence — can reach parts of the person that words alone cannot touch. The incarnation is, among other things, God's declaration that the body matters.</p>
-
-<blockquote>"The wound that is not acknowledged cannot be healed. And the church that refuses to acknowledge the wounds it has inflicted will continue to inflict them — not out of malice, in most cases, but out of the willful ignorance that is malice's most respectable cousin."</blockquote>
-
-<h2>Resources for Recovery</h2>
-
-<p>For those who are navigating religious trauma, the path forward typically involves several elements, and it is worth naming them plainly because many survivors do not know where to begin.</p>
-
-<p>Professional therapy with a clinician trained in religious trauma is the single most important resource. Not every therapist understands the specific dynamics of religious harm. Organizations such as the Religious Trauma Institute, founded by Brian Peck, provide directories of trained clinicians and educational resources. The Secular Therapy Project maintains a database of therapists who are specifically equipped to work with clients recovering from religious harm without dismissing the significance of their former faith.</p>
-
-<p>Peer support communities offer what individual therapy cannot: the experience of being understood by people who have walked the same path. Groups like Recovering from Religion provide online and in-person support. The Clergy Project serves as a confidential network for religious leaders who no longer hold supernatural beliefs but remain in ministry — a situation more common than most churchgoers would imagine.</p>
-
-<p>Literature can serve as companionship on the way. Winell's <em>Leaving the Fold</em> remains one of the most practical guides for those leaving authoritarian religion. Judith Herman's <em>Trauma and Recovery</em> (1992) offers a clinical model for the stages of healing. For those who wish to reconstruct rather than abandon faith, Brian McLaren's <em>Faith After Doubt</em> and Peter Enns's <em>The Sin of Certainty</em> offer frameworks for a faith honest about its own fragility.</p>
-
-<p>It must also be said that some people who leave harmful religious environments will not return to any form of faith, and the church must respect this without treating it as a failure. If a person's experience of God has been so thoroughly mediated by an abusive system that they cannot separate the two, their departure from religion may be an act of psychological self-preservation. The church that drove them away does not get to dictate the terms of their recovery.</p>
-
-<p>Religious trauma is real. It is documented. It is widespread. And it is, in a great many cases, preventable. The church that refuses to reckon with this reality will continue to produce it. The church that takes it seriously — that listens to survivors, reforms its structures, revises its theology where revision is warranted, and commits to the slow work of healing — has a chance of becoming what it has always claimed to be: a place where broken people encounter not judgment but grace, not control but freedom, not fear but the love that casts it out.</p>`
-  }
-]
