-
-<p>But here is what non-denominational churches rarely acknowledge: the constellation of elements described above is itself a tradition. It is a specific, historically situated set of theological commitments, worship practices, and organizational assumptions that can be traced to identifiable sources in the late twentieth century. It is not the absence of tradition. It is a tradition that does not know it is one.</p>
-
-<h2>The Rise of the Non-Denominational Movement</h2>
-
-<p>The non-denominational church is a relatively recent phenomenon. As recently as the 1960s, the vast majority of American Protestants belonged to a denomination, and denominational loyalty was a significant part of religious identity. You were a Baptist, a Methodist, a Presbyterian, or a Lutheran, and that affiliation carried theological content, social meaning, and family history.</p>
-
-<p>Several converging forces eroded this system. The Jesus Movement of the late 1960s and 1970s brought thousands of countercultural converts into Christianity who had no interest in inherited denominational structures. Calvary Chapel, founded by Chuck Smith in Costa Mesa, California, became the prototype: an independent church with informal worship, verse-by-verse Bible teaching, and no denominational affiliation. The charismatic movement of the same period produced independent congregations that crossed denominational lines. And the church growth movement, pioneered by Donald McGavran and popularized by figures like Rick Warren and Bill Hybels in the 1980s and 1990s, encouraged churches to remove every barrier to entry — including denominational labels that might confuse or repel seekers.</p>
-
-<p>The result was an explosion of independent churches. By 2020, "non-denominational" had become the fastest-growing religious category in America, surpassing every individual denomination. The Pew Research Center found that roughly 6 percent of all American adults — and a much higher percentage of regular churchgoers under 40 — identified with a non-denominational church.</p>
-
-<p>The sociology behind this shift extends beyond religion. Robert Putnam's landmark study <em>Bowling Alone</em> documented the collapse of associational life in America — the decline of civic clubs, fraternal organizations, political parties, and, yes, denominational loyalty. Americans in the late twentieth century became less willing to affiliate with any large institution, and denominational Christianity was no exception. The same cultural forces that produced independent voters, freelance workers, and a la carte cable packages produced independent churches. The non-denominational movement is, in this sense, not merely a religious phenomenon but a reflection of broader American individualism applied to the life of faith.</p>
