-<h2>The Accountability Gap</h2>
-
-<p>Perhaps the most significant structural issue with non-denominational churches is the absence of external accountability. In a denominational system, a pastor who teaches heresy, engages in financial misconduct, or abuses their authority can be investigated, disciplined, or removed by a denominational body — a presbytery, a bishop, a district superintendent, an association. The system does not always work (denominational structures have their own failures of accountability, as the Southern Baptist Convention's sexual abuse crisis has demonstrated), but the structure exists.</p>
-
-<p>In a non-denominational church, the lead pastor is often the highest authority. The church may have an elder board, but in many cases the elders are selected by the pastor, serve at the pastor's pleasure, and lack the independence or the theological training to provide genuine oversight. The legal structure of the church — typically a nonprofit corporation — may vest ultimate authority in a self-perpetuating board of directors that is, in practice, the pastor and the pastor's inner circle.</p>
-
-<p>The consequences of this accountability deficit have been visible in a series of high-profile scandals: Mars Hill Church under Mark Driscoll, Harvest Bible Chapel under James MacDonald, Willow Creek Community Church under Bill Hybels. In each case, a charismatic and gifted pastor built a large, influential, non-denominational church with inadequate governance structures, and when the pastor's behavior became destructive, there was no external authority with the standing or the power to intervene. The elder boards, handpicked by the pastors themselves, were unable or unwilling to act until the damage was catastrophic.</p>
-
-<p>This is not an argument that non-denominational churches are inherently corrupt. It is an argument that organizational structure matters, that accountability requires independence, and that the absence of external oversight is a structural vulnerability, not a feature.</p>
-
-<h2>The Branding Phenomenon</h2>
-
-<p>A paradox has emerged within the non-denominational movement: the very churches that reject denominational identity have, in many cases, created brand identities that function exactly like denominations — except without the accountability structures that denominations provide. Multi-site churches like Life.Church, Elevation Church, and Transformation Church operate campuses across multiple states, broadcasting a single pastor's sermon via video to every location. Church planting networks like Acts 29, the Association of Related Churches (ARC), and the Send Network function as de facto denominational structures, providing training, funding, assessment, and theological alignment for their member churches.</p>
-
-<p>These networks represent a new form of denominationalism — one organized around shared methodology, leadership culture, and brand identity rather than around shared confessional documents. An ARC church in Texas and an ARC church in Florida will share a worship style, a leadership philosophy, a church growth strategy, and even a similar building aesthetic, while each describing itself as "non-denominational." The label has, in many cases, become less a theological statement than a marketing decision — a way of signaling contemporary relevance and independence to a consumer base that associates denominational labels with institutional decline.</p>
-
-<p>The theological implications of this shift are significant. When a church's identity is organized around a brand rather than a confession, the center of gravity moves from doctrine to personality, from theological tradition to organizational culture, from the substance of the faith to the experience of consuming it. This is not inevitable — many networked non-denominational churches maintain robust theological commitments — but it is a tendency that the movement has not adequately reckoned with.</p>
-
-<h2>Genuine Strengths</h2>
-
-<p>Fairness requires acknowledging what non-denominational churches do well, and it is a substantial list. They are often remarkably effective at reaching people who would never walk into a traditional denominational church. Their informality, their contemporary worship, their practical teaching, and their lack of insider jargon make them accessible to seekers and the unchurched in ways that more traditional churches often are not. They are typically faster to adapt to cultural change, quicker to adopt new technologies, and more willing to experiment with new forms of ministry. They are not burdened by the bureaucratic overhead, the denominational politics, or the institutional maintenance costs that consume enormous amounts of energy in denominational life.</p>
-
-<p>Many non-denominational churches have also developed sophisticated small group systems, robust community service programs, and effective discipleship pathways. The best of them combine contemporary accessibility with genuine theological depth — churches like Redeemer Presbyterian (technically PCA, but functioning in many ways like a non-denominational church) under Tim Keller, or The Village Church under Matt Chandler, which combined reformed theology with contemporary worship in ways that attracted young, educated believers.</p>
-
-<p>There is also something genuinely admirable about the non-denominational movement's willingness to take risks. Denominational churches can be paralyzed by institutional caution — every decision must be approved by a committee, every change must clear a bureaucracy, every innovation must survive the objections of those who prefer the status quo. Non-denominational churches, at their best, are laboratories of ecclesial creativity. They pioneer new forms of community, new approaches to discipleship, new models of outreach. The small group movement, the multisite church model, the integration of digital technology into pastoral care — all of these innovations emerged primarily from non-denominational contexts, and all of them have been adopted, eventually, by denominational churches that initially resisted them.</p>
-
-<h2>What to Ask Before Joining</h2>
-
-<p>For the Christian considering a non-denominational church, several questions are worth asking. What does the church believe about the essential doctrines of the faith — not just in its statement of faith, but in its preaching, its teaching, and its practice? Who holds the lead pastor accountable, and what mechanisms exist for addressing pastoral misconduct? What is the church's theology of baptism, communion, and church membership? Is the church affiliated with any network, association, or movement that provides external accountability, even informally? Who owns the church property — the congregation, a board of directors, or the pastor? What happens to the church if the lead pastor leaves?</p>
-
-<p>These are not hostile questions. They are the questions that any mature Christian should ask before committing to a community that will shape their faith, form their children, and steward their financial contributions. A church that is unwilling to answer them clearly is a church that is hiding something — possibly from itself. And a church that responds to these questions with hostility or defensiveness — that treats the desire for theological clarity and structural accountability as a lack of faith or a failure of trust — is revealing precisely the kind of leadership culture that makes such questions necessary in the first place.</p>
-
-<p>The non-denominational movement has brought genuine gifts to the body of Christ: energy, accessibility, creativity, and a willingness to meet people where they are. But it has also, in many cases, traded the hard-won wisdom of the historic church for the illusion of a fresh start. The Christian who joins a non-denominational church is not escaping tradition. They are entering one — and the question is whether they will enter it with their eyes open, aware of its strengths, its weaknesses, and its debts to the traditions that came before.</p>`
