-<p>Both of these are Christian worship. Both involve the reading of Scripture, prayer, singing, and preaching. But they are built on different foundations, shaped by different histories, and — most importantly — they form different kinds of Christians. The question of liturgical versus contemporary worship is not, at bottom, a question of preference. It is a question of formation: What is worship <em>for</em>, and what does it do to us over time?</p>
