-<h2>What Liturgy Actually Means</h2>
-
-<p>The word "liturgy" comes from the Greek <em>leitourgia</em>, which means "the work of the people." In ancient Athens, a liturgy was a public work performed by a wealthy citizen for the benefit of the community — funding a warship or sponsoring a festival. The early church adopted the term for its public worship, and the etymology is revealing: liturgy is not something performed by clergy for a passive audience. It is the common work of the gathered community, in which every person participates.</p>
-
-<p>In its Christian usage, liturgy refers to a structured, repeating pattern of worship that follows a set order — an <em>ordo</em> — rooted in the practice of the early church. The basic shape of this pattern is remarkably consistent across traditions that practice liturgical worship (Catholic, Orthodox, Anglican, Lutheran, and many Reformed and Methodist churches): <em>gathering</em> (the community assembles and is called to worship), <em>word</em> (Scripture is read and preached), <em>table</em> (the Eucharist is celebrated), and <em>sending</em> (the community is dismissed to serve in the world).</p>
