-<p>The contemporary worship movement did not emerge from a vacuum. It was a response to genuine problems in the liturgical tradition as practiced in mid-twentieth-century American Protestantism. Mainline churches in the 1950s and 1960s were, in many cases, liturgically correct but spiritually moribund — going through the motions of worship without passion, conviction, or connection to the lives of ordinary people. The language of the liturgy was archaic, the music was inaccessible, the preaching was cerebral and detached, and the overall atmosphere was formal, reserved, and unwelcoming to outsiders.</p>
