-<p>The contemporary worship movement sought to address these failures by making worship accessible, emotionally engaging, and culturally relevant. Its roots are multiple: the Jesus Movement of the 1970s, which brought folk and rock music into worship; the Vineyard movement of the 1980s, which developed the "worship set" format of extended, emotionally building musical worship; the seeker-sensitive movement of the 1990s, pioneered by Willow Creek Community Church, which redesigned worship to remove barriers for unchurched visitors; and the global influence of Hillsong Church, which produced a stream of worship songs that became the de facto hymnody of evangelical Christianity worldwide.</p>
-
-<p>What the contemporary movement gained was substantial. It made church accessible to millions of people who would never have crossed the threshold of a traditional church. It recovered the emotional dimension of worship — the sense that worship should involve the whole person, not just the intellect. It demonstrated that the gospel could be communicated in the cultural language of the present, not just the cultural language of the sixteenth or seventeenth century. It created a participatory atmosphere in which people felt free to express their devotion physically — through raised hands, through tears, through spontaneous prayer.</p>
