-
-<p>But the gains came at a cost, and the cost has become increasingly visible as the contemporary movement has matured. Several losses deserve attention.</p>
-
-<p>The first is <em>depth of content</em>. The hymns of the liturgical tradition — "A Mighty Fortress Is Our God," "O Sacred Head Now Wounded," "Come, Thou Fount of Every Blessing" — are dense with theological content, drawing on centuries of doctrinal reflection. The average contemporary worship song is built on a much narrower theological range: God's love, God's greatness, the worshipper's response of praise. Studies of the most popular contemporary worship songs have found that they overwhelmingly focus on the subjective experience of the worshipper ("I will praise you," "You are good to me") rather than on the objective acts of God in history (creation, incarnation, crucifixion, resurrection, ascension, coming judgment). The effect, over time, is a congregation that knows how it feels about God but is less clear about who God is and what God has done.</p>
-
-<p>The second loss is <em>continuity with the historic church</em>. When a congregation recites the Nicene Creed, it is saying the same words that Christians have said since 381 AD. When it follows the lectionary, it is reading the same passages that Christians around the world are reading on the same day. When it observes the church calendar — Advent, Christmas, Epiphany, Lent, Holy Week, Easter, Pentecost — it is living inside a narrative structure that shapes the entire year around the story of Christ. The contemporary worship movement, by and large, abandoned these connections. Most non-denominational churches do not follow a lectionary, do not observe the church calendar (beyond Christmas and Easter), do not recite the creeds, and do not celebrate the Eucharist weekly. The result is a form of worship that is cut off from the church's memory — a perpetual present tense with no sense of the centuries of faith that preceded it.</p>
-
