-
-<p>The fourth loss is <em>corporate identity</em>. Liturgical worship is, by design, communal. The congregation speaks together, sings together, confesses together, receives communion together. The individual is absorbed into the body. Contemporary worship, despite its communal setting, can be a surprisingly individualistic experience — a room full of people having private emotional encounters with God, mediated by music and lighting designed to create a particular mood. The shift from hymnals to projected lyrics is emblematic: the hymnal was a shared text that connected the congregation to each other and to a tradition; the projected lyric, appearing and disappearing on a screen, connects the individual to a momentary experience.</p>
-
-<h2>The Worship Wars</h2>
-
-<p>The so-called "worship wars" of the 1990s and 2000s — the bitter conflicts within congregations over music style, instrumentation, and worship format — were never really about music. They were about two competing visions of what the church is and what worship is for. The liturgical vision holds that worship is the church's participation in the eternal worship of God — an objective reality that exists whether or not the worshippers feel anything. The contemporary vision holds that worship is the church's response to God's presence — an experience that should be felt, expressed, and emotionally authentic.</p>
-
