-<h2>The Apostles' Creed</h2>
-
-<p>The Apostles' Creed is the oldest and simplest of the Western creeds. Despite its name, it was not written by the twelve apostles. The legend that each apostle contributed one article is a medieval invention. The creed developed gradually over the second and third centuries, evolving from the baptismal interrogation used in the Roman church: "Do you believe in God the Father Almighty? Do you believe in Jesus Christ, his only Son, our Lord? Do you believe in the Holy Spirit?" The candidate answered each question affirmatively and was immersed three times.</p>
-
-<p>The creed reached its present form by the eighth century, but its core content is much older — it represents the doctrinal consensus of the second-century church. Its structure is Trinitarian: it moves from the Father (creator of heaven and earth), through the Son (conceived, born, suffered, crucified, buried, descended, risen, ascended, seated, coming again), to the Holy Spirit (the holy catholic church, the communion of saints, the forgiveness of sins, the resurrection of the body, the life everlasting).</p>
-
-<p>Every clause of the Apostles' Creed guards against a specific error. "Maker of heaven and earth" rejects the Gnostic claim that the material world was created by an evil deity. "Conceived by the Holy Spirit, born of the Virgin Mary" affirms both the divinity and the genuine humanity of Christ, rejecting Docetism (the denial that Christ had a real body) and Adoptionism (the claim that Jesus was an ordinary man adopted by God). "Suffered under Pontius Pilate" anchors the faith in history — this is not a myth but an event that happened under a particular Roman governor, in a particular place, at a particular time. "He descended into hell" (or "to the dead") affirms that Christ's death was real and complete. "The resurrection of the body" rejects the Greek philosophical preference for the immortality of the soul and insists on the Jewish and Christian hope for the redemption of the whole person — body included.</p>
-
-<h2>The Nicene Creed</h2>
-
-<p>The Nicene Creed is the most important theological document in the history of Christianity. It was produced at the Council of Nicaea in 325 AD and expanded at the Council of Constantinople in 381 AD (hence its technical name: the Niceno-Constantinopolitan Creed). It is affirmed by Catholics, Orthodox, and most Protestants — the only doctrinal statement that can claim truly universal Christian authority.</p>
-
-<p>The creed was forged in the fires of the Arian controversy. Its central concern is the relationship between the Father and the Son, and its key term is <em>homoousios</em> — "of one substance" or "of one being" with the Father. The Son is "God from God, Light from Light, true God from true God, begotten, not made, of one Being with the Father." Every phrase is a rejection of Arian theology. "Begotten, not made" denies that the Son is a creature. "Of one Being with the Father" denies that the Son is a lesser deity. "Through him all things were made" ascribes to the Son the divine prerogative of creation.</p>
-
-<p>The creed also expanded the pneumatological section, affirming the full divinity of the Holy Spirit: "the Lord, the giver of life, who proceeds from the Father, who with the Father and the Son is worshipped and glorified." This was directed against the Pneumatomachians ("Spirit-fighters"), who accepted the divinity of the Son but denied the divinity of the Spirit.</p>
-
-<p>The most famous controversy surrounding the Nicene Creed is the <em>filioque</em> — the Latin word meaning "and the Son," which the Western church added to the creed's statement about the Holy Spirit. The original text says the Spirit "proceeds from the Father." The Western addition says the Spirit "proceeds from the Father <em>and the Son</em>." This addition, which was made unilaterally by the Western church without the consent of an ecumenical council, was one of the theological factors in the Great Schism of 1054 between East and West. The Orthodox churches regard the <em>filioque</em> as both theologically erroneous (it subordinates the Spirit to the Son) and ecclesiologically illegitimate (it altered a conciliar text without conciliar authority). The dispute remains unresolved.</p>
-
-<h2>The Chalcedonian Definition</h2>
-
-<p>The Council of Chalcedon in 451 AD addressed the question that the earlier councils had left partially unanswered: if Christ is fully divine (Nicaea) and fully human (against Apollinarianism, which denied Christ's human mind), how do the two natures relate in one person? The council produced not a creed but a "definition" — a precise, technical statement that established the boundaries within which all subsequent Christological reflection would take place.</p>
-
-<p>The Definition declares that Christ is "acknowledged in two natures, without confusion, without change, without division, without separation." Each of the four negatives targets a specific heresy. "Without confusion" rejects Eutychianism, which taught that Christ's two natures were blended into a single hybrid nature. "Without change" rejects any suggestion that the divine nature was altered by the Incarnation. "Without division" rejects Nestorianism, which was understood to teach that Christ was two persons — one divine and one human — rather than one person with two natures. "Without separation" rejects any attempt to pull the two natures apart as though they were merely associated rather than united.</p>
-
-<p>The Chalcedonian Definition is not a positive explanation of how the Incarnation works. It is a set of boundary markers that defines the space within which orthodox Christology operates. It tells you what you cannot say about Christ (you cannot say the natures are confused, changed, divided, or separated), but it does not tell you exactly how two natures subsist in one person. The mystery remains. The definition protects the mystery from being dissolved by premature explanation.</p>
-
-<h2>Reformation Confessions</h2>
-
-<p>The Protestant Reformation produced its own body of confessional documents, which functioned differently from the ancient creeds. Where the creeds were produced by ecumenical councils and claimed universal authority, the Reformation confessions were produced by particular churches and claimed authority only within those communities.</p>
-
-<p>The Augsburg Confession (1530), written primarily by Philip Melanchthon and presented to the Holy Roman Emperor Charles V, was the foundational confession of Lutheranism. It is irenic in tone, seeking to demonstrate that Lutheran teaching was consistent with the catholic faith and differed from Rome only on specific points of abuse and corruption. The Westminster Confession of Faith (1646), produced by the Westminster Assembly during the English Civil War, became the foundational confession of Presbyterianism. It is comprehensive, precise, and rigorously systematic — a complete theological system in thirty-three chapters. The Heidelberg Catechism (1563), produced in the Palatinate region of Germany, is structured around three themes — human misery, divine deliverance, and grateful obedience — and is notable for its pastoral warmth and devotional quality. The Belgic Confession (1561), written by Guido de Bres, a Dutch Reformed pastor who was martyred for his faith, is a personal and passionate defense of Reformed theology.</p>
-
-<p>These confessions share a common structure: they affirm the authority of Scripture, summarize the doctrines of the Trinity and the person of Christ, articulate a Protestant understanding of justification and sanctification, describe the nature of the church and the sacraments, and address the relationship between church and state. They differ in emphasis, in scope, and in the degree of detail with which they treat disputed questions, but they represent a shared tradition of confessional Protestantism that takes doctrine seriously, believes that the church's faith can and should be articulated in precise language, and submits to the authority of confessional documents as subordinate standards under Scripture.</p>
-
-<h2>Why Some Churches Refuse Creeds</h2>
-
-<p>Not all Christians accept the authority of creeds and confessions. Baptists, for instance, have historically been suspicious of creedalism, insisting that the Bible alone is sufficient as a rule of faith and practice. The Baptist theologian E.Y. Mullins articulated the principle of "soul competency" — the idea that every individual is competent, under the Holy Spirit's guidance, to interpret Scripture for themselves, without the mediation of creeds, confessions, or ecclesiastical authority.</p>
-
-<p>The Restoration Movement (Churches of Christ, Christian Churches) took the anti-creedal position even further, arguing that creeds and confessions are inherently divisive — that they elevate human opinion to the level of divine authority and create tests of fellowship that go beyond Scripture. Thomas Campbell's famous Declaration and Address (1809) argued that "nothing ought to be inculcated upon Christians as articles of faith; nor required of them as terms of communion, but what is expressly taught and enjoined upon them in the word of God."</p>
-
-<p>These traditions do not, however, operate without doctrinal standards. Baptists produce "statements of faith" (the Baptist Faith and Message, for instance) that function in practice very much like confessions — they define the theological boundaries of the community, serve as the basis for hiring decisions in denominational institutions, and articulate the consensus of the denomination on disputed questions. The difference between a "statement of faith" and a "confession" is, in many cases, more rhetorical than substantive. The anti-creedal tradition has creeds; it simply does not call them that.</p>
-
-<h2>The Difference Between a Creed, a Confession, and a Statement of Faith</h2>
-
-<p>The terms are often used interchangeably, but they carry different levels of authority and serve different functions. A <em>creed</em> (from the Latin <em>credo</em>, "I believe") is a concise summary of essential Christian doctrine, typically produced by an ecumenical council and claiming universal authority over all Christians. The Apostles' Creed and the Nicene Creed are the two primary examples. A <em>confession</em> is a more detailed and comprehensive statement of doctrine, produced by a particular church or tradition and claiming authority within that community. The Westminster Confession, the Augsburg Confession, and the Belgic Confession are examples. A <em>statement of faith</em> is typically the least formal and most flexible of the three — a document produced by a particular church or organization that summarizes its theological commitments but may be revised more easily than a confession.</p>
-
-<p>The relationship between these documents and Scripture is crucial. In all traditions that use them, creeds, confessions, and statements of faith are understood as <em>subordinate</em> to Scripture — they are authoritative insofar as they faithfully summarize what Scripture teaches, but they do not possess the same authority as Scripture itself. They can, in principle, be corrected if they are shown to be inconsistent with the biblical text. The difference between Catholic, Orthodox, and Protestant understandings of this relationship is largely a difference about how much weight to give to the church's traditional interpretation of Scripture — but all three families affirm that Scripture is the ultimate norm.</p>
-
-<h2>Why Every Christian Should Know the Nicene Creed</h2>
-
-<p>Even if your church does not recite the Nicene Creed — and many do not — there are compelling reasons to know it intimately. First, it is the closest thing Christianity has to a universally accepted summary of the faith. It is affirmed by Catholics, Orthodox, and the vast majority of Protestants. To know the Nicene Creed is to know what unites the church across its deepest divisions.</p>
-
-<p>Second, the creed is a remarkably efficient theology lesson. In fewer than three hundred words, it covers the doctrine of God, the doctrine of creation, the person and work of Christ (incarnation, crucifixion, resurrection, ascension, and second coming), the person of the Holy Spirit, the nature of the church, the meaning of baptism, and the Christian hope for the resurrection of the dead and the life of the world to come. There is no more compressed summary of Christian doctrine in existence.</p>
-
-<p>Third, the creed connects you to the church across time. When you say, "We believe in one God, the Father Almighty, maker of heaven and earth," you are saying the same words that Athanasius said, that Augustine said, that Thomas Aquinas and Martin Luther and Dietrich Bonhoeffer said. You are joining a conversation that has been going on for seventeen centuries, and you are affirming your continuity with every generation of Christians that preceded you. In an age of rootlessness and historical amnesia, this is no small gift.</p>
-
-<p>Finally, the creed teaches you how to think theologically. Its structure — Father, Son, and Spirit — is the structure of the Christian faith itself. Its careful, precise language — "begotten, not made," "of one Being with the Father," "who proceeds from the Father" — models the kind of theological precision that is necessary when speaking about matters of ultimate importance. Sloppy language about God produces sloppy thinking about God, and sloppy thinking about God produces a faith that cannot withstand pressure. The creed is not a substitute for Scripture. It is a guide to reading Scripture rightly — a map that keeps you on the road that the church has traveled for two millennia, a road that runs straight through the heart of the biblical witness to the God who creates, redeems, and sustains all things.</p>`
