-    body: `<p>In 1960, Dennis Bennett, the rector of St. Mark's Episcopal Church in Van Nuys, California, stood before his congregation and announced that he had received the baptism of the Holy Spirit and spoken in tongues. The announcement produced an immediate crisis. Some parishioners walked out. Bennett resigned under pressure. The story made national news — not because speaking in tongues was new (Pentecostals had been doing it for half a century) but because Bennett was not a Pentecostal. He was an Episcopalian, a mainline Protestant, a man in a clerical collar with a graduate degree. The Holy Spirit, it seemed, had crossed a social boundary that American Christianity thought was firmly established.</p>
-
-<p>Bennett's experience was not an isolated event. It was the opening act of a movement that would, over the next six decades, penetrate every major Christian tradition on earth — Catholic, Orthodox, Anglican, Lutheran, Presbyterian, Methodist, Baptist — and reshape the landscape of global Christianity in ways that are still unfolding. The charismatic movement is, by virtually any measure, the most significant development in Christianity since the Reformation. Understanding it requires understanding its three distinct waves, its theological claims, its extraordinary gifts, and its equally extraordinary dangers.</p>
-
-<h2>The First Wave: Classical Pentecostalism (1901-1960)</h2>
-
-<p>The origins of Pentecostalism are conventionally traced to January 1, 1901, when Agnes Ozman, a student at Charles Parham's Bethel Bible School in Topeka, Kansas, reportedly spoke in tongues after Parham laid hands on her and prayed. Parham, a former Methodist minister, had been studying the book of Acts and had concluded that the "initial evidence" of baptism in the Holy Spirit was speaking in tongues — a doctrine that would become the defining distinctive of classical Pentecostalism.</p>
-
-<p>But it was the Azusa Street Revival of 1906-1909, led by William Seymour, an African American Holiness preacher and former student of Parham, that transformed Pentecostalism from a local curiosity into a global movement. Seymour established a mission in a converted stable at 312 Azusa Street in Los Angeles, and for three years, services ran almost continuously — morning, afternoon, and night — characterized by speaking in tongues, prophetic utterances, healings, and what participants described as overwhelming encounters with the presence of God.</p>
-
-<p>The Azusa Street Revival was remarkable for several reasons beyond its pneumatological claims. It was radically interracial at a time when American society was rigidly segregated. Frank Bartleman, a white journalist who covered the revival, wrote, "The color line was washed away in the blood." Black, white, Hispanic, and Asian worshippers prayed together, laid hands on each other, and shared leadership in ways that were extraordinary for 1906 Los Angeles — or for most of American Christianity a century later. This interracial character was not incidental to the movement's theology; Seymour understood the crossing of racial boundaries as itself a sign of the Spirit's work, a fulfillment of the Pentecost narrative in which the Spirit was poured out "on all flesh."</p>
-
-<p>The interracial unity did not last. Within a decade, the Pentecostal movement had largely segregated along racial lines, producing separate denominational structures for black and white believers. The Assemblies of God, founded in 1914, was predominantly white. The Church of God in Christ, which had actually predated the Assemblies of God as an organized body, became the primary African American Pentecostal denomination. The racial division of the movement was one of its earliest and most consequential failures — a failure that Pentecostal historians have increasingly confronted with candor.</p>
-
-<p>Classical Pentecostalism established several theological distinctives that would shape all subsequent waves: the doctrine of baptism in the Holy Spirit as a distinct, post-conversion experience; the expectation of speaking in tongues as the initial evidence of this baptism; the continued operation of all the gifts of the Spirit described in the New Testament (prophecy, healing, miracles, discernment of spirits); and an eschatological urgency rooted in the conviction that the outpouring of the Spirit was a sign of the last days.</p>
-
-<h2>The Second Wave: Charismatic Renewal (1960s-1980s)</h2>
-
-<p>Dennis Bennett's 1960 announcement marked the beginning of what historians call the charismatic renewal — the penetration of Pentecostal experience into the mainline Protestant and Catholic churches. The distinction between "Pentecostal" and "charismatic" is significant: Pentecostals left (or were expelled from) their existing denominations and formed new ones. Charismatics, by contrast, remained within their denominations, seeking to renew them from within.</p>
-
-<p>The charismatic renewal spread with remarkable speed across denominational lines. By the mid-1960s, there were charismatic prayer groups and renewal movements among Episcopalians, Lutherans, Presbyterians, and Methodists. The movement attracted both clergy and laity, and it produced a substantial body of theological reflection that attempted to integrate charismatic experience with the doctrinal traditions of the host denominations.</p>
-
-<p>The most significant — and to many observers, the most surprising — development was the emergence of the Catholic charismatic renewal. In February 1967, a group of faculty and students at Duquesne University in Pittsburgh attended a retreat during which several participants reported being baptized in the Holy Spirit and speaking in tongues. The movement spread rapidly to the University of Notre Dame and the University of Michigan, and within a decade, it had become one of the largest movements in the Catholic Church, with millions of participants worldwide.</p>
-
-<p>The Catholic charismatic renewal was theologically distinctive in ways that set it apart from both classical Pentecostalism and Protestant charismatic renewal. Catholic charismatics did not generally teach that tongues were the initial evidence of Spirit baptism — a doctrine that sat uneasily with Catholic sacramental theology, which understood baptism and confirmation as the normative channels of the Spirit's gift. Instead, they spoke of a "release" or "renewal" of the Spirit already received in the sacraments. They maintained their commitment to the Mass, to the Marian doctrines, to papal authority, and to the full range of Catholic devotional practice. The renewal was not a Protestantization of Catholicism; it was an experiential intensification of an existing sacramental framework.</p>
-
-<p>Pope Paul VI cautiously welcomed the movement, and subsequent popes — John Paul II, Benedict XVI, and Francis — have affirmed its place within the life of the church, while also warning against excesses. Today, the Catholic charismatic renewal claims over 120 million participants worldwide, making it one of the largest movements in the history of Catholicism.</p>
-
-<h2>The Third Wave (1980s-Present)</h2>
-
-<p>The "Third Wave" is a term coined by C. Peter Wagner, a former professor at Fuller Theological Seminary's School of World Mission, to describe a movement of Christians who practice charismatic gifts (healing, prophecy, spiritual warfare) without identifying as either Pentecostal or charismatic. Third Wave theology emphasizes "signs and wonders" as normative for Christian ministry, and it tends to be less interested in the doctrine of Spirit baptism (the defining concern of classical Pentecostalism) and more interested in the practical exercise of spiritual power.</p>
-
-<p>The Vineyard movement, founded by John Wimber in the early 1980s, was the most influential institutional expression of the Third Wave. Wimber, a former musician and church growth consultant, combined Reformed evangelical theology with an openness to the gifts of the Spirit that he called "power evangelism." Vineyard worship — intimate, emotionally intense, focused on personal encounter with God — became the template for contemporary worship in evangelical churches worldwide, whether or not those churches identified as charismatic.</p>
