-<p>The Third Wave also produced the controversial "Toronto Blessing" (1994) and the Brownsville Revival (1995), both characterized by unusual physical manifestations — "holy laughter," falling, shaking, animal sounds — that deeply divided the evangelical world. Supporters saw these manifestations as evidence of the Spirit's overwhelming presence. Critics saw them as emotional excess, psychological manipulation, or worse. The debates revealed a fault line within evangelical Christianity between those who regarded experiential phenomena as inherently suspicious and those who regarded the absence of experiential phenomena as evidence of spiritual deadness.</p>
-
-<h2>Cessationism vs. Continuationism</h2>
-
-<p>The theological debate behind the charismatic movement is, at its core, a debate about whether the miraculous gifts described in the New Testament — tongues, prophecy, healing, miracles — continue to operate in the church today or ceased with the death of the apostles and the completion of the New Testament canon.</p>
-
-<p>Cessationism — the view that the miraculous gifts ceased — has been the dominant position in Reformed and confessional Protestant theology since the Reformation. Its most influential modern articulation is Benjamin Warfield's <em>Counterfeit Miracles</em> (1918), which argued that miracles served as the credentials of the apostles and were no longer necessary once the apostolic testimony was inscripturated in the New Testament. John MacArthur's <em>Strange Fire</em> (2013) represented a more combative version of the same argument, accusing the charismatic movement of blasphemy against the Holy Spirit.</p>
-
-<p>Continuationism — the view that all the gifts continue — argues that there is no biblical warrant for a cessation of any spiritual gift. The New Testament nowhere teaches that the gifts will cease before the return of Christ. Paul's statement that tongues "will cease" and prophecy "will pass away" (1 Corinthians 13:8) is set in the context of the coming of "the perfect" (1 Corinthians 13:10), which continuationists identify not with the completion of the canon but with the return of Christ and the full consummation of the kingdom.</p>
-
-<p>A significant middle position has emerged in recent decades, sometimes called "open but cautious" — the view that the gifts have not necessarily ceased but that their contemporary manifestations should be evaluated with rigorous biblical and theological discernment. This position, associated with scholars like D.A. Carson and Sam Storms, accepts the theoretical possibility of continued miraculous gifts while maintaining a high bar for their authentication.</p>
-
-<h2>What the Movement Contributed</h2>
-
-<p>The charismatic movement's contributions to global Christianity are substantial and should be acknowledged even by those who disagree with its theological claims. First, it recovered the experiential dimension of faith in traditions that had become arid and intellectualized. The mainline Protestant churches of the mid-twentieth century were, in many cases, so focused on theological precision and social respectability that they had lost the capacity to speak of — or expect — a living encounter with God. The charismatic renewal reminded these churches that Christianity is not merely a system of beliefs but a relationship with a living person, and that the Holy Spirit is not merely a doctrine but a presence.</p>
-
-<p>Second, the movement democratized ministry. Classical Pentecostalism's insistence that the Spirit's gifts are available to all believers — regardless of education, ordination, gender, or race — was a genuinely radical claim with practical consequences. It empowered lay people, elevated women in ministry (many early Pentecostal denominations ordained women long before mainline Protestants did), and created space for leadership from the margins of society.</p>
-
-<p>Third, the charismatic movement has been the primary engine of Christian growth in the Global South. The explosive growth of Christianity in sub-Saharan Africa, Latin America, and East Asia over the past century has been overwhelmingly Pentecostal and charismatic in character. Philip Jenkins's observation that the "typical" Christian in the twenty-first century is not a European or American churchgoer but an African or Latin American Pentecostal is one of the most important demographic facts about contemporary Christianity.</p>
-
-<h2>What the Movement Risks</h2>
-
-<p>The charismatic movement's vulnerabilities are as significant as its strengths, and honesty requires naming them. The first risk is <em>anti-intellectualism</em>. The emphasis on experience over doctrine, on encounter over analysis, on the Spirit's leading over theological education, has in many contexts produced a Christianity that is emotionally vibrant but theologically shallow. When "God told me" becomes a substitute for careful biblical exegesis, the result is a faith that is vulnerable to every wind of doctrine.</p>
-
-<p>The second risk is <em>authoritarianism</em>. The charismatic movement's emphasis on prophetic authority — the claim that specific individuals can receive and communicate direct messages from God — creates a structural vulnerability to spiritual abuse. When a pastor or prophet claims to speak with divine authority, dissent becomes not merely disagreement but rebellion against God. The history of the charismatic movement is littered with leaders who used claims of prophetic authority to control their followers, suppress criticism, and shield themselves from accountability.</p>
-
-<p>The third risk is <em>emotional manipulation</em>. The worship practices of charismatic churches — extended singing, repetitive musical patterns, high emotional intensity, altar calls, prayers for healing — can produce genuine spiritual encounters, but they can also produce manufactured emotional experiences that are mistaken for the work of the Spirit. The line between the two is not always easy to discern, and the charismatic movement has not always been careful about discerning it.</p>
-
-<h2>The New Apostolic Reformation</h2>
-
-<p>The most controversial development within the charismatic world in recent decades is the New Apostolic Reformation (NAR) — a loosely organized movement that teaches that God is restoring the offices of apostle and prophet to the contemporary church, and that these new apostles and prophets have authority to direct the church and to influence the political and cultural direction of nations. The movement's leading figures — C. Peter Wagner (who coined the term before his death in 2016), Che Ahn, Bill Johnson, Mike Bickle, and others — teach that the church is entering a new era of spiritual power in which believers will exercise dominion over every sphere of society: government, business, education, media, arts, family, and religion.</p>
-
-<p>The NAR has drawn sharp criticism from both cessationists and mainstream charismatics. Its theology of apostolic authority — which effectively places certain individuals above the accountability structures of the local church — creates obvious potential for abuse. Its "dominion theology" — the teaching that Christians are called to take control of societal institutions before Christ's return — represents a significant departure from the eschatological theology of classical Pentecostalism, which was premillennial and generally apolitical, expecting the world to grow worse before Christ's return rather than better.</p>
-
-<p>The intersection of NAR theology with American political movements — particularly its involvement in the events surrounding the 2020 presidential election and its aftermath — has brought the movement unprecedented public scrutiny. Self-identified prophets within the NAR prophesied specific political outcomes that did not occur, generating a crisis of credibility that the movement has not fully reckoned with. The episode revealed the dangers of combining charismatic authority claims with political activism — a combination that can produce a form of Christianity that is impervious to correction, because its leaders claim to speak for God and its followers have been conditioned to regard skepticism as a lack of faith.</p>
-
-<p>The charismatic movement, in all its waves and expressions, represents one of the most consequential developments in the history of Christianity. Its best impulses — the recovery of spiritual experience, the empowerment of the laity, the expectation that God acts in the present tense — are genuine gifts to the church. Its worst impulses — authoritarianism, anti-intellectualism, the confusion of emotional intensity with spiritual depth — are genuine dangers. The movement cannot be dismissed, and it cannot be embraced uncritically. It can only be engaged with the kind of careful, biblically grounded, theologically informed discernment that the best of the charismatic tradition itself calls for — the discernment of spirits, exercised not in fear but in love, and not in hostility but in hope for the church's faithfulness to the God who is, as the charismatics never tire of reminding us, still speaking.</p>`
