-<p>Diana Butler Bass, in her work on the changing landscape of American religion, has drawn a useful distinction between "believing, behaving, belonging" — the traditional model in which one first accepts a set of doctrines, then conforms one's behavior to institutional expectations, and finally joins a community — and a newer pattern in which people seek belonging first, then explore behavior and belief within a context of genuine relationship. The institutional church, structured around the old model, is struggling to make space for the new one.</p>
-
-<p>The theologian Karl Rahner once wrote of "anonymous Christians" — people who live in authentic relationship with God without the explicit framework of Christian doctrine or institutional membership. The concept was controversial in his day and remains so. But it points to a reality that the church has never been entirely comfortable with: God's work in the world is not limited to the church's walls, and the Spirit moves where the Spirit wills, institutional boundaries notwithstanding.</p>
