-
-<p>The most revealing data about church leavers concerns not what they have rejected but what they still desire. Study after study shows that people who leave the church continue to hunger for community, for meaning, for moral formation, for connection to something larger than themselves. They want to ask the big questions — about suffering, purpose, death, justice, love — in a space that takes those questions seriously without demanding premature answers.</p>
-
-<p>They want authenticity. Not perfection, not performance, not the polished production values of a megachurch service — but genuine human encounter, honest acknowledgment of doubt and struggle, leadership that is willing to say "I don't know" and "I was wrong." They want a community that practices what it preaches, not one that preaches ever more loudly to compensate for its failure to practice.</p>
