-    body: `<p>In certain corners of American Christianity, "deconstruction" has become the most dangerous word in the vocabulary. It is spoken with the same hushed alarm once reserved for words like "liberal" or "heretical." Pastors preach against it. Conferences are organized to combat it. Books are written to warn young Christians away from it. The assumption, sometimes explicit and sometimes merely implied, is that to deconstruct one's faith is to destroy it — that the person who begins asking hard questions about what they were taught will inevitably end up with nothing at all.</p>
