-<h2>What Deconstruction Actually Means</h2>
-
-<p>The term "deconstruction" entered popular use through the work of the French philosopher Jacques Derrida, but its current usage in religious contexts bears only a loose relationship to Derridean philosophy. In the context of faith, deconstruction refers to the process of critically examining the beliefs, assumptions, and practices one has inherited — distinguishing between the core of the faith and the cultural packaging in which it arrived, between what one genuinely believes and what one was simply told to believe, between the living God and the institutional structures that claim to represent God.</p>
