-<p>This is not, despite what its critics insist, an inherently nihilistic process. It is an act of discernment. The person who deconstructs is not necessarily saying, "None of this is true." They are saying, "I need to determine for myself what is true, rather than accepting a pre-packaged set of conclusions I was handed before I was old enough to evaluate them." This is not the destruction of faith. It is the maturation of faith — the movement from a borrowed faith to an owned faith, from inherited certainty to hard-won conviction.</p>
