-<p>The second mistake is assuming that the nones are ignorant of Christianity and need to be educated. In fact, many nones — particularly those raised in Christian households — are quite knowledgeable about Christianity. They have not failed to hear the church's message. They have heard it, evaluated it, and found it wanting — not necessarily the message of Jesus, but the message of the institution that claims to represent Jesus.</p>
-
-<p>The third mistake is approaching the nones apologetically — in the philosophical sense, offering arguments for the existence of God, the reliability of the Bible, or the historical evidence for the resurrection. These arguments have their place, but they are largely irrelevant to a population whose objections are not primarily intellectual but moral and institutional. A person who left the church because it covered up sexual abuse does not need a better argument for the resurrection. They need to see an institution that tells the truth and protects the vulnerable.</p>
-
-<p>The fourth mistake is assuming that the nones will "come back" when they get older, get married, or have children. This assumption is based on historical patterns that no longer hold. Previous generations did tend to return to church during the family-formation years. The current generation is not following that pattern. Nones who marry tend to marry other nones, and nones who have children tend to raise them without organized religion. The pipeline that once returned lapsed Christians to the pews has been broken.</p>
-
-<h2>What the Nones Might Actually Respond To</h2>
-
-<p>If the nones are not a marketing problem, an education problem, or an apologetics problem, what are they? They are, above all, a credibility problem. The church has lost credibility with a significant portion of the population, and credibility cannot be restored through better programming or more persuasive arguments. It can only be restored through changed behavior sustained over time.</p>
-
-<p>The nones respond to authenticity — not the curated authenticity of a pastor sharing a carefully calibrated vulnerability from the stage, but the genuine authenticity of a community that acknowledges its failures, welcomes doubt, practices generosity without strings, and treats every human being with dignity regardless of their beliefs, identity, or behavior.</p>
-
-<p>The nones respond to justice. They care about the poor, the marginalized, the planet. They are drawn to communities that show up for the vulnerable — not as a recruitment strategy but as a fundamental expression of what they believe. A church that is visibly, consistently, and sacrificially committed to justice will earn the respect of nones even if it does not earn their membership.</p>
-
-<p>The nones respond to honesty about complexity. They live in a world of irreducible complexity — moral, intellectual, spiritual — and they are drawn to communities that can hold that complexity without collapsing it into simple answers. A church that can say "this is hard" and "we are not certain" and "we are still learning" is a church that speaks the language the nones actually speak.</p>
-
-<blockquote>The rise of the nones is not a verdict on God. It is a verdict on the church. And verdicts, unlike sentences, can be appealed — but only if the institution is willing to change the behavior that produced them.</blockquote>
-
-<p>The church cannot reach the nones by becoming something it is not. But it might reach them by becoming what it claims to be — a community shaped by the radical love, disarming honesty, and costly justice of the one it calls Lord. The nones are not waiting for a better church service. They are waiting for a better church. Whether the institution can become one remains the open question of American religion in the twenty-first century.</p>`,
