-    body: `<p>The hashtag appeared on Twitter in 2016, coined by Blake Chastain, a podcaster and writer from the American Midwest. #Exvangelical. It was not a theological treatise or a manifesto. It was a naming — an attempt to give language to an experience that millions of people were living but that had no public vocabulary. Within months, it had become a movement, or at least the outline of one: a sprawling, decentralized community of people who had left evangelicalism and were trying to figure out who they were on the other side.</p>
-
-<p>The timing was not accidental. The year 2016, with the election of Donald Trump and the overwhelming support he received from white evangelical voters, was a catalytic event for many who had been quietly uncomfortable with the direction of American evangelicalism. For some, it was the final confirmation of suspicions they had held for years. For others, it was a sudden, disorienting rupture — the moment when the gap between the Jesus they had been taught to follow and the political movement that bore his name became too wide to bridge.</p>
-
-<p>But the exvangelical movement did not begin in 2016. It began in living rooms and therapist's offices and late-night conversations, years and sometimes decades before a hashtag gave it a name. It began every time a person raised in the evangelical world looked at what that world had become and said, quietly or loudly, "I cannot do this anymore."</p>
-
-<h2>Who Exvangelicals Are</h2>
-
-<p>The first and most important thing to understand about exvangelicals is what they are not. They are not, as a group, atheists. They are not angry people who want to destroy Christianity. They are not victims of secular culture who were seduced away from the truth. They are, overwhelmingly, people who took their faith seriously — often more seriously than the institutions they left — and who found that the version of Christianity they were given could not sustain the weight of their honest experience.</p>
-
-<p>Many exvangelicals remain Christians. They have moved to mainline Protestant churches, to Catholic or Orthodox traditions, to progressive evangelical communities, or to non-denominational churches that share none of the cultural markers of the evangelicalism they left. They still pray. They still read Scripture. They still follow Jesus. What they have left is not Christianity but a particular cultural expression of Christianity that they found to be toxic, dishonest, or untenable.</p>
-
-<p>Others have moved to a more ambiguous spiritual space — still believing in God, still shaped by the moral and spiritual formation of their evangelical upbringing, but no longer able to identify with any particular tradition. They are, in sociological terms, "spiritual but not religious," though that phrase does not capture the specificity of their experience. They are not people who never had a religion. They are people who had one — intensely, formatively, all-consumingly — and who had to walk away from it for the sake of their integrity, their mental health, or both.</p>
-
-<p>A smaller number have left religious belief entirely. For them, the process of examining evangelicalism led to the conclusion that the entire framework — not just the evangelical version, but the theistic worldview itself — could not be sustained. These are the genuine deconverts, and their experience deserves respect rather than the contemptuous dismissal it often receives from the communities they left.</p>
-
-<h2>The Distinction That Matters</h2>
-
-<p>The evangelical establishment has a strong institutional incentive to collapse the distinction between leaving evangelicalism and leaving Christianity. If every departure can be framed as apostasy, then the institution is absolved of responsibility. The problem is not with us, the logic goes; the problem is with people who lack the faith to endure. This framing protects the institution but bears almost no relationship to the actual experience of most exvangelicals.</p>
-
-<p>Leaving evangelicalism is not the same as leaving Christianity, any more than leaving a particular marriage is the same as rejecting the concept of love. Evangelicalism is a specific cultural, theological, and political tradition within the broader Christian family. It has distinctive beliefs (biblical inerrancy, penal substitutionary atonement, the necessity of personal conversion), distinctive practices (altar calls, quiet times, accountability groups), and distinctive cultural markers (Christian music, purity culture, political conservatism). A person can reject every one of these distinctive features and still be a follower of Jesus. The conflation of evangelicalism with Christianity is itself a form of the tribalism that drives people away.</p>
-
-<p>This distinction matters practically as well as theologically. When a person leaves evangelicalism and the community responds as though they have left God, the community inflicts a wound that is both unnecessary and deeply damaging. The person who is already grieving the loss of their community is told that their grief is a sign of spiritual failure. The person who is trying to preserve their faith in a new form is told that any form other than the one they left is no faith at all. This response does not protect faith. It destroys it.</p>
-
-<h2>Common Catalysts</h2>
-
-<p>The catalysts that precipitate an exvangelical departure are varied, but several recur with striking frequency across the literature, the podcasts, and the personal testimonies that constitute the movement's archive.</p>
-
-<p>The Trump era crystallized a dissonance that many had felt for years. When 81 percent of white evangelicals voted for a candidate whose personal life, business practices, and rhetorical style represented the antithesis of the values evangelicalism claimed to hold — when evangelical leaders who had spent decades insisting on "character" in political leaders suddenly discovered that character was negotiable — the hypocrisy was too flagrant to ignore. For many exvangelicals, the question was not "How can Christians support this?" but rather "What does it say about the movement that this support was so easy and so enthusiastic?"</p>
-
-<p>The church's posture toward LGBTQ+ people has been another major catalyst. A generation of evangelicals grew up being taught that homosexuality was a uniquely grievous sin, that LGBTQ+ people were threats to the family and to civilization, and that the church's opposition to LGBTQ+ rights was a matter of unwavering biblical fidelity. Then they met actual LGBTQ+ people — or discovered that they themselves were LGBTQ+ — and found that the theology they had been given could not account for the full, complex, dignified humanity of the people it condemned. The choice between their theology and their conscience was not a choice between faith and the world. It was a choice between a theology of exclusion and the inclusive love they believed Jesus actually embodied.</p>
-
-<p>Abuse — sexual, spiritual, and emotional — has driven countless departures. The revelations about Ravi Zacharias, Bill Hybels, Mark Driscoll, Jerry Falwell Jr., and the broader SBC abuse crisis created a cascading loss of trust that extended far beyond the specific perpetrators. If the most celebrated leaders of the movement were predators, and if the institutional structures of the movement protected them rather than their victims, then the movement's claim to moral authority was not merely damaged but forfeit.</p>
-
-<p>Racial justice has been an increasingly significant catalyst, particularly for exvangelicals of color but also for white evangelicals whose commitment to racial equity brought them into conflict with their communities. The evangelical church's response to the Black Lives Matter movement, to police violence against Black Americans, and to the broader conversation about systemic racism revealed a fault line that many could no longer straddle. A tradition that could not say "Black lives matter" without qualification had, for many, revealed something fundamental about its moral priorities.</p>
-
-<blockquote>The exvangelical departure is not a rejection of faith. It is an insistence that faith must be more than the cultural and political package in which it was delivered.</blockquote>
-
-<h2>Where They Go</h2>
-
-<p>The geography of exvangelical life after departure is varied and often surprising. Some find homes in traditions they never expected. The migration of former evangelicals to the Episcopal Church, the ELCA, the United Church of Christ, and other mainline traditions has been well documented. These churches offer liturgical depth, intellectual freedom, social justice commitments, and a theological tradition that does not demand the sacrificium intellectus — the sacrifice of the intellect — that evangelicalism so often requires.</p>
-
-<p>Others have found their way to the Catholic Church or to Eastern Orthodoxy, drawn by the historical rootedness, the sacramental theology, and the contemplative tradition that evangelicalism, in its emphasis on personal experience and biblical propositionalism, often lacks. The irony is not lost on anyone: traditions that evangelicalism spent decades dismissing as dead, ritualistic, or insufficiently biblical are becoming places of refuge for people whom evangelicalism failed.</p>
-
-<p>A significant number have not landed anywhere institutionally. They exist in a kind of ecclesiastical diaspora — reading theology, listening to podcasts, meeting informally with others on similar journeys, practicing faith in forms that do not fit any denominational category. This is not, as critics charge, mere consumerism or spiritual laziness. It is the honest response of people who have been deeply wounded by institutional religion and who need time — sometimes years — before they can trust an institution again.</p>
-
-<h2>The Grief of Leaving</h2>
-
-<p>What is often overlooked in discussions of the exvangelical movement is the grief. Leaving evangelicalism is not like leaving a club or changing a preference. For people raised in the tradition, evangelicalism was not a part of their life. It was their life. It was their community, their social world, their moral framework, their understanding of reality, their source of meaning and identity. To leave it is to lose, in one motion, friends, family relationships, community, certainty, and a coherent narrative about who you are and what the world means.</p>
-
-<p>The grief is compounded by the fact that evangelical communities often treat departure as betrayal. The person who leaves is not merely someone who made a different choice. They are a backslider, a prodigal, a cautionary tale. Former friends distance themselves. Family relationships become strained or fractured. The leaver finds themselves grieving not only the community they lost but the relationships that community withdrew.</p>
-
-<p>This grief is real and should be honored. It is not a sign of weakness or indecision. It is the natural response of a human being who has lost a world. And it is, for many exvangelicals, one of the most painful experiences of their lives — more painful, often, than the specific grievances that precipitated their departure.</p>
-
-<p>The church that takes this grief seriously — that acknowledges its own role in causing it, that reaches out with compassion rather than condemnation, that says "we understand why you left and we are sorry for the pain we caused" — is a church that has begun to reckon with the reality of what the exvangelical movement represents. It represents not the triumph of secularism or the weakness of faith but the failure of a particular tradition to embody the love it proclaims.</p>
-
-<p>The exvangelical is not the enemy of Christianity. The exvangelical is the church's most honest mirror. And what the mirror shows is not comfortable — but it is, for those with the courage to look, a gift.</p>`,
