-    body: `<p>In 1993, the Southern Baptist Convention launched a campaign called "True Love Waits." Teenagers were invited to sign pledge cards committing to sexual abstinence until marriage. The cards were collected, stacked, and displayed in public spectacles designed to demonstrate the movement's scale: 210,000 cards staked on the National Mall in Washington, D.C., 350,000 more displayed at subsequent events. The campaign spread to churches across the evangelical world, spawning an entire industry of conferences, curricula, rings, t-shirts, and branded merchandise built around a single message: your sexual purity is the most important thing about you, and if you lose it, you have lost something that can never be recovered.</p>
-
-<p>Four years later, Joshua Harris, a homeschooled Christian who had written it at twenty-one, published <em>I Kissed Dating Goodbye</em>, a book that extended the purity framework beyond sex to encompass all romantic relationships. Harris argued that the modern dating system was fundamentally broken, that emotional attachments before marriage were spiritually dangerous, and that the godly alternative was "courtship" — a parent-supervised process in which romantic relationships were undertaken only with the explicit intention of marriage. The book sold over a million copies and became a foundational text for a generation of young evangelicals.</p>
-
-<p>These two phenomena — True Love Waits and Harris's book — were the most visible expressions of what has come to be called "purity culture," a broad movement within American evangelicalism that placed sexual behavior at the center of Christian identity and made virginity the primary marker of spiritual faithfulness. The movement was enormous in its reach, deeply formative in its effects, and, as a growing body of testimony and research now makes clear, profoundly damaging in its consequences.</p>
-
-<h2>The Theology Behind the Movement</h2>
-
-<p>Purity culture presented itself as simple biblical fidelity — a straightforward application of scriptural teaching about sexual ethics to the challenges of contemporary adolescence. But the theology that actually undergirded the movement was considerably thinner and more ideologically driven than its proponents acknowledged.</p>
-
-<p>The movement drew selectively from Scripture, emphasizing certain texts (Paul's instructions about sexual immorality, the Proverbs warnings about the "adulterous woman") while ignoring others (the Song of Solomon's celebration of erotic desire, the complex sexual ethics of the patriarchal narratives, the conspicuous absence of sexual purity as a topic in Jesus's recorded teaching). It constructed a theology of sexuality that was, in practice, less about what the Bible says than about what a particular strand of twentieth-century American evangelicalism believed the Bible should say.</p>
-
-<p>At the center of this theology was a set of interconnected assumptions. First, that sexual sin was categorically different from and worse than other sins — that it damaged the soul in a way that lying, greed, or cruelty did not. Second, that sexual purity was primarily a female responsibility, since women were the "gatekeepers" of sexual behavior and bore the greater consequence of its violation. Third, that virginity was a kind of spiritual commodity — something possessed, something that could be "given away" or "lost," something whose absence permanently diminished a person's value as a marriage partner and, implicitly, as a human being.</p>
-
-<p>Fourth, and perhaps most damaging, purity culture taught that sexual desire itself was dangerous and suspect — that the body's natural response to attraction was a temptation to be suppressed rather than a capacity to be understood, stewarded, and eventually celebrated within its proper context. The message was not "sexuality is a gift that requires wisdom and care." The message was "sexuality is a threat that requires constant vigilance and control."</p>
-
-<h2>The Gendered Architecture of Purity</h2>
-
-<p>Purity culture was ostensibly directed at both young men and young women, but its actual architecture was profoundly gendered. Young women bore the disproportionate weight of the movement's expectations and the disproportionate punishment for its violations.</p>
-
-<p>Girls were taught that their bodies were sources of temptation to boys and men — that the way they dressed, moved, and presented themselves was responsible for the sexual thoughts and behaviors of the males around them. The modesty teachings that accompanied purity culture placed the burden of male self-control on female bodies. A girl who wore a tank top was not simply making a clothing choice; she was "causing her brother to stumble." The theological framework that was supposed to protect young women instead taught them that they were inherently dangerous — that their bodies were problems to be managed rather than selves to be inhabited.</p>
-
-<p>Boys received a different but equally distorted message. They were taught that they were fundamentally unable to control their sexual impulses — that male sexuality was a raging fire that, if not rigorously contained, would inevitably consume everything in its path. This teaching was simultaneously demeaning and exculpatory: it reduced young men to their libidos while providing a ready-made excuse for predatory behavior. "Boys will be boys" is not a Christian theology of masculinity. It is the abdication of one.</p>
-
-<p>The combined effect of these gendered messages was a system in which girls were responsible for everyone's purity and boys were responsible for no one's. When sexual boundaries were violated — and in the real world, they frequently were — the girl bore the shame. She was the one who had "lost" something. She was the one who was damaged goods. She was the one who would need to confess her failure to a future spouse and hope for his gracious acceptance of her diminished state.</p>
-
-<blockquote>Purity culture did not elevate women. It commodified them. It reduced their worth to a biological status and their identity to a single metric of compliance.</blockquote>
-
-<h2>What the Research Shows</h2>
-
-<p>The empirical evidence on purity culture's effects has been accumulating for two decades, and it paints a damning picture. First, on its own terms, purity culture failed. Studies consistently show that abstinence pledges have little long-term effect on sexual behavior. A landmark study by Peter Bearman and Hannah Bruckner, published in the <em>American Journal of Sociology</em> in 2001, found that while virginity pledges delayed the onset of sexual activity by an average of eighteen months, their 2005 follow-up in the <em>Journal of Adolescent Health</em> found that pledge-takers who did become sexually active were significantly less likely to use contraception than their non-pledging peers. They were not having less sex. They were having less safe sex.</p>
-
-<p>A subsequent study by Janet Rosenbaum, published in <em>Pediatrics</em>, found that five years after taking a virginity pledge, pledgers and non-pledgers had the same rates of premarital sexual activity and sexually transmitted infections — but pledgers were less likely to have used contraception. The pledge produced guilt, not abstinence; shame, not safety.</p>
-
-<p>Beyond the public health data, a growing body of research documents the psychological damage purity culture inflicts. Linda Kay Klein, in her book <em>Pure: Inside the Evangelical Movement That Shamed a Generation of Young Women and How I Broke Free</em>, draws on over a decade of interviews with women raised in purity culture. Her findings are consistent and disturbing: anxiety, depression, sexual dysfunction, chronic shame, difficulty experiencing pleasure, inability to trust their own bodies, and a persistent sense of being fundamentally damaged — even among women who "did everything right" and remained virgins until marriage.</p>
-
-<p>The sexual dysfunction finding is particularly significant. Purity culture teaches young people to suppress and pathologize their sexual responses for years or decades, then expects them to flip a switch on their wedding night and experience sexuality as a joyful gift. The psychological research on conditioned responses makes clear that this expectation is not merely unrealistic but neurologically incoherent. You cannot train a person to associate sex with shame, danger, and contamination for fifteen years and then expect that association to disappear because a ceremony was performed.</p>
-
-<h2>The Theological Correction</h2>
-
-<p>The theological problems with purity culture are not minor. They are foundational. And correcting them does not require abandoning a Christian sexual ethic. It requires recovering one.</p>
-
-<p>First, purity culture's elevation of sexual sin above all other sins has no basis in the teaching of Jesus. In the Gospels, Jesus reserves his harshest condemnation not for sexual sinners but for religious hypocrites — for those who use their piety as a weapon against the vulnerable, who bind heavy burdens on others' shoulders while refusing to lift a finger to help. The woman caught in adultery is not condemned; the Pharisees who brought her are challenged. The tax collectors and prostitutes, Jesus says, are entering the kingdom of God ahead of the religious establishment (Matthew 21:31). Purity culture inverted Jesus's moral priorities.</p>
-
-<p>Second, the Bible's understanding of purity is not primarily about sex. The Hebrew concept of <em>taharah</em> (ritual purity) encompasses a vast range of conditions and practices — dietary laws, skin diseases, bodily emissions, contact with death — and it is fundamentally about readiness to enter God's presence, not about moral status. When Jesus declared all foods clean (Mark 7:19) and touched lepers without contracting impurity, he was dismantling the purity system's power to exclude. Purity culture, ironically, rebuilt a system of exclusion that Jesus came to abolish.</p>
-
-<p>Third, the commodification of virginity — the idea that a person's worth is diminished by sexual experience — is incompatible with a gospel of grace. If the central claim of Christianity is that human worth is not earned by performance but given by God, then a theology that locates a person's value in a biological status is not Christian theology. It is works-righteousness applied to the body. The doctrine of grace means, among other things, that no experience — sexual or otherwise — can diminish a person's worth in the eyes of God.</p>
-
-<p>Fourth, the biblical tradition contains a rich, positive theology of sexuality that purity culture systematically ignored. The Song of Solomon celebrates erotic desire with language so explicit that rabbinical tradition forbade its reading by anyone under thirty. The creation narrative in Genesis describes the union of man and woman not as a concession to human weakness but as a fundamental expression of what it means to be made in God's image. Paul's metaphor of the body as a temple of the Holy Spirit (1 Corinthians 6:19) is not, in context, a warning against contamination. It is a statement of dignity — the body is sacred, not because it is fragile and easily ruined, but because it is inhabited by God and therefore worthy of honor.</p>
-
-<h2>Toward a Healthy Sexual Theology</h2>
-
-<p>A Christian sexual theology worthy of the name would look radically different from what purity culture offered. It would begin not with prohibition but with affirmation — the affirmation that sexuality is a good gift, created by God, embedded in the very structure of human existence, and intended for joy, intimacy, and connection. It would treat sexual desire not as a problem to be solved but as a capacity to be understood and wisely directed.</p>
-
-<p>It would place sexual ethics within the broader framework of Christian ethics — the ethics of love, justice, mutuality, and care for the other. The question would not be "How far can I go without sinning?" but rather "How can I honor the full humanity of the other person and my own?" This is a more demanding ethic than purity culture's rule-based approach, because it requires moral discernment rather than mere compliance. But it is also a more humane one, because it treats people as moral agents rather than potential contaminants.</p>
-
-<p>It would refuse the gendered double standard that purity culture both created and reinforced. It would insist that men are capable of self-control and that women are not responsible for men's thoughts. It would teach young people of all genders that their bodies are their own — sacred, worthy of care, and not available for the regulation of others.</p>
-
-<p>It would hold whatever boundaries it holds with compassion rather than shame. A tradition that believes certain sexual behaviors are reserved for certain contexts can hold that belief without treating those who fall short as damaged goods. The difference between a boundary held with love and a boundary enforced with shame is the difference between a community that forms people and a system that breaks them.</p>
-
-<blockquote>The church does not need to abandon sexual ethics. It needs to abandon the toxic distortion of sexual ethics that reduced human bodies to commodities, human worth to a biological status, and God's grace to a reward for compliance.</blockquote>
-
-<p>Joshua Harris himself eventually came to this reckoning. In 2019, he publicly apologized for the harm caused by <em>I Kissed Dating Goodbye</em>, asked his publisher to discontinue the book, announced the end of his marriage, and declared that he no longer considered himself a Christian. The trajectory of his personal journey — from purity culture's most visible spokesman to its most visible casualty — encapsulates the movement's fundamental problem: it built a theology on a foundation too narrow to sustain a human life.</p>
-
-<p>The wreckage of purity culture is real, and it is extensive. It lives in the bodies and psyches of a generation of adults who were taught to fear their own desires, to measure their worth by their virginity, and to believe that God's love was conditional on their sexual performance. Healing that damage requires more than better sex education or more progressive theology. It requires a fundamentally different understanding of what the body is, what sexuality means, and what kind of God we are dealing with. It requires, in other words, the gospel — the actual gospel, not the counterfeit one that purity culture sold in its name.</p>`,
