-<p>This narrative is tidy, morally flattering, and almost entirely false.</p>
-
-<p>The actual history of the Religious Right's formation is more complicated, less noble, and far more revealing about the relationship between American evangelicalism and political power. It is a history in which the initial mobilizing issue was not abortion but segregation, in which the key players were not pastors but political operatives, and in which theological conviction was not the driver of political engagement but its casualty. Understanding this history is essential for anyone who wants to understand how a tradition that once prized its distance from worldly power became one of the most potent political forces in American life — and what it lost in the transaction.</p>
-
-<h2>The Myth of Apolitical Fundamentalism</h2>
-
-<p>It is often said that fundamentalist and evangelical Christians were apolitical before the late 1970s — that they retreated from public life after the Scopes Trial of 1925 and did not re-emerge until Jerry Falwell summoned them back. This is an oversimplification, but it contains an important truth. For much of the twentieth century, the dominant posture of conservative Protestantism toward politics was one of suspicion or indifference. The world was fallen, politics was worldly, and the Christian's primary obligation was evangelism — saving souls, not reforming systems.</p>
