-<p>The political abstention was also selective. While fundamentalists and many evangelicals avoided electoral politics as a form of organized engagement, they were deeply embedded in the structures of American society — particularly in the Jim Crow South, where white evangelical churches were foundational institutions of a racially segregated social order. The idea that these communities were "apolitical" ignores the most basic insight of political analysis: the defense of an existing social order is itself a political act, even when it does not take the form of organized political advocacy.</p>
