-
-<p>Paul Weyrich, a Catholic political strategist with deep ties to the conservative movement, saw in evangelical Christians an untapped political resource — a large, geographically concentrated, socially connected population that shared conservative cultural values but had not been organized politically. Weyrich and his allies — including Richard Viguerie, a direct-mail pioneer, and Howard Phillips, a conservative activist — set about building the organizational infrastructure that would turn this population into a political force.</p>
-
-<p>Jerry Falwell, the pastor of Thomas Road Baptist Church in Lynchburg, Virginia, became the movement's public face. In 1979, Weyrich and Falwell co-founded the Moral Majority, an organization dedicated to mobilizing conservative Christians to vote on a platform that included opposition to abortion, opposition to the Equal Rights Amendment, opposition to homosexual rights, and support for school prayer and a strong national defense. The name itself was Weyrich's suggestion — a masterstroke of branding that implied that conservative Christians represented the majority of Americans and that their political agenda was, by definition, the moral one.</p>
