-
-<p>The result was a political machine of extraordinary effectiveness. By the early 1990s, the Religious Right was the most reliable constituency in the Republican coalition. Republican candidates could not win national elections without evangelical support, and evangelical leaders learned to convert that dependence into political influence. The relationship was symbiotic: politicians got votes; religious leaders got access, prestige, and the appearance of power.</p>
