-
-<p>The Religious Right reduced this vast moral landscape to two issues, creating what ethicist David Gushee has called a "thin" moral agenda that was conveniently aligned with the Republican Party platform on precisely the issues where it needed religious cover, and conveniently silent on the issues — poverty, healthcare, racial justice, environmental protection — where the Republican platform conflicted with the church's broader moral tradition.</p>
-
-<p>This narrowing was not accidental. It was strategic. A church that took the full scope of biblical justice seriously would be an uncomfortable ally for either political party. A church that reduced its moral agenda to abortion and same-sex marriage was a perfect fit for the Republican coalition. The narrowing served the party's interests admirably. Whether it served the gospel's interests is a different question entirely.</p>
