-<p>A church that has become a subsidiary of a political party cannot function prophetically. It cannot challenge the party's moral failures without jeopardizing its political access. It cannot speak against the party's candidate without fracturing its constituency. It cannot address the full scope of biblical justice without alienating the political allies on whom its influence depends. The result is a church that is prophetic about the sins of the opposing party and studiously silent about the sins of its own.</p>
-
