-
-<p>The problem is not that Christians participated in politics. Political participation is a legitimate and important expression of civic responsibility. The problem is that a particular political movement captured the church — co-opted its language, exploited its networks, and redirected its moral energy toward partisan ends — and the church, seduced by access to power, allowed it to happen.</p>
-
-<blockquote>The church did not enter politics. Politics entered the church. And once inside, it consumed the theology, replacing the prophetic with the partisan, the kingdom of God with the Republican platform, and the gospel of grace with a gospel of power.</blockquote>
-
-<h2>The Theological Wreckage</h2>
-
-<p>The political captivity of the church has produced theological consequences that extend far beyond the political arena. When a church organizes its identity around political positions, those positions gradually take on the character of doctrines — non-negotiable convictions that define who is in and who is out. Opposition to abortion becomes not merely a moral position but a test of Christian faithfulness. Support for a particular candidate becomes not merely a political choice but a spiritual obligation. Dissent from the political consensus becomes not merely a difference of opinion but evidence of spiritual compromise.</p>
-
-<p>This process — the theologization of politics — is the mirror image of the politicization of theology, and it is equally destructive. It produces a faith in which the boundary markers are political rather than theological, in which loyalty to the party is indistinguishable from loyalty to God, and in which the church's energy is directed not toward the formation of disciples but toward the mobilization of voters.</p>
-
-<p>The result is a generation of Christians who cannot distinguish between their faith and their politics — who genuinely believe that their political positions are the direct and necessary consequence of their theological convictions, and who therefore cannot understand why anyone who shares their faith might hold different political views. This is not theological seriousness. It is theological captivity. And it is the inevitable consequence of a church that traded its prophetic independence for a seat at the table of political power.</p>
-
