-    title: "Why Young Adults Aren't Coming Back",
-    slug: "why-young-adults-arent-coming-back",
-    excerpt:
-      "The church has spent two decades trying to make itself more appealing to young adults. The effort has largely failed — not because the tactics were wrong, but because the diagnosis was.",
-    pillar: "Prophetic Disruption",
-    readTime: "12 min",
-    body: `<p>The statistics have been recited so often that they have acquired the quality of liturgy. Roughly 60 to 65 percent of young adults who were regular churchgoers in their teens disengage from church life by their mid-twenties. The decline in religious affiliation among 18-to-35-year-olds has been steeper and faster than in any other age cohort. Only about 30 percent of millennials and an even smaller percentage of Gen Z say they attend religious services regularly. The church is aging, graying, and shrinking, and the generation that should be replenishing its ranks is, with remarkable consistency, choosing not to.</p>
-
-<p>The church's response to these statistics has been, for the most part, a case study in misdiagnosis. Assuming that the problem is one of presentation rather than substance, churches across America have invested heavily in aesthetic and experiential upgrades designed to make the church more attractive to young adults. Better coffee. Fog machines. LED light shows. Worship bands that sound like Coldplay. Sermon series with cinematic title sequences. Apps for everything. Pastors in skinny jeans. The operating assumption is that young adults are consumers who will attend church if the product is sufficiently polished, and that the church's competitive disadvantage is one of production values rather than credibility.</p>
-
-<p>This assumption is not merely wrong. It is insulting. And it has produced two decades of strategic investment in precisely the things that young adults are not looking for, while neglecting the things they are.</p>
-
-<h2>The Data Behind the Departure</h2>
-
-<p>The research on why young adults leave the church is extensive, and its findings are consistent across methodologies and research organizations. The Barna Group's landmark study, published as <em>You Lost Me</em> by David Kinnaman, identified six broad themes in the reasons young adults give for their disengagement.</p>
-
-<p>First, they experience the church as overprotective — creating an insular environment that shields its members from the broader culture rather than equipping them to engage it. Second, they find the church shallow — offering simplistic answers to complex questions and substituting emotional experience for intellectual substance. Third, they perceive the church as anti-science — demanding that they choose between their faith and the evidence of the natural world. Fourth, they experience the church as repressive regarding sexuality — imposing rigid rules without engaging the complexity of human sexual experience. Fifth, they find the church exclusive — unwilling to coexist with difference, unable to make space for those who do not conform to a narrow set of cultural expectations. Sixth, they perceive the church as hostile to doubt — treating questions as threats rather than signs of genuine engagement.</p>
-
-<p>The Public Religion Research Institute's data tells a complementary story. Among young adults who have left religion, the most commonly cited reasons involve the church's treatment of LGBTQ+ people, its association with partisan politics, and its perceived hypocrisy — the gap between what the church preaches and what it practices. These are not complaints about worship style or programming quality. They are indictments of institutional character.</p>
-
-<p>Sociologist Tim Clydesdale's longitudinal research, published in <em>The First Year Out</em>, found that most young adults do not experience a dramatic crisis of faith in college. Instead, they place their religious identity in a "lockbox" — setting it aside as they navigate the demands of early adulthood — and many simply never retrieve it. The church, which has long blamed secular universities for "stealing" its young people, has largely failed to reckon with the more prosaic reality: many young adults simply find that the church has become irrelevant to the life they are actually living.</p>
-
-<h2>What Doesn't Work</h2>
-
-<p>The strategies the church has deployed to attract young adults have, with some exceptions, failed — and the reasons for their failure are instructive.</p>
-
-<p><strong>The cool church strategy.</strong> The attempt to make church "cool" — through contemporary music, casual dress codes, trendy aesthetics, and culturally relevant branding — rests on a fundamental misunderstanding of what young adults are looking for. Young adults are not looking for a church that imitates the culture. They are looking for a church that offers something the culture does not. When a church invests its energy in replicating the sensory experience of a concert venue or a coffee shop, it communicates that it has nothing distinctive to offer — that its value proposition is the same as every other entertainment option, just slightly less competent. The young adult who wants a good concert will go to a concert. The young adult who wants a good latte will go to a coffee shop. The church that competes on these terms has already lost.</p>
-
-<p><strong>The personality-driven church.</strong> The megachurch model, built around the charisma and communicative gifts of a single pastor, has proven particularly fragile in its appeal to young adults. The celebrity pastor model — which concentrates spiritual authority in one individual, creates parasocial relationships between the pastor and the congregation, and measures success by attendance and platform — is precisely the model that young adults have identified as inauthentic, unsustainable, and prone to abuse. The collapse of high-profile personality-driven churches — Mars Hill, Hillsong, Willow Creek — has confirmed what many young adults already suspected: institutions built around individuals are institutions built on sand.</p>
-
-<p><strong>The apologetics approach.</strong> The assumption that young adults leave because they have unanswered intellectual objections — and that the solution is better arguments for God's existence, the reliability of Scripture, or the truth of Christian doctrine — misidentifies the nature of the departure. Most young adults who leave the church do not leave because they have been persuaded by the arguments of atheist philosophers. They leave because of what they have experienced within the church itself. A person who was shamed by purity culture, silenced when they expressed doubt, and watched their church fight against LGBTQ+ rights does not need a more sophisticated cosmological argument. They need an institution that behaves differently.</p>
-
-<p><strong>The programmatic approach.</strong> The creation of young adult-specific programs — young adult services, young adult small groups, young adult ministries with their own budgets and staffing — can inadvertently communicate that young adults are a problem to be solved rather than members of a community to be included. When the church creates a separate space for young adults rather than integrating them into the full life of the congregation, it often produces a ghetto rather than a home — a space where young adults are contained and managed rather than welcomed and empowered.</p>
-
-<h2>What They Actually Want: Authenticity</h2>
-
-<p>The word "authenticity" has been so overused in church leadership literature that it has nearly lost its meaning. But when young adults use the word — and they use it constantly — they are describing something specific and important. They are describing a community that does not perform its faith but practices it. A community where the pastor does not project a curated image of spiritual authority but speaks honestly about doubt, failure, and uncertainty. A community where the gap between what is preached on Sunday and what is practiced on Monday is acknowledged and addressed rather than papered over with better production values.</p>
-
-<p>Authenticity, for young adults, is not a style. It is a substance. It means telling the truth about the church's history — including its complicity in racism, colonialism, and abuse. It means acknowledging that the church has been wrong about things and is capable of being wrong about things now. It means creating space for people to bring their whole selves — including their doubts, their struggles, and their identities — without being required to leave parts of themselves at the door.</p>
-
-<p>Young adults have finely calibrated detectors for inauthenticity. They have grown up in a media environment saturated with branding, marketing, and performance. They can spot a curated image from a distance. And when they encounter one in a church — a pastor whose vulnerability is scripted, a community whose welcome is conditional, an institution whose values are selectively applied — they recognize it immediately and respond accordingly.</p>
-
-<h2>What They Actually Want: Justice</h2>
-
-<p>The generation that is leaving the church in the largest numbers has been profoundly shaped by movements for social justice. They have marched for Black lives. They have advocated for LGBTQ+ rights. They have organized around climate justice, economic inequality, immigration reform, and gun violence prevention. These are not peripheral concerns for this generation. They are central moral commitments that shape their identity and their understanding of what it means to live a good life.</p>
-
-<p>When they look at the institutional church, they see an institution that has historically been on the wrong side of virtually every major justice movement — that defended slavery, resisted civil rights, opposed women's suffrage, fought LGBTQ+ equality, and denied climate science. They see an institution that, in the present, is more likely to be found opposing justice movements than supporting them. And they draw a reasonable conclusion: if the church is consistently on the wrong side of justice, then the church's claim to moral authority is not credible.</p>
-
-<p>This is not to say that young adults expect the church to adopt a progressive political platform. Many young adults hold complex, non-partisan moral positions that do not map neatly onto either party's agenda. What they expect is moral seriousness — a church that cares about justice because Jesus cared about justice, that reads the prophets and takes them seriously, that sees the poor, the marginalized, and the oppressed not as objects of charity but as the people to whom the gospel is primarily addressed.</p>
-
-<blockquote>Young adults are not asking the church to be politically correct. They are asking the church to be prophetically faithful — to care about the things Jesus cared about with the same urgency and consistency.</blockquote>
-
-<h2>What They Actually Want: Honesty</h2>
-
-<p>The demand for honesty cuts across every other concern. Young adults want a church that is honest about the Bible — that can engage the complexities of biblical interpretation without resorting to fundamentalist rigidity or pretending that every difficult text has a simple explanation. They want a church that is honest about science — that does not demand they choose between their faith and the empirical evidence of the natural world. They want a church that is honest about its own failures — that does not rewrite its history, minimize its complicity, or dismiss its critics.</p>
-
-<p>They want a church that is honest about what it does not know. The posture of absolute certainty — the insistence that the church has definitive answers to every question, from the age of the earth to the mechanics of the afterlife — is not experienced as confidence by young adults. It is experienced as dishonesty. A church that says "we don't know, and we are comfortable not knowing, and we trust that God is bigger than our certainty" is far more credible to this generation than a church that claims to have every answer neatly systematized.</p>
-
-<p>They want a church that is honest about doubt. Not a church that reluctantly tolerates doubt as a temporary condition to be overcome, but a church that embraces doubt as a permanent and valuable feature of a living faith. The theologian Paul Tillich insisted that doubt is not the opposite of faith but an element within it, and young adults instinctively understand this even when they lack the theological vocabulary to articulate it. A faith that cannot accommodate doubt is a faith that cannot accommodate reality. And a church that cannot accommodate reality will not accommodate this generation.</p>
-
-<h2>The Hypocrisy Factor, Revisited</h2>
-
-<p>It is worth dwelling on the hypocrisy issue, because it appears in virtually every study of young adult disengagement and because it is the factor that church leaders are most likely to dismiss. "Everyone is a hypocrite," the standard response goes. "The church is a hospital for sinners, not a museum for saints. If you leave the church because of hypocrisy, you are holding an impossible standard."</p>
-
-<p>This response misses the point. Young adults are not leaving because individual Christians fail to live up to their ideals. Every human being fails to live up to their ideals. What young adults are identifying as hypocrisy is not personal imperfection but institutional dishonesty — the systematic gap between the church's public proclamations and its actual behavior, combined with the church's refusal to acknowledge that gap.</p>
-
-<p>A church that preaches grace but practices judgment. A church that proclaims the dignity of every person but actively works to deny rights to LGBTQ+ people. A church that demands sexual purity from its young people while covering up sexual abuse by its leaders. A church that preaches humility while building empires of personal brand and celebrity. These are not instances of individuals falling short. They are instances of institutions behaving in ways that systematically contradict their stated values. And the church's refusal to acknowledge this contradiction — its insistence that the critics are the problem, not the behavior — is itself the most damning form of the hypocrisy they are describing.</p>
-
-<h2>The Question the Church Must Answer</h2>
-
-<p>The church has spent two decades asking "How do we get young adults to come to church?" This is the wrong question. It assumes that the church is fine as it is and that the problem lies with the young adults who are not attending. It treats young adults as a demographic to be captured rather than a generation to be heard.</p>
-
-<p>The right question is: "What kind of church would young adults want to be part of?" And the answer, distilled from two decades of research, testimony, and conversation, is not complicated. They want a church that tells the truth. They want a church that practices what it preaches. They want a church that cares about justice. They want a church that welcomes doubt. They want a church that does not demand the surrender of their intellect as the price of belonging. They want a church that treats every human being with dignity. They want a church that is more interested in following Jesus than in defending its own institutional prerogatives.</p>
-
-<p>None of these desires are unreasonable. None of them require the church to abandon its core convictions. All of them require the church to become more fully what it already claims to be.</p>
-
-<blockquote>Young adults are not leaving the church because they want less. They are leaving because they want more — more honesty, more justice, more authenticity, more of the Jesus they read about in the Gospels and less of the institution that claims his name while contradicting his example.</blockquote>
-
-<p>The church that cannot hear this is a church that has chosen institutional preservation over prophetic faithfulness. And a church that has chosen institutional preservation over prophetic faithfulness has already lost the generation it is trying to reach — not because that generation is faithless, but because it is looking for a faith the institution is unwilling to embody.</p>
-
-<p>The young adults are not the problem. They may, in fact, be the answer — if the church has the courage to listen to what they are saying rather than devising new strategies to say it louder in return.</p>`,
-  },
