-    body: `<p>The question is not going away. It has not gone away in twenty-five centuries of sustained philosophical inquiry, and it will not go away because a New Atheist bestseller declares it settled or because a revival preacher insists it was never really a question at all. "Is God real?" is the question that sits beneath every other question a human being can ask — about meaning, about morality, about whether the universe is the kind of place that notices us or the kind of place that does not. It deserves better than the treatment it typically receives, which is to say it deserves more than slogans, more than bumper stickers, more than the false confidence of people who have never honestly reckoned with the strongest version of the opposing case.</p>
-
-<p>I am a pastor. I have staked my life on the conviction that God is real. But I have also sat across the table from enough honest atheists and enough anguished doubters to know that the question cannot be waved away with a Bible verse or a sunset. The arguments matter. The objections matter. And what we do with the space between the arguments and the objections — that matters most of all.</p>
-
-<h2>The Classical Arguments for God's Existence</h2>
-
-<p>The case for God's existence is not a single argument but a family of arguments, each approaching the question from a different angle. They do not prove God in the way a mathematical theorem proves a conclusion. But they were never designed to. They are arguments to the best explanation — attempts to account for features of reality that seem to require something beyond the natural order to explain.</p>
