-<p>The cosmological argument, in its simplest form, asks why there is something rather than nothing. Leibniz formulated it as the principle of sufficient reason: every contingent thing requires an explanation outside itself. The universe is contingent — it did not have to exist, and it has not always existed in its present form. The Big Bang, confirmed by Arno Penzias and Robert Wilson's detection of cosmic microwave background radiation in 1965, established that the universe had a beginning approximately 13.8 billion years ago. If the universe began to exist, what caused it? The argument does not claim to identify this cause as the God of Christianity specifically. It claims that the existence of the universe points beyond itself to something uncaused, necessary, and sufficient to account for why anything exists at all.</p>
