-<p>The field of textual criticism — the scholarly discipline devoted to reconstructing the original text from the available manuscripts — has produced a New Testament text that scholars across the theological spectrum regard as highly reliable. Bruce Metzger, perhaps the greatest textual critic of the twentieth century, estimated that the New Testament text is 99.5 percent certain, and that the remaining variants affect no significant doctrine. Bart Ehrman, Metzger's student and a prominent agnostic scholar, has acknowledged the reliability of the textual tradition even while disagreeing with its theological claims.</p>
-
-<p>The variants that do exist are overwhelmingly minor — spelling differences, word-order changes, the kind of scribal errors one would expect in a hand-copied tradition. The handful of significant variants — the longer ending of Mark, the Johannine Comma (1 John 5:7-8), the story of the woman caught in adultery (John 7:53-8:11) — are well known, are flagged in every modern translation, and do not affect any central Christian doctrine. The text of the Bible has been transmitted with a fidelity that is remarkable by the standards of the ancient world.</p>
-
-<h2>Genre Matters</h2>
-
-<p>The single most important skill for reading the Bible honestly is the ability to recognize genre. Poetry is not history. Apocalypse is not prediction. Parable is not journalism. Law code is not systematic theology. When Jesus says, "If your right eye causes you to sin, tear it out," he is not prescribing self-mutilation. He is using hyperbole — a recognized rhetorical device — to make a point about the seriousness of moral compromise. When the psalmist writes, "The Lord is my shepherd," he is not making a zoological claim about God's profession. He is using metaphor to express trust in God's provision and care.</p>
-
-<p>The creation accounts in Genesis 1-2 are a case study in the importance of genre. Genesis 1 is a highly structured, liturgical text — six days of creation arranged in two parallel triads (forming and filling), culminating in the Sabbath rest. It reads less like a scientific report and more like a hymn. Genesis 2 tells a different creation story with a different order of events (in Genesis 1, animals are created before humans; in Genesis 2, the man is created before the animals). Reading both as journalistic accounts of the same event creates an immediate contradiction. Reading them as two complementary theological narratives — one emphasizing the cosmic scope of God's creative work, the other emphasizing the intimacy of God's relationship with humanity — dissolves the contradiction and reveals the depth of both texts.</p>
-
-<p>The prophetic literature is not primarily about predicting the future. It is about calling the present to account. When Isaiah says, "Your new moons and your appointed feasts my soul hates" (Isaiah 1:14), he is not making a prediction. He is delivering a divine indictment of a religious establishment that practices ritual without justice. Reading the prophets as a crystal ball for contemporary geopolitics — as much popular prophecy teaching does — is a fundamental misreading of the genre.</p>
-
-<p>The book of Revelation, or the Apocalypse, is the most misread book in the Bible precisely because its genre is so unfamiliar to modern readers. Apocalyptic literature was a well-established genre in the ancient world, with recognizable conventions: symbolic numbers, cosmic imagery, beasts representing empires, a final confrontation between good and evil. First-century readers would have recognized these conventions instantly. Modern readers, unfamiliar with the genre, tend to read Revelation as a literal prediction of future events — an approach that produces endless speculation and very little understanding.</p>
-
-<h2>The Bible the Bible Actually Is</h2>
-
-<p>The Bible we actually have is stranger, more honest, and more durable than either the fundamentalist or the skeptic imagines. It contains genealogies that do not match, numbers that do not add up, and laws that later biblical texts modify or set aside. It includes the Song of Solomon, which is an erotic love poem, and Ecclesiastes, which flirts with nihilism. It preserves the story of David — the man after God's own heart — committing adultery, arranging a murder, and being confronted by a prophet who tells him a parable to trick him into pronouncing his own judgment. It does not protect its heroes. It does not sanitize its history. It does not resolve its tensions into a neat systematic theology.</p>
-
-<p>And it has endured. It has survived the Roman Empire's attempt to destroy it, the medieval church's attempt to control it, the Enlightenment's attempt to dismiss it, and the modern church's attempt to domesticate it. It has been translated into more than 700 languages. It has shaped the moral imagination of Western civilization — its concepts of human dignity, justice, care for the vulnerable, and the equality of persons before God have become so deeply embedded in Western thought that even secular people who reject the Bible's authority often hold values that are unintelligible apart from it.</p>
-
