-
-<p>Reading the Bible honestly means reading it on its own terms. It means paying attention to genre, to historical context, to the literary conventions of the ancient world. It means acknowledging the tensions without flattening them and sitting with the difficulties without pretending they do not exist. It means recognizing that the Bible was written in specific historical contexts by authors who shared the cosmological assumptions of their time — a flat earth, a solid dome over the sky, a three-tiered universe — and that these assumptions are part of the cultural medium through which the theological message was communicated, not part of the message itself.</p>
