-<h2>Eternal Conscious Torment: The Majority Tradition</h2>
-
-<p>The view most commonly associated with Christianity — and the one that has dominated Western theology since the fifth century — is eternal conscious torment (ECT). On this view, those who die without saving faith in Christ are consigned to hell, where they experience conscious suffering for all eternity. There is no exit. There is no reprieve. The punishment is infinite in duration, regardless of the finite nature of the sins committed.</p>
-
-<p>The theological foundations of this view were laid primarily by Augustine of Hippo (354-430), who argued in <em>The City of God</em> that the punishment of the wicked is eternal in the same sense that the blessedness of the righteous is eternal — both are without end. Augustine's influence on Western Christianity was immense, and his interpretation of hell became, for practical purposes, the default position of both Catholic and Protestant theology.</p>
