-<p>Jonathan Edwards, in his 1741 sermon "Sinners in the Hands of an Angry God," gave the doctrine its most vivid — and most terrifying — expression in the American context. Edwards described sinners dangling over the pit of hell like a spider held over a flame, preserved from falling only by the arbitrary mercy of a God who is under no obligation to sustain them. The sermon was a masterpiece of rhetorical terror, and it shaped American evangelicalism's relationship to hell for centuries.</p>
