-
-<p>Part of the difficulty in the hell debate is that the biblical data is less uniform than any of the positions typically admits. The key terms themselves are complex. "Sheol" in the Old Testament is the shadowy abode of the dead — all the dead, righteous and wicked alike — and carries no connotation of punishment. It is the grave, the underworld, the place where the dead exist in a diminished state. "Hades" in the New Testament roughly corresponds to Sheol and similarly does not always carry a punitive sense. "Gehenna," the term most often translated as "hell" in Jesus' teaching, refers to the Valley of Hinnom outside Jerusalem — a place associated with child sacrifice in Israel's history and, by the first century, with the burning of refuse. Whether Jesus intended "Gehenna" as a literal description of an afterlife destination or as a vivid metaphor for the consequences of rejecting God's reign is a matter of ongoing scholarly debate.</p>
