-    body: `<p>The narrative is so deeply embedded in Western culture that it feels like a fact: science and religion have always been at war, and science has been winning. The narrative has its canonical stories — Galileo persecuted by the Inquisition, Darwin demolishing Genesis, Copernicus defying the church — and its implicit moral: as scientific knowledge advances, religious belief retreats, because the two are fundamentally incompatible. One deals in evidence; the other in wishful thinking. One progresses; the other regresses. The choice between them is a choice between reason and superstition.</p>
-
-<p>This narrative is largely false. It is not false because the church has always been friendly to science — it has not. It is false because the relationship between science and faith is far more complex, far more collaborative, and far more interesting than the warfare metaphor allows. The conflict thesis, as historians of science call it, was itself an invention — a nineteenth-century polemical construction that serious historians have spent the last several decades dismantling.</p>
-
-<h2>The Conflict Thesis and Its Collapse</h2>
-
-<p>The idea that science and religion are inherently at war was popularized by two books: John William Draper's <em>History of the Conflict Between Religion and Science</em> (1874) and Andrew Dickson White's <em>A History of the Warfare of Science with Theology in Christendom</em> (1896). Both were enormously influential. Both were also deeply misleading. Draper and White had agendas — Draper was an anti-Catholic polemicist, and White was the president of Cornell University, which he had founded as a secular institution and needed to defend against religious critics. Their books were not dispassionate histories. They were advocacy documents dressed in historical clothing.</p>
-
-<p>The conflict thesis has been rejected by virtually every serious historian of science working today. David Lindberg and Ronald Numbers, in their landmark edited volume <em>God and Nature: Historical Essays on the Encounter Between Christianity and Science</em> (1986), demonstrated that the relationship between Christianity and the natural sciences has been characterized far more by complexity, collaboration, and mutual influence than by conflict. John Hedley Brooke's <em>Science and Religion: Some Historical Perspectives</em> (1991) came to the same conclusion. The consensus among historians of science is clear: the warfare metaphor is a myth.</p>
-
-<p>This does not mean there have been no conflicts. There have been real and significant episodes of tension between scientific findings and theological claims. But these episodes have been the exception, not the rule, and even the canonical examples — Galileo, Darwin — are far more complicated than the popular versions suggest.</p>
-
