-
-<p>The Galileo affair is the founding myth of the conflict narrative: a courageous scientist persecuted by an ignorant church for daring to assert that the earth moves around the sun. The actual history is considerably more tangled.</p>
-
-<p>Galileo Galilei (1564-1642) was a devout Catholic who never abandoned his faith. His heliocentric views, published in <em>Dialogue Concerning the Two Chief World Systems</em> (1632), drew on the work of Copernicus — who was himself a Catholic canon, and whose <em>De Revolutionibus</em> (1543) had been dedicated to Pope Paul III. The Copernican system had circulated for decades without provoking significant ecclesiastical opposition. Cardinal Robert Bellarmine, the most prominent theologian of the era, had stated explicitly that if heliocentrism were scientifically demonstrated, the church would need to reinterpret the relevant biblical passages.</p>
-
-<p>What turned the Galileo case from a scientific debate into a confrontation was a combination of personal politics, ecclesiastical power dynamics, and Galileo's own rhetorical missteps. Galileo's <em>Dialogue</em> placed the geocentric arguments in the mouth of a character named Simplicio — a name that could be read as "simpleton" — and Pope Urban VIII, who had previously been Galileo's patron, believed that Simplicio's arguments were a caricature of his own views. The prosecution of Galileo was driven as much by wounded pride and political calculation as by theological conviction. The Inquisition's condemnation was real, and it was wrong. But it was not a case of science versus religion in any straightforward sense. It was a case of institutional power, personal animosity, and theological overreach — a failure of the church to distinguish between the Bible's theological message and the cosmological assumptions of its authors.</p>
