-<h2>Darwin and the Church: The Actual Record</h2>
-
-<p>The popular narrative of Darwin's reception is similarly oversimplified. The story usually told goes like this: Darwin published <em>On the Origin of Species</em> in 1859, the church reacted with horror, and the famous debate between Thomas Henry Huxley and Bishop Samuel Wilberforce at Oxford in 1860 settled the matter in science's favor. The actual history is far more diverse.</p>
-
-<p>Many Christians — including prominent theologians and clergy — accepted evolution relatively quickly. Asa Gray, the foremost American botanist and a devout Christian, was Darwin's most important ally in the United States and saw no conflict between evolution and faith. Charles Kingsley, an Anglican clergyman and novelist, wrote to Darwin that it was "just as noble a conception of Deity to believe that He created primal forms capable of self-development" as to believe in special creation. B.B. Warfield, the Princeton theologian most associated with the doctrine of biblical inerrancy, accepted a form of theistic evolution — a fact that would astonish many of his modern admirers.</p>
