-<p>The Huxley-Wilberforce debate, far from being the decisive confrontation of popular memory, was actually a minor event at the time. The story was embellished and mythologized in subsequent decades, largely by Huxley's allies, to serve the narrative of science's triumph over religion. Contemporary accounts of the debate are inconsistent — witnesses disagreed about what was said, who won, and whether the exchange was as dramatic as later versions claimed.</p>
