-<h2>Christians and the History of Science</h2>
-
-<p>The conflict thesis obscures one of the most remarkable features of the history of science: the central role that Christian institutions and Christian thinkers played in the development of the scientific enterprise. The medieval university system — the institutional framework within which modern science emerged — was a creation of the church. The University of Paris, the University of Oxford, the University of Bologna — all were founded under ecclesiastical authority in the twelfth and thirteenth centuries. The monks and clerics who populated these institutions were not enemies of learning. They were its custodians.</p>
