-
-<p>The philosopher Alfred North Whitehead argued in <em>Science and the Modern World</em> (1925) that modern science was made possible by the medieval conviction, rooted in Christian theology, that the universe is rational and ordered — that it operates according to consistent laws because it is the creation of a rational God. This conviction, Whitehead argued, provided the philosophical foundation for the scientific method: the belief that the universe is intelligible, that its patterns can be discovered, and that the human mind is equipped to discover them. Without this conviction, the enterprise of science would have no warrant.</p>
-
-<h2>Scientists Who Believe</h2>
-
-<p>The claim that serious scientists cannot be serious believers is refuted by the existence of serious scientists who are serious believers. Francis Collins, who led the Human Genome Project and served as director of the National Institutes of Health, is an evangelical Christian who has written extensively about the compatibility of faith and science. John Polkinghorne, a Cambridge particle physicist who became an Anglican priest, developed a sophisticated theology of science that integrates quantum mechanics and trinitarian theology. Jennifer Wiseman, a senior astrophysicist at NASA, has spoken publicly about how her study of the cosmos deepens rather than diminishes her Christian faith.</p>
