-
-<h2>Models for the Relationship</h2>
-
-<p>If science and faith are not at war, what is their relationship? Several models have been proposed, each with strengths and limitations.</p>
-
-<p>Stephen Jay Gould proposed the NOMA model — Non-Overlapping Magisteria — in his 1999 book <em>Rocks of Ages</em>. On this view, science and religion address fundamentally different questions: science asks how the natural world works; religion asks what it means and how we should live. The two domains do not overlap, and conflict arises only when one trespasses on the other's territory. The NOMA model has the virtue of clarity, but many thinkers — both scientific and theological — find it too neat. If God created the universe, then God's creative action must have some relationship to the universe that science studies. And if science discovers features of the universe that bear on questions of meaning — the fine-tuning of cosmological constants, the emergence of consciousness — then the magisteria do overlap, whether we want them to or not.</p>
-
-<p>The dialogue model, associated with Ian Barbour and others, holds that science and religion address different questions but that their answers interact and can inform each other. Scientific discoveries about the age of the universe, the evolution of life, and the nature of consciousness raise questions that theology has resources to address. Theological convictions about the goodness of creation, the value of human life, and the reality of moral obligation raise questions that science alone cannot answer. The two disciplines are in conversation, not competition.</p>
-
-<p>The integration model goes further, arguing that science and theology can be woven into a single, coherent framework. Teilhard de Chardin, the Jesuit paleontologist, attempted a grand synthesis of evolutionary biology and Christian theology. More recently, scholars associated with the John Templeton Foundation have pursued research at the intersection of science and religion, exploring questions about the nature of consciousness, the fine-tuning of the cosmos, and the evolutionary origins of altruism.</p>
-
-<p>No single model captures the full complexity of the relationship. What all serious models share is the rejection of the warfare metaphor — the recognition that science and faith, whatever their tensions, are not enemies but neighbors, occupying adjacent rooms in the house of human inquiry, and that the conversation between them is one of the most important conversations a thoughtful person can have.</p>
-
-<h2>The Questions Science Cannot Answer</h2>
-
-<p>The limits of science are not weaknesses. They are the boundaries of a discipline that has defined its domain with admirable precision. Science investigates the natural world through observation, hypothesis, experimentation, and revision. It does this extraordinarily well. But there are questions that the scientific method, by its own design, is not equipped to answer. Why is there something rather than nothing? What grounds moral obligation? Does life have purpose? Is the universe meaningful, or is meaning a human projection onto an indifferent cosmos? What happens when we die?</p>
-
-<p>These are not failed scientific questions waiting for better instruments. They are philosophical and theological questions that require different tools — tools that include reason, tradition, experience, revelation, and the cumulative wisdom of communities that have wrestled with these questions for millennia. The scientist who says "science cannot answer that question" is not confessing a limitation. The scientist is defining a boundary. And the theologian who says "let me bring a different set of tools to the table" is not trespassing on science's territory. The theologian is working in an adjacent field where the soil is different and the methods must be different too.</p>
