-<p>The deepest mistake of the new atheism — the movement associated with Richard Dawkins, Sam Harris, Christopher Hitchens, and Daniel Dennett — was not its criticism of religion's failures, much of which was deserved. The deepest mistake was its claim that science could replace religion — that empirical investigation could answer every meaningful human question, and that any question science could not answer was therefore not a real question. This is not a scientific claim. It is a philosophical claim — the claim known as scientism — and it is a claim that most philosophers, including many who are atheists, regard as unjustified. Science is magnificent at telling us how the universe works. It has nothing to say about whether the universe matters.</p>
-
-<p>The war between science and faith is not a war that needs to be won. It is a war that never existed — except in the imagination of polemicists on both sides who found it more useful to fight than to think. The actual history is richer, messier, and more hopeful than the myth. And the sooner we leave the myth behind, the sooner we can get on with the real work: asking the deepest questions about the universe with every tool we have — including both the telescope and the prayer.</p>`,
