-    body: `<p>No topic in the conversation between faith and culture has been more badly handled than the relationship between evolution and Genesis. It has been turned into a litmus test for orthodoxy, a battleground for culture wars, and a stumbling block that has driven countless thoughtful people away from the church — not because they could not believe in God but because they were told that believing in God required them to reject the overwhelming consensus of modern science. That is a false choice, and the church should stop offering it.</p>
-
-<p>I say this as a pastor, not as a scientist. My training is in theology, not biology. But I have spent enough time in this debate to know that the options are more numerous, more carefully reasoned, and more theologically responsible than most churches acknowledge. The purpose of this essay is not to tell you what to believe about evolution and Genesis. It is to lay out the terrain honestly so that you can make an informed decision — and to insist that the decision is not between faith and science. It is between different ways of holding both.</p>
-
-<h2>What Genesis 1-3 Actually Says</h2>
-
-<p>The first step in this conversation is to read Genesis 1-3 carefully, on its own terms, in its own context. This is harder than it sounds, because most of us come to Genesis with centuries of interpretive assumptions already in place — assumptions we mistake for the text itself.</p>
-
-<p>Genesis 1 is a carefully structured, symmetrical text. It describes creation in six days arranged in two parallel triads. In days 1-3, God creates the domains: light and darkness, sky and sea, land and vegetation. In days 4-6, God fills the domains: sun and moon and stars (filling the domain of light), birds and fish (filling sky and sea), land animals and humans (filling the land). Day 7 is the Sabbath — God's rest. The structure is architectural, not chronological. It reads like a liturgical hymn, not a laboratory notebook.</p>
-
-<p>When Genesis 1 is read alongside the other creation texts of the ancient Near East — the Babylonian <em>Enuma Elish</em>, the Egyptian creation accounts, the Atrahasis epic — its distinctive theological claims come into sharp focus. Unlike the Babylonian account, in which the world is created from the dismembered body of a slain goddess, Genesis depicts creation as the free, intentional act of a single God who speaks the world into being. Unlike the Mesopotamian accounts, in which humans are created as slaves to serve the gods, Genesis declares humans to be created in the "image of God" — a royal designation in the ancient world, applied in other cultures only to kings. Genesis is making claims about who God is, what humans are, and what creation means. Whether it is also making claims about how many hours the process took is a question the text itself does not obviously answer.</p>
-
-<p>Genesis 2 tells a different creation story with a different sequence. In Genesis 1, the sequence is vegetation, animals, humans (male and female together). In Genesis 2, the sequence is the man, vegetation, animals, the woman. If both chapters are read as chronological accounts of the same events, they contradict each other. The early church fathers — including Origen, Augustine, and Basil — were aware of this, and none of them treated both accounts as straightforward chronological history. Augustine, in <em>The Literal Meaning of Genesis</em>, argued that the "days" of creation might not be ordinary days and that Christians should not make claims about the natural world that would make the faith look foolish to educated pagans. That was in the fifth century.</p>
-
-<h2>Young Earth Creationism</h2>
-
-<p>Young Earth Creationism (YEC) holds that Genesis 1-2 describes the creation of the universe in six literal twenty-four-hour days, approximately 6,000 to 10,000 years ago. The position is associated with the work of Henry Morris and John Whitcomb, whose <em>The Genesis Flood</em> (1961) launched the modern young-earth movement, and with organizations like Answers in Genesis, the Institute for Creation Research, and the Creation Research Society.</p>
-
-<p>YEC proponents argue that the Hebrew word <em>yom</em> (day), when accompanied by a number and the phrase "evening and morning," always refers to a literal twenty-four-hour day in the Old Testament. They argue that the genealogies of Genesis 5 and 11 provide a chronological framework that, when calculated, yields an age for the earth of roughly 6,000 years. They argue that the geological record is better explained by a global flood than by uniformitarian geology, and that the apparent age of the universe — the light from distant stars, the radiometric dating of rocks, the fossil record — is either the result of God creating the universe with the appearance of age or the product of catastrophic geological processes during and after the flood.</p>
-
-<p>The scientific difficulties with YEC are substantial and well-documented. The age of the universe — 13.8 billion years, established by multiple independent lines of evidence including cosmic microwave background radiation, the redshift of distant galaxies, and the abundance of light elements — is not a marginal finding. It is one of the best-established results in all of science. The age of the earth — 4.5 billion years, established by radiometric dating of meteorites and terrestrial rocks — is similarly well-established. The fossil record documents the sequential appearance of life forms over hundreds of millions of years, in a pattern that is consistent with evolutionary theory and inconsistent with the simultaneous creation of all species. To reject these findings requires rejecting not a single scientific claim but the entire framework of modern physics, chemistry, geology, and biology.</p>
-
-<p>This does not mean that people who hold YEC are unintelligent. Many are deeply thoughtful. But the scientific case against a young earth is overwhelming, and the church does itself no favors by pretending otherwise.</p>
-
