-
-<p>Old Earth Creationism (OEC) accepts the scientific evidence for the age of the universe and the earth while maintaining that God created the major kinds of life through direct, special creative acts. The position takes several forms.</p>
-
-<p>The day-age interpretation reads the "days" of Genesis 1 as long periods of time — eons rather than twenty-four-hour days. The Hebrew word <em>yom</em> can refer to an indefinite period (as in "the day of the Lord"), and proponents argue that this reading is linguistically legitimate and aligns the Genesis sequence roughly with the scientific sequence of cosmic and biological history. Hugh Ross, an astrophysicist and the founder of Reasons to Believe, is the most prominent advocate of this view.</p>
-
-<p>The gap theory posits a long interval between Genesis 1:1 ("In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth") and Genesis 1:2 ("The earth was without form and void"), during which the geological ages unfolded. The six days of creation in Genesis 1:3-31 describe a relatively recent re-creation or restoration of the earth. This view was popular in the nineteenth century but has largely fallen out of favor among both scientists and theologians.</p>
-
-<p>The framework interpretation, developed by Meredith Kline and Henri Blocher, reads the six days of Genesis 1 not as a chronological sequence but as a literary framework — a topical arrangement that presents God's creative work in two parallel triads (forming and filling). On this view, the "days" are a literary device, not a calendar, and the question of how long creation took is simply not a question Genesis is designed to answer.</p>
-
-<h2>Evolutionary Creation</h2>
-
-<p>Evolutionary creation (also called theistic evolution) accepts the full scientific account of evolution — common descent, natural selection, deep time — while affirming that the entire process is God's chosen method of creation. On this view, God did not create each species individually by direct intervention. God created a universe with the physical laws and initial conditions necessary for life to emerge and evolve, and God sustains and directs the process through natural means. Evolution is not an alternative to divine creation. It is the mechanism of divine creation.</p>
-
-<p>The BioLogos Foundation, founded by Francis Collins, is the most prominent advocate of evolutionary creation. Denis Lamoureux, a professor of science and religion at the University of Alberta, has developed a sophisticated theological framework for the position, arguing that just as God accommodated divine revelation to the scientific understanding of the biblical authors (who assumed a flat earth and a solid dome over the sky), so God accommodated the mechanism of creation to natural processes that science can investigate.</p>
-
-<p>Evolutionary creationists argue that the evidence for common descent is overwhelming: the shared DNA of all living organisms, the nested hierarchy of morphological similarities, the fossil record's documentation of transitional forms, the geographic distribution of species, the vestigial structures that make no sense apart from evolutionary history. They argue that this evidence is not a threat to faith but a source of wonder — that the God who could create a universe capable of generating life, consciousness, and love through the patient unfolding of natural processes is a more impressive God, not a lesser one.</p>
-
-<h2>What Is at Stake Theologically</h2>
-
-<p>The real theological questions in the evolution debate are not about the age of the earth or the mechanism of speciation. They are about deeper matters: the image of God, the fall, original sin, and the nature of death.</p>
-
-<p>If human beings evolved from earlier primates, what does it mean to say that humans are created in the "image of God"? The answer depends on what the image of God is. If it is a biological feature — a soul implanted in the first human pair at a specific moment in history — then evolutionary continuity with other species poses a challenge. But if the image of God is a relational and vocational category — the capacity to relate to God, to bear God's authority in creation, to exercise stewardship and moral responsibility — then it need not be located in a specific biological event. It can be understood as a divine calling conferred on humanity as humanity emerged into the kind of consciousness that could receive it.</p>
-
-<p>The fall is a more difficult question. If there was no historical Adam and Eve, no single moment of first disobedience in a garden, what do we make of the doctrine of original sin? Several options have been proposed. Some evolutionary creationists argue that Adam and Eve were historical figures — perhaps the first members of a population of early humans whom God called into covenant relationship. Others argue that the fall is a theological description of a universal human condition — the pervasive tendency toward selfishness, idolatry, and violence that characterizes every human society — rather than a historical account of a single event. The apostle Paul's use of Adam in Romans 5 must be taken seriously, but Paul's theological point — that sin and death are universal, and that Christ is the answer to both — does not necessarily depend on a particular theory of human origins.</p>
-
-<p>The question of death before the fall is also significant. If the earth is billions of years old and life has been evolving for hundreds of millions of years, then death — animal death, at a minimum — preceded any human sin. YEC proponents argue that this contradicts Romans 5:12 ("sin came into the world through one man, and death through sin"). But many scholars argue that Paul's reference is to human death, not animal death, and that the natural death of organisms is a feature of a good creation, not a consequence of the fall. Trees shedding leaves, predators consuming prey, organisms completing their life cycles — these are not evils to be explained. They are the normal operations of a living world.</p>
-
-<h2>How to Have This Conversation Honestly</h2>
-
-<p>The evolution-and-Genesis debate generates more heat than light because both sides often fight for things that are not actually at stake. The young-earth creationist fears that accepting evolution means abandoning the authority of Scripture, the reality of God's creative action, and the theological significance of human beings. These fears are understandable, but they are not warranted. One can affirm the authority of Scripture, the reality of divine creation, and the unique dignity of human beings while accepting the scientific evidence for evolution. The question is not whether God created. The question is how.</p>
-
-<p>The secularist, on the other hand, often assumes that evolution disproves God — that the mechanism of natural selection, once understood, eliminates the need for a Creator. This is a philosophical claim, not a scientific one. Natural selection explains how species change over time. It does not explain why there is a universe capable of producing life in the first place, or why that universe is governed by the kind of mathematical laws that make science possible, or why consciousness emerged from matter, or why human beings experience moral obligation. Evolution is a powerful explanation of biological diversity. It is not a metaphysical system.</p>
-
-<p>The church owes its people better than a false choice between faith and science. It owes them the truth: that the Christian tradition has always contained a diversity of views on how to read Genesis, that the greatest minds in the history of the church did not read Genesis 1 as a science textbook, and that the theological convictions that matter — that God is the Creator, that the universe is not an accident, that human beings bear the image of God and are called to a purpose — can be held with integrity regardless of one's view on the mechanism of creation.</p>
-
-<p>What cannot be held with integrity is the insistence that Christians must choose between their Bibles and their biology textbooks. That insistence is not a defense of the faith. It is an obstacle to it. And it is time the church stopped building obstacles and started building bridges — not by abandoning theological conviction, but by distinguishing conviction from interpretation, and interpretation from the Word of God itself.</p>`,
