-    body: `<p>The word "miracle" carries more baggage than almost any other word in the religious vocabulary. For the skeptic, it conjures images of credulous peasants, faith healers, and the suspension of rational thought. For the believer, it conjures images of divine power breaking into the natural order — parted seas, empty tombs, water turned to wine. Both images are distortions, and the honest conversation about miracles requires setting both aside long enough to ask the real question: Is the universe a closed system in which nothing can occur that is not determined by prior natural causes, or is it open to the action of a reality beyond itself?</p>
-
-<p>That is a philosophical question, not an empirical one. No experiment can determine whether the universe is open or closed. No measurement can detect the presence or absence of a God who acts. The question of miracles, at its deepest level, is a question about the nature of reality itself — and answering it requires philosophy and theology, not just science.</p>
-
-<h2>Hume's Argument and Its Problems</h2>
-
-<p>The most famous argument against miracles was advanced by David Hume in section 10 of his <em>An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding</em> (1748). Hume defined a miracle as a violation of a law of nature and argued that the evidence against any miracle must always outweigh the evidence in its favor. The reasoning goes like this: a law of nature is established by uniform experience — the sun has always risen, the dead have always stayed dead, water has always flowed downhill. Testimony that a miracle has occurred must be weighed against this uniform experience. Since the regularity of nature is supported by the entire weight of human observation, while testimony for a miracle is always limited and fallible, it is always more rational to reject the testimony than to accept the miracle.</p>
-
-<p>Hume's argument is elegant, but it has been challenged on several grounds. First, Hume's definition of miracle as a "violation" of natural law assumes a particular understanding of natural law — the Newtonian understanding, in which laws of nature are exceptionless regularities. But this understanding has been complicated by developments in physics. Quantum mechanics has introduced genuine indeterminacy into the foundations of physical reality. The laws of physics, as currently understood, are statistical generalizations, not iron decrees. This does not prove that miracles occur, but it undermines the assumption that the universe is a deterministic machine in which exceptions are logically impossible.</p>
-
-<p>Second, Hume's argument is circular. He argues that we should reject testimony for miracles because uniform experience testifies against them. But "uniform experience" already assumes that miracles have not occurred. If a miracle has occurred, then experience is not uniform. Hume's argument works only if the conclusion is already assumed — that is, only if we already know that miracles do not happen. As the philosopher John Earman argued in <em>Hume's Abject Failure</em> (2000), Hume's argument is not the decisive refutation of miracles it is often taken to be.</p>
-
-<p>Third, Hume's argument proves too much. If we should always prefer the more probable explanation — if testimony can never be strong enough to overcome the prior improbability of an event — then we should reject any sufficiently improbable historical event, including many that are well-attested. The emergence of life from non-living matter is extraordinarily improbable. The specific sequence of events that produced the universe in its current form is extraordinarily improbable. Yet these events occurred. Prior improbability, by itself, does not make an event non-actual.</p>
-
-<h2>The Biblical Category: Signs, Not Violations</h2>
-
-<p>The biblical understanding of miracles is different from the modern philosophical debate in important ways. The Bible does not use the concept of "violation of natural law," for the simple reason that the concept of natural law, as a fixed, autonomous system of regularities, did not exist in the ancient world. The biblical authors did not think of the universe as a machine that God occasionally overrides. They thought of it as God's creation, sustained moment by moment by God's will, in which God is free to act in ordinary and extraordinary ways.</p>
-
-<p>The primary biblical category for what we call miracles is "signs" — events that point beyond themselves to the reality and character of God. When Jesus turns water into wine at Cana, the Gospel of John calls it a "sign" that "revealed his glory" (John 2:11). The point of the miracle is not the violation of chemistry. The point is what the act reveals about who Jesus is and what God is doing through him. When Jesus heals the blind man in John 9, the theological focus is not on the restoration of physical sight but on the metaphor of sight and blindness — the capacity to see what God is doing in the world.</p>
-
-<p>This is not to say that the biblical authors did not believe the events actually occurred. They manifestly did. But the significance of the events, for the biblical writers, was not that they broke the rules. The significance was what they communicated. A miracle, in the biblical understanding, is not God cheating at his own game. It is God communicating through his own creation in a way that catches our attention.</p>
-
-<h2>The Cessationist Position</h2>
-
-<p>Within Christianity, the cessationist position holds that the miraculous gifts of the Spirit — healing, prophecy, tongues, and other extraordinary manifestations — ceased with the death of the apostles or with the completion of the New Testament canon. The argument, most fully developed by B.B. Warfield in <em>Counterfeit Miracles</em> (1918), holds that miracles served a specific purpose: to confirm the apostles as God's authorized messengers and to validate the truth of the gospel during the foundational period of the church. Once the canon was complete and the apostolic witness was established, the confirming purpose of miracles was fulfilled, and the miraculous gifts ceased.</p>
-
-<p>Cessationism has been the dominant position in Reformed and dispensationalist theology. It is grounded in a particular reading of 1 Corinthians 13:10 ("when the perfect comes, the partial will pass away"), interpreted as a reference to the completed canon of Scripture. But this interpretation is disputed. Many scholars argue that "the perfect" refers not to the canon but to the return of Christ — a reading that would undermine the cessationist argument.</p>
-
-<h2>The Continuationist Position</h2>
-
-<p>The continuationist position holds that the gifts of the Spirit have continued throughout church history and remain operative today. This is the position of Pentecostal and charismatic Christianity, which represents the fastest-growing segment of global Christianity — an estimated 600 million adherents and growing. Continuationists argue that neither the New Testament nor church history supports the cessationist claim. The book of Acts does not present miracles as restricted to the apostles — Stephen, Philip, and Ananias all perform miraculous acts, and none of them are apostles. Paul's lists of spiritual gifts in 1 Corinthians 12 and Romans 12 are addressed to ordinary congregations, not to apostles, and contain no suggestion that the gifts are temporary.</p>
-
-<p>Church history, continuationists argue, provides a continuous stream of miracle reports, from the early church fathers (Irenaeus, Augustine, and Gregory the Great all attested to miraculous occurrences) through the medieval period to the modern era. The cessationist claim that miracles ceased with the apostles requires ignoring or explaining away an enormous body of historical testimony.</p>
-
-<h2>The Evidence</h2>
-
-<p>Craig Keener, a New Testament scholar at Asbury Theological Seminary, published a two-volume work, <em>Miracles: The Credibility of the New Testament Accounts</em> (2011), that represents the most comprehensive scholarly examination of miracle claims in the modern era. Keener documented hundreds of cases of reported miraculous healings from around the world — cases attested by medical professionals, confirmed by medical records, and reported by credible witnesses. His cases include the healing of the blind, the deaf, and the paralyzed, as well as cases of recovery from medically terminal conditions.</p>
-
-<p>Keener does not claim that every reported miracle is genuine. He acknowledges the reality of fraud, exaggeration, and misattribution. What he does claim is that the sheer volume of testimony — from diverse cultures, across multiple continents, attested by educated and uneducated witnesses alike — cannot be dismissed as the product of credulity or psychological manipulation. The Western academy's dismissal of miracle claims, Keener argues, is itself a cultural bias — a product of Enlightenment rationalism that assumes in advance what it claims to have demonstrated: that miracles do not occur.</p>
-
-<p>Candy Gunther Brown, a professor of religious studies at Indiana University, conducted empirical studies of healing prayer, published in the <em>Southern Medical Journal</em> (2010), that documented statistically significant improvements in hearing and vision among subjects who received prayer. The studies were peer-reviewed and met the standards of medical research. They do not prove that God healed anyone — the improvements could, in principle, be attributed to psychosomatic factors, placebo effects, or other naturalistic explanations. But they do demonstrate that the effects of prayer are measurable and not merely imagined.</p>
-
-<h2>The Hardest Question: Why Not Everyone?</h2>
-
-<p>Any honest discussion of miracles must confront the hardest question head-on: if God heals some people, why not all? If miracles occur, they occur selectively — and the selectivity is, for many, the most devastating argument against them. The parent who prayed desperately for a dying child and received silence does not need a theology of miracles. They need an honest acknowledgment that the distribution of miraculous healing, if it occurs at all, is not fair — not fair by any standard of fairness that human beings can comprehend.</p>
-
-<p>This is the pastoral reality that any theology of miracles must reckon with. The prosperity gospel — the claim that sufficient faith guarantees healing — is not merely bad theology. It is cruelty dressed in religious language. It tells the person who was not healed that the problem is their faith, adding spiritual guilt to physical suffering. It is, as the theologian Jurgen Moltmann observed, a theology that works for the healthy and the wealthy and has nothing to say to anyone else.</p>
-
-<p>The honest answer is that we do not know why some prayers appear to be answered and others do not. Any claim to understand the pattern is a claim to understand the mind of God, and that is a claim that exceeds human competence. What can be said — what must be said — is that the God revealed in Jesus Christ is a God who heals and a God who suffers, and that these two truths must be held together. The same Jesus who healed the blind man also wept at the tomb of Lazarus. The same Jesus who raised the dead also died. The Christian hope is not that God will spare us from suffering. It is that God is present in the suffering, and that suffering is not the final word.</p>
-
-<h2>The Difference Between Demanding Proof and Remaining Open</h2>
-
-<p>The honest position on miracles is neither credulity nor closed-mindedness. It is openness — a willingness to evaluate claims on their merits, to take testimony seriously without accepting it uncritically, and to resist the temptation to decide in advance what is and is not possible.</p>
-
-<p>The demand for proof before believing is understandable. But it can become its own form of closed-mindedness — a commitment to a particular metaphysical framework (naturalism, the closed universe) that is held with the same dogmatic certainty that the critic attributes to the believer. The person who says "miracles are impossible" is not making a scientific statement. They are making a philosophical commitment — a commitment that the universe is the kind of place in which certain things cannot happen. That commitment may be correct. But it is a commitment, not a conclusion of evidence.</p>
-
-<p>The opposite error is equally dangerous: the willingness to believe any miracle claim without investigation, to attribute every coincidence to divine intervention, to see the hand of God in every parking space and every recovered set of keys. This kind of credulity cheapens the concept of miracle, trivializes the genuine suffering of those who pray and are not healed, and makes Christianity look foolish to precisely the people who most need to hear its message.</p>
-
-<p>The mature position is to hold the question open. To take the testimony of millions of Christians around the world seriously, without demanding that every claim be accepted. To acknowledge the limitations of the naturalistic framework without abandoning the critical thinking that framework has made possible. To pray for healing while knowing that healing is not guaranteed. To live in a universe that is, perhaps, stranger and more open than the Enlightenment taught us to expect — and to be surprised, if surprise comes, with gratitude rather than embarrassment.</p>
-
-<p>Whether miracles still happen is not a question that can be settled by argument. It is a question that is settled, if it is settled, by encounter. And encounter, by its nature, cannot be compelled or predicted. It can only be received.</p>`,
