-    body: `<p>The question comes from honest people, and it deserves an honest answer. In a world of multiple religious traditions — each with its own scriptures, its own saints, its own moral vision, its own account of the human condition — why should anyone prefer Christianity? The question is sharpened by awareness: the person asking it has met Buddhists who are more compassionate than most Christians they know, Muslims whose devotion to prayer puts the average churchgoer to shame, and secular humanists whose moral seriousness exceeds that of many congregations. The claim that Christianity is uniquely true can sound, to such a person, like nothing more than the parochialism of people who have never seriously encountered any alternative.</p>
-
-<p>I want to take the question seriously — not triumphally, not defensively, not with the false confidence of someone who has never felt the force of the objection. I have felt it. I have sat with it. And I have come to a set of convictions that I hold not because I think every other tradition is worthless — I do not — but because I believe Christianity makes claims that are genuinely distinctive, claims that address the deepest human needs in ways that no other tradition addresses them, and claims that I have found to be true in the demanding, unglamorous, daily sense of the word.</p>
-
-<h2>What Christianity Shares with Other Traditions</h2>
-
-<p>Honesty requires beginning with what Christianity shares with other religious traditions, because the common ground is real and significant. Virtually every major religious tradition affirms the existence of a moral order that transcends human preference. Virtually every tradition teaches that human beings are called to something higher than self-interest — to compassion, to justice, to love, to service. Virtually every tradition provides a community of practice, a rhythm of worship, and a framework for making sense of suffering and death. Virtually every tradition contains genuine wisdom, genuine beauty, and genuine encounters with the sacred.</p>
-
-<p>The Golden Rule appears in some form in nearly every tradition. Confucius taught, "Do not do to others what you would not have them do to you." The Hindu Mahabharata says, "This is the sum of duty: do not do to others what would cause pain if done to you." The Buddhist Dhammapada teaches compassion for all sentient beings. Islam grounds its ethic of justice and mercy in the character of Allah, the Most Compassionate, the Most Merciful. Judaism's Hillel summarized the Torah: "What is hateful to you, do not do to your neighbor. That is the whole Torah; the rest is commentary."</p>
-
-<p>Christians who pretend that these traditions contain nothing of value are not being honest. They are being defensive. And defensiveness is a poor foundation for conviction. The question is not whether other traditions contain truth. It is whether Christianity offers something that the others do not.</p>
-
-<h2>What Christianity Claims Uniquely</h2>
-
-<p>Christianity makes three claims that are, taken together, unique in the history of human religion. They are not merely distinctive. They are, if true, world-altering.</p>
-
-<p>The first is the incarnation: the claim that the Creator of the universe became a human being. Not that God sent a messenger, or inspired a prophet, or revealed a law — though Christianity affirms all of these. The claim is that God personally entered the human condition: was born, grew, ate, wept, suffered, and died. No other major religious tradition makes this claim. Islam regards the incarnation as blasphemy — the suggestion that God could become human diminishes God's transcendence. Judaism awaits a Messiah but does not expect the Messiah to be God incarnate. Hinduism has the concept of avatar — divine descent — but the avatars are not fully human in the way that Christian theology insists Jesus was fully human. Buddhism has no Creator God to become incarnate. The incarnation is Christianity's most scandalous claim and its most distinctive one.</p>
-
-<p>The second is the resurrection: the claim that Jesus of Nazareth was raised bodily from the dead, and that his resurrection is the firstfruits of a new creation in which death itself is defeated. Other traditions have concepts of life after death. Other traditions teach reincarnation, or the immortality of the soul, or the survival of consciousness beyond physical death. But the Christian claim is not merely that something survives death. It is that death is reversed — that a specific person who was dead is now alive, in a body, and that this reversal is the turning point of all history. The resurrection is not a symbol. It is not a metaphor for spiritual renewal. It is an event — something that either happened or did not — and Christianity stands or falls on whether it happened.</p>
-
-<p>The third is grace: the claim that the human problem — sin, alienation from God, moral failure, death — is solved not by human effort but by divine initiative. Every other major tradition, in one form or another, presents salvation or liberation as something the human being must achieve: through moral obedience (Judaism, Islam), through enlightenment (Buddhism), through liberation from the cycle of karma (Hinduism), through philosophical wisdom (Stoicism). Christianity reverses the direction. Salvation is not something we achieve. It is something we receive. "While we were still sinners, Christ died for us" (Romans 5:8). The initiative is God's. The work is God's. The gift is God's. The human role is not to earn but to trust.</p>
-
-<p>This is what Dietrich Bonhoeffer meant by "costly grace" — grace that is free but not cheap, grace that costs God everything and costs us our self-sufficiency. It is what Martin Luther meant when he described the gospel as an "alien righteousness" — a righteousness that comes from outside us, that is given to us rather than produced by us. It is the most counterintuitive claim in all of religion, and it is the one that, for millions of people across two millennia, has been the most liberating.</p>
-
-<h2>The Scandal of Particularity</h2>
-
-<p>The deepest objection to Christianity's unique claims is not that they are unverifiable. It is that they are particular. Why one person? Why one time? Why one place? If God wanted to save the world, why not do it in a way that was universally accessible — a truth written in the stars, a revelation given simultaneously to every culture, a law inscribed on every heart without the mediation of a specific historical event?</p>
-
-<p>The scandal of particularity is real, and it should not be minimized. But it should be noted that all significant events are particular. The American Revolution happened once, in one place, to one group of people, and its consequences are universal. The discovery of penicillin happened once, in one laboratory, by one researcher, and it transformed global health. The fact that an event is particular does not make it parochial. It makes it historical. And history, by its nature, is particular.</p>
-
-<p>The Christian claim is not that God is unavailable to those who have never heard of Jesus. The claim is that what God has done in Jesus is the fullest and most decisive revelation of God's character and purposes — and that this revelation, because it happened in history, must be communicated through history: through witness, through community, through the messy, imperfect, often unfaithful process of human beings telling other human beings what they have seen and heard. The particularity of the gospel is not a limitation on God's love. It is a consequence of God's decision to work through history rather than around it.</p>
-
-<h2>The Gospel as Public Truth</h2>
-
-<p>Lesslie Newbigin, the British missionary and theologian who spent decades in India before returning to a secularized England, argued that the gospel must be understood not as a private religious preference but as public truth — a claim about the way things actually are, a claim that has consequences for all of life, not just the "religious" part of life.</p>
-
-<p>In his landmark work <em>The Gospel in a Pluralist Society</em> (1989), Newbigin observed that Western culture had developed a fatal dichotomy between "facts" (the domain of science, publicly verifiable, universally binding) and "values" (the domain of religion, privately held, not subject to public adjudication). In this framework, religious beliefs are relegated to the category of personal preference — like a taste in music or a choice of diet. You may believe in Jesus the way you believe in the superiority of jazz, but you cannot claim that your belief is true in the same way that the law of gravity is true.</p>
-
-<p>Newbigin argued that this dichotomy is incoherent. The gospel makes claims about history, about the nature of reality, about the human condition — claims that are either true or false, not merely preferred or not preferred. To say "Jesus is Lord" is not to express a private religious sentiment. It is to make a claim about who actually rules the universe. And that claim either corresponds to reality or it does not. If it does, it has implications for politics, economics, education, science, art, and every other domain of human activity. It is not a private matter. It is the most public matter imaginable.</p>
-
-<p>The challenge for Christians is to hold this conviction without arrogance — to claim that the gospel is true without claiming that they possess the truth in its entirety, to proclaim Christ without demonizing those who do not follow Christ, to hold firm convictions with genuine humility. This is not easy. The history of Christianity is littered with examples of conviction without humility — crusades, inquisitions, forced conversions, cultural imperialism. These failures are real, and they must be owned.</p>
-
-<h2>Conviction Without Arrogance</h2>
-
-<p>The question of how to hold conviction without arrogance is not merely a tactical question about interfaith relations. It is a theological question at the heart of the gospel itself. If salvation is by grace — if the Christian's relationship with God is a gift, not an achievement — then the Christian has no grounds for superiority over anyone. The person who has been forgiven much has no basis for looking down on the person who has not yet received forgiveness. The proper posture of the Christian toward the non-Christian is not condescension but gratitude — the gratitude of one who has received what they did not deserve and cannot take credit for.</p>
-
-<p>Karl Barth, in his theology of election, insisted that God's grace in Christ is directed not toward a select few but toward all of humanity. The church's role is not to be the exclusive club of the saved but the community that knows and proclaims what God has done for everyone. The church does not own God's grace. It witnesses to it. And a witness who behaves as though the good news belongs to them rather than to the one they are testifying about has misunderstood the nature of testimony.</p>
-
-<h2>The Lived Test</h2>
-
-<p>There is a final criterion that operates below the level of formal argument, and it must be named. The ultimate test of a religious claim is not only whether it is intellectually coherent or historically grounded. It is whether it can bear the weight of an actual human life — with all its suffering, failure, ambiguity, and need. The question is not merely "Is this true?" but "Can I live this? Can this carry me through the worst night I will ever face? Can this make sense of the evil I have witnessed and the evil I have done?"</p>
-
-<p>I have watched the gospel do that work. I have watched it sustain people through the death of children, the collapse of marriages, the destruction of careers, the slow dismantling of health. I have watched it reach into the lives of addicts, of prisoners, of the terminally ill, and offer something that no philosophical system and no therapeutic technique could provide — not a solution but a presence, not a program but a person, not an explanation for suffering but a companion in it who has himself suffered. I have watched people who had every reason to abandon their faith hold on to it — not because they were credulous but because the one they trusted had proven trustworthy in the darkest rooms of their lives.</p>
-
-<p>This is not an argument that would satisfy a philosopher. It is a testimony that satisfies a pastor. And I offer it not as proof but as evidence — the kind of evidence that accumulates not in journals but in hospital rooms and living rooms and gravesides, in the places where abstractions collapse and only the real survives.</p>
-
-<p>Why Christianity and not something else? Not because Christians are better people — they manifestly are not. Not because the church has a spotless record — it manifestly does not. Not because other traditions have nothing to teach — they manifestly do. But because Christianity makes claims that, if true, change everything: that the God who made the universe entered it in person, that this God died and rose again, that this God offers reconciliation as a gift rather than demanding it as an achievement, and that this gift is not a private religious experience but the truth about the way things actually are.</p>
-
-<p>I hold these claims as a man who has been wrong about many things and may yet be wrong about more. I hold them not with the certainty of one who has figured everything out but with the trust of one who has been found by something — someone — too real and too costly to walk away from. That is not proof. It is testimony. And testimony is all any of us has.</p>`,
