-    title: "Raising Kids in a Post-Christian Culture",
-    slug: "raising-kids-post-christian",
-    excerpt:
-      "Your children will not inherit your faith by osmosis. They will inherit it only if it is real enough to survive contact with a world that no longer assumes it is true.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "12 min",
-    body: `<p>There is a moment every Christian parent dreads, though few will admit it publicly. It comes at the dinner table, or in the car, or at bedtime — the moment your child looks at you and asks, with genuine confusion, "Why do we believe this when nobody else does?" The question is not hostile. It is honest. And it exposes the central challenge of raising children in a culture that no longer treats Christianity as the default setting of civilized life.</p>
-
-<p>For most of American history, Christian parents operated within a supporting ecosystem. The culture reinforced what parents taught at home. Schools observed Christian holidays. Neighbors went to church. Television programming, for all its flaws, operated within broadly Christian moral assumptions. A child raised in a Christian home in 1975 could go an entire week without encountering a serious challenge to the faith their parents professed. The culture was the water, and Christianity was the current.</p>
-
-<p>That ecosystem has collapsed. The Pew Research Center's 2021 data shows that 29 percent of Americans are religiously unaffiliated — a number that rises to 34 percent among millennials and 36 percent among Gen Z. Your children's friends, by statistical probability, include a significant number who have no religious framework at all. Their teachers may be agnostic. Their coaches may be atheist. The cultural voices they consume on YouTube, TikTok, and podcasts are overwhelmingly secular. Christianity is no longer the current. It is a tributary, and a shrinking one.</p>
-
-<p>This is not a crisis. It may, in fact, be an opportunity. But it demands that Christian parents abandon strategies that worked when the culture did the heavy lifting and develop new ones suited to a world where faith must be chosen rather than assumed.</p>
-
-<p>The first thing to be honest about is the scope of the shift. A Christian child in a public school in 2026 is not merely encountering a few secular ideas. They are immersed in an alternative moral framework — one built on expressive individualism, therapeutic categories of selfhood, and a default skepticism toward institutional religion that is not hostile so much as indifferent. Their classmates are not anti-Christian. They are post-Christian. They have never considered Christianity as a live option, and the few impressions they have of it are likely shaped by culture war headlines and social media caricatures. Your child is not defending the faith against attacks. They are explaining it to people who have never seriously encountered it. That is a fundamentally different challenge, and it requires a fundamentally different preparation.</p>
-
-<h2>The Failure of Indoctrination</h2>
-
-<p>The instinct of many Christian parents, faced with a hostile or indifferent culture, is to double down on control. Homeschool the children. Restrict their media consumption. Surround them exclusively with Christian friends. Create a hermetically sealed environment in which the faith is never questioned because no alternatives are ever encountered.</p>
-
-<p>The research on this strategy is devastating. The Fuller Youth Institute's "Growing Young" study, led by Kara Powell and Brad Griffin, found that students who had been shielded from doubt and difficult questions in their formative years were more likely — not less — to abandon faith in college. The mechanism is straightforward: a faith that has never been tested is a faith that cannot withstand testing. And testing will come. It always comes.</p>
-
-<p>Christian Smith and Melinda Lundquist Denton, in their landmark National Study of Youth and Religion, found that the dominant religious posture of American teenagers — including those from devoutly Christian homes — was what they called "Moralistic Therapeutic Deism": the vague belief that God exists, wants people to be nice, does not need to be particularly involved in daily life, and exists primarily to make people feel good about themselves. This was not the result of secular indoctrination. It was the result of Christian formation that was a mile wide and an inch deep — that taught rules and stories without forming the intellectual and spiritual muscles needed to hold those stories under pressure.</p>
-
-<p>Indoctrination — the transmission of beliefs without the corresponding development of the capacity to think about those beliefs — produces one of two outcomes. Either the child accepts the beliefs uncritically and becomes a brittle fundamentalist who will shatter at the first serious challenge, or the child rejects the entire framework at the first available opportunity, often with a bitterness proportional to the rigidity of the system they escaped. Neither outcome is faithfulness. Both are failures of formation.</p>
-
-<p>The irony is brutal. The parents who work hardest to protect their children's faith are often the parents most responsible for its eventual destruction. The sealed environment does not produce certainty. It produces a faith that has never developed an immune system — that collapses at the first exposure to a world full of competing ideas, attractive alternatives, and persuasive objections. The child who has been taught that evolution is a lie will not be strengthened when they encounter the evidence in a college biology course. They will be shattered. And the shattering will extend far beyond biology. If the authority figures in their life were wrong about this, the child will reason, what else were they wrong about? The entire edifice comes down, not because the questions are unanswerable but because the child was never given permission to ask them.</p>
-
-<h2>Formation Over Information</h2>
-
-<p>The alternative to indoctrination is formation — a word that carries different weight. Indoctrination is about content: getting the right beliefs into the child's head. Formation is about character: shaping the kind of person who can hold those beliefs responsibly, question them honestly, and live them out under pressure. The distinction is not merely semantic. It represents two entirely different philosophies of spiritual development.</p>
-
-<p>James K.A. Smith, drawing on Augustine, has argued persuasively that human beings are not primarily thinking things but desiring things. We are not brains on sticks, making rational calculations about which worldview to adopt. We are embodied creatures whose deepest convictions are shaped less by arguments than by practices — by the rhythms of daily life, the stories we inhabit, the communities we belong to, the habits that form us below the level of conscious decision. "Before you think," Smith writes, "you love. Before you love, you are shaped by what you worship."</p>
-
-<p>This means that the most important thing Christian parents do is not the content they teach but the life they model. Children are not persuaded into faith by superior arguments. They are formed into it by the texture of daily life — by watching their parents pray not as performance but as genuine conversation with a God they actually seem to know. By overhearing their parents wrestle with hard questions rather than dismissing them. By experiencing a household where grace is not a theological concept but the actual atmosphere of the home.</p>
-
-<h2>Answering "Is God Real?"</h2>
-
-<p>When a child asks "Is God real?" the worst possible response is certainty. Not because certainty is always wrong, but because performed certainty — the kind that shuts down the question rather than honoring it — teaches the child that doubt is dangerous, that questions are threats, and that faith means pretending to know things you do not know.</p>
-
-<p>The better response begins with honesty: "I believe God is real, and here is why." The "here is why" matters enormously. If the only reason you can give is "because the Bible says so," you have given your child a circular argument that will not survive their first philosophy class. If the reason is "because I was raised to believe it," you have given them a sociological explanation that applies equally to every religion on earth.</p>
-
-<p>But if you can say, "I believe God is real because I have experienced something in prayer that I cannot explain any other way," or "because the story of Jesus makes sense of the world in ways that no other story does," or "because I have watched people transformed by this faith in ways that I cannot attribute to psychology alone" — then you have given your child something real. Not proof. Real faith has never operated on proof. But testimony — the honest account of one person's encounter with the divine — carries a weight that arguments cannot replicate.</p>
-
-<p>And then add the most important sentence: "But I understand why someone would not believe. There are smart, good, honest people who look at the same evidence and reach a different conclusion. Your faith has to be yours, not mine. I will not love you less if you reach a different answer."</p>
-
-<p>That sentence — "I will not love you less" — is the most evangelistically powerful thing a Christian parent can say. Because the child who knows that their parents' love is not contingent on their belief is the child who is free to believe honestly. And honest belief is the only kind worth having.</p>
-
-<h2>Creating Space for Doubt</h2>
-
-<p>There is a difference between creating space for doubt and manufacturing it. Creating space for doubt means that when your child says, "I'm not sure I believe in God anymore," you do not panic, lecture, or increase their church attendance. You say, "Tell me more about that." You listen. You take their intellectual development seriously enough to engage with their actual questions rather than the questions you wish they were asking.</p>
-
-<p>Manufacturing doubt, on the other hand, is the error of parents who are so anxious to produce critically thinking children that they introduce challenges before the child has developed the resources to process them. A six-year-old does not need to hear about the Documentary Hypothesis. A ten-year-old does not need a lecture on theodicy. Faith development, like every other form of development, is stage-appropriate. The child psychologist James Fowler mapped six stages of faith development, from the intuitive-projective faith of early childhood through the universalizing faith of mature adulthood. Each stage has its own integrity. Rushing a child through them is as harmful as preventing them from progressing.</p>
-
-<p>The goal is a home where questions are welcome but not weaponized — where a child can express doubt without being shamed and explore alternatives without being abandoned. This requires parents who have done their own intellectual work, who have confronted their own doubts, and who can model the integration of rigorous thinking and genuine faith. Children do not need parents who have all the answers. They need parents who are not afraid of the questions.</p>
-
-<h2>Practices That Form</h2>
-
-<p>The research on faith transmission is remarkably consistent on one point: practices matter more than programs. The National Study of Youth and Religion found that the single strongest predictor of a young person's faith in adulthood was not youth group attendance, not Christian schooling, not mission trips — but the religious practice of their parents at home. Children whose parents prayed with them, read Scripture with them, and discussed faith openly at home were far more likely to carry that faith into adulthood than children whose parents outsourced formation to the church.</p>
-
-<p>The dinner table is a formative space. Not because parents should turn meals into Bible studies — that is a reliable way to make children dread both dinner and Scripture — but because the table is where a family practices the disciplines of presence, gratitude, and conversation. A simple practice: before the meal, each person names one thing they are grateful for and one thing that was hard about the day. This is not a religious exercise in any formal sense, but it is a deeply formative one. It teaches children to pay attention to their own experience, to name both gift and struggle, and to do so in the presence of people who care. Over years, this practice shapes a posture toward life that is essentially prayerful — attentive, grateful, honest — even before the child has the vocabulary to name it as such.</p>
-
-<p>Bedtime prayers are another formative practice, provided they are not reduced to rote recitation. A child who learns to pray by hearing their parent pray aloud — honestly, in ordinary language, about real things — is learning something no Sunday school curriculum can teach. They are learning that God is someone you talk to, not someone you recite at. They are learning that prayer includes confusion, frustration, and need — not just thanksgiving and request. They are learning that the God their parents worship is big enough to handle the full range of human experience.</p>
-
-<p>Service is formative. Children who grow up watching their parents care for the vulnerable — visiting the sick, feeding the hungry, welcoming the stranger — are receiving a formation in the character of God that no theological lecture can replicate. They are learning that faith is not a set of beliefs to be defended but a way of life to be practiced. And that learning happens below the level of conscious decision, in the realm of habit and desire where the most durable convictions are formed.</p>
-
-<h2>Faith vs. Religion</h2>
-
-<p>The distinction between passing on faith and passing on religion is the most important distinction a Christian parent can learn to make. Religion, in the sense I mean here, is the external apparatus: the attendance, the vocabulary, the moral code, the tribal identity. Faith is the interior reality: the trust, the relationship, the transformation, the living encounter with the God who is actually there.</p>
-
-<p>It is entirely possible to pass on religion without passing on faith. This is, in fact, the default outcome of most Christian formation in America. The child learns to attend, to perform, to use the right words, to conform to the expected behavior — without ever encountering the living God who is supposed to be at the center of all of it. Christian Smith's research named this precisely: Moralistic Therapeutic Deism is religion without faith. It is the shell without the life.</p>
-
-<p>To pass on faith, you must have faith to pass on. This is the most uncomfortable truth in the entire conversation. If your own faith is merely habitual — if you attend church out of obligation, pray out of superstition, and read Scripture out of guilt — your children will absorb the habit without the substance. They will inherit your religion and discard it as soon as the cultural pressure to maintain it disappears. And that cultural pressure is disappearing rapidly.</p>
-
-<p>But if your faith is alive — if your children see you wrestle with Scripture, weep in prayer, confess failure, extend grace, and return again and again to a God you clearly know and not merely know about — then you are giving them something worth inheriting. Not a set of propositions to be memorized. Not a tribal identity to be maintained. But a relationship to be entered, a life to be lived, a reality to be encountered on their own terms, in their own time, with their own questions.</p>
-
-<p>You cannot control whether your children will believe. That is between them and God, and the sooner you make peace with that, the better parent you will be. What you can control is whether what you hand them is worth believing — whether the faith they see in you is substantial enough, honest enough, and alive enough to survive the world they are growing up in. That is the only question that matters. And it is a question addressed not to your children but to you.</p>`
