-    excerpt:
-      "The algorithm is not neutral. It is forming you into a particular kind of person — distracted, reactive, shallow, and perpetually outraged. The question is whether your faith is strong enough to resist it.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "11 min",
-    body: `<p>In the year 2007, the average American spent approximately 2.7 hours per day consuming digital media. By 2023, that number had risen to over seven hours. In the space of sixteen years, the screens in our pockets have colonized more than a third of our waking hours. This is not a technological development. It is a spiritual one. Because whatever commands that much of your attention is, functionally, your liturgy. It is forming you. The only question is: into what?</p>
-
-<p>The attention economy — the system in which technology companies compete for the limited resource of human attention in order to sell it to advertisers — is not merely an economic arrangement. It is, in the language of James K.A. Smith, a "liturgy of desire." It shapes what you want, how you see, and who you become. And it does so with a sophistication that would make the most accomplished spiritual director in Christian history weep with envy, because the algorithms that curate your digital experience know more about your desires, fears, and vulnerabilities than your pastor, your spouse, or your therapist.</p>
-
-<p>The church has been remarkably slow to reckon with this. The dominant Christian responses to digital technology have been either uncritical adoption (churches migrating to Instagram, pastors building personal brands on Twitter, youth groups moving to TikTok) or blanket rejection (screen time is bad, social media is evil, delete your accounts). Neither response takes seriously the depth of the challenge. The attention economy is not a tool to be used or a temptation to be resisted. It is an environment that shapes the soul, and the Christian tradition has more resources for understanding that shaping than it has yet deployed. The question is not whether you will be formed by your digital environment. You will be. The question is whether you will be formed intentionally or by default — and the default, as we shall see, is not neutral.</p>
-
-<h2>What the Attention Economy Does to Your Soul</h2>
-
-<p>The Desert Fathers and Mothers of the fourth century — Antony, Macarius, Evagrius, Syncletica — developed a sophisticated taxonomy of the spiritual dangers they called the <em>logismoi</em>, the patterns of thought that pull the soul away from God. Chief among them was <em>acedia</em> — a restless inability to stay present, a compulsive need for novelty and stimulation, a soul-deep resistance to the slow, patient work of prayer and self-examination. Evagrius called it "the noonday demon" because it struck hardest when the monk was supposed to be at prayer, filling the mind with thoughts of what else he could be doing, who else he could be talking to, what other place might be more interesting than this cell.</p>
-
-<p>It is impossible to read the Desert Fathers' descriptions of acedia without recognizing the pathology they describe in our relationship with our phones. The compulsive checking. The inability to sit in silence. The restless scrolling through an infinite feed, not because any particular content satisfies but because the act of scrolling itself provides just enough dopamine to keep the hand moving. The Desert Fathers would have recognized our condition immediately. They would have called it what it is: a spiritual sickness — a soul habituated to distraction and thereby rendered incapable of the sustained attention that prayer, Scripture, and genuine human encounter require.</p>
-
-<p>The neuroscience confirms what the monks intuited. Research from the laboratory of Adam Gazzaley at UC San Francisco has demonstrated that the constant task-switching demanded by digital media degrades the brain's capacity for sustained attention. The average attention span for a single task has decreased from twelve seconds in 2000 to approximately eight seconds — one second less than that attributed to a goldfish in the widely cited (though methodologically questionable) Microsoft study of 2015. Whether or not goldfish are the right comparison, the trend is not in dispute. We are becoming worse at paying attention, and the technologies we use are designed to make us worse.</p>
-
-<p>The dopamine system is the mechanism. Every notification, every like, every new piece of content triggers a small release of dopamine — not the dopamine of satisfaction but the dopamine of anticipation, the neurochemical signature of "there might be something good here." This is the same system exploited by slot machines, and the parallel is not accidental. Former Facebook vice president Chamath Palihapitiya said in 2017: "The short-term, dopamine-driven feedback loops that we have created are destroying how society works." Tristan Harris, former Google design ethicist, has been even more explicit: "Your phone is a slot machine. Every time you check it, you are pulling the lever."</p>
-
-<h2>The Performance of Faith Online</h2>
-
-<p>Social media has not merely distracted Christians from their faith. It has deformed the faith itself. The logic of social media — which rewards performance, controversy, brevity, and emotional intensity — is fundamentally incompatible with the logic of the gospel, which demands hiddenness, patience, nuance, and the willingness to say unpopular things without regard for the audience's reaction.</p>
-
-<p>Jesus was explicit about this incompatibility, though he spoke about it in terms of the performative religion of his own day: "When you pray, do not be like the hypocrites, for they love to pray standing in the synagogues and on the street corners to be seen by others. Truly I tell you, they have received their reward in full. But when you pray, go into your room, close the door and pray to your Father, who is unseen" (Matthew 6:5-6). The principle is clear: authentic spiritual practice is incompatible with public performance. The moment you pray for an audience, you have stopped praying to God.</p>
-
-<p>Social media is a street corner. Every post about your quiet time, every Instagram of your Bible and coffee, every tweet about what God is "teaching" you, every carefully curated image of your family devotions is a form of public prayer — prayer performed for the approval of the crowd. The likes and shares are the reward Jesus warned about. They are sufficient unto themselves and spiritually hollow.</p>
-
-<p>The outrage cycles are equally deforming. The algorithm has learned that anger is the most engaging emotion — content that provokes outrage is shared more widely, generates more comments, and keeps users on the platform longer than any other type of content. Christians have been spectacularly susceptible to this dynamic. The culture war — which is, in many ways, a product of social media's incentive structures rather than a reflection of genuine cultural reality — has turned many Christians into people whose primary public witness is not love but anger. Not grace but condemnation. Not the patient, suffering, enemy-loving posture of Christ but the perpetually offended, constantly triggered posture of the algorithm's ideal user.</p>
-
-<h2>Can You Be Formed Online?</h2>
-
-<p>The question of whether genuine spiritual formation can happen online is not a simple one, and the honest answer is: partially, but with severe limitations.</p>
-
-<p>The COVID-19 pandemic forced a massive experiment in digital worship. When churches closed their buildings in March 2020, millions of Christians experienced worship exclusively through screens for months or, in some cases, years. The results were instructive. On the positive side, digital worship provided access to people who had been excluded from physical church: the homebound, the disabled, those living in areas without a church that fit their tradition. Sermon content — the intellectual and homiletical dimension of worship — translated reasonably well to digital formats.</p>
-
-<p>But what did not translate was everything that makes worship more than a lecture. The embodied presence of other worshippers. The physical act of standing, sitting, kneeling, receiving bread and wine. The accidental conversations before and after the service that are often more pastorally significant than the sermon itself. The communal singing, which neuroscience has shown synchronizes heart rates and creates a physiological experience of unity that no video call can replicate. Digital worship, for all its accessibility, proved to be a reduced version of the real thing — serviceable in an emergency but insufficient as a long-term substitute.</p>
-
-<p>Online Bible studies, devotional content, theological podcasts, and virtual spiritual direction can supplement spiritual formation. They cannot replace it. The reason is not merely practical but theological: Christianity is an incarnational faith. It insists that God did not merely transmit information to humanity but took on flesh and dwelt among us. The medium was the message. And a faith that is rooted in incarnation cannot be fully practiced in a disembodied medium.</p>
-
-<h2>A Digital Rule of Life</h2>
-
-<p>The monastic tradition developed what it called a "rule of life" — a set of practices, rhythms, and boundaries designed to shape the monk's day around the central priority of prayer and communion with God. The Rule of Saint Benedict, written in the sixth century, governed everything from meal times to sleeping arrangements to the hours of prayer. Its purpose was not legalistic control but the creation of an environment in which spiritual growth could happen — because Benedict understood that environment shapes character more reliably than willpower.</p>
-
-<p>The contemporary Christian needs a digital rule of life for the same reason the sixth-century monk needed a monastic rule: because without intentional structure, the default environment will form you in ways you do not want to be formed. The algorithm's default formation is toward distraction, anxiety, comparison, outrage, and the endless consumption of novelty. A digital rule of life is a deliberate countermeasure.</p>
-
-<p>The first element is temporal boundaries. Choose specific times of day when you will not use your phone — not as an exercise in willpower but as a structural protection of the activities that matter most. The first hour of the morning. The last hour before sleep. Mealtimes. The time you spend with your children. Worship. These are the hours when your attention is most valuable and most vulnerable, and ceding them to the algorithm is a choice with spiritual consequences whether you recognize it or not.</p>
-
-<p>The second element is content curation. You are responsible for what you consume, and the algorithm will not curate your feed for your spiritual health. It will curate it for engagement, which means it will feed you whatever triggers the strongest emotional response — outrage, anxiety, envy, lust. Deliberately follow accounts and subscribe to content that forms you toward wisdom, compassion, and depth. Deliberately unfollow and mute content that forms you toward anger, comparison, and contempt. This is not avoidance. It is stewardship of the most limited resource you possess: your attention.</p>
-
-<p>The third element is Sabbath from screens. The Jewish practice of Shabbat — one day in seven set apart from productive labor — is not merely a quaint religious tradition. It is an act of resistance against the totalizing claims of work, consumption, and productivity. A digital Sabbath — one day per week in which you do not check email, browse social media, or consume digital content — is the contemporary equivalent. It is the declaration that you are not available to the machine for twenty-four hours, that your attention belongs to God and to the people in front of you, and that the world will survive without your participation in the scroll.</p>
-
-<p>The fourth element is silence. The Desert Fathers and Mothers understood that silence is not the absence of sound but the presence of attention. In silence, the soul hears what the noise has been drowning out — the voice of God, yes, but also the voice of your own deepest self, the self that has been buried under the avalanche of content, opinion, and stimulation. A daily practice of silence — even ten minutes — is profoundly countercultural. It is also profoundly necessary. Because a soul that cannot be silent cannot pray. And a soul that cannot pray is a soul that the algorithm has already claimed.</p>
-
-<p>The screen is not your enemy. But it is not your friend either. It is a tool, and like every tool, it will be used by whoever holds it. The question is whether you are holding your phone or your phone is holding you. The answer, for most of us, is more uncomfortable than we would like to admit. And the first step toward freedom is the willingness to admit it.</p>`
