-      "The era when Christianity was the cultural default is over. What comes next may be harder, smaller, and more costly. It may also be closer to the faith that Jesus actually described.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "13 min",
-    body: `<p>On February 27, 380 AD, Emperor Theodosius I issued the Edict of Thessalonica, declaring Nicene Christianity the official religion of the Roman Empire. With a single imperial decree, the faith of a persecuted minority became the faith of the state. Churches that had met in catacombs and private homes were suddenly building basilicas with public funds. Bishops who had been imprisoned became advisors to emperors. The cross, which had been the instrument of the movement's founder's execution, was mounted on the standards of the imperial legions. The transformation was so complete and so rapid that within a generation, it was nearly impossible to remember what Christianity had been before it had power.</p>
-
-<p>That transformation — from persecuted sect to established religion, from voluntary community to cultural default — is what historians call "Christendom." And for approximately 1,600 years, it was the dominant mode of Christian existence in the Western world. Whether you were born in medieval France, Reformation Germany, colonial New England, or mid-twentieth-century America, Christianity was the water you swam in — the assumed religious framework of your culture, the default setting of public morality, the invisible architecture of your social world. You did not choose Christianity. It chose you, by virtue of your birth into a civilization shaped by its institutions, assumptions, and calendar.</p>
-
-<p>Christendom is over. The sociological evidence is overwhelming and has been accumulating for decades. Charles Taylor, in his magisterial <em>A Secular Age</em>, traces the shift from a society in which belief in God was the unquestioned default to one in which it is merely one option among many — and not the most culturally prestigious option at that. In 1960, the sociologist Peter Berger predicted that by the twenty-first century, religion would persist only in small, embattled enclaves. He was partially wrong about the global picture — religion has proven far more resilient worldwide than secularization theorists predicted — but he was remarkably prescient about the West. In America, in Europe, in Canada, in Australia, the cultural dominance of Christianity has collapsed within a single lifetime.</p>
-
-<p>The question that confronts every thoughtful Christian is not how to reverse this collapse. That project has been tried, repeatedly and with increasing desperation, and it has failed. The question is what Christianity looks like when it is no longer the cultural default — when being a Christian is not something you inherit but something you choose, at a cost, against the grain of the culture rather than with it.</p>
-
-<h2>The End of Cultural Christianity</h2>
-
-<p>The most significant consequence of the end of Christendom is the disappearance of the cultural Christian — the person who identified as Christian not out of conviction but out of convention. In 1972, when the General Social Survey first asked Americans about their religious identity, less than 5 percent said "none." These were not all devout believers. Many were nominal — attending church on Easter and Christmas, checking "Protestant" on forms, vaguely assenting to theism without it making any discernible difference in their lives. They were Christian the way they were American: by accident of birth, reinforced by social expectation, never seriously examined.</p>
-
-<p>The rise of the "nones" — from 5 percent in 1972 to 29 percent in 2023 — does not primarily represent a wave of deconversion. It represents the departure of the nominally committed. The people who are leaving are, by and large, the people who were never really there — who attended out of social obligation, who identified out of cultural habit, who held the label without the substance. Their departure is not a loss of faith. It is the shedding of a pretense.</p>
-
-<p>This should not be mourned. It should be recognized for what it is: a clarification. For the first time in 1,600 years, the boundary between the church and the culture is becoming visible again. The people who remain — who continue to identify as Christian, who continue to attend worship, who continue to practice the faith — are increasingly people who have made a deliberate choice. They are there because they believe, not because they are expected to be there. And a church composed of people who have chosen to be there is a fundamentally different institution from a church composed of people who have never considered the alternative.</p>
-
-<h2>Before Constantine</h2>
-
-<p>The pre-Constantinian church — the church of the first three centuries — is the closest historical analogue to the situation Western Christians now face. And its characteristics are instructive, not as a template to be replicated but as evidence that Christianity can not only survive without cultural power but can thrive without it.</p>
-
-<p>The pre-Constantinian church was small. At the time of Constantine's conversion in 312, Christians comprised approximately 10 percent of the Roman Empire's population — perhaps six million people in an empire of sixty million. They were a minority, and they knew it. Their self-understanding was shaped not by triumphalism but by vulnerability. They were, in the language of 1 Peter, "foreigners and exiles" — people whose primary citizenship was elsewhere, who lived in the empire but did not fully belong to it.</p>
-
-<p>The pre-Constantinian church was communal. The primary unit of Christian life was not the individual believer or the nuclear family but the <em>ekklesia</em> — the gathered community. Early Christian worship took place in homes (the archaeological evidence for purpose-built churches before 250 is almost nonexistent), and the house church functioned not merely as a worship space but as an economic community. Members shared resources. The wealthy subsidized the poor. Widows, orphans, and the destitute were supported from a common fund. The Roman observer Lucian of Samosata, writing in the second century, noted with evident bafflement that Christians "look after their poor" and that "their first lawgiver persuaded them that they are all brothers."</p>
-
-<p>The pre-Constantinian church was costly. To become a Christian in the second century was to accept social exclusion, economic disadvantage, and the real possibility of state-sponsored violence. The catechumenate — the process of preparation for baptism — lasted up to three years and involved not merely doctrinal instruction but a comprehensive reordering of the candidate's social and economic life. Certain professions were incompatible with baptism: soldiers, gladiators, actors, prostitutes, magistrates who administered capital punishment. To be baptized was to accept that certain doors were now closed to you, certain social networks severed, certain career paths abandoned. It was, in every meaningful sense, a death — a voluntary death of the old self and its allegiances, in exchange for a new identity that the empire regarded with suspicion and occasionally with lethal hostility.</p>
-
-<p>The pre-Constantinian church was prophetic. Because it had no political power, it had no political obligations. It could speak truth to the empire without worrying about the electoral consequences. It could critique the emperor without fearing the loss of its tax-exempt status. It could champion the cause of the poor without calculating the impact on its donor base. The prophetic voice of the early church was credible precisely because it came from a position of powerlessness. The church that speaks from the steps of the Capitol does not sound the same as the church that speaks from the catacombs, even if it is saying the same words.</p>
-
-<h2>The Four Marks of Post-Christendom Faith</h2>
-
-<p>If the pre-Constantinian church is our closest historical precedent, then the faith that is emerging in the post-Christendom West will be marked by four characteristics that mirror the early church's experience — not because we are trying to replicate it, but because the same conditions produce the same adaptations.</p>
-
-<p>The first mark is <strong>voluntariness</strong>. Post-Christendom faith is chosen faith. Nobody drifts into it. Nobody inherits it without examination. Nobody maintains it out of social convention because there is no longer a social convention to maintain. This means that the Christianity that survives the end of Christendom will be a Christianity of conviction — smaller, yes, but more intentional, more committed, and more capable of articulating why it believes what it believes. The cultural Christian, who never had to give a reason for the faith, is gone. The post-Christendom Christian must know what they believe, why they believe it, and what it costs them — because someone will ask, and "because that's how I was raised" is no longer a sufficient answer.</p>
-
-<p>The second mark is <strong>costliness</strong>. When Christianity is the cultural default, believing costs nothing. When it is a minority position, believing costs something — social capital, professional advancement, the easy assumption that your worldview is normal. Dietrich Bonhoeffer's distinction between "cheap grace" and "costly grace" was prophetic: "Cheap grace is the preaching of forgiveness without requiring repentance, baptism without church discipline, Communion without confession. Cheap grace is grace without discipleship, grace without the cross, grace without Jesus Christ, living and incarnate." Christendom was, in many ways, a system for the mass production of cheap grace. Its end may be the recovery of the costly kind.</p>
-
-<p>The third mark is <strong>communality</strong>. The individualistic Christianity of modern America — in which faith is a personal relationship with Jesus that requires no community, no accountability, no institutional commitment — is a product of Christendom. It was possible to be a "lone ranger" Christian only because the culture did the work of formation that the community should have been doing. Without cultural Christianity to provide the ambient support, the individual Christian who is disconnected from community will not survive. The post-Christendom church will be, of necessity, a community of mutual formation — a people who gather not because it is socially expected but because they cannot sustain their faith alone.</p>
-
-<p>The fourth mark is <strong>prophetic witness</strong>. The church that has divested itself of political power is the church that is free to speak prophetically again. The reason the American church's moral voice has been so thoroughly discredited is not that it has spoken too much but that it has spoken from a position of power, using the language of prophecy to advance the interests of empire. The post-Christendom church, stripped of its political influence, can recover the prophetic voice — speaking truth to power rather than as power, advocating for the marginalized rather than for its own institutional interests, critiquing the culture from a position of costly solidarity rather than comfortable distance.</p>
-
-<h2>Why This Might Be the Best Thing</h2>
-
-<p>There is a persistent fear among American Christians that the end of Christendom is the end of Christianity. This fear is understandable but historically unfounded. Christianity was born as a minority movement in a hostile empire, and it not only survived but transformed that empire — not through political power but through the quality of its common life. The faith that survived the Roman arena, the barbarian invasions, the Islamic conquests, the Mongol devastation, the French Revolution, and Soviet persecution is not going to be defeated by the American nones.</p>
-
-<p>The end of Christendom is the end of a particular arrangement — the fusion of Christian faith with cultural power — that has been as much a liability as an asset for the church's mission. Christendom gave the church buildings, budgets, social prestige, and political influence. It also gave the church the Crusades, the Inquisition, the justification of slavery, the persecution of Jews, the suppression of scientific inquiry, and the confusion of the gospel with Western civilization. The ledger is, at best, mixed. And there is a strong case to be made that the liabilities have outweighed the assets — that the church's entanglement with power has done more to obscure the gospel than any persecution could have accomplished.</p>
-
-<p>The missionary Lesslie Newbigin, returning to England in 1974 after decades of service in India, was struck by a paradox: the post-Christian West was, in many ways, harder mission territory than pre-Christian India. India had never heard the gospel and was curious. England had heard it, domesticated it, and was bored. The challenge of post-Christendom is not ignorance of Christianity but familiarity with a distorted version of it — a version so entangled with power, privilege, and cultural conformity that the original message has been obscured beyond recognition.</p>
-
-<p>And yet the collapse of Christendom has also produced something unexpected: a generation of people who are genuinely hungry for the things the gospel offers — community, meaning, moral formation, hope in the face of death — but who have never encountered those things in a form untainted by institutional corruption and political manipulation. The post-Christian person is not, typically, hostile to Jesus. They are hostile to what the church has done with Jesus. And that distinction matters enormously, because it means the mission field is not closed. It is waiting for a version of the faith that matches the original. The sociologist of religion Rodney Stark demonstrated that the early church grew not through evangelistic campaigns but through the attractiveness of its common life — through the visible evidence that Christians loved each other and their neighbors in ways the surrounding culture could not replicate. The post-Christendom church has the opportunity to demonstrate the same thing, and the advantage of not having to overcome the baggage of establishment religion to do it.</p>
-
-<p>There are signs that this is already happening. The growth of intentional Christian communities, the renewed interest in ancient liturgical practices among young adults, the emergence of churches that prioritize justice, intellectual honesty, and hospitality over political alignment and cultural conformity — these are not the signs of a faith in retreat. They are the signs of a faith in renovation. The building is being stripped to its foundation, and what is being discovered beneath the layers of Christendom is a foundation that is more solid, more radical, and more compelling than many had dared to hope.</p>
-
-<p>The opportunity, then, is not to rebuild Christendom — to "take back" the culture, to restore the Christian consensus, to make Christianity the default again. That project is not only doomed to fail; it is theologically misguided. Jesus did not come to establish a civilization. He came to inaugurate a kingdom — a kingdom that operates by different rules than any empire, that measures greatness by service rather than dominance, and that advances not through the sword but through the cross.</p>
-
-<p>The post-Christendom church has the opportunity to recover that kingdom vision — to rediscover a faith that is voluntary rather than compulsory, costly rather than comfortable, communal rather than individualistic, and prophetic rather than politically captive. This will be a smaller church. It will be a poorer church. It will be a church without a seat at the table of power. But it may also be a church that more closely resembles the one Jesus described — the mustard seed, the leaven in the dough, the treasure hidden in a field. Small, hidden, leavening. Not the empire's religion. The empire's conscience.</p>
-
-<p>That is a future worth moving toward. Not with nostalgia for what has been lost, but with hope for what might be recovered — a faith stripped of its cultural privilege, reduced to its essence, and discovered, perhaps for the first time in sixteen centuries, to be enough.</p>`
