-    readTime: "14 min",
-    body: `<p>In January 2002, the Boston Globe's Spotlight team published the first of a series of investigative reports documenting the systematic sexual abuse of children by Catholic priests in the Archdiocese of Boston. The reporting revealed that Cardinal Bernard Law and his predecessors had known about the abuse for decades. They had not merely failed to stop it. They had actively enabled it — transferring accused priests to new parishes, suppressing complaints, pressuring victims into silence, deploying church lawyers to intimidate families who came forward. The institution had chosen its reputation over its children.</p>
-
-<p>The Boston reporting was not the beginning. Allegations had surfaced for years — in Louisiana in the 1980s, in documents uncovered by attorney Jeff Anderson, in the courageous testimonies of survivors who were dismissed as liars or opportunists. But the Globe's investigation achieved what individual complaints had not: it established a pattern. Not isolated cases. Not a few bad actors. A system. A system designed, at every level, to protect the institution at the expense of the vulnerable.</p>
-
-<p>What followed was a global reckoning. Grand jury reports in Pennsylvania documented over a thousand child victims and three hundred predator priests across six dioceses, spanning seven decades. Investigations in Ireland, Australia, Germany, Chile, and dozens of other countries revealed the same architecture of abuse and concealment. The case of Theodore McCarrick — a cardinal, one of the most powerful figures in American Catholicism — demonstrated that the rot reached the highest levels of church leadership. McCarrick had abused seminarians and minors for decades while ascending to positions of extraordinary influence. People knew. The institution knew. It advanced him anyway.</p>
-
-<h2>The Protestant Reckoning</h2>
-
-<p>For years, many Protestants watched the Catholic crisis with a mixture of horror and self-congratulation. The problem, they told themselves, was celibacy. The problem was hierarchy. The problem was a closed institutional culture unique to Rome. They were wrong on every count.</p>
-
-<p>In 2022, the Southern Baptist Convention released the findings of an independent investigation conducted by Guidepost Solutions. The report documented a pattern of abuse, cover-up, and institutional self-protection that mirrored the Catholic crisis in its essential features. The SBC's Executive Committee had maintained a secret list of accused abusers while publicly resisting calls to create a database or implement safeguards. Leaders who advocated for survivors were marginalized. Christa Brown, who had spent years pressing the SBC to act, was dismissed and demeaned by the very leaders charged with protecting the vulnerable. The Guidepost report revealed internal communications in which SBC leaders discussed strategies not for addressing abuse but for avoiding legal liability.</p>
-
-<p>The SBC was not alone. Willow Creek Community Church — once the flagship of the American megachurch movement — faced its own crisis when founder Bill Hybels was accused of sexual harassment and misconduct spanning decades. The church's initial response was to defend Hybels, attack his accusers, and circle the institutional wagons. An independent investigation eventually substantiated the allegations, but only after years of institutional resistance and incalculable damage to the women who came forward.</p>
-
-<p>Ravi Zacharias, perhaps the most prominent Christian apologist of his generation, was posthumously revealed to have engaged in a sustained pattern of sexual abuse, including the abuse of massage therapists at spas he owned. His organization, RZIM, had received complaints during his lifetime and failed to investigate them meaningfully. After his death, an independent investigation documented the scope of his predation. The organization that bore his name eventually shut down — but only after decades of institutional complicity.</p>
-
-<p>The pattern extends to independent churches, parachurch organizations, mission agencies, and Christian schools. Sovereign Grace Churches faced allegations of systematic abuse cover-up. The Institute in Basic Life Principles, founded by Bill Gothard, protected its leader despite years of sexual harassment complaints. Bob Jones University hired an independent firm to investigate its handling of sexual abuse — then fired the firm when its findings became uncomfortable. The list is long. It is still growing.</p>
-
-<h2>The Structure That Produces the Pattern</h2>
-
-<p>Individual predators exist in every institution. What distinguishes the church's abuse crisis is not the existence of abusers but the structural conditions that enable them and the institutional reflexes that protect them. These conditions are identifiable. They are predictable. And they are, in most churches, still firmly in place.</p>
-
-<p>The first condition is unaccountable authority. Churches that concentrate spiritual authority in a single leader — and that teach congregants to equate questioning that leader with questioning God — create the perfect environment for abuse. When a pastor is treated as God's anointed representative, challenging his behavior becomes an act of spiritual rebellion. Victims who come forward are not merely disbelieved. They are recast as enemies of the faith.</p>
-
-<p>The second condition is theological manipulation. Abusers in church settings routinely weaponize the language of faith to silence their victims. Forgiveness becomes a tool of coercion: victims are told that a failure to forgive their abuser is a sin. Submission becomes a mechanism of control: women and children are taught that obedience to authority is obedience to God. Grace becomes a shield for the perpetrator: calls for accountability are reframed as failures of Christian charity.</p>
-
-<p>The third condition is institutional self-preservation. When an organization believes it is doing God's work, any threat to the organization's reputation becomes, in the distorted logic of institutional theology, a threat to God's work. Protecting the institution is reframed as protecting the mission. Silencing a victim is reframed as preventing scandal that would harm the gospel. The institution's survival becomes the highest good, and individual victims are sacrificed on its altar.</p>
-
-<blockquote>The church did not merely fail to protect the vulnerable. It constructed theological justifications for leaving them unprotected. That is not a failure of implementation. It is a failure of theology.</blockquote>
-
-<h2>What Mandatory Reporting Means and Why Churches Resist It</h2>
-
-<p>In most U.S. states, clergy are mandatory reporters — legally required to report suspected child abuse to civil authorities. In practice, compliance is inconsistent. Many states include clergy exemptions when the information is obtained through confidential communications such as confession or pastoral counseling. Some denominations have actively lobbied to preserve these exemptions.</p>
-
-<p>The resistance to mandatory reporting reveals a deeper theological problem. It rests on the assumption that the church's internal processes — pastoral care, church discipline, denominational oversight — are sufficient to address abuse. They are not. Church discipline is designed for matters of spiritual accountability among consenting adults. It is structurally incapable of addressing criminal behavior, protecting victims from ongoing harm, or ensuring that predators face legal consequences. When a church handles abuse internally, it is not exercising spiritual authority. It is obstructing justice.</p>
-
-<p>Every church, regardless of denomination or polity, should adopt a clear, unambiguous policy: all suspected abuse is reported to civil authorities immediately. Not after an internal investigation. Not after consulting with denominational lawyers. Not after praying about it. Immediately. The safety of a child is not a matter for ecclesiastical deliberation.</p>
-
-<h2>Independent Investigations: Why They Matter</h2>
-
-<p>Churches cannot investigate themselves. This should be obvious, but it is a lesson the church has been extraordinarily slow to learn. When an institution investigates allegations against its own leaders, using its own personnel, following its own procedures, the outcome is predetermined. The investigation exists not to uncover truth but to manage liability.</p>
-
-<p>Independent investigations — conducted by qualified professionals with no institutional loyalty, full access to documents and personnel, and a mandate to publish their findings — are the minimum standard of accountability. The Guidepost investigation of the SBC demonstrated what independent scrutiny can accomplish: it uncovered what decades of internal processes had concealed. The Pennsylvania grand jury reports accomplished what diocesan review boards had not.</p>
-
-<p>The church's resistance to independent investigation is itself diagnostic. An institution confident in its integrity welcomes scrutiny. An institution that fights investigation is telling you, in the clearest possible terms, what it expects the investigation to find.</p>
-
-<h2>Victim-Centered Policy</h2>
-
-<p>The phrase "victim-centered" is not a concession to contemporary therapeutic culture. It is a theological imperative. The God revealed in Scripture is consistently, relentlessly oriented toward the vulnerable. The prophets measured the faithfulness of Israel not by the magnificence of its worship but by its treatment of the widow, the orphan, the stranger. Jesus reserved his most severe condemnation not for sinners but for religious leaders who burdened the vulnerable and failed to protect the weak. "If anyone causes one of these little ones to stumble," he said, "it would be better for them to have a large millstone hung around their neck and to be drowned in the depths of the sea." That is not a metaphor. It is a verdict.</p>
-
-<p>Victim-centered policy means that when an allegation of abuse is made, the institution's first question is not "How do we protect our reputation?" but "How do we protect this person?" It means believing survivors as a starting posture, not as a conclusion reached only after exhaustive institutional self-defense. It means providing material support — counseling, legal assistance, financial help — to those who have been harmed, without requiring them to sign nondisclosure agreements or waive their right to pursue justice. It means removing accused leaders from positions of authority immediately and permanently, regardless of their institutional value.</p>
-
-<p>It means, in short, treating the vulnerable the way Jesus treated the vulnerable. Which is to say: as the point of the entire enterprise.</p>
-
-<h2>What Genuine Repentance Looks Like</h2>
-
-<p>The church has become skilled at performing repentance without practicing it. Press conferences are held. Statements are issued. The language of sorrow is deployed with professional precision. And then the institution returns to its prior operations with minimal structural change. This is not repentance. It is public relations.</p>
-
-<p>Genuine repentance in the context of institutional abuse requires, at minimum, the following: a full and honest accounting of what happened, conducted by independent investigators and made public without redaction. Direct, personal accountability for leaders who enabled or concealed abuse, including removal from positions of authority and, where applicable, cooperation with criminal prosecution. Material restitution to survivors — not as a legal settlement designed to minimize liability but as an act of justice. Permanent structural changes to governance, accountability, and oversight that make future abuse more difficult and future concealment impossible.</p>
-
-<p>Dietrich Bonhoeffer distinguished between "cheap grace" and "costly grace." Cheap grace is grace without repentance, forgiveness without restitution, absolution without accountability. The church's response to the abuse crisis has been, overwhelmingly, an exercise in cheap grace — grace extended to the institution and its leaders, paid for by the suffering of victims who were told to forgive and move on.</p>
-
-<p>Costly grace demands more. It demands that the institution bear the full weight of what it has done. It demands that leaders who failed be held accountable, not shuffled to new positions. It demands that survivors be heard, believed, and compensated — not as an act of institutional generosity but as the minimum requirement of justice. It demands that the church become, in its structures and practices, the kind of institution where abuse is not merely condemned in sermons but made structurally impossible.</p>
-
-<p>We are not there. We are not close. The church has named the sin in the abstract while protecting the sinners in the particular. It has called for repentance while resisting the concrete changes repentance demands. It has wept publicly while fighting privately to preserve the structures that made the crisis possible.</p>
-
-<p>The reckoning is not optional. It is not a phase the church can wait out. It is the work of a generation — or it is the end of the church's moral credibility in the culture it claims to serve. Those are the only two options. There is no third way.</p>`
