-
-<p>The second casualty was witness. The New Testament presents the church as a community whose distinctive way of life serves as a sign — a visible demonstration that another way of being human is possible. The early church attracted converts not primarily through arguments but through the observable quality of its common life: its care for the poor, its inclusion of the excluded, its willingness to suffer rather than dominate. The culture war inverted this logic. It substituted political advocacy for embodied witness. It said to the watching world: we will change your behavior through legislation rather than attract your allegiance through demonstration. The result was a church perceived not as a community of sacrificial love but as a lobbying organization with a cross on its letterhead.</p>
