-<h2>The Idol of Influence</h2>
-
-<p>Beneath the culture war strategy lay a deeper theological error: the belief that the church's faithfulness is measured by its cultural influence. This belief is nowhere supported in the New Testament. Jesus explicitly rejected the model of a kingdom that operates through political power. "My kingdom is not of this world," he told Pilate — not because his kingdom is irrelevant to the world but because it does not operate by the world's mechanisms of coercion and control. The early church existed as a tiny, powerless, persecuted minority within the Roman Empire. It did not lobby Caesar. It did not form political action committees. It did not seek to impose its moral framework through legislation. It formed communities of radical mutual care, practiced an ethic that astonished its neighbors, and trusted that the quality of its common life would bear witness to the truth of its confession.</p>
-
-<p>The culture war represented a loss of nerve — a failure to trust that the church's witness could be sustained without the backing of political power. It was, at its root, an act of faithlessness disguised as an act of faithfulness. And the theological tradition has a word for the elevation of a good thing — cultural influence — to the status of an ultimate thing. The word is idolatry.</p>
