-
-<p>The end of the culture war opens the possibility of recovering this posture. Freed from the obligation to defend political allies and maintain political coalitions, the church can recover the prophetic freedom to speak truth to power regardless of which party holds it. Freed from the expectation that legislation will accomplish the work of formation, the church can rediscover its own formative practices — worship, prayer, community, service, the slow and patient work of discipleship that cannot be outsourced to a political movement. Freed from the illusion that cultural dominance is the measure of faithfulness, the church can rediscover what faithfulness actually looks like: not winning, but witnessing.</p>
