-    body: `<p>On May 10, 1845, in Augusta, Georgia, 293 delegates from Southern Baptist churches gathered to form a new denomination. The immediate cause was a dispute with their Northern counterparts over whether slaveholders could serve as missionaries. The Northern board had refused to appoint a slaveholder. The Southern delegates, unwilling to accept this restriction, withdrew and constituted themselves as the Southern Baptist Convention.</p>
