-<p>The SBC was not founded despite slavery. It was founded because of it. Its founding documents make this explicit. Its early leaders made it explicit. The theological case for slavery was not an embarrassing footnote to an otherwise noble enterprise. It was the enterprise. The denomination that would become the largest Protestant body in America — claiming over thirteen million members at its peak — came into existence for the express purpose of defending the right of Christians to own other human beings.</p>
-
-<p>This is not ancient history. The SBC did not formally apologize for its slaveholding origins until 1995 — one hundred and fifty years after its founding. And the apology, while significant as a symbolic gesture, did not include any concrete measures of restitution, structural reform, or sustained institutional reckoning with the theological traditions that had made the defense of slavery possible.</p>
-
-<h2>The Slaveholder's Theology</h2>
-
-<p>The Christian defense of slavery was not a crude rationalization imposed on Scripture by cynical men who knew better. It was a sophisticated, internally consistent theological system, constructed by some of the most respected biblical scholars of the antebellum South. This is what makes it so dangerous — and so relevant to the present.</p>
-
-<p>The slaveholder's theology rested on several pillars. First, the curse of Ham in Genesis 9, which was interpreted as a divine decree of perpetual servitude for the descendants of Canaan — identified, through a chain of exegetical reasoning that would not survive scrutiny, with the peoples of Africa. Second, the Pauline household codes, particularly Ephesians 6:5 ("Slaves, obey your earthly masters") and Colossians 3:22, which were read as divine endorsements of the institution of slavery rather than pastoral instructions addressed to people trapped within an existing social system. Third, a providentialist argument: God, in his sovereignty, had allowed slavery to exist; therefore slavery was part of the divine plan. To oppose it was to oppose God.</p>
-
-<p>What made this theology so durable was not its quality — by any serious hermeneutical standard, it was terrible exegesis — but its function. It provided religious legitimation for an economic system on which the entire Southern economy depended. It sacralized the social order. It transformed a brutal institution of human exploitation into a divinely ordained arrangement that was good not only for the slaveholder but, in the tortured logic of paternalism, for the enslaved. Ministers preached from their pulpits that slavery was a benevolent institution through which African people were introduced to Christianity — a spiritual benefit that, they argued, far outweighed the temporal suffering of bondage.</p>
-
-<p>The theological habits that produced this system — selective literalism, providentialist rationalization, the sacralization of existing power arrangements, the reading of Scripture through the lens of the interpreter's material interests — did not end with emancipation. They persisted. They adapted. They found new applications.</p>
