-
-<p>On April 16, 1963, Martin Luther King Jr. sat in a jail cell in Birmingham, Alabama, and wrote a letter. It was addressed not to the segregationists who had arrested him, not to the politicians who enforced Jim Crow, not to the Klan members who bombed churches and murdered children. It was addressed to eight white clergymen — Christian ministers and a Jewish rabbi — who had published a statement calling King's nonviolent protests "unwise and untimely."</p>
