-<p>The white church during the civil rights era was not, for the most part, actively engaged in the violent defense of segregation. It did something arguably worse. It was silent. It counseled patience. It urged moderation. It spiritualized the question — framing racial justice as a matter of individual hearts rather than structural change, assuring Black Americans that heaven would make up for the injustices of earth, treating calls for concrete political action as a distraction from the church's spiritual mission.</p>
