-
-<p>There were exceptions. There were white Christians who marched, who risked their safety, who stood with the movement at personal cost. Their existence is important and should be honored. But they were exceptions. The institutional white church — its denominations, its seminaries, its leadership structures — was overwhelmingly on the wrong side of the most significant moral struggle of the twentieth century. And it arrived there not despite its theology but because of it.</p>
-
-<h2>The Southern Strategy and Its Heirs</h2>
-
-<p>In 1968, Richard Nixon's presidential campaign developed what came to be known as the Southern Strategy: a deliberate effort to win the votes of white Southerners who had been alienated from the Democratic Party by its support for civil rights legislation. The strategy relied on coded racial appeals — "law and order," "states' rights," "welfare reform" — that communicated racial resentment without the vulgarity of explicit racism. Lee Atwater, a Republican strategist, later described the mechanism with brutal candor: "You start out in 1954 by saying, 'N-----, n-----, n-----.' By 1968, you can't say 'n-----' — that hurts you, backfires. So you say stuff like 'forced busing,' 'states' rights,' and all that stuff."</p>
-
-<p>The Southern Strategy succeeded in realigning American politics. It also succeeded in realigning American Christianity. White evangelicals, who had been politically diverse through the mid-twentieth century, became overwhelmingly Republican. The alignment was not primarily theological. It was racial. The issues that initially mobilized white evangelical political engagement — opposition to the IRS revoking the tax-exempt status of segregated Christian schools, as documented by Randall Balmer — were racial issues. The "family values" agenda that later became the public face of the religious right was, in significant part, a laundered version of racial politics.</p>
-
-<p>This does not mean that every white evangelical who voted Republican was motivated by racial animus. Individual motivations are complex and varied. But the structural alignment between white evangelicalism and a political movement built on racial resentment is not a matter of interpretation. It is a matter of documented historical fact. And its consequences — for the church's witness, for its credibility among people of color, for its capacity to speak with moral authority on questions of justice — have been devastating.</p>
-
-<h2>The Current Moment</h2>
-
-<p>In 2020, the murder of George Floyd by a Minneapolis police officer prompted a national reckoning with racial injustice. The response within white evangelicalism was revealing. Some churches and leaders spoke clearly about systemic racism and committed to concrete action. Many more retreated into the same patterns that have characterized white Christian responses to racial crisis for two centuries: spiritualization ("the real problem is sin, not systems"), individualization ("I am not personally racist, therefore there is no problem"), and deflection ("this is a political issue, not a church issue").</p>
