-
-<p>The irony was lost on few observers outside the white evangelical bubble. A tradition that had spent a century and a half avoiding, minimizing, and theologizing away its own racial history was now deploying a new set of theological and political tools to avoid, minimize, and theologize away the present consequences of that history. The content changed. The pattern did not.</p>
-
-<p>Jemar Tisby, in <em>The Color of Compromise</em>, documents this pattern with devastating thoroughness: complicity through silence, complicity through theology, complicity through institutional inaction, repeating across every era of American history. The white church has never been at the forefront of racial justice in America. It has consistently been behind the culture, not ahead of it — adopting positions on race only after those positions have become culturally safe, and treating each belated acknowledgment as evidence of moral progress rather than as an indictment of moral delay.</p>
-
-<blockquote>The question is not whether the white church has been complicit in racial injustice. The historical record is unambiguous. The question is whether the white church is willing to do what complicity demands: not an apology, which costs nothing, but repentance, which costs everything.</blockquote>
-
-<h2>What Repentance Actually Requires</h2>
-
-<p>The distinction between apology and repentance is not semantic. It is structural. An apology is a statement about the past. Repentance is a reorientation of the present. An apology says, "We are sorry for what was done." Repentance says, "We are changing what we are doing." The white church has become adept at apology. It has shown almost no capacity for repentance.</p>
-
-<p>Repentance for racial injustice would require, at minimum, the following. First, honest historical reckoning. Not sanitized narratives that treat slavery and segregation as regrettable episodes in an otherwise noble story, but unflinching confrontation with the theological systems that justified racial domination and the institutional structures that enforced it. This reckoning must be embedded in seminary curricula, preaching, and congregational education — not as a one-time event but as a permanent feature of the church's self-understanding.</p>
-
-<p>Second, structural change. Repentance that does not alter structures is performance. White-led denominations and churches must examine their governance, hiring, leadership development, and resource allocation through the lens of racial equity — not as a concession to secular pressure but as a requirement of biblical justice. The prophetic tradition is unambiguous: God judges communities not by their stated intentions but by their measurable practices.</p>
-
-<p>Third, material restitution. The wealth of white America — including the wealth of white churches and white Christian institutions — was built, in significant part, on the exploitation of Black labor. This is not a controversial claim. It is an economic fact. Restitution is not charity. It is justice. It is the return of what was taken. And the church, which claims to worship a God of justice, cannot credibly refuse it.</p>
-
-<p>Fourth, redistribution of power. The most consequential form of repentance is not what white Christians say about race but who they are willing to follow. Are white congregations willing to be led by pastors of color — not as tokens but as leaders with genuine authority? Are white denominations willing to submit their institutional priorities to the perspectives of their members of color? Are white Christians willing to listen without defensiveness, to learn without centering their own discomfort, to follow without insisting on control?</p>
-
-<p>Fifth, sustained commitment. Racial reconciliation is not a project with a timeline and a completion date. It is a permanent posture of repentance, learning, and relationship-building that will extend across generations. The damage was done over centuries. It will not be undone in a single denominational resolution or a single season of sermons.</p>
-
-<p>The white church will resist every item on this list. It will call the historical reckoning divisive. It will call the structural changes political. It will call the restitution unreasonable. It will call the redistribution of power impractical. It will call the sustained commitment exhausting. And in each case, the resistance will be framed in theological language — the language of unity, of gospel priority, of spiritual over political, of patience over urgency.</p>
-
-<p>That language is familiar. It is the same language the white church has always used to avoid the cost of justice. King heard it in Birmingham. The enslaved heard it on the plantation. And people of color in white evangelical churches hear it today, every time they raise the subject and watch the room go cold.</p>
-
-<p>The work is not optional. It is not a concession to culture. It is a requirement of the gospel the white church claims to preach. A church that cannot do justice cannot preach grace. A church that will not name its own sin cannot call others to repentance. A church that protects its comfort at the expense of its neighbor's dignity has not yet understood the cross it claims to carry.</p>`
