-      "The megachurch movement reached millions and revealed something important about American Christianity's hunger for accessibility. It also revealed something about what happens when the church mistakes growth for faithfulness.",
-    pillar: "Prophetic Justice",
-    readTime: "12 min",
-    body: `<p>In 1975, Bill Hybels rented a movie theater in Palatine, Illinois, and launched a church service designed for people who did not go to church. He had conducted door-to-door surveys in the surrounding suburbs, asking unchurched residents what they disliked about church. Their answers were consistent: it was boring, irrelevant, always asking for money, and made them feel guilty. Hybels built Willow Creek Community Church as the answer to those objections. The music was contemporary. The messages were practical. The atmosphere was welcoming, nonthreatening, and intentionally modeled on the spaces where suburban Americans already felt comfortable — corporate auditoriums, shopping malls, entertainment venues.</p>
-
-<p>It worked. Willow Creek grew to over twenty-five thousand weekly attendees. It became the prototype for a movement. Rick Warren applied similar principles at Saddleback Church in Lake Forest, California, which grew to comparable size and produced <em>The Purpose Driven Life</em>, one of the best-selling nonfiction books in American history. By the early 2000s, the megachurch model — defined as a church with weekly attendance of two thousand or more — had spread across the country. The Hartford Institute for Religion Research counted over seventeen hundred megachurches in the United States by 2020, a number that had more than doubled since 2000.</p>
-
-<p>The megachurch movement was the most significant development in American Protestant Christianity in the second half of the twentieth century. It reshaped the terrain of American religion, the expectations of churchgoers, the economics of ministry, and the culture of pastoral leadership. It did some things remarkably well. It did other things catastrophically badly. And the reckoning that followed has forced a conversation about what the church is actually for — a conversation that is still far from resolved.</p>
-
-<h2>What Worked</h2>
-
-<p>The megachurch movement's most significant achievement was accessibility. For millions of Americans who had been alienated by traditional church culture — its unfamiliar rituals, its insider language, its assumption that newcomers already knew the rules — the megachurch offered a genuine on-ramp. You could walk in without knowing anything about Christianity and not feel stupid, excluded, or judged. The music was in a key you recognized. The sermon addressed problems you actually had. The building looked like a place where normal people gathered, not a medieval artifact that required a guide.</p>
-
-<p>This accessibility was not superficial. It represented a serious theological commitment: the belief that the gospel is for everyone, and that the church bears responsibility for removing unnecessary barriers to encounter. The seeker-sensitive movement, at its best, was an exercise in contextual theology — translating the message of Christianity into the vernacular of contemporary American culture without compromising its substance. Paul did this in Athens. The Jesuits did it in China. Hybels and Warren did it in the suburbs.</p>
-
-<p>The megachurch also excelled at organizational excellence. Whatever criticisms can be leveled at the movement, it is difficult to argue that the average megachurch was not better organized, better managed, and more intentional about its operations than the average small church. Volunteer coordination, children's ministry, facility management, communication — the megachurch brought a level of institutional competence to these functions that smaller churches often lacked. For families with young children, in particular, the difference was palpable. A well-run children's ministry, a clean and safe facility, a service that started on time — these things matter, and the megachurch delivered them consistently.</p>
-
-<p>The movement also demonstrated the power of strategic thinking about mission. The megachurch leaders were, for all their flaws, deeply serious about reaching people outside the church. They thought carefully about demographics, communication, cultural barriers, and the psychology of spiritual seeking. They approached evangelism not as a duty performed through guilt but as a strategic enterprise that deserved the same level of planning and investment as any other serious undertaking.</p>
-
-<h2>What Didn't Work</h2>
-
-<p>The megachurch model contained structural flaws that became visible only over time — flaws that were not incidental to the model but embedded in its foundational assumptions.</p>
-
-<p>The first flaw was the celebrity pastor. The megachurch, by its nature, concentrates enormous attention, authority, and resources in a single leader. The senior pastor becomes the brand. The church's identity, its growth, its financial viability, and its public reputation are all tethered to one person. This structure creates conditions that are almost perfectly designed to produce the pathologies of celebrity: isolation, entitlement, unaccountability, the gradual erosion of the boundary between the leader's identity and the institution's mission.</p>
-
-<p>The Willow Creek Association, which Hybels founded to export the megachurch model to churches worldwide, marketed not just a methodology but a personality. When Hybels was accused of sexual harassment and misconduct — allegations that were eventually substantiated by an independent investigation — the entire enterprise was compromised. The model had no mechanism for holding its central figure accountable because the central figure was the model.</p>
-
-<p>The second flaw was consumer Christianity. The seeker-sensitive approach, which began as a missiological strategy, gradually became a market strategy. The church adopted the logic of the marketplace: the congregation as customer base, the sermon as product, the worship service as experience to be consumed. Satisfaction surveys replaced confession. Preference became the organizing principle of ministry. Churches competed for market share — not consciously, perhaps, but functionally — by offering better programming, more convenient services, superior production values.</p>
-
-<p>The result was a form of Christianity shaped more by consumer preference than by the costly demands of discipleship. The megachurch excelled at attracting people. It struggled to form them. Willow Creek's own internal research, published in the 2007 study <em>Reveal</em>, acknowledged this problem with surprising candor: the church had been more effective at creating consumers of religious programming than disciples of Jesus Christ. The most spiritually mature members, the study found, were the least satisfied — because the church had been designed for seekers, not for saints.</p>
-
-<p>The third flaw was shallow discipleship. The megachurch service, optimized for accessibility, often achieved accessibility at the expense of depth. Sermons were practical, relevant, and application-oriented — but they frequently lacked theological substance. Complex doctrines were simplified to the point of distortion. The Bible was mined for principles rather than encountered as a text. The liturgical traditions of the church — the creeds, the sacraments, the rhythms of the church year — were jettisoned as barriers to accessibility, and with them went centuries of accumulated wisdom about how to form the human soul.</p>
-
-<blockquote>The megachurch asked the right question: How do we reach people who are not here? But it failed to ask the harder question: What do we do with them once they arrive?</blockquote>
-
-<h2>The Reckoning</h2>
-
-<p>The scandals that engulfed megachurch leaders in the 2010s and 2020s were not isolated incidents. They were structural consequences of the model itself. Bill Hybels at Willow Creek. Mark Driscoll at Mars Hill. Carl Lentz at Hillsong New York. James MacDonald at Harvest Bible Chapel. Each case followed the same arc: a charismatic leader builds a massive institution around his personality, concentrates authority in himself, marginalizes or eliminates internal accountability, and eventually falls — taking the institution, or large parts of it, down with him.</p>
-
-<p>Driscoll's case is particularly instructive. Mars Hill Church in Seattle grew from a handful of people to over fourteen thousand weekly attendees in fifteen years. Driscoll's aggressive, confrontational style — described by Christianity Today as "the cussing pastor" — attracted young men in numbers that few other churches could match. But the same authoritarianism that drove the church's growth eventually destroyed it. Former elders described a culture of fear, bullying, and spiritual abuse. When the church's board attempted to hold Driscoll accountable, he resigned and the church collapsed, closing all of its campuses within months.</p>
-
-<p>Lentz at Hillsong was fired for "moral failures" — a euphemism that later expanded into a broader picture of a church culture built on celebrity, image management, and the systematic prioritization of the brand over the well-being of staff and congregants. MacDonald at Harvest was removed after recordings surfaced of him making vulgar, racially insensitive, and threatening remarks — but only after years of financial misconduct, bullying of staff, and suppression of dissent that the church's governance structures had failed to address.</p>
-
-<p>The pattern is not coincidental. It is architectural. Build a church around a personality. Give that personality unchecked authority. Remove the structural safeguards — elder boards with real power, financial transparency, congregational governance — that might constrain that authority. And wait. The fall is not guaranteed, but the conditions for it are. The megachurch model, as commonly practiced, is a machine for producing exactly the kind of leadership failures it has produced.</p>
-
-<h2>What Comes Next</h2>
-
-<p>The post-megachurch reality is still emerging, but its contours are becoming visible. The movement is not dying — megachurches continue to exist and, in some cases, to thrive. But the assumptions that drove the movement are being questioned in ways they have not been before, and the alternatives being explored suggest a significant shift in what American Protestantism considers a healthy church.</p>
-
-<p>Smaller is being reconsidered. The assumption that bigger is better — that numerical growth is the primary indicator of ministerial health — is being challenged by a growing number of pastors, theologians, and church planters who argue that the most significant work of the church happens at a scale that megachurches cannot reach. Relationships of genuine depth. Mutual accountability. The slow, patient work of formation that requires a pastor who knows your name, your story, your struggles — not a communicator on a stage who delivers content to an audience.</p>
-
-<p>Deeper is being prioritized. The reaction against seeker-sensitive shallowness has produced a renewed interest in theological depth, liturgical richness, and the formative practices of the historic church. Young evangelicals are discovering the church fathers, the Book of Common Prayer, the contemplative tradition, the rhythms of the church year. They are hungry not for relevance but for rootedness — not for a Christianity that accommodates the culture but for one that offers a genuine alternative to it.</p>
-
-<p>More accountable structures are being designed. The celebrity pastor model is being replaced, in some quarters, by team leadership, plural eldership, and governance structures that distribute authority rather than concentrating it. Churches are implementing financial transparency, independent audits, and formal accountability mechanisms that do not depend on the goodwill of the leader being held accountable.</p>
-
-<p>None of this guarantees a better outcome. Small churches can be abusive. Liturgical churches can be shallow. Accountability structures can be performative. The problem is not merely structural but spiritual — rooted in the human capacity for self-deception and the institutional tendency toward self-preservation. But the megachurch era has taught the American church something it needed to learn: that growth is not the same as health, that efficiency is not the same as faithfulness, and that the size of the crowd tells you nothing about the depth of the formation happening within it.</p>
-
-<p>The church Jesus described — a community of mutual service, sacrificial love, genuine accountability, and radical commitment to the way of the cross — has never been efficiently scalable. That may be the point.</p>`
