-      "The church that tells a depressed person to pray harder is not offering spiritual counsel. It is practicing spiritual malpractice. And the Bible it claims to follow knows better.",
-    pillar: "Prophetic Justice",
-    readTime: "12 min",
-    body: `<p>In 2013, Matthew Warren — the twenty-seven-year-old son of Rick Warren, pastor of Saddleback Church and author of <em>The Purpose Driven Life</em> — died by suicide after a lifelong struggle with mental illness. Rick Warren, one of the most influential pastors in America, a man whose book had sold over fifty million copies and whose church had helped shape the theology and practice of a generation of evangelical Christianity, could not save his own son. Not because he lacked faith. Not because he lacked resources. Not because he failed to pray. Because mental illness is an illness, and prayer, however essential to the life of faith, is not a substitute for clinical treatment.</p>
-
-<p>Warren's tragedy forced an unwelcome confrontation with a reality that the American church has spent decades avoiding: the church's dominant framework for understanding mental health is not merely inadequate. It is harmful. It has caused measurable damage to real people. It has driven the suffering away from the one community that claims, as its founding mission, to welcome the broken and bind their wounds.</p>
-
-<p>The church has a mental health problem. Not merely in the sense that its members struggle with mental illness — though they do, at rates comparable to the general population. The church has a mental health problem in the sense that its theology, its culture, and its institutional practices have made it one of the worst places in American life for a person struggling with mental illness to seek help.</p>
-
-<h2>The Damage of Spiritual Bypassing</h2>
-
-<p>Spiritual bypassing is the use of spiritual beliefs and practices to avoid confronting painful emotions, unresolved psychological wounds, or legitimate mental health conditions. In the church, it takes predictable forms. A person struggling with clinical depression is told to "pray harder," "trust God more," or "find their joy in the Lord." A person experiencing anxiety is told that anxiety is a sin — a failure of faith — and that the solution is repentance rather than treatment. A person with PTSD is told to "give it to God" and "let go of the past." A person considering medication is warned that pills are a crutch, that true Christians rely on God alone, that seeking help from a therapist is a sign of spiritual weakness.</p>
-
-<p>These responses are not well-intentioned but misguided. They are destructive. They compound the suffering of the person seeking help by adding a layer of spiritual shame to their psychological pain. The depressed person is now depressed <em>and</em> convinced that their depression is evidence of spiritual failure. The anxious person is now anxious <em>and</em> guilty about being anxious. The person in crisis is now in crisis <em>and</em> afraid to seek professional help because their faith community has told them that doing so would be a betrayal of God.</p>
-
-<p>The theology behind spiritual bypassing is simple and wrong. It assumes that all human suffering is spiritual in origin and therefore amenable to spiritual solutions. If you are depressed, the reasoning goes, the root cause must be a spiritual deficiency — insufficient faith, unconfessed sin, distance from God. The solution, therefore, is spiritual: more prayer, more Scripture, more church involvement, more faith. If the depression persists, the implication is clear: you have not prayed enough, believed enough, or surrendered enough. The failure is yours.</p>
-
-<p>This framework is not merely bad psychology. It is bad theology. It attributes to sin what is often attributable to neurochemistry, trauma, genetics, or the ordinary brokenness of the human condition. It treats the brain as the one organ exempt from the fall — the one part of human experience where physical malfunction is impossible and spiritual failure is the only explanation. A church that would never tell a diabetic to pray instead of taking insulin routinely tells the depressed to pray instead of seeking treatment. The inconsistency is glaring. The cost is measured in suffering, isolation, and, in the most devastating cases, lives lost.</p>
-
-<h2>The False Dichotomy</h2>
-
-<p>The church's resistance to mental health treatment is rooted in a false dichotomy: the belief that faith and therapy, prayer and medication, spiritual care and clinical care are competing rather than complementary. This dichotomy has no basis in Scripture, no basis in the Christian theological tradition, and no basis in the observable reality of how human beings are constituted.</p>
-
-<p>Human beings are integrated wholes — body, mind, and spirit inextricably woven together. This is not a concession to secular anthropology. It is a biblical claim. The Hebrew word <em>nephesh</em>, often translated "soul," denotes the whole person — the embodied, thinking, feeling, relational creature that God formed from dust and breath. There is no biblical warrant for treating the spiritual dimension of human experience as separable from the physical and psychological dimensions. A person struggling with depression is not experiencing a purely spiritual crisis. They are experiencing a crisis of the whole person — a crisis that may involve neurochemistry, life circumstances, relational wounds, existential questions, and spiritual dimensions all at once.</p>
-
-<p>Effective care for the whole person requires multiple forms of intervention. A depressed person may need medication to address the neurochemical dimensions of their illness. They may need therapy to process the relational and experiential dimensions. They may need spiritual direction, prayer, and community to address the existential and spiritual dimensions. These are not competing treatments. They are complementary dimensions of care for an integrated human being. The church that insists on spiritual solutions alone is not treating the whole person. It is treating a fraction of the person while ignoring the rest — and calling this truncated care "faith."</p>
-
-<h2>What the Bible Actually Says</h2>
-
-<p>The church's approach to mental health is ironic in the extreme, because the Bible it claims to follow contains some of the most honest, unvarnished depictions of mental anguish in all of literature.</p>
-
-<p>David, the man after God's own heart, wrote psalms of such raw psychological distress that any contemporary therapist would recognize the symptoms of clinical depression. "My bones wasted away through my groaning all day long," he writes in Psalm 32. "I am weary with my moaning; every night I flood my bed with tears," he writes in Psalm 6. "Why are you cast down, O my soul? Why are you in turmoil within me?" he asks in Psalm 42 — a question that names, with remarkable psychological precision, the experience of depression as a state that the sufferer can observe but not control.</p>
-
-<p>Elijah, after his greatest prophetic victory on Mount Carmel, collapsed into a depression so severe that he asked God to take his life. "It is enough; now, O Lord, take away my life," he said in 1 Kings 19. God's response is instructive. God did not rebuke Elijah for lack of faith. God did not deliver a sermon on the sin of despair. God sent an angel with food and water and told Elijah to sleep. God addressed the physical needs of the prophet's body before addressing the spiritual needs of his soul. The first act of divine care for a depressed man was not a Bible study. It was a meal and a nap.</p>
-
-<p>Jeremiah, the weeping prophet, expressed such sustained anguish that his writings have given the English language the word "jeremiad." "Cursed be the day on which I was born," he wrote in Jeremiah 20:14 — a statement that, in a contemporary church context, would likely prompt a referral to the pastor rather than inclusion in the canon of Scripture. Job sat in ashes and cursed the day of his birth. Jonah asked God to kill him. The psalmist in Psalm 88 — the darkest psalm in the Psalter — ends without resolution, without comfort, without the expected turn to hope. "Darkness is my closest friend," it concludes. The Bible includes that psalm. The church should learn from its inclusion.</p>
-
-<p>The biblical witness does not treat mental anguish as a moral failing. It treats it as a human reality — one that can coexist with deep faith, prophetic calling, and intimate relationship with God. The church's insistence on treating mental illness as a spiritual deficiency is not a biblical position. It is a betrayal of the Bible's own testimony.</p>
-
-<blockquote>The God who sent an angel with bread for a suicidal prophet did not ask Elijah to pray harder. The church should take the hint.</blockquote>
-
-<h2>Trauma-Informed Ministry</h2>
-
-<p>Trauma-informed ministry begins with a simple recognition: a significant number of the people sitting in church pews on any given Sunday are carrying the effects of traumatic experiences — abuse, neglect, violence, loss, abandonment, betrayal — that shape how they hear the gospel, how they experience community, and how they relate to God. A church that is not aware of this reality will inadvertently retraumatize the people it is trying to help.</p>
-
-<p>Consider the standard evangelical altar call. A preacher asks people to come forward, publicly, in front of the entire congregation, to confess their brokenness and surrender to God. For many people, this is a meaningful and genuine spiritual experience. For a survivor of sexual abuse — for whom public vulnerability was precisely the mechanism of their violation — this invitation may trigger a trauma response. The church, without knowing it, has reenacted the dynamics of the survivor's abuse in a spiritual register.</p>
-
-<p>Trauma-informed ministry does not abandon the gospel. It delivers the gospel with awareness of how it will be received by wounded people. It trains pastors and lay leaders to recognize the signs of trauma. It creates multiple pathways for response that do not require public vulnerability. It ensures that counseling ministries are staffed by people with actual training, not merely good intentions. It maintains clear boundaries around confidentiality, physical contact, and the use of authority. It recognizes that spiritual care and clinical care are partners, not competitors, and it builds relationships with licensed mental health professionals to whom it can refer people whose needs exceed its competence.</p>
-
-<p>This is not a capitulation to secular psychology. It is an application of the biblical mandate to love one's neighbor — a mandate that requires caring enough about the neighbor's actual experience to learn how to help without doing harm.</p>
-
-<h2>Becoming Places of Healing</h2>
-
-<p>The church has the potential to be one of the most powerful resources for mental health in American life. It has what no clinical practice can offer: a sustained community of care that extends beyond the fifty-minute therapeutic hour, a framework of meaning that addresses the existential dimensions of suffering, rituals and practices that orient the sufferer toward hope without demanding premature resolution, and a theological tradition that insists — against every culture of toxic positivity — that suffering is real, that lament is faithful, and that God is present in the darkness.</p>
-
-<p>To realize this potential, the church must first do no harm. It must stop treating mental illness as a spiritual failing. It must stop offering spiritual solutions to clinical problems. It must stop stigmatizing therapy, medication, and professional mental health care. It must stop pretending that prayer and Bible study are sufficient responses to clinical depression, anxiety disorders, bipolar disorder, PTSD, and the full spectrum of mental health conditions that affect its members.</p>
-
-<p>Then it must take positive steps. Train pastoral staff in mental health first aid. Build relationships with licensed therapists and counselors in the community. Create support groups for people living with mental illness and their families. Preach honestly about mental health — not as a special topic for a designated Sunday but as an integrated dimension of the church's ongoing pastoral care. Normalize the language of mental health in the same way the church has normalized the language of physical health. When someone says, "I am seeing a therapist," the response should be the same as when someone says, "I am seeing a doctor": support, not suspicion.</p>
-
-<p>The church that tells a depressed person to pray harder is not exercising spiritual authority. It is exercising spiritual malpractice. And the most faithful thing it can do — the thing most consistent with the example of a God who sent bread to a suicidal prophet and let the darkest psalm stand in the canon without resolution — is to sit with the suffering, take it seriously, and offer every resource available to address it. Spiritual and clinical. Prayer and treatment. Community and competence. The whole response for the whole person.</p>
-
-<p>That is not a compromise of faith. It is faith doing what faith is for.</p>`
