-      "The church that confuses aggression with authority and domination with leadership has not recovered biblical manhood. It has baptized the very thing the gospel came to crucify.",
-    pillar: "Prophetic Justice",
-    readTime: "12 min",
-    body: `<p>In 2006, Mark Driscoll stood before a conference of pastors and declared that the problem with the American church was that it had become too feminine. The church needed men, he argued — real men, tough men, men who would fight and lead and dominate. He mocked pastors who were gentle, who wept, who preferred diplomacy to confrontation. He described Jesus as a cage fighter, an alpha male, a warrior who would return at the end of days to deliver divine violence. The message was clear: godly manhood was aggressive, confrontational, and unapologetically dominant. Anything else was weakness. Anything else was feminization. Anything else was failure.</p>
-
-<p>Driscoll was not an outlier. He was the loudest voice in a movement that had been building for decades — a movement that defined masculinity in terms of power, authority, and control, and that located these qualities at the center of God's design for men. John Eldredge's <em>Wild at Heart</em>, published in 2001 and eventually selling over four million copies, told men that they were designed to be warriors, adventurers, and rescuers — and that the modern church, with its emphasis on gentleness, servanthood, and emotional vulnerability, had stolen their masculine identity. The Council on Biblical Manhood and Womanhood, founded in 1987, codified a vision of gender in which male leadership and female submission were presented as the eternal, unchangeable order of creation.</p>
-
-<p>The result was a theology of masculinity that bore almost no resemblance to the man at the center of the Christian story. It looked, instead, remarkably like the cultural ideal of the American alpha male — competitive, dominant, emotionally controlled, sexually aggressive — dressed in theological clothing and marketed as God's original design.</p>
-
-<h2>The Driscoll Playbook</h2>
-
-<p>Mark Driscoll built Mars Hill Church in Seattle from a handful of people meeting in a living room to over fourteen thousand weekly attendees in fifteen years. His appeal was concentrated among young men — a demographic that most churches struggled to reach. He succeeded by offering them a Christianity that affirmed the version of masculinity they already held: aggressive, competitive, uncompromising, and contemptuous of softness in any form.</p>
-
-<p>Driscoll's preaching was laced with the language of domination. He publicly humiliated men he considered insufficiently masculine. He described stay-at-home fathers as failures. He joked about men who displayed emotion. He wielded theological authority as a weapon, framing disagreement with his leadership as rebellion against God. Former elders and staff members have described a culture of intimidation, bullying, and spiritual coercion in which Driscoll's word was effectively law and dissent was treated as disloyalty.</p>
-
-<p>The Mars Hill experiment ended in 2014, when the accumulated weight of Driscoll's behavior — plagiarism, financial impropriety, bullying, abuse of authority — finally exceeded what the institution could sustain. The church dissolved. Its campuses closed. Thousands of members were scattered, many of them deeply wounded by an experience they had been told was biblical Christianity.</p>
-
-<p>But Driscoll did not disappear. He moved to Arizona and planted a new church, which grew rapidly. The market for his version of masculinity had not diminished. It had, if anything, expanded — fueled by a broader cultural anxiety about masculinity that Driscoll was positioned to exploit. The pattern repeated itself, with new allegations of authoritarian leadership, staff abuse, and institutional control emerging from the new church. The product had not changed. It did not need to. The demand was still there.</p>
-
-<h2>The Warrior-Poet Myth</h2>
-
-<p>Eldredge's <em>Wild at Heart</em> offered a softer version of the same essential message. Men, Eldredge argued, are hardwired for three things: a battle to fight, an adventure to live, and a beauty to rescue. These desires are not cultural constructions but divine implants — the fingerprints of God on the male soul. The modern church, Eldredge argued, had domesticated men, turning them into "really nice guys" who had lost touch with their primal masculine energy.</p>
-
-<p>The appeal is understandable. Many men in the church do feel that their experience is not reflected in the prevailing culture of evangelical Christianity — a culture that often emphasizes emotional expression, relational intimacy, and gentle compliance in ways that feel foreign to their lived experience of masculinity. Eldredge gave these men permission to be something other than domesticated — to embrace risk, physicality, and strength as features, not bugs, of their masculine design.</p>
-
-<p>The problem is not the affirmation of masculine energy per se. The problem is the definition of masculine energy that Eldredge assumes. The warrior-poet framework draws its vision of manhood not from the life of Christ but from romantic mythology, Hollywood action narratives, and Jungian archetypes. The "battle" is framed in terms of conquest and competition. The "adventure" is framed in terms of individual heroism. The "beauty" is framed in terms of rescue — positioning women as objects to be saved rather than agents in their own right. The entire framework is self-referential: it centers male experience, male desire, and male self-actualization. The other — whether woman, neighbor, or God — exists primarily as a mirror for the man's heroic arc.</p>
-
-<p>Jesus does not appear in this framework except as an afterthought — a divine endorsement of the masculine ideal the author has already constructed. And the Jesus who does appear is selectively edited: the Jesus who overturned tables makes the cut, but the Jesus who washed feet does not. The Jesus who confronted the Pharisees makes the cut, but the Jesus who wept at Lazarus's tomb is treated as an anomaly rather than a revelation.</p>
-
-<h2>What Biblical Manhood Actually Looks Like</h2>
-
-<p>The New Testament presents a vision of manhood that is, by the standards of both first-century Mediterranean culture and twenty-first-century American culture, profoundly countercultural. It does not affirm the alpha-male ideal. It crucifies it.</p>
-
-<p>Jesus wept. Publicly. At the tomb of a friend. In a culture where male tears were a sign of weakness, Jesus wept without apology, without explanation, without the macho caveat that he was only crying because he was about to do something powerful. He wept because his friend was dead and grief was the appropriate response to death. The Son of God modeled emotional vulnerability as an expression of strength, not a lapse from it.</p>
-
-<p>Jesus washed feet. The act of a slave — the lowest act of service in the ancient household — performed by the one who held all authority. He knelt before his disciples, took off his outer garment, and did the work that no self-respecting man would do. And when Peter objected — when Peter displayed the masculine instinct to refuse the humiliation of being served — Jesus told him that unless he accepted it, he had no part in the kingdom. The kingdom's definition of greatness was not power exercised over others but service rendered to others. "The greatest among you shall be your servant," Jesus said. He was not speaking metaphorically.</p>
-
-<p>Jesus refused violence. In the Garden of Gethsemane, when Peter drew a sword to defend him, Jesus told him to put it away. "Those who live by the sword will die by the sword." He submitted to arrest, trial, torture, and execution without resistance — not because he lacked power but because the kingdom he embodied operated on fundamentally different terms than the kingdoms of the world. The cross is not a warrior's victory. It is a servant's sacrifice. And it is the central symbol of the faith that the toxic masculinity movement claims to represent.</p>
-
-<p>Paul's vision of manhood is consistent with Jesus' example. The fruit of the Spirit — love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness, self-control — reads like a deliberate inversion of the warrior-male ideal. Gentleness is listed as a spiritual virtue, not a feminine weakness. Self-control is valued over aggression. Patience is prized over decisive action. The man shaped by the Spirit looks nothing like the man shaped by the Driscoll playbook. He looks, instead, like someone whose strength is expressed in service, whose authority is exercised in humility, and whose courage is demonstrated not in domination but in vulnerability.</p>
-
-<blockquote>The church does not need more alpha males. It needs more men willing to do what Jesus did: kneel, serve, weep, and absorb the world's violence rather than return it.</blockquote>
-
-<h2>The Damage Done</h2>
-
-<p>The toxic masculinity movement in the church has caused damage in every direction. It has damaged women, who have been told that their role is to submit, support, and admire — and that resistance to male authority is resistance to God. Women in churches shaped by this theology report feeling invisible, silenced, and instrumentalized — valued for their compliance rather than their gifts, their beauty rather than their wisdom, their willingness to serve male vision rather than their capacity to lead in their own right.</p>
-
-<p>It has damaged men, who have been given a vision of manhood that is impossible to sustain without constant performance and self-suppression. Men who do not fit the alpha-male template — men who are gentle, artistic, emotionally expressive, contemplative — are made to feel that their masculinity is deficient, that they are not the men God designed them to be. The suicide rate among men, the epidemic of male loneliness, the crisis of male emotional illiteracy — these are not unrelated to a cultural and religious framework that tells men their value lies in dominance and their vulnerability is weakness.</p>
-
-<p>It has damaged families, where the theology of male headship has been used to justify patterns of control, emotional abuse, and domestic violence. Not every complementarian household is abusive. But a theology that locates final authority in the husband and frames wifely submission as a divine mandate provides structural cover for abuse when it occurs. The wife who questions her husband's authority can be told she is disobeying God. The husband who controls his wife can frame his control as spiritual leadership. The children who grow up in these households learn that power is gendered, that authority flows in one direction, and that the strongest person in the room is, by definition, the one God has placed in charge.</p>
-
-<p>It has damaged the church's witness. A church that preaches a gospel of sacrificial love while modeling a culture of masculine domination is not merely inconsistent. It is unintelligible. The watching world sees the contradiction even when the church does not. And the watching world draws the obvious conclusion: the church's gospel is not powerful enough to transform its own culture.</p>
-
-<h2>Healthy Masculinity in Pastoral Leadership</h2>
-
-<p>Healthy masculinity in the pulpit does not require the abandonment of strength, courage, or conviction. It requires the redefinition of these qualities in light of the gospel rather than in light of cultural mythology.</p>
-
-<p>Strength, in the gospel, is the capacity to bear the weight of another's suffering without flinching — not the capacity to impose one's will. Courage is the willingness to be vulnerable, to admit error, to name one's own sin before naming others' — not the willingness to dominate a room. Conviction is fidelity to the truth even when the truth is costly — not the rhetorical force to shout down disagreement.</p>
-
-<p>A pastor who leads with healthy masculinity creates space for others rather than filling it himself. He listens more than he speaks. He asks questions rather than issuing pronouncements. He shares power rather than hoarding it. He admits what he does not know. He models emotional honesty — not the performed vulnerability of the confessional sermon illustration but the genuine, unscripted honesty of a man who has learned that his authority does not depend on his perfection.</p>
-
-<p>He holds his authority lightly, knowing that it belongs to God, not to him. He exercises discipline when necessary — but with grief, not satisfaction. He confronts sin — but his own first. He leads — but toward the cross, not toward the throne.</p>
-
-<p>This is not weakness. It is the hardest kind of strength. The strength to serve rather than dominate. The strength to absorb rather than inflict. The strength to follow a Savior who demonstrated, in the most definitive way possible, that the way up is down, the way to power is through service, and the way to life is through death.</p>
-
-<p>The church that grasps this will not need to manufacture masculine energy or market warrior theology. It will have something far more compelling: men who look like Jesus. And that — not aggression, not domination, not the swagger of the celebrity pastor — is what the world has never seen enough of and has always, whether it knows it or not, been waiting for.</p>`
