-  {
-    title: "What Christians Can Learn From Judaism",
-    slug: "what-christians-can-learn-from-judaism",
-    excerpt:
-      "Christianity emerged from Judaism and then spent two millennia pretending it had outgrown its parent. The cost of that pretension has been theological impoverishment, moral blindness, and a faith unmoored from the tradition Jesus himself practiced.",
-    pillar: "Theological Depth",
-    readTime: "12 min",
-    body: `<p>Jesus was Jewish. This statement is so obvious that it should not need saying, and so consistently ignored in its implications that it cannot be said often enough. Jesus was circumcised on the eighth day. He was presented at the temple according to the law of Moses. He attended synagogue. He observed the feasts. He wore <em>tzitzit</em>. He debated Torah with rabbis. He quoted the Psalms from the cross. The prayer he taught his disciples — the Lord's Prayer — is structured on Jewish liturgical patterns. The meal he shared on his last night — the Last Supper — was a Passover seder. The faith that Christians call their own was born inside Judaism, shaped by Jewish categories, expressed in Jewish idiom, and proclaimed by Jewish apostles to a world that had no framework for understanding it apart from the Jewish story of God, creation, covenant, and redemption.</p>
-
-<p>And yet, within a few centuries, Christianity had not merely distinguished itself from Judaism but defined itself against it. The synagogue became the enemy. The Torah became the old dispensation, superseded and surpassed. The people of Israel became the people who had rejected their own Messiah — a rejection that, in the hands of Christian theologians from John Chrysostom to Martin Luther, justified centuries of persecution, forced conversion, ghettoization, and, in its most catastrophic expression, the theological soil in which the Holocaust took root.</p>
-
-<p>The recovery of Christianity's Jewish roots is not a matter of interfaith politeness. It is a matter of theological survival. A Christianity severed from Judaism is a Christianity that has lost access to the intellectual, spiritual, and moral resources of its own foundation. And the things that Judaism preserves — things that Christianity has largely forgotten — are precisely the things that the contemporary church most desperately needs.</p>
-
-<h2>Torah Study as a Way of Life</h2>
-
-<p>In Judaism, the study of Torah is not preparation for something else. It is the thing itself. The <em>Talmud Torah</em> — the study of Torah — is considered one of the highest forms of worship, an act of devotion that is its own reward. The Talmud teaches that the study of Torah is equal to all the other commandments combined. This is not because Judaism is a religion of the intellect rather than the heart. It is because Judaism understands that the intellect and the heart are not separable — that the rigorous, disciplined engagement with a sacred text is itself a form of encounter with the God who gave it.</p>
-
-<p>The contrast with contemporary evangelical Christianity is stark. In much of American Christianity, the Bible is a resource to be mined for principles, a sourcebook for sermon illustrations, a collection of promises to be claimed. It is rarely studied with the kind of sustained, rigorous, communal attention that characterizes Jewish Torah study. The average evangelical sermon extracts a verse, derives an application, and moves on. The text is instrumental — a means to an end, not an end in itself. The depth, complexity, and internal tensions of the text are smoothed over in the service of a tidy takeaway.</p>
-
-<p>Jewish Torah study operates on entirely different assumptions. The text is inexhaustible. Every word, every letter, every apparent contradiction is significant. The purpose of study is not to resolve the text but to inhabit it — to live inside its questions, to sit with its tensions, to discover that the same passage yields different meanings at different stages of life. The rabbinical tradition encourages multiple interpretations of the same text, holding them in creative tension rather than resolving them into a single correct reading. The Talmud records minority opinions alongside majority ones, preserving dissent as part of the tradition rather than erasing it.</p>
-
-<p>Christians who adopted this posture toward Scripture would find their reading immeasurably enriched. They would discover that the Bible is not a flat, self-evident manual of propositions but a living text that resists easy consumption and rewards sustained, patient, communal engagement. They would learn to sit with difficulty rather than resolving it prematurely. They would recover the ancient Christian practice of <em>lectio divina</em> — slow, meditative reading that allows the text to read the reader — which is, in its essential posture, closer to Jewish Torah study than to the evangelical quiet time.</p>
-
-<h2>Shabbat and the Discipline of Rest</h2>
-
-<p>The Jewish Sabbath — Shabbat — is not a day off. It is a day of resistance. In a world organized around productivity, efficiency, and the relentless accumulation of more, Shabbat declares that the world does not depend on human effort. It will continue without our work. God does not require our productivity. The command to rest is not a concession to human weakness but an assertion of divine sovereignty: the world belongs to God, not to us, and our refusal to stop working is not diligence but idolatry — the worship of our own indispensability.</p>
-
-<p>Abraham Joshua Heschel, in <em>The Sabbath</em>, described Shabbat as a "palace in time" — a sacred architecture built not in space but in the rhythms of the week. Six days belong to the world of things. The seventh belongs to the world of meaning. On Shabbat, the observant Jew does not merely refrain from work. She enters a different mode of being — one oriented not toward doing but toward being, not toward producing but toward receiving, not toward mastering the world but toward delighting in it.</p>
-
-<p>Christianity has, for the most part, lost the Sabbath. The Protestant tradition, in its anxiety about legalism, jettisoned Sabbath observance along with other aspects of the Torah that it deemed no longer binding. What replaced it — the "Lord's Day" of Sunday worship — retained the communal gathering but lost the comprehensive rest. Contemporary American Christians work on Sundays, shop on Sundays, check email on Sundays. The day has been absorbed into the culture of productivity that Shabbat was designed to resist.</p>
-
-<p>The recovery of Sabbath — not as a legalistic obligation but as a spiritual discipline, a weekly act of resistance against the tyranny of productivity — would address one of the deepest pathologies of contemporary Christian life. The epidemic of pastoral burnout, the culture of overwork in ministry, the inability of Christians to distinguish their worth from their output — these are, at root, Sabbath problems. They are the symptoms of a community that has forgotten how to stop.</p>
-
-<h2>Questioning as Worship</h2>
-
-<p>In the Christian tradition, doubt is generally treated as a problem to be solved — a spiritual deficiency that needs correction, a phase to be moved through on the way to certainty. In Judaism, questioning is not a phase. It is a practice. It is, in a profound sense, worship itself.</p>
-
-<p>The rabbinical tradition is built on argument. The Talmud — the central text of rabbinic Judaism — is structured as a record of debates among the rabbis, with competing interpretations set side by side, often without resolution. The tradition does not merely tolerate disagreement. It enshrines it. The minority opinion is preserved not because it might someday become the majority but because the act of wrestling with the text — of pushing against it, questioning it, arguing with it — is itself a form of faithfulness.</p>
-
-<p>This posture extends to the relationship with God. Abraham argues with God over the fate of Sodom. Moses argues with God over the fate of Israel. Job argues with God over the meaning of suffering. Jacob wrestles with God physically and is renamed Israel — a name that means, literally, "one who wrestles with God." The tradition derived from these stories does not treat wrestling with God as a sign of weak faith. It treats it as the defining characteristic of the people of God.</p>
-
-<p>The church that cannot make room for doubt, argument, and unresolved questioning is not more faithful than the rabbinical tradition. It is less honest. It has confused certainty with faith, compliance with devotion, and the absence of questions with the presence of God. A faith that cannot survive questioning was never strong enough to be called faith in the first place. Judaism knows this. Christianity keeps forgetting it.</p>
-
-<h2>Supersessionism: The Error That Shaped Everything</h2>
-
-<p>Supersessionism — the theological claim that the church has replaced Israel as the people of God, that the new covenant has rendered the old covenant obsolete, that Christianity has superseded Judaism — is the root theological error from which nearly all Christian anti-Judaism flows.</p>
-
-<p>The claim has a long history. The Epistle of Barnabas, written in the early second century, argued that the Jews never properly understood their own Scriptures and that the covenant belongs exclusively to Christians. Justin Martyr, writing around 155 CE, told Trypho the Jew that "the Scriptures are not yours, but ours." John Chrysostom preached sermons against the Jews of such vitriol that they were used to justify persecution for over a millennium. Augustine developed the "witness doctrine," arguing that Jews were preserved by divine providence not as bearers of a living covenant but as witnesses to Christian truth — their degraded state serving as proof that God had transferred his favor to the church.</p>
-
-<p>The theological consequences of supersessionism have been catastrophic. If the church has replaced Israel, then Jewish existence is theologically unnecessary. If the old covenant is obsolete, then Judaism is an anachronism — a religion that should have ended when Christianity began. If the Jews rejected their own Messiah, then they bear a collective guilt that justifies their marginalization, persecution, and worse. The line from supersessionist theology to the pogroms of medieval Europe to the ghettos of early modern Europe to the ovens of Auschwitz is not straight — history is never that simple — but it is traceable. The theological soil was prepared by the church. Others planted the seeds and reaped the harvest. But the soil was ours.</p>
-
-<p>Paul, in Romans 9-11, explicitly rejects supersessionism. "Did God reject his people? By no means," he writes. "God's gifts and his call are irrevocable." The olive tree metaphor in Romans 11 is unmistakable: Gentile Christians are branches grafted into an existing tree — the tree of Israel. They do not replace the tree. They do not supersede it. They are guests in someone else's story. "Do not be arrogant toward the branches," Paul warns. "You do not support the root; the root supports you."</p>
-
-<p>The church largely ignored Paul's warning. It was arrogant toward the branches. And the consequences of that arrogance — theological, moral, and historical — are still being reckoned with.</p>
-
-<h2>Dialogue Without Conversion Pressure</h2>
-
-<p>Honest interfaith dialogue between Christians and Jews requires Christians to abandon the assumption that the dialogue's purpose is to bring Jews to Christ. This is not a concession to pluralism or a surrender of Christian conviction. It is a recognition that the relationship between Christianity and Judaism is unique among all interfaith relationships and cannot be forced into the standard missionary paradigm.</p>
-
-<p>Judaism is not a religion that has failed to hear the gospel. It is the religion that gave birth to the gospel. The Jewish people are not an unreached people group. They are the original covenant community from which Christianity emerged. To treat Judaism as simply one more religion to be evangelized is to misunderstand both Judaism and Christianity's relationship to it.</p>
-
-<p>Dialogue between Christians and Jews, at its best, is a recovery of family relationship — a conversation between siblings who share a parent, a library, and a story, even though they have told the story differently for two thousand years. Such dialogue requires Christians to listen to Jewish self-understanding on its own terms, not through the filter of Christian categories. It requires Christians to take seriously the possibility that God's covenant with Israel remains operative — that Judaism is not an empty shell awaiting Christian fulfillment but a living tradition with its own integrity, its own relationship with God, and its own gifts to offer the world.</p>
-
-<p>This does not require Christians to abandon their own convictions about Christ. It requires them to hold those convictions with the humility appropriate to a tradition that has spent two millennia getting its relationship with its parent tradition catastrophically wrong. The church that approaches Judaism with anything less than deep humility, genuine curiosity, and a willingness to learn has not yet reckoned with its own history. And until it does, it has nothing worth saying.</p>`
