-<h2>The Five Pillars and the Discipline of Submission</h2>
-
-<p>The Five Pillars of Islam — the <em>shahada</em> (declaration of faith), <em>salat</em> (prayer five times daily), <em>zakat</em> (almsgiving), <em>sawm</em> (fasting during Ramadan), and <em>hajj</em> (pilgrimage to Mecca) — constitute a comprehensive framework of spiritual discipline that shapes every dimension of a Muslim's life. They are not optional. They are not suggestions. They are the non-negotiable architecture of a life oriented toward God.</p>
-
-<p>The contrast with much of contemporary Christianity is instructive. The average American Christian attends church sporadically, prays inconsistently, gives modestly, fasts rarely or never, and undertakes no form of pilgrimage. The spiritual disciplines that the Christian tradition has historically valued — regular prayer, fasting, almsgiving, pilgrimage — exist in theory but have largely evaporated in practice. The Muslim who prays five times a day, facing Mecca, interrupting whatever else is happening to kneel before God, is practicing a level of spiritual discipline that most Christians admire in the abstract and avoid in practice.</p>
-
