-<h2>Justice as a Non-Negotiable</h2>
-
-<p>The Islamic emphasis on justice — <em>adl</em> — runs through every dimension of the tradition. The Quran identifies justice as one of God's essential attributes and one of the community's essential obligations. <em>Zakat</em> — the third pillar — is not charity in the Christian sense of a voluntary act of generosity. It is an obligation: a fixed percentage of one's wealth, distributed annually to those in need. The right of the poor to a share of the wealthy person's resources is not a matter of the wealthy person's discretion. It is a matter of divine command.</p>
-
-<p>This understanding of economic justice is deeply consonant with the biblical prophetic tradition. Amos, Isaiah, Micah, and the other Hebrew prophets thundered against economic exploitation and demanded structural justice for the poor — not as a supplement to worship but as a precondition for it. "I hate, I despise your religious festivals," God says through Amos. "But let justice roll on like a river." The prophetic tradition insists that piety without justice is not merely incomplete. It is offensive to God.</p>
-
-<p>Christianity has largely domesticated this tradition, treating generosity as a personal virtue rather than a structural obligation. The tithe, where it is practiced, is voluntary. Giving is framed as a response to blessing rather than a requirement of justice. The radical economic vision of the early church — "they held all things in common" — has been reduced to a historical curiosity. Islam's insistence that economic justice is not optional, not a matter of personal preference, not separable from the life of faith, recovers a dimension of the prophetic tradition that Christianity has allowed to atrophy.</p>
-
-<h2>Common Ground</h2>
-
-<p>The common ground between Christianity and Islam is more substantial than either tradition typically acknowledges. Both are monotheistic — both confess one God, creator of all things, sovereign over history, judge of all people. Both trace their lineage to Abraham. Both honor the Hebrew prophets. Both affirm the virgin birth of Jesus. The Quran speaks of Jesus (<em>Isa</em>) with profound reverence — as a prophet, as the Messiah, as one born of the Spirit of God, as a worker of miracles. Muslims do not accept the divinity of Christ or the doctrine of the Trinity, but the Jesus of the Quran is not a marginal figure. He is one of the most honored prophets in the Islamic tradition.</p>
-
-<p>Both traditions share a moral seriousness that is increasingly rare in the secular West. Both insist that human life has meaning, that moral choices have consequences, that the universe is not indifferent to how we live. Both reject the nihilism and consumerism that characterize late-modern Western culture. Both maintain that worship — the orientation of the whole person toward God — is the highest human activity. Both affirm the reality of the afterlife, the certainty of judgment, and the importance of living in light of eternity.</p>
-
-<p>These commonalities do not erase the differences, which are real and significant. The Christian confession of the Trinity, the divinity of Christ, and the atoning work of the cross are claims that Islam explicitly rejects. The Islamic understanding of revelation — that the Quran is the literal, uncreated word of God, dictated verbatim to Muhammad — differs fundamentally from the Christian understanding of Scripture as divinely inspired but humanly mediated. These differences matter. They should not be papered over in the name of false harmony.</p>
-
-<p>But the differences should not be weaponized, either. The fact that Christians and Muslims disagree about the nature of Christ does not mean they have nothing to say to each other about the nature of God, the demands of justice, the practice of prayer, or the meaning of human existence. Honest interfaith dialogue lives in the tension between genuine commonality and genuine difference — refusing to collapse either into the other.</p>
-
-<h2>What Honest Dialogue Looks Like</h2>
-
-<p>Honest interfaith dialogue is not a debate to be won. It is a conversation to be entered. It begins with the willingness to listen to the other tradition on its own terms — not through the filter of one's own categories, not in search of ammunition for apologetic arguments, but with genuine curiosity about how the other sees God, understands the world, and practices faithfulness.</p>
-
-<p>For Christians, this means setting aside the caricatures. Islam is not monolithic. It contains within it a vast diversity of traditions — Sunni and Shia, Sufi mysticism and Salafi rigorism, the philosophical tradition of al-Farabi, Ibn Sina, and Ibn Rushd, the poetic tradition of Rumi and Hafiz, the legal traditions of the four Sunni schools and the Ja'fari school of Shia jurisprudence. Reducing this richness to a single image — the terrorist, the oppressor, the civilizational threat — is not merely unfair. It is intellectually lazy. And intellectual laziness is a poor foundation for the pursuit of truth that both traditions claim to value.</p>
-
-<p>Dialogue also requires Christians to reckon with their own tradition's failures toward the Muslim world. The Crusades were not defensive operations launched to protect Christendom from aggression. They were wars of conquest, marked by massacre, plunder, and the slaughter of civilians. The sack of Jerusalem in 1099, in which Crusaders waded through blood in the streets and murdered Muslims and Jews indiscriminately, is a stain on Christian history that no amount of revisionism can erase. The colonial subjugation of the Muslim world in the nineteenth and twentieth centuries — justified, in part, by Christian civilizational pretensions — has left wounds that are still open. Christians who enter dialogue with Muslims without acknowledging this history are not ready for honest conversation.</p>
-
-<p>The goal of dialogue is not agreement. It is understanding — the kind of understanding that allows two people who disagree profoundly about ultimate questions to recognize each other as fellow seekers of the God who, both traditions confess, is greater than any human understanding of him. That recognition does not require the surrender of conviction. It requires the expansion of imagination — the willingness to consider that God's truth may be larger than any single tradition's apprehension of it, and that the encounter with the other may reveal dimensions of one's own faith that were previously invisible.</p>
-
