-  {
-    title: "Single Women in the Church: More Than 'Waiting'",
-    slug: "single-women-in-church",
-    excerpt:
-      "The church learned to talk about single women as a category defined by absence. Scripture, and the single Christ at the center of it, refuses that grammar.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "12 min",
-    body: `<p>A woman in my congregation told me once that she had stopped counting the sermons on marriage. Not out of bitterness. Out of arithmetic. She was forty-one, she had never married, and she had done the math on what the church had said to her and about her over two decades of faithful attendance. The overwhelming category into which she had been sorted was <em>not yet</em>. She was a person defined by what had not happened.</p>
-
-<p>I did not have a good answer for her that day. I have one now, and it starts with a confession. The church I serve, the church I was formed by, has a grammar of singleness that is almost entirely a grammar of absence. Single people are waiting. Single people are in a season. Single people are being prepared. Every one of those sentences locates the meaning of a person's life in a future event that may never come. We would never say it out loud in those terms. But the woman doing the arithmetic heard it clearly, because arithmetic does not lie.</p>
-
-<h2>The category the New Testament does not use</h2>
-
-<p>Here is what should stop us. The center of the Christian faith is a single man. Jesus of Nazareth never married, never fathered children, and died at roughly thirty-three. If the fullest human life is the married one with a household of children, then the most fully human person who ever lived missed it. That sentence is either blasphemy or it means our arithmetic is broken.</p>
-
-<p>Paul, the man who wrote most of the New Testament, was almost certainly unmarried when he wrote his letters, and in 1 Corinthians 7 he does something the modern church has quietly buried. He commends singleness. Not as a holding pattern. As a gift. "I wish that all were as I myself am. But each has his own gift from God, one of one kind and one of another" (1 Corinthians 7:7). Read that slowly. The gift language, the Greek <em>charisma</em>, is the same word Paul uses for prophecy and teaching and the other manifestations of the Spirit. Singleness is not the absence of the gift of marriage. It is a gift of its own.</p>
-
-<p>Then he presses further, and this is the verse we skip. "The unmarried man is anxious about the things of the Lord, how to please the Lord. But the married man is anxious about worldly things, how to please his wife, and his interests are divided" (1 Corinthians 7:32-33). Paul is not saying marriage is bad. He says it plainly a few verses later that the one who marries does well. He is saying the single person has an undivided attention that the married person, by the good and God-given fact of a spouse, does not. The single woman in my congregation was not missing something. She had something. The church had never told her what it was.</p>
-
-<h2>How we got the arithmetic wrong</h2>
-
-<p>I want to push the origin of this back further than the awkward small group, because the small group did not invent it. Barry Danylak, in his study <em>Redeeming Singleness</em> (2010), traces the theology carefully, and what he shows is that the ancient world had a coherent reason to treat the unmarried person as incomplete. Under the old covenant, the promise of God moved through the body. Offspring were the mechanism of the covenant. To be barren, to be childless, to die without a son, was to be cut off from the promise itself. That is why Hannah wept. That is why the daughters of Zelophehad in Numbers 27 fought for an inheritance. The stakes were not sentimental. They were covenantal.</p>
-
-<p>And then something happens that the church has never fully metabolized. Isaiah, writing to exiles who felt cut off from every promise, says this: "Let not the eunuch say, 'Behold, I am a dry tree.' For thus says the Lord: 'To the eunuchs who keep my Sabbaths, who choose the things that please me and hold fast my covenant, I will give in my house and within my walls a monument and a name better than sons and daughters'" (Isaiah 56:3-5). The dry tree. The one whose body will never carry the promise forward. God says: you will have a name better than sons and daughters. The covenant is being pried loose from biology decades before Christ arrives to finish the work.</p>
-
-<p>By the time we reach the New Testament, the mechanism has changed entirely. The promise now moves through the new birth, not through natural birth. Jesus says his mother and brothers are whoever does the will of God (Mark 3:35). The single person is no longer a dry tree. In Christ, the single woman is as fruitful as any mother in Israel, and the fruit is other believers, formed and carried and raised in the faith. We inherited the old covenant's arithmetic and kept applying it after Christ had already changed the equation. We did this. I have preached the very sermons that did it.</p>
-
-<h2>What the church actually does to single women</h2>
-
-<p>Let me be specific, because vagueness is a way of not repenting. The church does three things to single women, and it does them without malice, which is what makes them so hard to name.</p>
-
-<p>First, it makes them auxiliary. The married couple is the unit; the single woman is the extra. She is invited to serve the families, to babysit, to bring a dish, to be useful around the edges of the households that constitute the real church. She is welcome. She is just not central. Rosaria Butterfield has written with unusual honesty about how her own conversion happened inside a pastor's home that practiced what she calls radically ordinary hospitality, a home where the single and the married ate at the same table as equals. That table is rarer than we admit.</p>
-
-<p>Second, the church makes her a problem to be solved. The impulse to set her up, to ask when, to reassure her that God has someone, is well-meant and it is corrosive. It communicates that her current life is a waiting room. Nobody asks a married woman when she will finally do something with her life. We ask single women constantly, and we do not hear ourselves.</p>
-
-<p>Third, and this is the cruelest, the church often treats the single woman's celibacy as a deprivation to be pitied rather than a discipline to be honored. In a culture drowning in the assumption that sexual expression is a human necessity, the woman living a chaste single life is doing something genuinely counter-cultural and genuinely hard, and the church that should honor it instead quietly agrees with the culture that she is missing out. We adopted the world's anthropology and then wondered why our single members felt unseen.</p>
-
-<blockquote>She was not a person on the way to becoming a real member of the church. She already was one. The church just kept scheduling its life as if she were a guest.</blockquote>
-
-<h2>The undivided life</h2>
-
-<p>So what is the gift, concretely? I have watched it in my own congregation and I want to name it rather than theorize it.</p>
-
-<p>The single women I know who have made peace with the shape of their lives, who have stopped waiting and started living, have a mobility that married people do not have. When someone in the church is dying, they can go. When a family is drowning, they can move in for a week. When the mission field calls at an inconvenient hour of life, there is no household negotiation. Paul's word was <em>undivided</em>, and it is not an insult to marriage to say that marriage divides your attention. It is the plain fact that a woman who has given herself to a husband and children has given herself, rightly and gloriously, and cannot also give herself elsewhere without cost. The single woman can. That is not consolation. That is vocation.</p>
-
-<p>There is also a witness in it that the married cannot give. The single Christian who lives with joy is a living argument that a human being does not require romantic or sexual fulfillment to be whole. In an age that has made sexual self-expression into the very definition of authentic selfhood, that argument is not small. Stanley Hauerwas has said that singleness in the church is a sign that the church does not depend on biological reproduction to grow, because it grows by conversion and the making of disciples. The single woman is not the exception to that truth. She is its clearest embodiment.</p>
-
-<h2>The difference between chosen and unchosen singleness</h2>
-
-<p>I have to be honest about a place where much of the theology of singleness, mine included, tends to cheat. It is easy to celebrate the gift of singleness when singleness was chosen. Paul chose it. The monastics chose it. A man or woman who deliberately forgoes marriage for the sake of undivided service has embraced something, and embrace is a different experience than endurance. But a great many single women in the church did not choose it. They wanted marriage. They prayed for it. It did not come, for reasons no one can explain, and to tell such a woman that her singleness is a gift can land like a cruelty, as if her disappointment were ingratitude for a present she never asked to receive.</p>
-
-<p>So I want to say the harder thing. A gift is not always something you would have chosen, and it is not less a gift for arriving unrequested. The manna in the wilderness was not what Israel wanted; they wanted the food of Egypt, and they said so bitterly (Exodus 16:3). It was still bread from heaven. Paul himself calls his thorn in the flesh a thing he begged God three times to remove, and then reports the answer: "My grace is sufficient for you, for my power is made perfect in weakness" (2 Corinthians 12:9). The thorn was not chosen. The grace within it was real. A single woman who longs for a marriage that has not come is not required to pretend the longing is fine. She is invited to believe that the God who did not give her the thing she asked for has not therefore given her nothing, and that the undivided life she did not choose can still become, by grace, the undivided life she offers. That is not a denial of the grief. It is what the grief can become when it is carried toward God rather than away from him.</p>
-
-<p>The church fails such a woman in two opposite directions, and I have watched it fail in both. It fails her by pretending the disappointment does not exist, by rushing her past the loss to the lesson. And it fails her by treating the disappointment as the whole truth, by pitying her into a permanent posture of deprivation, agreeing with her at her lowest that her life is a lesser life. The narrow path between those failures is to grieve with her and refuse to grieve for her, to hold the loss without conceding that the loss is the last word. That is hard pastoral work, and it cannot be done from a script.</p>
-
-<h2>What repentance looks like</h2>
-
-<p>I am wary of ending on application, because clean application is the death of the actual work. But I owe the woman doing arithmetic something more than a better theology of singleness. I owe her a changed church, and change is concrete or it is nothing.</p>
-
-<p>It means preaching that does not assume the pew is full of married couples with children, because it is not, and it never was. It means a life together in which single people host and lead and are followed, not merely helped. It means old married men and women who take a younger single woman seriously as a whole person and not a case pending resolution. It means, above all, that we stop treating her life as a sentence with a missing word.</p>
-
-<p>It also means something harder for the married among us, myself included. It means we stop treating the single woman's presence in our homes as a favor we extend and start treating it as a gift she brings. When she is at our table, she is not being included in a real family from the outside; she is part of the family of God that our little household is only a fragment of. The church is the primary family, and the biological household is the secondary one, a truth the New Testament states so plainly that we have had to work hard to reverse it. Jesus promised that everyone who has left houses and family for his sake will receive "a hundredfold now in this time, houses and brothers and sisters and mothers and children" (Mark 10:29-30). The single woman is owed that hundredfold, and we are the ones who are supposed to be it.</p>
-
-<p>The single woman in my congregation is fifty now. She never married. She has buried three people from our church who had no family to sit with them at the end, and she sat with all of them. She has taught more children to read Scripture than any parent among us. If the resurrection is true, and I have staked my life on the claim that it is, then on the last day she will not be introduced as the woman who waited. She will be introduced by a name better than sons and daughters, because that is exactly what God promised the dry tree, and God does not do arithmetic the way we do.</p>
-
-<p>The church owes her that name now. Not at the resurrection. Now.</p>`,
