-  {
-    title: "Women and Work: Calling Beyond the Home Debate",
-    slug: "women-and-work",
-    excerpt:
-      "The evangelical fight over whether women should work has argued for decades on a battlefield the Bible never chose. There is an older and larger question underneath it.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "12 min",
-    body: `<p>I came to faith as an adult, out of atheism, and one of the strange things about arriving late to the church is that you can still see its arguments from the outside before you learn to feel them from the inside. The first evangelical fight I stumbled into was the one about women and work. Should Christian women have careers? Should mothers stay home? I remember thinking, with the flat literalism of a new reader, that I could not find the debate in the Bible I was reading. I found something larger and stranger, and it did not fit either side.</p>
-
-<p>Two decades later I have five sons and a working understanding of how much labor a household actually requires, and I am more convinced than ever that the standard evangelical argument about women and work is fought on a field the Scriptures never chose. Both sides have accepted a framing that would have baffled the people who wrote the book we all claim to obey.</p>
-
-<h2>The field nobody chose</h2>
-
-<p>The framing is this: there is the home, and there is work, and they are two places, and the question is which one a woman belongs in. That framing feels ancient and biblical. It is neither. It is roughly a hundred and eighty years old, and it is a product of the Industrial Revolution.</p>
-
-<p>Before the factory, the home and the workplace were the same building. The household was the unit of production. A farm, a shop, a trade, a bakery, a weaving business, all of it happened where the family lived, and everyone in the family labored, including the wife, including the children, including the servants and apprentices. The Greek word for household, <em>oikos</em>, is the root of our word economy, and that is not a coincidence. The household <em>was</em> the economy. The idea that a man goes away to a place called work while a woman stays in a place called home would have been unintelligible to Abraham, to Boaz, to Priscilla, to almost everyone in Scripture.</p>
-
-<p>What separated the two was the factory. When production moved out of the home and into the mill, men followed the paying labor out the door, and the home was left as a place of consumption and childrearing rather than production. Only then did a new ideology arise to explain the arrangement, what historians call the doctrine of separate spheres, the belief that the man's proper realm is the harsh public world of commerce and the woman's is the tender private world of the domestic hearth. The historian Barbara Welter named a related nineteenth-century ideal the Cult of True Womanhood in a landmark 1966 essay, and its four cardinal virtues were piety, purity, submissiveness, and domesticity. That fourth one, domesticity, is the one the church baptized. We took a Victorian economic accident and called it the created order.</p>
-
-<h2>What Proverbs 31 actually describes</h2>
-
-<p>Nothing exposes this more sharply than the passage most often preached to keep women at home. Proverbs 31. The excellent wife. If you have only heard it read at Mother's Day, you have heard it sentimentally, and you have almost certainly missed what she does with her days.</p>
-
-<p>She evaluates a field and buys it (Proverbs 31:16). She plants a vineyard from her own earnings. She "perceives that her merchandise is profitable" (31:18), which means she is running a business with a margin. She makes linen garments and sells them, and delivers sashes to the merchant (31:24). She is a manufacturer with a distribution channel. "She considers a field and buys it; with the fruit of her hands she plants a vineyard." The verbs are commercial. She is trading, negotiating, producing, and profiting, and the text holds all of this up not as scandalous but as the very definition of the woman "who fears the Lord" (31:30).</p>
-
-<p>The passage does not describe a woman confined to a private sphere. It describes a woman whose economic activity extends into the marketplace as a matter of course, because in the ancient world there was no wall between her household and the economy. She is praised in the gates, the place of public commerce and civic life. The chapter we cite to send women home is a chapter about a woman running an enterprise.</p>
-
-<blockquote>We turned a Victorian factory arrangement into a doctrine and then found a Hebrew businesswoman inconvenient. So we read her sentimentally, which is a polite way of not reading her at all.</blockquote>
-
-<h2>The word the whole thing turns on</h2>
-
-<p>Underneath the work debate is a bigger idea, and if you get the bigger idea the smaller debate mostly dissolves. The idea is vocation.</p>
-
-<p>Vocation comes from the Latin <em>vocare</em>, to call, and it was one of the recoveries of the Reformation. Before the sixteenth century, the language of calling was reserved for the clergy and the religious orders. You had a vocation if you were a priest, a monk, a nun. Everyone else just had a job, spiritually second-class labor performed while the truly called people prayed. Martin Luther detonated that in the 1520s. In his treatise on the estate of marriage and throughout his writing on the priesthood of all believers, Luther argued that the milkmaid and the magistrate and the mother have callings as holy as the monk, that God milks the cows through the milkmaid, that the humblest domestic labor done in faith is worship. The word for calling, in German <em>Beruf</em>, comes to mean both the divine summons and one's ordinary occupation. Those two meanings fused in the Reformation and have never fully come apart.</p>
-
-<p>Now hold that recovery against the work debate, and watch what happens. If vocation is the summons of God to serve your neighbor through your labor, then the question is never whether a woman should work. All labor that serves a neighbor in faith is a calling, whether it happens over a keyboard or a cradle. The mother teaching a two-year-old to speak is doing holy work. The physician setting a broken arm is doing holy work. The single woman running an accounting firm is doing holy work. None of these is a fallback from the others. Each is a place where God is at work through a human being for the sake of other human beings.</p>
-
-<h2>Where the harder truth lives</h2>
-
-<p>I do not want to make this too clean, because clean is usually a sign that I have stopped telling the truth. There are real tensions here that the vocation framework does not dissolve, and pretending otherwise would be a kind of lie.</p>
-
-<p>The first is that children are not a job you can do in your spare hours, and someone has to do it. In our house, with five sons, the sheer volume of formation required, the meals and the reading and the discipline and the presence, is not a hobby squeezed around careers. It is a vocation in the fullest Reformation sense, and it is enormous, and it usually falls disproportionately on my wife in ways that our theology of vocation honors and our economy of vocation does not pay for. To say a mother's work is holy and then to structure a household as if it were free is a contradiction I have lived and not resolved. The dignity we assign in the sermon has to show up in the division of labor at home, or the sermon is a decoration.</p>
-
-<p>The second tension is that work under the fall is not pure calling. Genesis 3 promises that the ground will bring forth thorns and that labor will come with sweat (Genesis 3:17-19), and that curse falls on the woman's work as well as the man's. A great deal of what women do for pay is exhausting, underpaid, and unseen, and a theology that only celebrates work as fulfillment has not read past Genesis 3. Sometimes work is just the thorns. A robust doctrine of vocation has to hold both, the dignity of the calling and the groaning of the cursed ground, without collapsing into either the prosperity gospel of the fulfilled career or the false piety that treats all ambition as pride.</p>
-
-<h2>The women the New Testament put to work</h2>
-
-<p>If the separate-spheres ideology were the created order, the early church did not get the memo, and the evidence is embarrassing to anyone who wants to keep women out of visible labor in the kingdom. Read the sixteenth chapter of Romans, where Paul greets the people he is relying on, and count the women. He commends Phoebe, a deacon of the church at Cenchreae, and calls her a patron, the Greek <em>prostatis</em>, a word for a benefactor with the means to support others (Romans 16:1-2). He greets Prisca, whom he names before her husband Aquila, a couple who "risked their necks for my life" and who had earlier taken the eloquent Apollos aside and taught him the way of God more accurately (Acts 18:26). He greets Mary, who "worked hard" for the church, and Tryphaena and Tryphosa, "workers in the Lord," and Persis, "who has worked hard in the Lord" (Romans 16:6, 12). The verb for hard labor, <em>kopiao</em>, is the same one Paul uses for his own exhausting apostolic work.</p>
-
-<p>These were not women confined to a private hearth doing invisible domestic tasks. They were financing the mission, hosting the church in their homes, teaching, traveling, and laboring in ways the church visibly depended on. Lydia, the first convert in Europe, was a dealer in purple cloth, a businesswoman running a trade in luxury goods, and the church at Philippi met in her house (Acts 16:14-15). The early Christian movement, born into a Roman world with its own rigid ideas about a woman's place, kept putting women to work in the most consequential labor there was. The Victorian doctrine that a woman's calling ends at her front door cannot survive a single reading of Romans 16.</p>
-
-<p>I raise this not to settle every question about office and authority, which is a separate matter that faithful churches dispute, but to make a narrower and undeniable point. The reflex that treats a woman's work outside the home as a concession to modernity, a departure from the biblical pattern, has the history exactly backward. The biblical pattern is a church staffed and funded and advanced by women who worked hard in the Lord, and Paul knew their names and wrote them down so we would too.</p>
-
-<h2>The verdict the debate was hiding</h2>
-
-<p>So I want to end by naming what I think the home-versus-work debate was actually protecting, because it was protecting something real, and I do not want to be glib about people who felt they were defending the family.</p>
-
-<p>What it was protecting was the conviction that children matter, that formation is not automatic, that a household is a garden that dies untended, and that a culture which treats childrearing as an obstacle to real life has lost its mind. That conviction is right. The church that fought for it was fighting for something worth fighting for. It just chose the wrong hill. It defended the family by defending a Victorian floor plan, and in doing so it told half the church that their labor in the marketplace was a compromise and the other half that their labor at home was invisible. Both of those messages were false, and both of them wounded women I have pastored.</p>
-
-<p>I have watched women wounded by both halves of this. I have watched a gifted woman apologize for her ambition as though the drive to build something good were a character flaw peculiar to women who would not stay in their lane. And I have watched a mother, worn to nothing by the unpaid and unending labor of forming small souls, absorb the message that she had opted out of anything that counted, that the real action was happening in offices she had left. I have watched the same woman, years later, told by a different corner of the church that she had betrayed her home the moment she took a paying job to keep the lights on. She could not win, because the game was rigged from the start against the possibility that her labor, wherever it happened, was simply good. Both women were lied to. Both were doing holy work. The lie in each case was the same lie wearing opposite clothes: that there is a hierarchy of callings with a woman's true place fixed somewhere on it, and that her worth is a matter of standing in the right spot. There is no such hierarchy. There is only the summons, and the faith with which she answers it.</p>
-
-<p>The truth underneath is older and harder and better. Every woman is called. The mother and the merchant, the teacher and the trader, the one raising sons and the one running a firm and the many doing both at once, are all standing in the same place, the place where God works through human hands for the sake of a neighbor. The question was never whether a woman should work. The question is the one the whole Bible asks of everyone: what has God called you to, and are you doing it in faith? That question does not sort women into spheres. It summons them. And a summons is a heavier and more honorable thing than a place to belong.</p>`,
