-  {
-    title: "Female Friendship: The Relationships That Sustain You",
-    slug: "female-friendship",
-    excerpt:
-      "The church has praised marriage, honored motherhood, and left the friendships between women almost entirely undescribed. Scripture takes them more seriously than we do.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "11 min",
-    body: `<p>I have five sons and no daughters, and one consequence of that arithmetic is that I have spent very little of my life inside the world of women's friendships. I have watched it from the edges. I have watched my wife on the phone with a friend for two hours after a hard week and get up from the couch a different person than she sat down. I have seen a woman in my congregation held together, quite literally, by three other women in a season when nothing else was holding. And I have noticed something that took me too long to notice: the church has almost nothing to say about it.</p>
-
-<p>We have shelves of books on marriage. We have ministries for mothers. We have discipleship programs and small groups. But the friendship between two women, the thing that in my observation actually carries many of them through the years the marriage books never mention, is nearly absent from our teaching. We praise the covenant relationships and leave the sustaining ones undescribed. That is a strange omission for a book that opens its account of human need not with a spouse but with the plain observation that it is not good for a person to be alone.</p>
-
-<h2>The friendship the Bible chose to canonize</h2>
-
-<p>If you want to know how seriously Scripture takes friendship, notice which one it made a whole book out of. The book of Ruth is not, at its center, a romance. The romance with Boaz is the resolution. The engine of the story is the loyalty between two women, Naomi and Ruth, a widowed mother-in-law and a foreign daughter-in-law who owed her nothing.</p>
-
-<p>Ruth's speech to Naomi in the first chapter is one of the most quoted passages at weddings, which is a small irony, because it was not spoken to a husband. It was spoken by one woman to another. "Where you go I will go, and where you lodge I will lodge. Your people shall be my people, and your God my God. Where you die I will die, and there will I be buried" (Ruth 1:16-17). That is covenant language. It is the language of a binding, self-giving loyalty, and Ruth aims it at a bitter old woman who has just told her to go home. The Bible looked at the relationship between two women in grief and said: this is worth a book. This is worth putting in the line of the Messiah, because Ruth becomes the great-grandmother of David and stands in the genealogy of Christ. The friendship is not a footnote to the story. It is the story.</p>
-
-<blockquote>We read Ruth's vow at weddings and forget she spoke it to a woman who had nothing to offer her. Which means we have heard the most famous words about loyalty in the Bible and missed who they were for.</blockquote>
-
-<h2>What was lost when friendship became sentiment</h2>
-
-<p>To feel how thin our idea of friendship has become, it helps to know it was once thicker. The ancient world took friendship with a seriousness we have lost. Aristotle devoted two of the ten books of his <em>Nicomachean Ethics</em> to it and distinguished three kinds: friendships of utility, friendships of pleasure, and the friendship of virtue, in which two people love each other for the sake of the good and become better through the loving. That third kind, Aristotle said, was rare and slow and the closest thing to the good life a human could have.</p>
-
-<p>The Christian tradition inherited this and deepened it. In the twelfth century, a Cistercian abbot named Aelred of Rievaulx wrote a whole treatise called <em>Spiritual Friendship</em>, arguing that friendship between believers is a place where the love of God is made concrete, that "a friend is the medicine of life," and that to move from friendship to the friendship of Christ is a natural ascent. Aelred was not talking about acquaintances. He was talking about the kind of bond in which two souls are so joined that they can bear each other's burdens and speak each other's sins and still remain.</p>
-
-<p>Something happened to that idea, and I think we can date it roughly. As romantic marriage rose in the modern West to absorb all the weight of human intimacy, friendship deflated. C.S. Lewis, in <em>The Four Loves</em> (1960), diagnosed it precisely. Friendship, he wrote, is the least natural of the loves, the least instinctive, the least necessary to survival and reproduction, and for exactly that reason the modern world, obsessed with what is natural and necessary, cannot see it. "To the ancients, Friendship seemed the happiest and most fully human of all loves; the crown of life and the school of virtue. The modern world, in comparison, ignores it." Lewis said the modern world barely believes friendship is a real love at all. We have not disproven him.</p>
-
-<h2>What women's friendship carries that marriage cannot</h2>
-
-<p>Here I have to speak as an observer rather than an insider, and I will try to do it without pretending to know from the inside what I only know from the edge.</p>
-
-<p>What I have watched, over fifteen years of pastoring, is that the friendships between women carry a specific weight that marriage is not designed to carry and often cannot. A wife cannot be her husband's only interpreter of her own experience, because he does not share it. My wife's friends understand things about her life, her body, her fears, her exhaustion, that I structurally cannot understand no matter how much I love her. When she comes home from a friend, she has been known in a register I do not have access to. That is not a threat to our marriage. It is a gift to it. A wife whose only intimacy is her husband is a wife with too much weight on one relationship, and I have seen that weight crush marriages that could have survived if the woman had not been so alone.</p>
-
-<p>The friendships also do something marriages are not built to do: they witness a whole life. A husband arrives partway through the story. A friend of thirty years remembers who you were at twenty, holds the earlier selves you have half-forgotten, and can tell you when you are drifting from the person you meant to become. That kind of long witness is a form of accountability the culture has almost entirely lost, and it is one of the reasons the older women in Titus 2:3-5 are told to teach the younger. The mechanism of that teaching is not a classroom. It is friendship across the generations, the older woman who has walked the road speaking to the younger one who is on it.</p>
-
-<h2>The friendship that also has to be able to wound</h2>
-
-<p>There is a strand in Scripture on friendship that the sentimental version leaves out entirely, and it is the strand that separates real friendship from mere companionship. A real friend can hurt you on purpose, for your good, and a companion who only ever soothes is not the deeper thing. "Faithful are the wounds of a friend; profuse are the kisses of an enemy" (Proverbs 27:6). And a few verses on: "Iron sharpens iron, and one man sharpens another" (Proverbs 27:17). Iron sharpening iron is not a gentle image. It is friction, sparks, the grinding of one hard thing against another until an edge appears. The friendship that only comforts and never corrects is missing half of what the book of Proverbs thinks friendship is for.</p>
-
-<p>I raise this because women's friendships, for all their depth, face a particular temptation, which is the temptation to make loyalty mean agreement. To be a good friend can quietly come to mean always taking her side, always affirming, never being the one who says the hard true thing about the man she is dating or the choice she is making or the grudge she is nursing. That is not friendship at its fullest. That is a mutual non-aggression pact dressed as intimacy. The friend who will tell you that you are wrong, who will risk the friendship to say what needs saying, is rarer and more valuable than the friend who only ever agrees, and the difference between them is the difference between iron and cotton.</p>
-
-<p>The reason this matters theologically is that God himself is described as a friend who wounds. "The Lord your God is in your midst," Zephaniah says, and the same God who "will rejoice over you with gladness" is the God who disciplines those he loves (Zephaniah 3:17; Hebrews 12:6). The friendship of God is not flattery. Jesus called his disciples friends, "for all that I have heard from my Father I have made known to you" (John 15:15), and what he made known to them included their coming failures, their pride, their need. The model of divine friendship is a candor that costs something. A women's friendship that has grown into that kind of candor, that can bear a hard word and survive it, is a stronger and holier thing than one that has stayed comfortable. Comfort is not the goal. It is a byproduct of a bond secure enough to tell the truth.</p>
-
-<h2>Why the church should build for it</h2>
-
-<p>Let me name what I think we have gotten wrong, and let me implicate myself first, because I have designed church programs for years and I have almost never designed for this.</p>
-
-<p>Our default church structures are built around the family unit and the affinity group. We gather people who are alike, young marrieds with young marrieds, moms with moms, and we call it fellowship. But the friendships that actually sustain women often cut across the categories we sort them into, and they take a kind of time and depth that a weekly small group with a curriculum does not produce. Real friendship is slow. It is inefficient. It cannot be programmed, only made room for. And the modern church, optimized for attendance and engagement, is not good at making room for slow, unproductive, deep human bonds. We are good at events. We are bad at friendship. I have run the events.</p>
-
-<p>The correction is not another program, which would miss the point entirely. It is a change of imagination. It is recognizing that when a woman is drowning, the thing that most often saves her is not the pastor and not the marriage seminar but two or three friends who will not leave. It is building a church culture that honors those friendships out loud, that names them from the pulpit as a form of the love of God, that treats a woman's deep friendships not as a private matter beside her real spiritual life but as part of it.</p>
-
-<h2>The weight this leaves</h2>
-
-<p>I said I watch this from the edge, and I do. But there is one thing I have seen clearly enough to stake a claim on.</p>
-
-<p>At the funerals I have officiated for women, the marriage is honored, and the children speak, and those things are right and good. But over and over, the person who cannot get through the eulogy, the one whose grief is a different kind, is the friend of forty years. The one who was there before the husband and after the children left. The one who knew. Scripture put a friendship at the head of the line of David and called it worthy of a book. The church, for all its talk of relationship, has never quite believed how much a friend can carry.</p>
-
-<p>And there is a warning folded into all of this for the church that means to take friendship seriously. Friendship cannot be manufactured, but it can be starved, and the modern church has a genius for starving it. A congregation that fills every night of the week with programs, that measures its health by the busyness of its calendar, leaves no room for the slow, unscheduled, unproductive hours in which real friendship is made. Friendship grows in margin, in the leftover time, in the meal that runs long because nobody had to be anywhere. A church can crowd it out with good things and never notice the loss, because the loss does not show up on any attendance sheet. It shows up years later, in the woman who is surrounded by a busy congregation and has no one to call at midnight. The remedy is not another initiative but a strange kind of restraint, the willingness to leave a night empty so that people have time to become something to one another. A church that guards margin the way it guards its programs would be doing more for the friendships of its women than any curriculum could, and it would cost nothing but the courage to do less. That courage is rarer among pastors than it should be, and I include myself in the shortage, because an empty night on the calendar feels like a failure of ministry rather than a gift of it, and I have filled far too many of them with things that mattered less than the friendships they crowded out.</p>
-
-<p>The women in your congregation who are still standing after everything are usually standing on the shoulders of a friend you have never thought to thank. It is not good for a person to be alone. God said that first, before the marriages, before the children, before any of it. We keep forgetting what he meant.</p>`,
-  },
