-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "12 min",
-    body: `<p>A woman sat across from me in my office and could not say the word. She circled it for twenty minutes. She was frustrated, she said. She was hurt. She was disappointed. She was struggling. What she was, plainly, to anyone who could see her hands, was furious, and she had been taught for forty years that the fury was not permitted to her, was in fact evidence that something was spiritually wrong with her, and so she had built a vocabulary of softer words to avoid the one true one. When I finally said it for her, "It sounds like you are angry," she cried, because no one had ever given her permission to be.</p>
-
-<p>I have had that conversation more times than I can count, and almost always with women. Men in my office name their anger freely, sometimes too freely, sometimes proudly. Women arrive having been trained out of the word. And I have come to believe that the church, my church, the tradition that formed me, taught women a specific and damaging lie about anger, and dressed the lie up as virtue.</p>
-
-<h2>The lie about meekness</h2>
-
-<p>The lie has a text, and the text has been abused. "Blessed are the meek, for they shall inherit the earth" (Matthew 5:5). Somewhere along the way, meekness got translated into the absence of strong feeling, and for women in particular it got translated into the absence of anger. The meek woman is the calm woman, the soft woman, the woman who does not raise her voice or her objection. The word became a leash.</p>
-
-<p>But the Greek word behind meek, <em>praus</em>, does not mean the absence of strength or the absence of anger. In classical Greek it was used of a war horse that had been broken to obedience, a horse with all its power intact and now under command. Meekness is not weakness. It is strength under control, force with a bit in its mouth. The meek person is not the one with no anger. The meek person is the one whose anger is harnessed to a purpose larger than the self. To teach a woman that meekness means feeling nothing is to teach her to be broken in a different sense of the word, and the church has done exactly that.</p>
-
-<blockquote>We told women that a godly woman does not get angry, and then we handed them a Bible in which God is angry on nearly every page. The contradiction was ours. They just paid for it.</blockquote>
-
-<h2>The God who is angrier than they were allowed to be</h2>
