-<p>The research the culture draws on is not fluff. The World Health Organization has documented for years that women are diagnosed with depression at roughly twice the rate of men, a disparity that holds across cultures and cannot be explained away as mere reporting bias. Whatever the tangled causes, biological, social, and circumstantial, the weight really does fall harder on women, and a church that will not say so is refusing to see what is in front of it. The secular world saw it first, named it, and stopped shaming the sufferer, and every one of those was a mercy the church should have offered and did not.</p>
-
-<p>But the secular account, strong as it is on the symptom, tends to be thin on the anthropology, and this is where Scripture goes deeper rather than merely disagreeing. The culture can tell a woman her depression is not her fault. It struggles to tell her why she matters when she cannot function, because it has largely located human worth in autonomy, productivity, and self-actualization, the very things depression strips away. A therapeutic culture that grounds a person's value in her flourishing has nothing to say to the person who cannot flourish, and so it can remove the shame of the illness without answering the deeper terror underneath it, which is the fear that a self that produces nothing is a self worth nothing. Only a gospel that grounds worth outside the self, in the love and the purchase of God, can reach that terror. The culture got the symptom right and the anthropology incomplete. The church should have offered both and for too long offered neither.</p>
-
-<h2>The weight this leaves</h2>
-
-<p>The woman who came to me afraid to tell anyone got help, real help, medical and communal and slow. She is well now, mostly, in the way that people who have been to the bottom are well, which is to say she carries the memory of it and it has made her gentler with everyone else who goes there. But I think often of the fear she named, the certainty that the church would find the sin. She was not paranoid. She had watched us do it to others. We had trained her to expect a suspicion in the place where she should have found a shelter.</p>
-
-<p>I think often, too, about how much of what I mistook for a spiritual condition was simply a body that had run out of what it needed to keep going. The line between the soul and the body is far thinner than my early theology allowed. When Elijah wanted to die, God did not begin with his theology; God began with sleep and bread, because sometimes the most spiritual thing a crushed person needs is rest and food and a doctor, and the reordering of the heart comes after the body has been given a chance to recover. A pastor who cannot tell the difference between a sin to be confessed and an illness to be treated will wound the very people he most wants to help, and I have been that pastor, reaching for repentance where I should have reached for a physician's number and a warm meal and a promise to stay.</p>
-
-<p>That is the failure I cannot argue my way around. The Bible we hold gives us Psalm 88 and Elijah under the tree and Christ in Gethsemane, a whole scriptural vocabulary for the faithful in the dark, and we set it aside to interrogate the suffering. The weight nobody sees is heavy enough. The church was never supposed to add to it. Too often it has, and I among them. The verse I should have led with, every time, was not the one about anxiety. It was the one that says he is near to the brokenhearted, and saves the crushed in spirit (Psalm 34:18). Near. Not diagnosing from across the room. Near.</p>`,
