-    title: "Raising Daughters in a World That Objectifies Them",
-    slug: "raising-daughters",
-    excerpt:
-      "I have five sons and no daughters, which means I write this as an outsider to the specific war being waged on the girls in your house. But I have watched the war, and I know the lie at the center of it.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "12 min",
-    body: `<p>I have five sons and no daughters. I want to say that at the top, because it would be a kind of dishonesty to write about raising daughters as if I had done it. I have not. What I have done is pastor the fathers and mothers of daughters for fifteen years, watch the girls in my congregation grow up inside a machine that means them harm, and raise five boys who are, whether I like it or not, part of the world those girls have to survive. So I write this from beside the problem rather than inside it, and I write it partly as a confession, because the sons I am raising are being formed by the same machine, on the other side of it, to consume what it teaches them to see.</p>
-
-<p>The machine has a name, or several. The academic term is the sexual objectification of women, and the developmental research on it is now substantial enough that I do not have to speculate. The girls in your house are being trained, from an age earlier than you think, to experience themselves as things to be looked at. That is the war. And the church has too often responded to it with a counter-lie that is only the same lie in a longer skirt.</p>
-
-<h2>The name for what is happening to her</h2>
-
-<p>In 1997, two psychologists, Barbara Fredrickson and Tomi-Ann Roberts, published a paper in the journal <em>Psychology of Women Quarterly</em> that named the mechanism with unusual precision. They called it objectification theory, and its central claim is this: when a culture persistently treats girls and women as bodies to be evaluated by others, the girls internalize the outside gaze. They begin to watch themselves from the outside. They stop asking what does my body feel and start asking what does my body look like to someone watching. Fredrickson and Roberts called this self-objectification, and their research, replicated many times since, linked it to a cluster of afflictions that fall disproportionately on girls: anxiety, depression, disordered eating, and a diminished ability to be present in one's own life because part of the mind is always occupied monitoring one's appearance.</p>
-
-<p>Sit with the horror of that for a moment. A girl learns to spend a permanent fraction of her attention watching herself be watched. She is never fully inside her own experience, because a portion of her is always outside it, checking the picture. And the machine that trains her, the advertising, the algorithms, the pornographic wallpaper of the culture, the filtered images that even her friends are posting, does this to her before she is old enough to name it or resist it. This is not a moral panic. It is measured, published, and replicated developmental psychology, and it describes a genuine assault on the interior life of the girls we are raising.</p>
-
-<blockquote>She is taught to watch herself being watched, until a part of her is always standing outside her own life, checking the picture. That is not vanity. It is an injury, and it is being inflicted on purpose.</blockquote>
-
-<h2>The church's counter-lie</h2>
-
-<p>Here is where I have to turn on my own house, because the church saw this assault and often responded with something that was not the gospel but its counterfeit. It responded with the purity culture of the 1990s and 2000s, and I watched that culture form a generation of girls, and I have since counseled the women it wounded.</p>
-
-<p>The counter-lie went like this. The world says your body is a thing to be looked at and consumed. The church replied: yes, your body is a thing to be looked at, and therefore you must cover it, guard it, and manage it, because it is a stumbling block, a temptation, a danger to the boys around you. Do you hear what happened? The church accepted the world's basic premise, that the girl's value and meaning are located in her body as an object of the male gaze, and then simply reversed the instruction. The world said display it; the church said hide it. But both agreed on the thing that mattered: she is, first and fundamentally, a body that men will look at, and her spiritual task is to manage that looking.</p>
-
-<p>The fruit of this was bitter, and I have tasted it in my office. Women raised in purity culture who could not experience their own bodies as good even inside a marriage the church itself had blessed, because they had been trained to see their bodies as landmines. Women who carried shame for having been looked at, as if the looking were their sin. Women who had been told, in youth group, that their bodies were the reason a boy might fall, which placed on a fourteen-year-old girl the responsibility for the imagination of every boy who saw her. That is a monstrous burden, and it is the same objectification the world practices, baptized and handed back to her as holiness. We did not free her from the gaze. We just added God's name to it.</p>
-
-<h2>What Scripture actually says about the daughter's body</h2>
-
-<p>The recovery is not a better modesty policy. It is a different anthropology, and it is right there in the text we claimed to be defending.</p>
-
-<p>The first thing Scripture says about the human body, male and female, is that it was made in the image of God. "So God created man in his own image, in the image of God he created him; male and female he created them" (Genesis 1:27). The daughter's body is not first an object of anyone's gaze. It is first an image of God, a bearer of divine likeness, made to be a subject who looks out at the world and acts within it, not primarily an object that is looked at. Every regime that reduces her to a thing to be evaluated, whether it hands her a bikini or a burden of blame, is denying Genesis 1:27, and the church that adopted the world's premise denied it too.</p>
-
-<p>And Scripture is relentless that the value of a person is not in the surface the world evaluates. "Charm is deceitful, and beauty is vain, but a woman who fears the Lord is to be praised" (Proverbs 31:30). Peter writes to women that their true adornment is not the external, the braided hair and the gold, but "the hidden person of the heart with the imperishable beauty of a gentle and quiet spirit, which in God's sight is very precious" (1 Peter 3:3-4). Read that phrase carefully, because purity culture abused it too: <em>in God's sight</em>. The verse relocates the audience. The girl trained by the machine performs for the gaze of the watching world. Peter tells her the audience that matters is God, who sees the hidden person and not the surface, and who has already declared her precious before she managed a single thing about her appearance. That is not another instruction about how to be looked at. It is an escape from the whole economy of being looked at.</p>
-
-<h2>What I am trying to do with my sons</h2>
-
-<p>I said this was a confession, and here is the confessing part. The war on daughters cannot be won on the daughters' side alone, because half the machine runs through boys, and I am raising five of them. The girls in your congregation will grow up into a world staffed by men, and some of those men will be mine, and the question of whether they consume women as objects or honor them as image-bearers is being decided right now, in my house, on my watch.</p>
-
-<p>So the work I actually control is not the daughters' modesty. It is my sons' vision. I am trying, imperfectly and against the same machine, to raise boys who see a woman as a whole person before they see her as a body, who are not trained by a decade of pornographic saturation to evaluate before they encounter, who understand that the gaze the world taught them is not neutral looking but a habit of consumption that damages the person consumed and hollows out the one consuming. When Jesus said that a man who looks at a woman to lust for her has already committed adultery in his heart (Matthew 5:28), he located the sin where it belongs, in the one doing the looking, not the one being looked at. Purity culture inverted that verse and hung it on the girls. I am trying to hang it back where Christ put it, on the boys, on my boys, where the responsibility actually lives.</p>
-
-<h2>What a father is actually for</h2>
-
-<p>I came to faith from atheism, and I was raised without a father, and those two facts collide most sharply for me right here, because I am now the thing I never had, to sons who will help decide how a generation of daughters is treated. A girl raised with a father who delighted in her mind before her appearance, who was interested in what she thought and not merely how she looked, who was safe and present and did not leave, has been handed a set of instruments for detecting the counterfeit long before the counterfeit arrives. She has felt what it is to be loved as a person by a man, and so the man who wants to love her only as a body has a harder time convincing her that consumption is the same as care. She has a reference point. The absence of that reference point is one of the machine's quietest advantages, and I know it from the inside, because I grew up without the male love that would have taught me what love from a man was supposed to feel like.</p>
-
-<p>The research bears this out with unusual consistency, and I state it carefully because the topic is prone to overreach. Studies of paternal involvement, including the work summarized by researchers like Ronald Rohner on the effects of father acceptance and rejection, have found across cultures that a father's warm, engaged presence correlates with better outcomes for daughters in self-image, in the timing and health of their relationships, and in their resistance to exploitation. This is not a promise, and it is not a mechanism a father can operate like a lever, because children are not machines and God is sovereign over ends no parent controls. But it points at something true and old: that the way a girl is treated by the first man who loves her shapes what she will tolerate from the men who come after. The father is a witness. His love is testimony, admitted into evidence long before she can weigh it, about whether a woman is a subject to be known or an object to be used.</p>
-
-<p>God knew this about himself before any of us knew it about our fathers. The name he most insists on, over against every distortion, is Father, and the psalm calls him "Father of the fatherless and protector of widows" precisely at the point where the human father has failed or vanished (Psalm 68:5). I take that verse personally, because I was one of the fatherless it names, and I have staked my life on the claim that the God it describes is real. A daughter whose earthly father fails her, or dies, or was never there, is not therefore doomed, because there is a Father who does not leave, and the church that mediates his love to the fatherless girl is doing the most direct possible work against the machine. But where a human father is present, he is not off the hook. He is on the front line, and most of us do not treat the assignment with the seriousness the girls deserve.</p>
-
-<h2>The weight this leaves</h2>
-
-<p>I cannot tell you how to raise your daughter, because I have never raised one, and I distrust the men who write confidently about a war they have only watched. But I can tell you what I have seen from beside it, and I can tell you the one thing I would stake everything on.</p>
-
-<p>Your daughter is being told, by a machine of enormous power and no conscience, that she is a surface. The world says the surface should be displayed. Some of the church said the surface should be hidden. Both were lying to her about the most basic fact of her existence, which is that she is not a surface at all. She is an image of God, a subject with a soul and a will and a mind, made to look out at the world and love it and act in it, precious in God's sight before and beneath and entirely apart from whether anyone ever finds her beautiful. If she believes that, all the way down, the machine loses its grip, because the machine only works on a girl who has agreed to be a surface. And if she does not believe it, no modesty policy in the world will save her, because she will only be a well-covered object instead of an exposed one, still watching herself be watched.</p>
-
-<p>The war for your daughter is not, finally, a war over her clothing. It is a war over whether she is a person or a thing. The world has picked its side. Too often the church, meaning to fight the world, picked the same side in different words. I have five sons and no daughters, and the most useful thing I can do in this war is make sure the men I am sending into it have learned to see a person where the machine trained them to see a thing. That is my part. The daughters are worth every hour of it, and I am not certain we have given them the hours they deserve.</p>`,
-  },
