-  {
-    title: "Women in the Bible They Don't Teach You About",
-    slug: "women-in-the-bible",
-    excerpt:
-      "Deborah judged Israel. Huldah authenticated Scripture. Junia was called an apostle. The Bible is fuller of women with authority than the sermons I grew up on ever let on.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "13 min",
-    body: `<p>I came to faith late, and I came to it suspicious. When you arrive at Christianity from atheism, you do not arrive with the assumptions a churched child carries. You arrive with a list of accusations, and near the top of my list was this one: the Bible erases women. I had heard that outside the church, and then I walked inside the church and heard it confirmed by omission. The women I met in sermons were quiet, supportive, and safely offstage. Sarah laughed. Ruth gleaned. Mary treasured things in her heart. I was not told there were women who commanded armies and authenticated Scripture, and I want to know why I was not told.</p>
-
-<p>So let me tell you what I found when I finally read the text instead of the paraphrase of the text I had been handed.</p>
-
-<p>I should say at the outset why this matters to me beyond the intellectual accusation. I have five sons and no daughters, which means I have spent two decades thinking about how a man forms boys. But I pastor a church full of daughters, and I was raised by a woman who held a household together alone because my father was not there to help her do it. The women who kept me alive as a boy were not offstage in my life. They were the whole stage. When I opened the Bible expecting them to be absent and found them everywhere, something in me that had been braced for disappointment relaxed, and then something else in me got angry, because I understood how much had been kept from me and from everyone in the pews beside me.</p>
-
-<h2>Deborah, who was already leading before the men agreed to it</h2>
-
-<p>Judges 4 does not soften what it says. "Now Deborah, a prophetess, the wife of Lappidoth, was judging Israel at that time" (Judges 4:4). The Hebrew verb there, shaphat, is the same verb used of Gideon, of Samson, of every judge God raised up in that book. She is not filling in. She is not a placeholder until a real leader shows up. She holds court under a palm tree and Israel comes to her for judgment, which in that era meant she carried both civil and spiritual authority for a nation.</p>
-
-<p>Barak, the general, will not go to war unless she comes with him. Read that slowly. The military commander of Israel refuses to move without the woman prophet at his side. And Deborah tells him plainly that because he insisted on her presence, the glory of the victory will go to a woman, and it does, to Jael with a tent peg (Judges 4:9). The book of Judges is a book about the failure of leadership in Israel, one long descent, and sitting near the top of that descent as one of its bright moments is a woman.</p>
-
-<p>I was taught that Deborah was an exception God permitted because no man would step up. I have heard pastors say it from the pulpit as though it were in the text. It is not in the text. The text does not apologize for her. The text does not explain her away. We do that. The apology is ours, not God's.</p>
-
-<p>And there is more. The victory song in Judges 5, which scholars regard as one of the oldest passages in the entire Hebrew Bible, some dating its composition to within a century or two of the events it describes, is attributed to Deborah herself. "Then sang Deborah and Barak the son of Abinoam on that day" (Judges 5:1). She is credited as a poet and a theologian of Israel's history, composing the sacred song that interprets what God has done. In that song she names herself: "until I arose, I, Deborah, arose as a mother in Israel" (Judges 5:7). A mother in Israel, and also its judge, its prophet, and the author of one of its oldest hymns. The categories we like to keep separate, the maternal and the authoritative, she held together in a single breath, and the text celebrates the union rather than straining at it.</p>
-
-<h2>Huldah, who told the king whether the Bible was real</h2>
-
-<p>In 622 B.C., during the reign of Josiah, a scroll was found during the temple renovation. Second Kings 22 tells the story. The scroll appears to be a portion of Deuteronomy, and it terrifies the king because it names judgments Judah has been ignoring for generations. Josiah needs to know if the scroll is authentic, if it truly speaks for God. He sends the high priest Hilkiah and the royal officials to find out.</p>
-
-<p>They do not go to Jeremiah, who was prophesying at that time. They do not go to Zephaniah. They go to Huldah, a prophetess living in Jerusalem (2 Kings 22:14). And Huldah renders the verdict. She authenticates the word of God for the king of Judah. "Thus says the LORD, the God of Israel" (2 Kings 22:15), she begins, and the reform of Josiah, one of the great revivals in the Old Testament, proceeds on the authority of her judgment about what counted as Scripture.</p>
-
-<p>Think about what that means. At a hinge moment in the formation of the canon, the question of whether a text was truly the word of God was answered by a woman, and the priests and the king accepted her answer without recorded objection. I went thirty years without hearing her name. That is not the Bible's failure. That is a failure downstream of it, and I have participated in it.</p>
-
-<h2>Junia, the apostle they turned into a man</h2>
-
-<p>Romans 16 is Paul's long list of greetings, and it is full of women doing real work. In verse 7 Paul writes, "Greet Andronicus and Junia, my kinsmen and my fellow prisoners. They are well known to the apostles, and they were in Christ before me." Junia is a woman's name. It was a common Roman woman's name. And Paul calls her, along with Andronicus, "well known to the apostles," or as many scholars render the Greek, "well known among the apostles," which would make her an apostle herself.</p>
-
-<p>John Chrysostom, preaching in the fourth century, took it plainly. "Oh how great is the devotion of this woman," he said, "that she should be even counted worthy of the appellation of apostle." He did not blink at a female apostle. The blinking came later. Sometime in the medieval period the name was quietly altered in some manuscripts and translations to Junias, a masculine form, a name for which we have no attestation anywhere in the ancient world. The scholar Eldon Jay Epp documented this in his 2005 study, tracing how a woman named Junia became a man named Junias in the printed Greek New Testament for much of the twentieth century, until the textual evidence forced the correction back.</p>
-
-<p>We changed her name. Not God. We did. Faced with a woman the text called an apostle, translators found it easier to change the woman than to change their assumptions. I want to sit in that discomfort rather than rush past it.</p>
-
-<p>I want to be fair to the other reading, because honesty demands it. Some scholars argue that the Greek phrase means Junia was well known to the apostles rather than counted among them, which would make her esteemed by the apostles without holding the office herself. That is a legitimate grammatical possibility and I will not pretend the case is closed. But notice that even the more modest reading leaves us with a woman whom Paul names alongside her husband, calls his kinsman and fellow prisoner, and says was in Christ before he was, a woman esteemed by the apostolic circle and imprisoned for the faith. Whichever way the grammar finally falls, the attempt to erase her by turning her into a man named Junias was not a grammatical decision. It was a decision made in advance about what a woman was permitted to be, and then imposed on the text. That is the part I cannot excuse, and the part I recognize in myself, because I have read my own assumptions back into Scripture more times than I have caught myself doing it.</p>
-
-<h2>Phoebe, who carried the letter</h2>
-
-<p>Romans opens the sixteenth chapter this way: "I commend to you our sister Phoebe, a servant of the church at Cenchreae" (Romans 16:1). The word translated servant is diakonos, deacon, the same word Paul uses of himself and of Timothy. He continues, "that you may help her in whatever she may need from you, for she has been a patron of many and of myself as well" (Romans 16:2). The word patron there, prostatis, carries the sense of a benefactor, a protector, one who stands before others on their behalf.</p>
-
-<p>Phoebe was almost certainly the person who carried the letter to the Romans from Corinth to Rome. That is a staggering thing to consider. The most theologically dense letter in the New Testament, the document that lit the fuse under Augustine and Luther and Wesley, was entrusted to a woman to deliver, and as the courier she would have been the one to read it aloud and answer the first questions the Roman church had about it. The first interpreter of Romans was Phoebe. I was not taught that either.</p>
-
-<h2>Priscilla, who taught the great preacher</h2>
-
-<p>Acts 18 introduces Priscilla and her husband Aquila, tentmakers who work alongside Paul. When the eloquent preacher Apollos comes to Ephesus preaching accurately but incompletely, it is Priscilla and Aquila who "took him aside and explained to him the way of God more accurately" (Acts 18:26). Notice the order. Luke names Priscilla first in that verse, and in most of the places the couple appears, her name comes first, which in the ancient convention often signaled prominence. A woman helped correct and complete the theology of one of the early church's most gifted preachers.</p>
-
-<p>Think about what a strange sentence that is for those who insist a woman may never instruct a man in matters of doctrine. Here is a man, Apollos, described as eloquent and "competent in the Scriptures" (Acts 18:24), being taken aside and taught the way of God more accurately by a married couple in which Luke, contrary to his culture's convention, names the wife first. The verb Luke uses is not a soft word for suggestion. They explained, they set forth, they instructed. And the New Testament records it without a hint of apology or qualification, as simply what happened, the way the truth got to Apollos so that he could go on to be a powerful help to the believers in Achaia. The church that produced the New Testament did not seem to find Priscilla's teaching of Apollos remarkable enough to comment on. The later church found it remarkable enough to explain away. The distance between those two reactions is the distance I am trying to name in this whole essay.</p>
-
-<h2>The daughters of Zelophehad, who changed the law</h2>
-
-<p>In Numbers 27 five sisters, Mahlah, Noah, Hoglah, Milcah, and Tirzah, come before Moses and the whole assembly. Their father has died without sons, and under the existing law his inheritance and name would vanish. They argue their case. "Why should the name of our father be taken away from his clan because he had no son? Give to us a possession among our father's brothers" (Numbers 27:4). And here is the sentence I cannot get past: Moses brings their case before the LORD, and the LORD says, "The daughters of Zelophehad are right" (Numbers 27:7). God amends the inheritance law of Israel because five women stood up and said the law as it stood was unjust to them. Their appeal did not merely get an exception. It changed the statute for everyone after them.</p>
-
-<h2>Mary Magdalene, the apostle to the apostles</h2>
-
-<p>All four Gospels place women at the empty tomb first. In John 20, Mary Magdalene meets the risen Christ, mistakes him for the gardener, and then hears him speak her name. He gives her the commission: "go to my brothers and say to them, I am ascending to my Father and your Father, to my God and your God" (John 20:17). She goes and announces to the disciples, "I have seen the Lord" (John 20:18).</p>
-
-<p>The first witness of the resurrection, the first evangelist of the risen Christ, the first person commissioned to carry the news on which all of Christianity stands, was a woman. The medieval church called her apostola apostolorum, the apostle to the apostles, and they were right to. In a culture where a woman's testimony was not admissible in a court of law, God chose women as the first witnesses of the central event of history. If the early church had invented the resurrection story, as skeptics like me once assumed, they would never have staked it on witnesses no court would believe. The women at the tomb are, oddly, one of the strongest arguments for the thing actually happening.</p>
-
-<h2>So why the silence</h2>
-
-<p>I do not want to overstate my case, and I will not pretend the Bible reads like a modern manifesto. It was written into patriarchal worlds and it names those worlds honestly, including their cruelty to women. But the honest reader has to reckon with how much more the text contains than the tradition passed down. The erasure I once blamed on the Bible was, I discovered, largely an erasure performed by its readers. We inherited a curated Bible. Someone chose which women to preach and which to skip, and the ones they skipped were often the ones with authority.</p>
-
-<p>I say we, and I mean it. I have preached forty sermons on David and Paul before I ever preached one on Deborah or Huldah. That was a choice, and it was mine, and it taught a room full of daughters that Scripture had little for them but a supporting role. It was not the text that taught them that. It was me.</p>
-
-<p>The women are in the book. They were always in the book. The question is whether we have the courage to preach the book we actually have instead of the one we were handed. Deborah is still sitting under her palm tree. Huldah is still holding the scroll. Mary is still running from the tomb with the only news that finally matters. They have been there the whole time. We are the ones who looked away.</p>`,
-  },
-  {
-    title: "Biblical Womanhood Beyond the Debate",
-    slug: "biblical-womanhood-beyond-debate",
-    excerpt:
-      "Complementarian and egalitarian Christians have fought over a handful of verses for decades. Both sides have something true and something to answer for. Here is the harder question underneath the debate.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "13 min",
-    body: `<p>There is a debate that has consumed a great deal of energy in the American church for the last fifty years, and I want to walk into it carefully, because I have watched it wound people I love. It is the debate over what the Bible teaches about women, men, marriage, and the church. The two camps have names. Complementarian and egalitarian. Most Christians have never used either word out loud and yet have strong feelings about the thing the words describe. Let me try to state each position the way its own best defenders would state it, before I say anything else. If you can tell from my summary alone where I land, I have failed the first task.</p>
-
-<h2>The complementarian case, at its strongest</h2>
-
-<p>The complementarian argues that God created men and women equal in worth and dignity, both bearing his image (Genesis 1:27), and different in role, and that this difference is not a curse of the fall but a feature of the good creation before the fall. He points to the order of creation in Genesis 2, to which Paul appeals directly in 1 Timothy 2:13. He points to Ephesians 5, where the husband is called to a headship defined entirely by self-giving love, "as Christ loved the church and gave himself up for her" (Ephesians 5:25), a headship that looks like a cross and not a throne. He points to 1 Corinthians 11:3 and the ordering it describes, and to the pastoral qualifications of 1 Timothy 3 and Titus 1, which assume male elders.</p>
-
-<p>The strongest version of this position, associated with scholars like Wayne Grudem and the late thoughtful pastor Tim Keller, is not about competence or capacity. It does not claim women are less intelligent, less gifted, or less spiritual. It claims that God has assigned distinct and complementary callings, that authority in the home and the ordained office of elder are given to qualified men, and that this is not a demotion of women any more than the Son's submission to the Father is a demotion of the Son within the equal Trinity. The best complementarians grieve the abuse done in the name of male headship and insist that a headship not spent in sacrifice is a counterfeit. They say the world's problem is not too much biblical masculinity but too little of the real thing.</p>
-
-<p>Let me press their case even harder than they sometimes press it themselves, because a steelman that stops early is a straw man in better clothing. The complementarian can point out that the surrounding culture's account of equality has often meant sameness, an erasure of any given difference between men and women, and that this erasure has not obviously served women well. He can note that the therapeutic and consumer culture that promised women liberation through sameness has left a great many of them exhausted, and that the biblical vision of distinct and honored callings might be a mercy the culture cannot recognize. He can observe that the male headship the Bible describes is not the male dominance the world knows, that Ephesians 5 gives the husband no rights and only a costly duty, to love his wife as Christ loved the church and died for her, which is the most demanding assignment in the entire passage. The wife is asked to submit. The husband is asked to die. If you are keeping score of who got the harder command, the complementarian will tell you it was the man, and the honest reader has to concede he has a point about the text.</p>
-
-<h2>The egalitarian case, at its strongest</h2>
-
-<p>The egalitarian argues that the same Bible, read whole, points toward the full and equal participation of women in every sphere including church leadership. She points to Genesis 1, where both male and female are given dominion together with no hierarchy named. She argues that the subordination of woman to man in Genesis 3:16, "your desire shall be for your husband, and he shall rule over you," is described as a consequence of the fall, part of the brokenness Christ came to reverse, not a creation ordinance to preserve.</p>
-
-<p>She points to Galatians 3:28, "There is neither Jew nor Greek, there is neither slave nor free, there is no male and female, for you are all one in Christ Jesus," and argues that if we rightly read the slave and free clause as undoing an entire social hierarchy, we should read the male and female clause the same way. She points to the women I named earlier, Deborah judging, Huldah authenticating, Phoebe carrying Romans, Junia the apostle, Priscilla teaching Apollos. She reads the restrictive passages, 1 Timothy 2 and 1 Corinthians 14, as addressed to specific first-century situations, disorder in the Corinthian assembly, false teaching spreading through the Ephesian church, rather than as timeless prohibitions. The New Testament scholars Gordon Fee and Ben Witherington and, on the theological side, the late thoughtful writer scholars associated with the group Christians for Biblical Equality have made this case with rigor. The best egalitarians are not importing a secular agenda. They are asking the church to be more biblical, not less, by attending to the whole witness of Scripture rather than a favored shelf of it.</p>
