-<p>And here too let me press the case past where its advocates sometimes leave it. The egalitarian can point out that the same interpretive move used to keep women from the pulpit, reading a first-century instruction as a timeless creation ordinance, was once used with equal confidence to keep slaves in their place, since Paul told slaves to obey their masters and grounded it in the household order of his day. The church eventually learned to read those texts as bound to a social world Christ was quietly undoing rather than sanctioning forever. The egalitarian asks, with real force, why the male and female clause of Galatians 3:28 should be treated differently from the slave and free clause sitting right beside it in the same sentence. She can note that the trajectory of Scripture, from the shared dominion of Genesis 1 through the reversal of the curse in Christ to the outpouring of the prophetic Spirit on daughters at Pentecost, bends toward inclusion, and that a faithful reading attends not only to isolated verses but to the direction the whole story is traveling. This is not the culture talking. This is a serious argument from the shape of redemption, and the honest complementarian has to answer it rather than dismiss it.</p>
-
-<h2>Naming the order of the question</h2>
-
-<p>Before I go further I owe you honesty about how much this question weighs. The historic creeds, Nicaea and Chalcedon, do not settle it. The Apostles' Creed does not settle it. This is not a first-order question on the level of the Trinity or the bodily resurrection of Christ, where the faith itself is at stake. It is a second-order question, one over which faithful churches divide and have divided for a very long time without either side ceasing to be the church. That matters enormously for how we treat each other in it. A person who lands differently than you on the ordination of women is not thereby a heretic, and the moment we speak as though they are, we have inflated a second-order question into a first-order one, which is its own kind of error.</p>
-
-<p>I hold a view. I am not going to pretend I do not, and it would be cowardice to hide behind a false neutrality. But I am going to hold it in front of you in a way that keeps a brother or sister on the other side reading, because if I cannot do that, my view is not worth much.</p>
-
