-<p>Here is the strongest objection I have not fully answered, and I will not pretend I have. Paul grounds his instruction in 1 Timothy 2 not in the local situation but in the creation order itself: "For Adam was formed first, then Eve" (1 Timothy 2:13). That is not a cultural argument. That is an appeal to something before the fall, before culture. The honest egalitarian has to do real work with that verse, and the honest complementarian is right to press it. I do that work, and I find the reading available, but I will not tell you it is effortless or that the complementarian is a fool for weighing that verse more heavily than I do. He is weighing a real thing.</p>
-
-<h2>The harder question underneath</h2>
-
-<p>Now I want to say the thing that the whole debate has been distracting us from. Both camps have been so busy fighting over who can stand behind a pulpit that we have largely ignored a prior and heavier question. What is the church for? And what is a human being for?</p>
-
-<p>The debate over roles assumes we already know what leadership is, what authority is, what a good life is, and we are only arguing about who gets to have it. But the New Testament keeps refusing that assumption. When the disciples argued about who was the greatest, Jesus did not adjudicate the argument. He overturned the table it was sitting on. "Whoever would be great among you must be your servant, and whoever would be first among you must be slave of all" (Mark 10:43 to 44). The kingdom does not run on the logic of who gets the authority. It runs on the logic of who spends themselves last.</p>
-
-<p>I have watched complementarian men use headship as a warrant for control, which is the exact opposite of the cross-shaped headship their own best theologians describe, and I have watched egalitarian churches chase the culture's account of power so closely that they forgot the cross-shaped part entirely, treating leadership as a prize to be distributed fairly rather than a burden to be carried sacrificially. Both errors share a root. Both have accepted the world's definition of what authority is and only argued about its allotment.</p>
-
-<h2>What my own house taught me</h2>
-
-<p>I have five sons. I did not have a father in my own house growing up, and so I have had to learn fatherhood from scratch, without a template, in real time, in front of them. And the thing I have learned, slowly and often too late, is that the authority that actually forms a child is almost never the authority that announces itself. It is the authority of the parent who is up at three in the morning, who apologizes first, who is present when it costs something. My sons have never once obeyed me because I cited a verse about my position. They have followed me, when they have followed me at all, because they watched me stay.</p>
-
-<p>That has quietly reordered how I read the whole debate. The question the New Testament is pressing on both camps is not chiefly who is in charge. It is whether anyone in charge is willing to be poured out. And on that question, the honest complementarian and the honest egalitarian are closer to each other than either is to the surrounding culture, which believes that power is for having and keeping.</p>
-
-<p>The sociologist Christian Smith, in his 2005 study Soul Searching, gave a name to the actual folk religion of much of American church life, moralistic therapeutic deism, a faith in which God exists mainly to make us feel good about ourselves and to solve our problems on request. I raise it here because I have come to think the roles debate often runs on that unexamined substrate. Both sides, at their weakest, argue about women's roles as though the point of the church were the flourishing and self-actualization of its members, with the only question being how to distribute the opportunities fairly or how to protect a divine order that guarantees them. But the church does not exist to actualize anyone. It exists to form a people who look like the crucified Christ. The moment the debate becomes a fight over who gets to flourish in which role, it has already accepted a therapeutic account of the church that neither Paul nor Jesus would recognize, and both camps have breathed it in without noticing.</p>
-
-<h2>The test</h2>
-
-<p>So here is my test, and I hold it against myself first. If a woman who has landed on the other side of this question from me reads what I have written and feels beaten rather than understood, I have failed, no matter how right my exegesis. And if the debate over women's roles has made either camp more certain of its tribe and less able to see the cruciform pattern that judges every account of authority, then the debate has cost us more than it has taught us.</p>
-
-<p>The church did not need another position on women. It needed to become a place where the last are actually first, where authority looks like a basin and a towel, where the daughters in the pews are formed by watching sacrifice and not by hearing arguments. We have given them the arguments. We owe them the sacrifice. Until we pay it, the debate is just noise we make while the actual work goes undone.</p>`,
