-    body: `<p>I want to write about something I have watched from the outside for twenty years of pastoring and from the inside of a house with five sons, though never from the inside of the experience itself, which I need to say plainly at the start. I have never carried a child, never nursed one through a fever at three in the morning while my own name slowly went quiet in my mouth. My wife has. I have watched it happen to her and to hundreds of women in my congregation, and I have watched the particular way the world and, God help us, the church tell a mother that the price of loving her children well is to disappear.</p>
-
-<h2>Two versions of the same lie</h2>
-
-<p>The secular version is loud. It tells a woman that her worth is measured in output, in career advancement, in visible achievement, and that the years given to small children are years subtracted from a real life, a gap on a resume, an interruption of the self she is supposed to be building. Under this account, motherhood is a threat to identity, a thing that shrinks you, and the honest thing to do is to minimize it and get back to the work that actually counts.</p>
-
-<p>The church has a version too, and this is the one I am more responsible for, because it wears my clothes. In its worst form it tells a woman the opposite thing that arrives at the same place. It tells her that motherhood is her entire identity, that a godly woman is defined completely and without remainder by her children and her home, that ambition beyond the domestic sphere is a spiritual failure, and that to have a self apart from her role as mother is a kind of selfishness. The secular lie says a mother is not enough. The pious lie says a mother is nothing but. Both tell her to disappear. One tells her to disappear into her career and one tells her to disappear into her family, and both agree that the actual woman, the one who existed before the children and will exist after they leave, is not supposed to remain visible.</p>
-
-<p>I have preached closer to the second lie than I am comfortable admitting. I have praised the mothers in my congregation in ways that flattened them, that reduced a whole human being made in the image of God to a function she performs for others. I meant it as honor. It landed, for some of them, as erasure.</p>
-
-<p>I want to be precise about how the pious version does its damage, because it rarely announces itself as a lie. It arrives dressed as praise. It says a mother's love is the highest calling, which is a beautiful thing to say and half true, and then it quietly closes the door on any other calling she might have, so that the beautiful sentence becomes a cage. It tells her that to want work beyond the home, or friendship that is hers alone, or a mind kept sharp on something other than the children, is a species of selfishness, a failure to die to self. And because it uses the language of the cross, of dying to self, of pouring out, it is nearly impossible for a devout woman to resist without feeling she is resisting God. That is the cruelty of it. It weaponizes her devotion against her own personhood, and it does so in a vocabulary she cannot question without feeling faithless.</p>
-
-<h2>What the theologians of the self can and cannot see</h2>
-
-<p>The Canadian philosopher Charles Taylor, in his massive work A Secular Age published in 2007, describes what he calls the buffered self, the modern individual sealed off and defined from the inside, whose deepest imperative is to be true to an authentic self discovered within. And the sociologist Robert Bellah, in Habits of the Heart in 1985, named the American disease as expressive individualism, the conviction that the self is a private project to be expressed and never subordinated. These are real diagnoses. They got something right. The pressure a mother feels to protect her identity from being consumed is partly the pressure of a culture that has told her the self is the highest good and anything that costs the self is oppression.</p>
-
-<p>But here is where the secular account runs out of road. It can name the fear of losing the self. It cannot make sense of the possibility that losing the self is sometimes the way to find it. And that possibility sits at the dead center of the Christian faith. "Whoever finds his life will lose it, and whoever loses his life for my sake will find it" (Matthew 10:39). The gospel does not tell a mother that her self does not matter. It tells her that the self is not saved by being hoarded. The secular account got the symptom right, the ache of erasure, and got the anthropology wrong, because it assumes the self grows only by acquisition and never by gift.</p>
-
