-
-<p>So here is the line I want to hold, and it is a narrow one. The love a mother pours into her children is not the disappearance of her identity. It is one of the truest expressions of it, if it is freely given and not coerced, if it flows from a self that is secure in God and not from a self that is being told she has no other worth.</p>
-
-<p>The difference between the pious lie and the gospel is the difference between erasure and self-giving, and it is everything. Erasure says you have no self to give, so pour yourself out until there is nothing left, and call the emptiness holy. Self-giving says you are a beloved image-bearer with a name God knew before your children existed, and out of that fullness you choose to spend yourself, and the spending does not diminish you, because your worth was never in the ledger to begin with. One is the theft of a self. The other is the gift of a self. They can look identical from the outside. A tired mother at midnight looks the same either way. The question is what she believes about herself while she does it, and that belief is the whole difference between a woman being formed and a woman being ground down.</p>
-
-<h2>The years that seem invisible</h2>
