-<p>I want to speak directly to the mother in the invisible years, the ones no one applauds, the years of diapers and repetition and a body that no longer feels like her own and a mind that struggles to finish a thought. The culture tells you these years are subtracted from your real life. I need to tell you the Bible does not agree, and neither does the God it reveals.</p>
-
-<p>The kingdom of God runs on exactly the kind of hidden, unapplauded, repetitive faithfulness that the world cannot see and cannot value. "Truly, I say to you, as you did it to one of the least of these my brothers, you did it to me" (Matthew 25:40). The care poured into a child who cannot thank you, cannot remember it, cannot repay it, is not invisible to God. It is the specific shape of the thing Jesus said he would count on the last day. The world's economy cannot see it because the world's economy only counts what is visible and returnable. God's economy counts precisely what is hidden and given away.</p>
