-<p>The philosopher James K. A. Smith, in his 2009 book Desiring the Kingdom, argues that human beings are formed less by the ideas they consciously hold than by the habits and routines that shape their loves over years of repetition, what he calls liturgies. He is writing about worship and culture, but the point lands with unexpected force on a mother's ordinary day. The repeated, unglamorous rhythms of a home, the meals, the bedtimes, the thousand small acts of care that no one records, are precisely the liturgy by which a child's deepest loves are formed, long before the child can articulate a single belief. The mother in the invisible years is not waiting for her real work to begin. She is doing the most formative work there is, the slow shaping of what a human being will come to love, and she is doing it through exactly the repetition the culture calls mindless. The culture cannot see it because the culture has forgotten that we are formed by liturgies and not by lectures. The mother is running a liturgy, and it is holy, whether or not anyone claps.</p>
