-<h2>And yet</h2>
-
-<p>And yet I refuse to close there, because a certain kind of pastor closes there and uses it to keep a woman in a box. The truth that the invisible years matter to God is not a warrant to tell a mother she may have no other calling, no mind of her own, no work beyond the home, no self that persists. Deborah judged Israel and was called "a mother in Israel" in the same breath (Judges 5:7). The two were not in competition. The woman of Proverbs 31 buys a field and runs a business and speaks with wisdom and is also a mother, and the text presents this as one integrated life, not a woman torn between selves.</p>
-
-<p>The gospel does not ask a mother to disappear. It asks her to be more fully herself, an image-bearer who gives freely because she is already loved, not a servant who empties herself because she has been told she is nothing else. If the church cannot tell the difference between those two things, then the church has been preaching the pious version of the world's lie in the voice of holiness, and the women who have quietly disappeared under our praise deserve an apology I am not sure we have ever offered.</p>
-
-<p>You are not your usefulness. You were a self before you were a mother, and you are a self in the middle of it, and you will be a self when the house is finally quiet. God knew your name before your first child had a heartbeat. Pour yourself out if you choose to, and it is a holy thing to choose. But do it as a gift from someone who is here, not as the vanishing of someone who was told she should never have taken up so much room.</p>`,
