-      "The women were already leading in Acts. The question of women and church leadership is older and more open than the certainties on either side pretend, and the deeper problem is what we think leadership is.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "13 min",
-    body: `<p>I want to take up the question of women and leadership in the church directly, and I want to do it without the confidence that usually accompanies it. On this subject the loudest voices are the surest ones, and I have come to distrust the correlation between volume and truth. Let me start with what is not actually in dispute, because a great deal is not, and then move to what is, and then to the thing underneath both that I think we have missed.</p>
-
-<h2>What is not in dispute</h2>
-
-<p>It is not in dispute that women led in the earliest church. I do not mean this as an argument for any particular position. I mean it as a fact of the text that both sides have to account for. Phoebe was a diakonos, a deacon, of the church at Cenchreae, and the courier of the letter to the Romans (Romans 16:1 to 2). Priscilla taught Apollos (Acts 18:26). Junia was, in Paul's words, "well known among the apostles" (Romans 16:7). The four daughters of Philip the evangelist "prophesied" (Acts 21:9). Women prayed and prophesied in the Corinthian assembly, which is why Paul gives instructions about how, not whether, they should do it (1 Corinthians 11:5). The prophecy Peter quotes at Pentecost is explicit: "your sons and your daughters shall prophesy" (Acts 2:17).</p>
-
-<p>Whatever a church concludes about the office of elder, it has to reckon with the plain fact that women in the New Testament taught, prophesied, served as deacons, hosted churches in their homes, funded the mission, and carried the theology. The image of the early church as a male institution with women in the pews is not a picture the New Testament supports. It is a picture we painted later.</p>
-
-<p>Consider how many of the first churches met in homes owned or hosted by women. There was Lydia, the dealer in purple goods, in whose house the church at Philippi first gathered (Acts 16:15, 16:40). There was Nympha, in whose house a church met in Colossae (Colossians 4:15). There was Mary the mother of John Mark, whose home in Jerusalem was a gathering place for prayer (Acts 12:12). In a world where hosting the assembly meant a measure of oversight and provision and standing within it, the record keeps naming women as the ones who held that place. None of this settles the question of the ordained office. But it means that any honest account of leadership in the earliest church has to begin by admitting how much of the load women were already carrying, in public, with the church's blessing and Luke's plain approval.</p>
-
-<h2>What is in dispute</h2>
-
-<p>What is in dispute is narrower than the volume of the argument suggests. It is the question of the ordained office of elder or pastor, and whether the New Testament restricts it to qualified men. Two texts carry most of the weight. In 1 Corinthians 14:34 Paul writes that "the women should keep silent in the churches." In 1 Timothy 2:12 he writes, "I do not permit a woman to teach or to exercise authority over a man; rather, she is to remain quiet."</p>
-
-<p>The complementarian reads these as normative and permanent, grounded, in the 1 Timothy case, in the order of creation rather than in local circumstance, since Paul immediately appeals to "For Adam was formed first, then Eve" (1 Timothy 2:13). That is the strongest card in the complementarian hand and I will not pretend it is weak. It is not a cultural argument. It reaches back before the fall.</p>
-
-<p>The egalitarian reads these texts as bound to their occasions. The Corinthian silence, she notes, sits three chapters after Paul assumed women were prophesying aloud in the same assembly, which means "keep silent" cannot be absolute or Paul contradicts himself within one letter, so it must address a specific disorder. The Ephesian prohibition, she argues, addresses a specific crisis of false teaching in that city, in which women appear to have been particular targets and carriers of the error, and the appeal to Adam and Eve answers a specific false teaching about creation rather than legislating for all time. New Testament scholars like Gordon Fee and Craig Keener have made this case carefully, and it is not a case built on ignoring the text but on reading it in its situation.</p>
-
-<h2>The order of the question, again</h2>
-
-<p>I said it in another place and I will say it here because it governs everything: this is a second-order question. The creeds do not settle it. Chalcedon does not settle it. Faithful, Bible-believing, gospel-preaching churches have landed on both sides for centuries and remained the church. That fact should make all of us slower and gentler than we tend to be. When a complementarian speaks of an egalitarian church as though it had denied the faith, or an egalitarian speaks of a complementarian church as though it were merely bigotry in a collar, both have made a second-order question do first-order work, and both are wrong to.</p>
-
-<p>I hold a view. I lean toward the fuller participation of women, including in teaching and, in my reading, in the eldership, for reasons drawn from the whole sweep of Scripture and the clear leadership of women within it. And the strongest objection to my view, which I have not fully dissolved, remains that appeal to creation in 1 Timothy 2:13, which does not read like a cultural aside. An honest man holds his position with that unanswered pressure in view and does not pretend the other side is composed of fools. It is not.</p>
-
-<h2>The thing underneath</h2>
-
-<p>But I said there was something underneath both positions, and this is what I most want you to hear. The entire debate assumes we already know what church leadership is, and I am no longer sure we do.</p>
-
-<p>We argue about who gets to lead as though leadership were a possession, a status, a platform, an authority to be distributed. And every time the New Testament touches the subject of leadership among God's people, it does something disorienting. It refuses to define leadership as status and redefines it as service, to the point of reversal. "You know that those who are considered rulers of the Gentiles lord it over them, and their great ones exercise authority over them. But it shall not be so among you. But whoever would be great among you must be your servant" (Mark 10:42 to 43). Jesus, the night before he died, took off his outer garment, wrapped a towel around his waist, and washed his disciples' feet, the task of the lowest slave in the house, and then said, "I have given you an example, that you also should do just as I have done to you" (John 13:15).</p>
-
-<p>The disciples spent that entire evening arguing about who was the greatest. It is almost comic. On the night of the foot washing, at the Last Supper itself, Luke tells us "a dispute also arose among them, as to which of them was to be regarded as the greatest" (Luke 22:24). They could not stop competing for the top even as the Lord knelt at their feet demonstrating that the top was the bottom. And I read that scene and I recognize the whole church, complementarian and egalitarian alike, arguing over who gets the authority while the basin sits unused in the corner.</p>
-
-<p>Lesslie Newbigin, the missionary theologian who spent decades in India before returning to a secularized Britain he no longer recognized, argued that the church's fundamental problem in the modern West was that it had absorbed the culture's account of power and freedom without noticing, so that even its internal debates were being fought on terms borrowed from the world rather than from the gospel. I think that diagnosis lands directly on the leadership question. We have, most of us, an account of leadership drawn from the corporation, the campaign, and the platform, an account in which leadership means visibility, influence, and the authority to direct others. And then we open the New Testament, which defines leadership as the willingness to be the servant and the slave of all, and we quietly keep the corporate definition while arguing about who may hold the corporate office. The debate feels biblical because it uses biblical texts. But the underlying picture of what a leader is has often been imported whole from a world that has never washed a foot in its life.</p>
-
-<h2>What I learned from having no one to imitate</h2>
-
-<p>I did not grow up with a father, which means I did not grow up watching a man lead a home, and when I became a pastor I had no inherited model of what spiritual authority looked like up close. I had to build it from the text and from failure. And the thing I built, slowly, is a conviction that has quietly reordered how I read this whole debate.</p>
-
-<p>The authority that actually forms people is not the authority that is granted by an office. I have known men with the office of elder who could not lead a family out of a burning building, and I have known women with no office at all whose spiritual authority in my congregation was undeniable, unappointed, and real, women whose counsel elders sought, whose prayers moved things, whose presence made a room more honest. The Spirit had granted them an authority no committee had voted on. And I began to suspect that the church's obsession with who may hold the office had blinded us to who actually carries the weight, which is often not the same set of people.</p>
-
-<p>This does not resolve the debate about ordination. I am not pretending it does. A church still has to decide who stands in the pulpit and who governs, and that decision matters and cannot be dodged by spiritualizing it away. But it changes the spirit in which we hold the decision. If leadership is fundamentally a basin and a towel, then the highest ambition available to any Christian, man or woman, ordained or not, is already open to all of them, and no council can grant it or withhold it. The greatest in the kingdom is the servant of all, and that door has no gatekeeper.</p>
-
-<h2>The verdict I will actually stand behind</h2>
-
-<p>So here is where I land, and I hold it against myself first. I want the women in my congregation to lead in every way my reading of Scripture permits, and my reading permits a great deal. But more than I want them to have the office, I want the whole church, myself at the front of the line, to stop treating leadership as a prize and start treating it as a cross. Because a church that gives women the pulpit and keeps the world's account of what a pulpit is for has not been reformed. It has only rearranged the seating. And a church that keeps women from the pulpit while its men grasp at authority like Gentile rulers has denied the very thing the office is supposed to embody.</p>
-
-<p>The women were already leading in Acts. They were leading the way the kingdom leads, by serving, prophesying, hosting, funding, teaching, dying. The question was never really whether women could lead. The question is whether any of us, women or men, have understood what leading in this kingdom costs. The basin is still in the corner. It has been there the whole time. We keep stepping over it to argue about the pulpit.</p>`,
