-      "A generation of women was handed a theology of the body that taught them to be ashamed of it or afraid of it. The gospel has something different to say, and the difference is not a slogan.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "12 min",
-    body: `<p>I need to write carefully here, because this subject has left real wounds, and I have watched women in my congregation carry them for decades. I am not a counselor, and nothing I write is a substitute for the help of a good one, which some readers of this will need and should seek. What I want to offer is a theological account of the body that is truer than the two false accounts a lot of us were handed, one from the culture and one, to our shame, from the church.</p>
-
-<h2>Two accounts of the body, both false</h2>
-
-<p>The culture's account of a woman's body is that it is a project and a product, endlessly to be improved, displayed, and evaluated, its worth set by how closely it approximates an image she can never actually reach because the image is manufactured and moves. A generation raised on filtered photographs and metrics has been taught to look at their own bodies from the outside, as objects to be judged, and to find them always wanting. This is not vanity. It is a wound the culture inflicts and then sells the cure for.</p>
-
-<p>The church, in the movement that came to be called purity culture, roughly from the mid-1990s through the 2000s, offered a counter-account that landed, for many women, as its own kind of wound. In its worst expressions it taught that a woman's body was primarily a source of danger, a thing to be covered and controlled so as not to cause men to stumble, and that her value was bound up with a virginity framed as a physical prize that could be given away and thereby diminish her. The metaphors did the damage. A woman was compared to a piece of tape that loses its stickiness with each use, to a chewed piece of gum, to a rose passed around a youth group until it arrived at the last hand crushed and soiled. I have heard those object lessons taught. I did not teach them, but I stood in rooms where they were taught and said nothing, and my silence was its own kind of teaching.</p>
-
-<p>Here is what those two accounts, the cultural and the churchly, share underneath their apparent opposition. Both locate a woman's worth in her body's condition and its effect on others. One says her worth rises as her body approaches an image. The other says her worth falls as her body is touched. Both make her a body to be evaluated. Both taught women to relate to their own flesh through shame and fear. The one dressed the lesson in advertising and the other dressed it in Scripture, and that second costume is the more culpable, because it took the name of God in vain to teach a lie.</p>
-
-<p>There was a further cruelty in the way the church's version assigned responsibility. In the worst of the purity teaching, a girl was made the custodian of the thoughts of the boys and men around her. If a man lusted, the question raised was whether she had dressed to provoke it, whether her body had caused him to stumble. This is a moral inversion so complete it is hard to see once you are inside it. Jesus, addressing the very subject of lust, placed the entire weight of responsibility on the one doing the lusting. "But I say to you that everyone who looks at a woman with lustful intent has already committed adultery with her in his heart" (Matthew 5:28). He did not say, and let the woman take care not to be looked at. He did not turn to the women in the crowd and assign them the management of the male gaze. He turned to the men and told them the problem was in their own hearts and eyes, so much so that he used the shocking image of tearing out the offending eye. Purity culture took a command Jesus aimed squarely at men and pointed it back at the bodies of girls. That is not a small misreading. It reverses the direction of the entire teaching.</p>
-
-<h2>What the doctrine of creation actually says</h2>
-
-<p>Against both accounts stands the first thing the Bible says about the human body, which is that God made it and called it good. "So God created man in his own image, in the image of God he created him, male and female he created them" (Genesis 1:27). And at the end of the sixth day, "God saw everything that he had made, and behold, it was very good" (Genesis 1:31). The body is not the enemy of the soul. It is not a lower thing the spirit is trapped inside. That idea, that the material body is a prison the pure spirit longs to escape, is not Christianity. It is Gnosticism, an ancient heresy the church fought and rejected in its first centuries. The church that taught women to be ashamed of their bodies was, whether it knew it or not, teaching a heresy the early church had already condemned.</p>
-
-<p>Christianity is the most embodied of the great faiths. Its central claim is that God took on a body. "And the Word became flesh and dwelt among us" (John 1:14). He did not merely appear to have a body. He had one, hungered in it, wept in it, was tortured in it, and, most scandalously to the ancient mind, rose again in it, a real physical resurrection, the tomb empty, the wounds touchable (John 20:27). And the faith's final hope is not the escape of the soul from the body but the resurrection of the body itself, "the redemption of our bodies" (Romans 8:23). A religion whose God took flesh, died in flesh, rose in flesh, and promises to raise our flesh cannot coherently teach that the flesh is shameful. If your theology of the body left you ashamed of yours, it was not the Christian theology of the body. It was something else wearing the collar.</p>
-
-<p>The early church fought this battle explicitly and won it, and we forgot we had already won it. In the second century, teachers we now group under the name Gnostic taught that the material world was the botched work of a lesser god, that the true self was a spark of spirit trapped in the prison of flesh, and that salvation meant escaping the body altogether. Against them stood Irenaeus of Lyons, who around the year 180 wrote his great work Against Heresies, insisting that the same God made both flesh and spirit and called the flesh good, that Christ redeemed the whole person body and soul, and that the resurrection of the actual body was the church's hope. Tertullian would later put it bluntly, that the flesh is the hinge of salvation, caro salutis est cardo, because it was in the flesh that Christ saved us. The church decided, definitively and long ago, that the body is not the enemy. When a modern teacher tells a woman to be ashamed of her flesh, that teacher is not passing on the historic faith. They are reviving a heresy the church buried eighteen centuries ago, and doing it in the name of holiness.</p>
-
-<h2>The temple, and what it was meant to mean</h2>
-
-<p>Paul does call the body a temple. "Do you not know that your body is a temple of the Holy Spirit within you, whom you have from God?" (1 Corinthians 6:19). Purity culture quoted that verse constantly, and it usually quoted it as a threat, a warning against defilement. But read what a temple is. A temple is not a thing you fear. A temple is the place where the holy God chose to dwell, the most honored structure a people could build, the place of his presence. To call the body a temple is not to say it is fragile and easily ruined. It is to say it is honored, indwelt, made a dwelling place for God himself. Paul is not shaming the body. He is exalting it. We turned the highest possible honor into a source of dread, which is a spectacular inversion of what the text does.</p>
-
-<h2>On virginity, shame, and the actual gospel</h2>
-
-<p>I want to speak plainly to a woman who was taught that a sexual past, whether chosen or, God have mercy, done to her without her consent, has permanently diminished her worth before God. That teaching is not the gospel. It is the direct opposite of the gospel. The entire message of Christianity is that no one is defined by their past and no one is beyond the reach of a grace that makes all things new. "If anyone is in Christ, he is a new creation. The old has passed away; behold, the new has come" (2 Corinthians 5:17). There is no asterisk on that verse. It does not read "unless." Grace does not restore a discounted version of you. It makes you new.</p>
-
-<p>And to the woman who was violated and was made to feel, by a culture or a church or her own tormented conscience, that the violation left a stain on her, I say this with everything I have: what was done to you against your will is not a sin you committed, and it did not diminish your standing before God by so much as a single degree. The shame belongs entirely to the one who acted, not to you. Any teaching that put that shame on you was a lie, and it was a cruel one, and I am sorry it was ever spoken in the name of the God who is nearer to the brokenhearted than to anyone else. "The LORD is near to the brokenhearted and saves the crushed in spirit" (Psalm 34:18).</p>
-
-<h2>Where healing actually begins</h2>
-
-<p>Healing from these wounds does not come from a slogan, and I will not insult you with one. It usually comes slowly, often with the help of a wise counselor, in a community that tells you the truth about your worth until you can begin to believe it, and in a long and patient re-learning of what God actually thinks about the body he made and indwells. That re-learning is real theological work, not a feeling to be conjured, and it is worth doing.</p>
-
-<p>But it begins with a single foundational fact that both the culture and the church too often obscured. Your worth was never located in your body's appearance or your body's history. It was located, from before you were born, in the God who made you in his image and calls that image very good. "For you formed my inward parts; you knitted me together in my mother's womb. I praise you, for I am fearfully and wonderfully made" (Psalm 139:13 to 14). That was written of your body, the one you have been taught to be at war with. It is fearfully and wonderfully made. It was knitted by God. It is not your enemy, and it never was.</p>
-
-<p>The lie was that your body was a problem to be managed, by improvement or by concealment. The truth is that your body is a good gift, made and indwelt and destined to be raised. A generation of women was handed the lie in two costumes and told to choose. The gospel offers a third thing, older than both, that neither the marketers nor the moralists could see, because it required believing that God became a body and was not ashamed. He was not ashamed of a body. Do not let anyone teach you to be ashamed of yours.</p>`,
-  },
-  {
-    title: "Women and Doubt: The Questions They Weren't Allowed to Ask",
-    slug: "women-and-doubt",
-    excerpt:
-      "In a lot of churches the men were allowed to wrestle and the women were expected to keep the peace. But doubt is not a failure of faith. It is often faith refusing to lie.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "12 min",
-    body: `<p>I came to faith from atheism, which means I arrived with doubt as my native tongue. I did not learn to doubt inside the church. I brought a lifetime of it in the door with me, and one of the first things I noticed, once I was inside, was that the church did not seem to know what to do with a person who kept asking hard questions after they were supposed to have stopped. And I noticed something else, quieter and more troubling. The permission to keep asking was not distributed evenly. The men in my early churches were allowed a certain amount of wrestling, valorized even, the doubting Thomas who becomes strong. The women, in a lot of the same rooms, were expected to be the ones who kept the peace, held the family's faith together, and did not rock the boat with their questions.</p>
-
-<h2>The unequal distribution of permission</h2>
-
-<p>I want to be careful, because this was rarely stated as a rule. No one handed the women a document telling them not to doubt. It came through the air. It came through who got invited to the theology discussion and who was thanked for bringing the casserole. It came through the assumption that a woman's spiritual role was to nurture and sustain the faith of others, her husband, her children, which quietly implied that her own faith had to be always intact and available, never in crisis, never in question, because too many people were leaning on it. A doubting man was a man wrestling with God. A doubting woman was a crack in the foundation of a home.</p>
-
-<p>So the women learned to doubt in private. I have sat with more women than I can count who confessed, often with genuine fear, questions they had been carrying for years, sometimes decades, and had never spoken aloud in a church, because they had absorbed the lesson that a good Christian woman does not have these questions, or if she has them, she keeps them to herself and prays them away. They doubted the goodness of God after a child died. They doubted the justice of God reading certain passages about women. They doubted whether any of it was true at all, in the small hours, and then got up and led the family devotions because that was their assigned role. The doubt did not make them faithless. In many cases the doubt was the most honest part of their faith, and they had been taught to be ashamed of it.</p>
-
-<p>I think I understand part of how this happened, and it is not always malice. A church leans on its most faithful members, and its most faithful members are very often its women. They teach the children, they organize the meals, they carry the prayer lists, they hold the emotional weather of a congregation. And a community that depends on someone tends, without deciding to, to need that person to be solid, because if the load-bearing wall shows a crack the whole structure feels the danger. So the women who bore the most weight were, precisely because they bore it, the ones least permitted to wobble. Their strength became a trap. The very reliability that made them indispensable made their doubt feel, to everyone including themselves, like a threat to the building. And so they carried their questions the way you carry a secret illness, alone, afraid of what the disclosure would cost the people leaning on you.</p>
-
-<h2>Doubt is not the opposite of faith</h2>
-
-<p>Here is the theological correction underneath the pastoral one. The church has often treated doubt as the opposite of faith, as though the two were enemies and the presence of one proved the absence of the other. That is not how the Bible treats doubt at all.</p>
-
-<p>The Psalms are full of it, and they are Scripture, inspired and preserved for our instruction. "My God, my God, why have you forsaken me? Why are you so far from saving me?" (Psalm 22:1). Those are not the words of settled faith. They are the words of a man who cannot find God and says so to God's face, and Jesus quoted them from the cross. The book of Job spends thirty-eight chapters letting a man accuse God of injustice, and when God finally speaks, he does not rebuke Job for the questions. He rebukes Job's friends, the ones who defended God with easy answers, and says of Job, the one who raged, "you have not spoken of me what is right, as my servant Job has" (Job 42:7). Read that slowly. God preferred the honest accusation of the sufferer to the tidy theology of the defenders. Habakkuk opens his book demanding, "O LORD, how long shall I cry for help, and you will not hear?" (Habakkuk 1:2). These are canonical doubts. God put them in the book.</p>
-
-<p>And when a father brings his sick son to Jesus and Jesus says all things are possible for one who believes, the man cries out the truest sentence any doubter ever prayed: "I believe; help my unbelief!" (Mark 9:24). Jesus does not send him away to sort out his faith first. He heals the boy. Faith and doubt were living in the same sentence, in the same man, in the same breath, and Jesus met the faith without despising the doubt.</p>
-
-<p>Even Thomas, whose name we have turned into an insult, was not rebuked the way we imagine. He said he would not believe the resurrection unless he could put his hand in the wounds, and a week later Jesus appeared and offered him exactly that, held out the wounds and invited the touch (John 20:27). Christ did not shame the demand for evidence. He met it. And notice that the church which passed down that Gospel had no interest in hiding its own founders' doubts. Matthew, describing the very moment of the Great Commission on the mountain, writes that when they saw the risen Lord "they worshiped him, but some doubted" (Matthew 28:17). Doubted, at the resurrection, with the risen Christ standing in front of them, and the text simply reports it and moves on to the commission, given to the worshiping and the doubting alike. If the eleven could doubt in the presence of the risen Christ and still be sent to make disciples of all nations, then the woman doubting quietly in a pew is in company far better than the company that shamed her.</p>
-
-<h2>The scholars who took the ache seriously</h2>
-
-<p>The philosopher Charles Taylor, in A Secular Age in 2007, argued that we now live in what he called the immanent frame, a way of experiencing the world in which belief in God is no longer the default and doubt is available to everyone at every moment, believer and unbeliever alike. He calls this the condition of cross-pressure, the sense of being pulled in two directions at once, and he insists it is now the common weather, not a personal failing. A woman doubting in a pew is not spiritually weaker than her grandmother. She is living later, in a world where the doubt is simply nearer to hand for everyone. To shame her for the cross-pressure is to shame her for the century she was born into.</p>
-
-<p>Tim Keller, in The Reason for God in 2008, made a point that I have leaned on for years. He argued that a faith without doubts is like a body without antibodies, untested, and therefore fragile in a way it does not know. "A faith without some doubts," he wrote, "is like a human body without any antibodies in it." The faith that has never questioned anything is not stronger than the faith that has wrestled. It is more brittle, because it has never been under load. The women who were taught to suppress their questions were not being protected. They were being handed a faith that had never been stress-tested, and then blamed if it cracked.</p>
-
-<h2>What we did, naming it plainly</h2>
-
-<p>I have to say we here, because I have been part of the machinery. I have led rooms where the men's doubts were treated as intellectual seriousness and the women's were treated as emotional instability, and I did not see it at the time, and that not seeing was its own indictment. I have thanked a woman for her steady faith in a way that told her, without my meaning to, that her steadiness was her assignment and her questions were unwelcome. The church did not usually forbid women to doubt with a rule. We did something quieter and more effective. We made it socially costly, and then we called the resulting silence peace.</p>
-
-<p>The cost of that silence was not only to the women. A church that only hears its men's questions is a church half-thinking, missing the interrogation of exactly the readers who most needed the faith to be true, the mothers holding grief, the daughters reading the hard texts about their own place. When we silenced the women's doubts, we did not protect the faith. We amputated part of the church's capacity to think, and we told the amputees it was for their own good.</p>
-
-<h2>To the woman doubting in the dark</h2>
-
-<p>So let me say directly to the woman who has been carrying questions she has never been allowed to speak. Your doubt is not a betrayal of your faith. It may be the most faithful thing about you, because it means you refuse to pretend, and God has never once asked you to pretend. He put Job's rage in the Bible. He put the psalmist's abandonment in the Bible. He answered "help my unbelief" with healing and not with a lecture. The God you are questioning is not fragile, and he is not offended by your honesty. He is far more grieved by the pious performance that hides the ache than by the ache itself.</p>
-
-<p>You were not put here to be the emotional caretaker of everyone else's faith while your own goes unexamined and unfed. You are a thinking, questioning image-bearer of God, and your questions belong in the church, spoken out loud, taken seriously, sat with by people who will not rush to resolve them because they are afraid of them. The church that could not hold your doubt was too small. It was not your faith that failed. It was our courage.</p>
-
-<p>Keep the question inside the faith rather than leaving to keep it. The doubt is not the door out. It can be the way in, because a God who is true has nothing to fear from an honest question, and the church that finally learns this will discover it was the doubters, all along, who were taking God the most seriously. They were the ones who refused to lie about him. That was never the weak faith. We only called it that because it frightened us.</p>`,
-  },
-  {
-    title: "The Proverbs 31 Woman They Turned Into a Checklist",
-    slug: "proverbs-31-checklist",
-    excerpt:
-      "The eshet chayil is not a to-do list for exhausted women. She is a woman of valor, a warrior word, and the poem was written to be sung over women, not held against them.",
-    pillar: "Integrated Life",
-    readTime: "12 min",
-    body: `<p>There is a passage of Scripture that has been used to exhaust more women than perhaps any other in the Bible, and it was never meant to. Proverbs 31, the closing poem of the book, the portrait beginning "An excellent wife who can find? She is far more precious than jewels" (Proverbs 31:10). I have watched women read it and wilt. I have watched it printed on the walls of women's ministry rooms as a standard to reach, a list of everything a godly woman is supposed to be doing all at once, and I have watched it land as an accusation. She rises while it is still night. She works with willing hands. She plants a vineyard. She makes her own clothing. She is never idle. And the woman reading it, running on four hours of sleep with a sink full of dishes, hears one thing: you are not enough, and here is thirty verses of proof.</p>
-
-<p>That reading is not just discouraging. It is a mistranslation of the poem's whole purpose, and I want to show you why by going back to the Hebrew and to the shape of the text, because when you see what it actually is, it stops being a weapon and becomes what it was always meant to be, a blessing.</p>
-
-<h2>Eshet chayil, a woman of valor</h2>
-
-<p>The poem opens, in Hebrew, with the phrase eshet chayil. Our English versions render it "excellent wife" or "virtuous woman," and those translations are not wrong exactly, but they are pale. The word chayil is a warrior word. It is used throughout the Old Testament for military strength, for armies, for might, for valor in battle. When it describes Gideon, it is translated "mighty man of valor" (Judges 6:12). When it describes the men David gathered, it means warriors, men of strength. It is a word of force and courage and power.</p>
-
-<p>So eshet chayil does not mean a tidy, compliant homemaker. It means a woman of valor. A mighty woman. A woman of strength and force. The same word used for warriors is used for her, and it is used deliberately. The poet is not describing a delicate domestic ideal. The poet is describing a woman with the strength of an army, and the whole poem that follows is an unfolding of what her valor looks like in the ordinary theater of a life.</p>
-
-<p>The Jewish tradition understood this. In observant Jewish homes, Proverbs 31 is sung, not assigned. On Friday evening, at the beginning of the Sabbath, the husband sings this poem to his wife, aloud, as a blessing over her, a song of praise and honor for the woman she is. It is not a performance review. It is a serenade. It was never meant to be a standard she has to meet by Monday. It was meant to be a song sung over her, a naming of her strength, a public honoring of her valor. Somewhere between that Sabbath table and the American women's ministry wall, we turned a husband's love song into a manager's checklist, and we should grieve the loss of everything that transformation cost.</p>
-
-<p>Notice, too, where this poem sits in the book. Proverbs is bracketed by two female figures. It opens with Lady Wisdom, personified Wisdom, a woman who calls out in the streets, who was beside God at the creation of the world, "then I was beside him, like a master workman" (Proverbs 8:30). And it closes with this woman of valor. The book that has spent thirty-one chapters commending wisdom to a young man ends by embodying that wisdom in a woman, so that the eshet chayil is not merely a good wife but a living portrait of Wisdom herself walking around in an ordinary life, buying fields and feeding her household and opening her mouth with instruction. To read her as a domestic checklist is to miss that she is the book's final and fullest picture of what a life of wisdom actually looks like. She is the answer to the whole book, and the answer wears the face of a woman.</p>
-
-<h2>The shape of the poem tells you it is a song</h2>
-
-<p>There is a structural feature of Proverbs 31 that most English readers never see, because it cannot survive translation. The poem is an acrostic. Its twenty-two verses each begin, in order, with a successive letter of the Hebrew alphabet, aleph to tav, the first letter to the last. This is a poetic form, a deliberate literary architecture, the same form used in several of the Psalms and in the book of Lamentations.</p>
-
-<p>An acrostic is not a to-do list. No one writes a checklist as an alphabetic poem. The acrostic form signals completeness, an A-to-Z portrait, a way of saying this is the full picture, the whole of a life, sung in a form that is meant to be beautiful and memorable and total. The very structure of the passage tells you it is a work of praise, a poem, a song, and not a set of tasks to be audited against a woman's Tuesday. When you read it as a checklist, you are reading a wedding toast as if it were a job description, and no wonder it exhausts.</p>
-
-<h2>The scholar who reintroduced the valor</h2>
-
-<p>The writer Rachel Held Evans, in her 2012 book A Year of Biblical Womanhood, did the church a real service on this point, whatever else one makes of her broader work. She spent time learning from Jewish women who told her that in their tradition, eshet chayil is not an unreachable standard but a blessing women speak over one another. A friend finishes a hard degree, and the other women say eshet chayil, woman of valor. A mother gets through a brutal week, and her friends say eshet chayil. It is spoken as praise, as recognition, as honor, in real time, over ordinary faithfulness. It is the Hebrew equivalent of "well done, you mighty thing," said between women who see each other's strength.</p>
-
-<p>That is what the phrase was for. It was a way of catching each other doing something valorous and naming it out loud. It was never a bar set impossibly high and then held over a woman's head to prove her inadequate. We took a phrase women were meant to speak over each other in celebration and turned it into a phrase men could hold against women in judgment. That is nearly the exact opposite of its purpose, and the reversal is on us.</p>
-
-<p>There is one other woman in Scripture called by this exact phrase, and she is worth knowing, because she demolishes the domestic-checklist reading all by herself. In the book of Ruth, Boaz says to Ruth, "for all my fellow townsmen know that you are a worthy woman" (Ruth 3:11). The Hebrew there is eshet chayil, the very phrase from Proverbs 31, a woman of valor. And who is Ruth at that moment? She is a foreigner, a Moabite, a childless widow with no property and no security, a woman who has gleaned in the fields at the edges of other people's harvests to keep herself and her mother-in-law alive. She has not planted a vineyard. She does not run a household of servants. She owns nothing. And she is named a woman of valor because of her loyalty, her courage, her refusal to abandon Naomi, her willingness to risk everything on the kindness of a God she barely knew. The Bible attaches the phrase eshet chayil not to a woman who has completed a domestic to-do list but to a destitute immigrant whose valor was entirely a matter of character. If Ruth is a woman of valor, then the phrase was never about the checklist. It was always about the heart.</p>
-
-<h2>What she actually is</h2>
-
-<p>Now look at what the woman of valor actually does when you read her as a portrait rather than a mandate. She is not confined to a narrow sphere. "She considers a field and buys it; with the fruit of her hands she plants a vineyard" (Proverbs 31:16). She is engaged in real estate and agriculture, making economic decisions with independent judgment. "She perceives that her merchandise is profitable" (Proverbs 31:18). She runs a business; the text says she trades. "She opens her hand to the poor and reaches out her hands to the needy" (Proverbs 31:20). She has a public ministry of mercy. "She opens her mouth with wisdom, and the teaching of kindness is on her tongue" (Proverbs 31:26). She is a teacher of wisdom, torat chesed, the law of kindness, on her tongue.</p>
-
-<p>This is not a shrinking domestic figure with no world beyond her doorstep. She is a businesswoman, a landowner, a manufacturer, a philanthropist, a teacher, and a mother and wife, and the poem presents all of it as one integrated life, held together without apology and without the sense that any part is in competition with another. The woman the church sometimes uses to shame ambitious women out of the public sphere is, in the actual text, a woman running enterprises and speaking wisdom in the gate. We did not just turn her into a checklist. We turned her into a smaller woman than the poem describes, and then used the shrunken version to keep women small.</p>
-
-<h2>The verse everyone skips</h2>
-
-<p>And here is the verse the checklist readers always skip, the one that dismantles the entire performance reading if you let it. Near the end, after all the labor and the valor, the poem says, "Charm is deceitful, and beauty is vain, but a woman who fears the LORD is to be praised" (Proverbs 31:30). The whole thing does not finally rest on her productivity at all. It rests on her fear of the LORD, her relationship with God, the reverent trust that is the beginning of wisdom in this very book (Proverbs 1:7). Everything else, the rising early, the vineyard, the trading, flows out of that one thing and is not a substitute for it. Strip away the fear of the LORD and the valor has no root. Keep the fear of the LORD and the specific tasks were never the point.</p>
-
-<p>The poem is not saying, do all these things and then you will be worthy. It is saying, here is what a life rooted in the reverence of God can look like, sung in praise, and the root is the reverence, not the tally. A woman who does none of the specific things listed, who does not sew or trade or plant, but who fears the LORD, has the one thing the poem says finally matters. The checklist gets that exactly backward, making the works the foundation and the God an afterthought, which is the same reversal that turns all of grace into all of law.</p>
-
-<h2>The verdict</h2>
-
-<p>So I want to take the poem back from the way we have used it. It was a husband's Sabbath song, sung over a woman to honor her. It was a warrior word, eshet chayil, naming her strength like an army's. It was an acrostic, a complete and beautiful portrait, not a punch list. It was a picture of a woman with a full public life and a business and a ministry and a mouth full of wisdom, not a woman confined to a kitchen. And it ended by saying that none of the labor was the point, only the fear of the LORD underneath it.</p>
-
-<p>We took all of that and made it a stick to beat tired women with. I have watched it done, and I have stood by while it was done, and the women who wilted under it deserved the song and got the checklist instead. If your church handed you Proverbs 31 as a standard you were failing to meet, your church handed you the poem upside down. Turn it back over. It was always meant to be sung to you, not held against you. You are the woman of valor. Someone was supposed to be telling you so this whole time. Let me be the one who finally does. Eshet chayil. You mighty thing. Well done.</p>`,
-  },
-];
